   485	- **The Deepstacks.** Inside the iron door: a sliding bar; **twenty-seven steps of the dark stone in three flights of nine**; a cross-passage; three galleries side by side, cut in the bluff's pale bedded rock (the rock was cut first; the dark stone laid against it second; the town's mortared work third — Karis's first finding, undated). **The middle gallery** is about a hundred and ten strides, shelved to the roof with nothing ticketed; sorting tables from ninety strides; Cleon's sloped desk at the end under a fixed lamp on an iron arm, with a shelf above it (a cup, a jar, a thick bundle tied both ways in brown tape, and a black oak plank in sacking). The eastern gallery holds the old town's tall tally-books; the western, quarrymen's gear. **The fifty-two sheets lie on three cleared tables, 11 / 29 / 12 (the Ring, the Stair, the Hall), as they lay on the floors, and stay there; the boxes go up at the clerk's bell each evening and come down each morning on a fresh card.**
   486	- **Cleon.** Protected, all his: "I know that hand." (of the Hall's fourth sheet); "Being believed — … It's the end of being able to be wrong."; "Give me a week." (the 284th); "They're orders."; the two-part limit ("I can tell you these are orders. I can't tell you what they order…"; "It doesn't work on a river, and it doesn't work on a man, and it doesn't work on a tree… It costs me my eyes for an hour after and nothing else."); "Same make."; and the 287th close ("documentation… built the same way twice… never once slept well since."), in which he uses Karis's name (he heard Brom say it). Karis: "Older than [UNBOUND]." **His Path has been shown from OUTSIDE only**: he looks at a made thing from its foot upward, without blinking, and says which signs BEAR (white), which are CARRIED (black) and which are NOTHING (red, packing); his plank's four rows are the same lines as rows on the Hall's fourth, the Stair's ninth and twenty-first, and the Ring's sixth sheets. **On the 287th he set his own week to run from that day to the 293rd, with everything else put aside**; at its end every sheet goes up. The bundle on his shelf ("Paper. Not stone.", from the same shelf as the plank) is tied and unread. He has not noticed Cael, who holds his lamp ("a hand to the left"); he knows nothing of the alley, the fragments or the mechanism.
   487	- **The decipherment (Karis alone).** Of the forty-one kinds of sign: **seven bear, twenty-three are carried, eleven are nothing**; the carried are what a word is spelled in. The plank's cutter counted each row in figures (a ring is five, a peck one) and then wrote the number out in words, in the old town's tongue or its near kin; she proved it against the old tally-books by pattern alone before looking. **Fourteen of twenty-three letters by the 290th; she reads numbers and number-words only**, has refused to guess one tempting six-sign word, has not untied the bundle, and has not shown Cleon Vastin's copied page (it is in her coat). **The leaf has not appeared.**
   488	- **The yard door, the 285th.** The clerk's form had no line for matter that had gone up and come back; he asked one question ("The same?") and was about to send up for the under-registrar. **Vastin gave his old name, rank and office aloud** (protected: "Vastin. Archmarshal, Compact enforcement. You'll find the name is —"); the clerk's pen came up and **wrote nothing of it**. **Seln, posing as a porter on the hatch's bench, answered truthfully** (protected, whole: "He's on the count. Four hands, same as yesterday, entered by the practitioner and answered for by her, and the form asks what a hand's for, not what he was. The researcher's at your counter and she'll want to be told we're through,"). The clerk wrote *The same* and *four*, and has done so each day since. **The cost: a Treswick registry clerk now knows Seln's face and voice.** "I'm sorry." (Vastin) / "Don't be sorry. Be nobody." (Seln). Seln watches from the roof above the cut's head from then on. No restricted name has been written.
   489	- **Brom (the 286th)** righted a broker's dray off a lad's foot in the registry's yard with his back and **no Path opened**, wrenching the LEFT (good) shoulder; a broker's man offered a guard's post inside the second wall (a sealed monthly pass, a bed, the house's coat, four marks and found; "Ask for the factor."); "I'll think on it."; he took up his box on the bound RIGHT shoulder ("Cart was heavier"), and carries it there — **a debt Cael has entered, owed at the mill to two healers**. "You think Karis is right?…" / "Good. I'd hate to have turned down a real job for a wrong hunch." His two toes were done on the 289th.
   490	- **Seln's night walk (the 286th night):** from a warehouse roof below the blue gate he looked at the registry, its three doors (the front for names; the yard for loads; a returns hatch at the side where a courier may lay a sealed thing unasked) and the station's light, and at the case on his chest; **he decided nothing**. "I looked at it." The case is **shut** (never write "unopened": this edition's Book 6 opened it for the five); its contents are named nowhere in this book. He has seen the same watcher three times (horseman's boots with the spur-rests cut away).
   491	- **Vastin** went toward the lamp four times on the 287th, twice on the 288th, not at all on the 290th. "How long did it take you." / "I was fourteen. I'm nineteen. I'm not there yet either." (protected).
   492	- **The alley (the 290th dusk)** — the cut of twenty-two steps (an iron rail at its mouth). Six, hooded, faces tied: two came in by the yard gate with shod staves and barred it; four held the head (a crossbow, a stave, one who came down to the plane, and a signaller with a shuttered lantern). **Vastin turned uphill and struck Iron Wall on the ninth step, wall to wall** — "In that place there is no round." Two bolts broke on it. Nobody else declared: Brom tipped the loose third tread and let it go, and sat on his box; Lira dropped from the landing and put her knife into a stave's shaft (her arm touched nothing; the right shoulder took the wall); **Cael went one step up and stood square at the plane's foot, the reason, for a minute and some (eighty-one counts)**; Seln's weighted cord from the roof bound the signaller's arm to the rail and the lantern was never shown. They left at a walk: none taken, no faces, nobody dead; a shortened watchman's stave and two plain bolts left behind.
   493	- **THE ELEVENTH.** In that quiet minute **Cael acquired Iron-Wall-adjacent, from Vastin** — it did not fill, it *set*, "as a post is stood in its hole"; its prompt is *This far?*; he has told it no each time. **Not deployed. Not told to Vastin.** The protected lines are Cael's own cataloguing, printed once in italics (not a system block): "Iron-Wall-adjacent. Vastin. Partial. Iron family. Held. … Tier equivalent unknown. Deployment: none. Eleventh." **Eleven fragments; deployment since the Line: thirty-two.** Vastin: "I put the wall in front of it anyway. I'd like that entered…" ("it" is what Cael is, which he could not read) / "It's entered," — and Cael has entered more than the man knows: **a debt he cannot yet pay** ("Not told. I can't tell the taking without telling the rest, and the rest is six people's"). Lira has read the log.
   494	- **Costs.** **Karis's LEFT palm** cut on a step's edge: seven stitches, Brom's, bound in a strip of his shirt (no healer inside the gate will touch a hand without writing a name; she writes with the right). **Vastin's right knee has gone again on the eleventh day of Oryn's thirty** (a wall on it, against her order, which he heard word for word and asks that she be told): a lath cracked, re-splinted with a slat from Brom's box, twice its size; on the night of the 290th he cannot stand or hold a bowl. Brom: both shoulders. Lira: the right shoulder bruised; day twenty-five out of wool.
   495	- **Seln's finding (a finding, not a fact):** the six opened no Path because they cannot be written down in that town either; they wanted one of the crew to declare, a light shown for it to a watcher below, and a box carried off; they saw a wall and a rock and nothing of quiet ground or what Cael carries. **They know the loft and have shown that they know** (the watcher stood in the light of the painted door). Cael has ruled only that the cut is not climbed at dusk again.
   496	- **Money:** a second week of the loft paid on the 289th (to the 295th): **purse 134 marks**. **Names:** none minted. **Length:** about 36,000 against 35,000, accepted.
