/bin/zsh -lc "nl -ba editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/STATE_LEDGER.md | sed -n '839,863p'" in /Users/drive/fractured-path-monroe13
 succeeded in 0ms:
   839	- **The seep (48:131–143; 48:147–209; 48:213–285; 48:289–311).** A fold in the ridge's southern flank; a band of dark shale across it; a weeping ledge and a trodden pan under the band's western end; nineteen shale-backs, four of them calves. Teague: they lick the wet at first light and at dusk and mow along the band between; a Force shifted a block under a ledge herd on the cold road in Pike's time and "they found two mules" (this is Book 7's tale, with Teague now saying that he has stood where it was done). His rule for the fold: in by the west along the band's foot, never on the fall of the hill under a beast, two at a time, three steps and a stand, nobody steps quick near a calf. A skin takes more than six minutes at the drip. **The tall man stops the boy with a flat hand on the breastbone (48:197–209).** The read finds a bucket of water behind a loose leaf of shale; Pressure is wanted and refused; Brom draws the leaf with both arms; the tall man lifts his free hand from his knee and brings it down once. The old cow stands over them for the last skins. The mule drinks four skins from Oryn's canvas; the tall man and then the boy fetch four more at dusk; the bow takes one skin out of the boy's hand. Seln finds a keg's hoop and five made heels at the pan, the newest a week old: "a posting sends for its water."
   840	- **The name (49:77–83).** Teague has had it from three carters on a salt road two days north that looks down into the place from far off: a hole where the country's water goes, and a span over it, sunk in the dish. "The Sunken Span." He calls the hole "the sink".
   841	- **The fourth place's ground (49:85–97; 49:127–145; 50:3–9; 50:155; 50:231; 51:203–207; 51:261–267).** A dry ravine comes down from the north; for a hundred yards it runs along the outside of the fence, and the level strip between its lip and the irons is the shelf (a cart wide; the drop three men deep on the left hand going east; the irons on the right; the line a stride past them). Two squared timbers on trestles at the shelf's narrowest, forty yards in from its mouth; behind them five strides of width, a hut of turf and stone with a blanket for a door, a fire, a keg, a hide weighted with stones; at the east end the ravine bends inward and its lip comes to within a stride and a half of the last bar; beyond that gap a trodden path a pace wide goes on round the fence. The ravine cuts the fence there (the bars stop on one lip and begin on the other, twelve yards off) and runs to the middle. **The Span:** two piers of the grey rising out of the ravine's rock walls, with no course and no join, and an arch in one piece; on its back the grey comes up out of the turf a cart wide, with no kerb and no step, rises to a crown and goes down into the grass again; no wheel has marked it. Twenty yards beyond the arch the ravine's bed ends in a round hole as wide as a cart is long, with no bottom to be seen.
   842	- **The watch (49:95–111; 49:223–255; 50:9–27; 50:131–135; 50:219–225; 50:293–299).** Seven, hooded, faces tied even at night among themselves: two crossbows, four shod staves, one empty-handed practitioner whose Path is not named; he makes a point of white light the size of a fist and hangs it over the shelf, and calls, "This ground is posted. Stand where you are, and declare." He keeps the ravine's side of the shelf always and never comes within three yards of the bars. One of the seven has a wet cough; one walks the path two hundred paces east and back. Leaving, they take the broken bow and leave the sound one on the baulk, with a stave and their fire.
   843	- **Seln's reading of the iron (49:187–219).** Three ages in the fence: most bars scaled like the Stair's old stake; here and there a newer one driven into an old hole; and every thirty or forty paces a thicker bar under a heavier mallet's head, whose scale "had scaled again". All three kinds carry thirty-four with the stroke and bar. The path is a groove a hand deep in the shale. He thinks of the sheets that a posting of seven needs, and of "that house's hands"; the case is on his breastbone, shut; nothing about it is altered. "That made eight, with himself, for fifteen years."
   844	- **The fight's choreography inside the plan's four exchanges (50).** Positions: Seln in the grass above the path sixty yards east of the gap; the healers and the mule at the old ruts; nine at the shelf's mouth with Brom in front. *One:* two bolts on Iron under a buttoned coat — the breastbone and the left forearm; the neck and hands go "the colour of a slate that is drying after rain"; no sound. *Two:* Teague walks, since the Force wants three breaths of stillness; Cael keeps his left with the shortened watchman's stave from the alley (Seln's, handed over behind the brow) and takes the third stave across the left collar-bone without giving the stride; the tall man keeps the right with the ash crosswise and the steel high; both timbers and their trestles go a body's length back in one piece; five hoods go down; a crossbow's stock breaks under the lower baulk; nobody is under a timber; the wrist clicks and then the elbow. "Through" is Teague's, said to the place where the timbers stood (50:115–117). *Three:* the watch reforms behind the timbers where they lie, one bow spanned, the light relit; Teague cannot shut his right hand; he sees the width past the iron and asks Karis whether it is ground ("It is ground."). **(r1) Cael's entry, made in his head before a boot goes over, "in the tense that it would have to be true in afterward": *Nothing was gained inside.* (50:173–177; the sentence at 50:175). Teague then turns his head for the first time that morning and hands him the ground past the iron: "You've stood on three of them. I've stood on none. Say how." / "By count," said Cael (50:179–185).** Cael over first ("Grass"); Lira a stride in and a stride back ("Gone." / "Back. All of it." — "It's the same"); Karis; the boy when Teague looks at him; Vastin ("I am forbidden a wall. I am not, I think, forbidden to stand."). Sixty strides, two paces inside the iron; the watch's bow cannot turn without giving its side to Brom, the spear and the broad man's crossbow; the empty-handed man hauls back by the collar the one stave who starts for the iron. *The pinch:* eleven counts; **(r1) between two and three the empty-handed man calls "You'll die in there." across the iron, "as a person calls to a child on thin ice", and Cael answers "We've been in three times," for the crew that has stood on three such floors (50:253–259);** at four the watch's bowman looses at the broad man, the bolt takes his crossbow in the stock, his hip goes and a yard of rotten lip goes with it; Teague checks for half a stride and does not turn; the boy looks at Lira's boots and stays; at eleven the first stave is dropped and seven go through the gap singly. Seln counts seven through the gap and again over the second rise, a mile on (51:11).
   845	- **How the thirteenth comes (50:117–129).** Up through his own heels, out of the same stone, to the right hand, where it finds nothing to be laid against; "It lay along him from the heel to the palm, as a prop lies under a beam before the wedges are driven. Then it listened." It does not ask. It attends to whatever braced thing stands in front of him (the timbers; the ravine's lip; the grey of the Span's crown; a slab on the shale), and the old three ribs, low on the left, ache where its road would leave him.
   846	- **Costs carried forward.** The broad man's right shin (the good leg), broken in the ravine's bed; drawn straight on the older healer's board, knit by Oryn in a quarter of an hour, bound to the board in four turns of webbing; he walks on a trestle's leg from the 305th. Teague's right arm, wrist and elbow, in linen to the shoulder on a slat; he cannot feel the fingers. Brom: a round bruise the size of a saucer on the breastbone and a ridged one along the left forearm; Oryn will not spend on them ("You'll have a week of colours"). Cael: a welt across the left collar-bone, "a bruise on a bone", ten days. A crossbow with a bolt standing in its split stock.
   847	- **After the fall (50:307–367; 51:3–75).** The boy runs when Teague lifts two fingers and holds the broad man's head off the stones. Oryn asks; a nod; "I don't go in on a nod. I'll have it in a word."; the word is given and does not carry up the rock. The two healers nod, once each, across the leg. Vastin, coming out over the iron: "It is all there … To the grain." Brom carries the broad man's pack as the contract's second head ("I can carry what was on his back") and leaves three coppers of his own on the watch's keg for a skin of water. Cael to Lira: "Thirteenth. His. A line." / "It listens. I'd have liked it better if it asked." The tier in the fragment's lines is the thing that he once declined to read off that wrist at a marsh's edge.
   848	- **Teague's window (51:79–175).** He counts three by breathing and the spear by a shadow at the gap. The elbow has gone once before, "a long while ago, on a ledge". The bow to him: "And you never turned your head." / "If you'd turned, I'd have had something to say to you from the bottom of that hole"; "It's the good one"; of Oryn, "She asked me first. With my leg lying two ways." The boy asleep sitting, the dry cloth in his fist. Teague's reckoning: the boy "would go wherever he was looked at, a half-beat before he had seen what was there", and held at the grass by a girl's boots; "He would have to think about whose boots a boy watched." Of Cael: a left side kept with a stick and nothing else; "Teague could put that in a column." **Under the hide:** a sack and a half of meal, a bundle of bolts with no thread on their feathers, a mallet, a tarred cord knotted every few feet, and a dozen new bars, never driven, each stamped with two figures and the stroke and bar. He does not read it further; "It was not his to read."
   849	- **Noon at the Span (51:179–241).** Oryn at the irons, two fingers on Brom's wrist, asked first: "Four. The same." She will not be told how many "across a row of iron". Six go down: Karis, Lira, Brom, Seln, Vastin, Cael. The twelfth leans at the whole of the thing and is refused by counting bootlaces. On the crown: nothing; the thirteenth listens to the grey; the ribs.
   850	- **The notch (51:245–259).** Lira sees it from the crown: a gap low in the second ridge, square; "The brass goes through it. I'd lay my knife." Seln looks at it longer than either.
   851	- **The piers' inner faces (51:261–327).** Reached from the ravine's bed by a rib of rock on the western side; five courses to the height of a man, in the floors' hand, unworn; Karis draws nine sheets in two hours on a board of Brom's, with a tick where each run ends. **The lowest course of the west pier has fifty-two signs; Cael counts fifty-one, twice, and fifty-two with Lira's finger beside his** (the Book 11 plant; ruling *hold*, 51:351). The twelfth takes that course for a breath: *Goes*, eastward, out of the face toward the other pier. Karis can turn one group in the second course and reads it off the stone once: "Through" (51:329). (r1: she looks under the hide and comes back saying nothing; she keeps no bar.)
   852	- **The 304th's log (51:335–353).** After the fixed text Cael sees that he has written *tomorrow* for three days and lets it stand: "It was not a true count; it was the truth about how near the thing had come."
   853	- **The 305th's road (52:3–37).** The tall man cuts the broad man's stick out of the watch's trestle. The file goes round the outside of the iron by the trodden path; from the far side the road on the Span's back lies end-on and the same line runs out to the gap. A captain's pace "that a child could have kept"; two sticks at the back that stop within a breath of one another and never look round. Stepped shale with a thin red moss and no water. Camp in the middle of the afternoon under a step a man's head high, with forty yards of flat before it and the gap four or five miles off. Seln goes on alone.
   854	- **The blanket (52:41–101).** Brom's blanket, twenty yards upwind of the fire. In the middle the hide, turned until its pencil line runs at the gap, the Ternhall brass upon it; one sheet from the Fallow, three from the Stair, one from the hall and the nine of the piers, each turned to lie as its wall stood (she had noted on every sheet which wall and which way it faced); the flat at the head with its six rows; her notebook at the list, Cleon's roll and the sheet of three boxes at the foot; the fair copy from Cael's coat at one corner, folded, under a pebble, not opened. **The lean:** kneeling to hold a corner, Cael has the twelfth take all fourteen sheets in a breath: *Goes*, every run, one way, along the brass, east. **(r1)** He has it by the brass: whatever leans on the blanket lies with that strip and nothing goes across it; "They go east." He tells Karis, Lira and Brom (Vastin is at the fire). Karis enters it as his, as one unchecked glance — "that the rows agree with the brass, and which way" — and will not ask for a second. Lira offers "Then they point on … Each one at the next"; Karis refuses it: "*The next* is yours, and I shall not enter it … Going east, the next is the older, every time … A thing that leans at what was there before it may as well be looking back as going on. I have that they lie one way. I have not what for." (52:77–97).
   855	- **The corner (52:105–157).** The two signs in the corner of the two-bar sheets are two of the twenty-three letters: the first letters of a year's number-words, as the old town's clerks headed a page when short of room; an office's clerk has copied them forward "as he would copy a blot". **(r1)** She says that she has exactly one other year in that count, at the head of a thing that she read aloud once; she lays nothing beside anything, does not turn back a leaf, and says only that she will work it that night, alone, and say a figure once. "The office wrote down that this place is older than two hundred and six, by leaving the figure out. That is as much as the corner has said aloud." **The thumbnail ring** goes round the prick; "I shall ink that when I have had a cord round it." Vastin reads the oldest row standing ("I have never read a paper standing that I did not afterward wish I had read sitting").
   856	- **The lamps finding's ground (52:161–221).** A ruled table kept since the Fallow: who crossed, what each carries, of what rank, and the stride; three ranks of Iron, a Copper, a Bronze, and since the 304th the boy and "a man who signed for other people's for forty years"; she has asked six people beside herself. The same stride for every one, whole, no dimming, back whole. After Vastin's stopped sentence and her rule she tears the ruled leaf out and carries it the length of the blanket to the corner farthest from the folded paper: "Two pages … Whoever wants a line from the one to the other will have to find something to rule it with." **Cael's third page** (52:213–221): the technician's sheet lies at the foot of the second row; he does not know to which corner it would lie the nearer, and keeps it off the wool. Nothing is said or written.
   857	- **The two slips (52:225–285).** Folded small and pinned under the knot of the oldest row's tape; a wafer of red wax on the directive and a broader, nearly black one on the objection, with different impressions. **(r1)** Brom: "Who's the somebody else? … who it is that can say no to a thing like that, and put wax on it." Vastin: "The Compact," — he hears how that sounds, and looks at the two wafers — "Yes. Part of it." (52:255–261). Vastin knows both seals at a glance and will not name them ("I should have drafted nine. I can feel all of them."). Karis pins them back where they were and enters the day, the place, and "read aloud and understood in part".
   858	- **One fire (53:3–71).** Wood for one, carried by the boy from the watch's stack; seven bowls and five. Vastin moves the foot of his stick a hand to the left: "I am informed that the ground falls away on that side." The two sticks stand in one crack. **The healers' third exchange:** the older woman holds two turns of her own webbing apart and Oryn's hands go in under them. Teague to Cael: "You kept my left … I never had the place behind my shoulder stay so full."
   859	- **Force, dry (53:75–151).** Behind the step's corner: the right heel on a rib of rock, the right palm on a fallen slab, a body's length apart; the line found by moving the hip a finger's breadth; the thing "standing, as a prop stands when the weight has come onto it"; the ribs give "one clear line of warning … such as a cracked board gives". Three reasons for not letting it go: never fired, and a camp forty yards off; the ribs unread for this; its owner round the corner, not told. Lira: "You looked like him." Brom has the read and the thirteenth said at last: "you'll pay it when it can be paid, and not a day sooner."
   860	- **Karis's night (53:155–229).** **(r1)** She works the reduction on the lid, twice, and the page does not count with her: "a year that is written by its first letters is a year with a door left open in it"; her entry is "*not shown to be the same year; shown to be near it.*"; she will say the figure to the nearest hundred, and "or near it", once. Being believed is heavier than not being believed. The ruled leaf and her copy of the skin lie on the lid with a hand's breadth of bare wood between; she knows that a sentence exists and that it is six words long, as she once knew the length of a word under a glove; she covers the wood with her bound hand, does not say it, and writes: "*Second page: nothing entered. Not for want of a pencil.*"
   861	- **Seln on the gap (53:233–261).** A gap in a ridge, square because both walls are rock; a dry bed going in; the only way through for five miles on either hand; he lay a mile off until dark and saw no fire, no person and no heel-mark. "A watch that I can count is a watch that I can plan for." Teague will not move a stick until he has looked again from nearer at first light.
   862	- **Roles (all unnamed):** the keeper of the hold south of the cairn; the man on a bay; the people of the two barred holds; the seven of the watch (the empty-handed man; the two bowmen; the four staves, one with a cough); Teague's tall man, bow (the broad man), boy (the young one) and old woman; the miller's man (spoken of).
