   365	It went down from his boots in broad shallow steps, and then it left off stepping. There was a plain. It was pale, and very wide, and it had a low light lying on it; and there was not a tree in it anywhere, or a roof, or the smoke of a fire.
   366	
   367	A small pale woman had ruled a line once, on a calfskin, along a strip of brass. He had walked along it since a table in a healer's yard. He had thought of it, on every one of those days, as a thing made with a pencil.
   368	
   369	He looked back. Four people were setting a blanket down on a flat of rock, in the mouth of a gap.
   370	
   371	[[TIER-B: the far-mouth realization — the plan gives it only in summary (at the far mouth Cael understands something about what the line had been drawn in), with the fragment "was not ink" fixed. Staged here: he has come out by the eastern mouth behind the blanket and stands on the lip above the plain; the three paragraphs above bring him to the pencil line and then to the blanket being set down behind him, and stop. I have not written the realization in other words. It closes the chapter — coordinator to supply protected text]]

