     1	# Chapter 56 — Eleven
     2	
     3	They buried him at the eastern mouth, on a flat of rock above the dry bed, where a person lying down would have had the whole plain in front of him.
     4	
     5	It was Teague's crew that did it, and they did it in an order. Nobody gave the order aloud. Cael stood off with the rest of the six, on the bed, and watched three people go about a thing that they had plainly done before for somebody else; and he understood that no part of it was his to offer.
     6	
     7	The lean old woman was first. She knelt by the blanket with a skin of water, and turned back one fold of it, and drew the cloth out of the boy's belt.
     8	
     9	It was the one that he had wrung out for a wrist on every cold night since he joined them. She wet it. She washed the face with it, slowly, as she bound a shin, without once looking at her own fingers; and when she had done she folded it small and put it into his two hands, and laid the blanket over.
    10	
    11	The tall man had been gathering all that while. He had brought flat stones up out of the bed, one at a time, and set them edge to edge upon the rock in a shape as long as a person. He lifted the blanket onto it by himself. Cael had not known until then how slight a boy could be who was all wrists and collar.
    12	
    13	Then the broad man came, on his stick.
    14	
    15	He had a shoulder bound across his chest, and a shin on a board, and he could not stoop. He stood over the place for a while. The tall man put a stone into his left hand, a round one, the size of a loaf; and the bow went down on his one sound knee, which took him a long time, and set it at the feet.
    16	
    17	That was the first stone, and it was set by the man for whom the boy had gone out.
    18	
    19	Brom had been watching it with his jaw shut. His own left forearm was wound in linen for the whole of its length, and the bolt was out of it; a healer had seen to that under the southern wall, when she had finished with a shoulder. He went up onto the flat, heavily, and stopped a yard from a captain.
    20	
    21	"There's a good deal of stone in that bed," he said. "We'd carry it. If you'd have it carried."
    22	
    23	Teague looked at him, and past him, at four people standing on the stones and an old man who lay on them.
    24	
    25	"Carry," he said.
    26	
    27	So the six carried, all but the one who could not, and they laid nothing.
    28	
    29	It was the only thing that they had been given leave to do, and they did it for the better part of an hour. Brom took two at a time in the crook of his right arm. Lira went farthest down the bed, where the big ones lay, and Seln brought small flat ones that would lock. Karis carried them one by one against her chest, since she was not to lean on a hand; and every stone that came up was taken at the edge of the flat by the tall man or the old woman, and set by them, and by nobody else.
    30	
    31	Cael carried with his right arm.
    32	
    33	Each stone was a line drawn low across his left side, from front to back, and he found that he could not draw a whole breath under it. He had not told a healer. She sat against the southern wall with her head on her knees, the colour of old ash, as she had sat in a ravine; and he thought that he had earned the walk up that bed a good many times over, and went down it again.
    34	
    35	The old man could not carry.
    36	
    37	A healer had told him that he would not get up, and for an hour he had not. Then he had asked it of Karis; and she had got him onto his feet, with her shoulder under his arm and the stick in his other hand. He stood now at the edge of the flat with the most of his weight upon her, as upright as he had stood at a post in a mill yard. Nobody had told him to stand at this.
    38	
    39	"He was a stride from it," said Vastin.
    40	
    41	He said it very low, to nobody. It was Karis who answered him.
    42	
    43	"You could not have moved it."
    44	
    45	"No. I could not. That is the nature of the thing." He was looking at the cairn. "I have known what it would not do for the whole of my service. I have never before been made to watch what that costs somebody else."
    46	
    47	She did not tell him that he was not to blame. Cael marked that she did not, and thought the better of her for it. She stood at his elbow with a stone held against her coat, and waited until the tall man came and took it.
    48	
    49	Teague set the last.
    50	
    51	He had stood by the whole time with his right arm on its slat, and had not carried, since he could not. When the heap was as high as a man's hip he took the stone that the old woman held out to him, left-handed, and laid it upon the top, and pressed it down until it sat.
    52	
    53	Nobody had said the name. It had been said once, in a stream-bed, and that seemed to be all the saying that it was to have.
    54	
    55	Teague straightened. He looked at his own, as he had looked at them on the lip of a ravine: at the tall man, with dust to the elbows, and the broad man on his stick, and the old woman, who was on nobody's count. Cael saw his lips shape the beginning of a figure.
    56	
    57	He shut his mouth on it.
    58	
    59	"We'll lie here," he said. "Out of that gap, on the open rock, where I can see what's coming."
    60	
    61	"There is a mule on the far side of the heap," said Seln. "She has three boxes on her."
    62	
    63	"I'll go," said Lira.
    64	
    65	She had said it before he had finished. She was looking at the ridge, and she did not look at anybody else.
    66	
    67	---
    68	
    69	Seln took her by the team's own road, since there was no other that a mule could come back by.
    70	
    71	It went up the stair, and through the cleft at the shelf's end, and out upon the back of the ridge. He climbed it without haste. He had gone up the other way that morning, on his fingers, over rock that had been a quilt of loose stone an hour before; and he found that he did not wish to hurry over anything for the rest of that day.
    72	
    73	He had been through the hollow first, alone, while the stone was being carried.
    74	
    75	It had told him what such places generally told him. There were nine heaps of bedding under the spilled heap, and the place for a tenth, swept and never lain on. There was a kettle. There were two bundles of bolts, with never a thread of colour upon a feather, and a second maul. He had turned over every blanket that he could reach, and there was not a scrap of paper in the whole of it, or a mark burned into a stave, or a name scratched on a spoon.
    76	
    77	The timber of the frames was grey right through the checks. He had put his thumbnail into one. It had stood in that weather for two cold terms, and he would have laid a month's pay upon three.
    78	
    79	So it had been built for whoever came. He had said the same of some iron, at a fence, and he had been right there by a good deal more than he knew.
    80	
    81	But there was a thing that had not been the same. At the fence a man had hung a light in the air and called to them to stand and declare. In the gap nobody had called. Three bows had loosed on a big man who was setting an old one down, without a word, at twenty strides.
    82	
    83	He set the two side by side, as he would have set two returns from one office. He did not go past what they said. At the fence they had been told to hold a place, and to see what came to it. Here somebody had been told to stop a crew. And between the one order and the other, seven people had walked east for a day with what they had seen across a row of bars.
    84	
    85	That was a finding. He would give it as one, tonight, and say that it was no more.
    86	
    87	The path ran along the ridge's back, a pace wide, trodden to the rock. It swung out presently to the southern edge, above the gap, and the girl stopped there.
    88	
    89	He came up beside her and looked down.
    90	
    91	He had not seen it from above. The slab lay under them like the side of a roof, grey and clean, with the sun on it. He could see the place where he had gone up. It had seemed a long way at the time, and it was nothing: forty feet of bare rock that a child might have managed. He could see the shelf, and a notch on its lip where a wedge had been.
    92	
    93	Beneath it the mouth was stopped. The heap filled it from wall to wall, and the dead pine lay along the top, quite white, with its roots in the air. On the near side of the heap, upon the stones of the bed, there was a long pale shape drawn in dust. It was broad at one end and came to nothing at the other.
    94	
    95	At its narrow end there was a mark where a man had been sitting.
    96	
    97	"He asked me where I'd be," said Lira.
    98	
    99	She had not turned her head. She stood with her arms hanging, looking down at the dust.
   100	
   101	"Teague's boy. At that halt, before the gap. He'd asked what it was like under one, and I told him. I told him that the whole trick was to stay. Then he asked where I'd be, and I said at the back." She breathed. "He watched my boots, at the fence. When the bow went over that edge. I saw him do it; he looked down at my feet, and he stayed where he was, and I thought, good. That's a thing I can give him. I'll stand where he can see me."
   102	
   103	Seln said nothing. He had learned in a long service that there is a kind of return which must be let run to its foot.
   104	
   105	"I was twenty strides behind him, with a knife out, minding an old man that nobody ever came near."
   106	
   107	"You were put there by the person whose field it was," said Seln.
   108	
   109	He said it once, and mildly, as he said anything.
   110	
   111	"You were set to keep the one thing which was keeping eleven people alive, and it was kept. I do not tell you so to ease you. It will not. I tell you because it is the entry. You will wish to alter it tonight, and tomorrow night, and it will still be the entry."
   112	
   113	She was silent for a little.
   114	
   115	"Is that what you do with yours?"
   116	
   117	"I have gone over mine three times since noon," he said.
   118	
   119	It was true, and he had not meant to say it.
   120	
   121	---
   122	
   123	He gave it to her as they walked, since she had asked, and since he had found that a sum which will not lie down is sometimes quieter for being said to another person.
   124	
   125	"A boy was killed by the sixth flight. I went over the lip as the seventh was loosed. There is one flight between the two, and I have asked myself where I lost it." He kept his eyes on the path. "Before the fourth there was no slab. There was a bank that would not have borne a cat. From the foot of the bare rock to the lip took me three flights, at the pace that a man goes who must not be seen. I have made it that each time. It does not come out as two."
   126	
   127	"So there wasn't one to lose."
   128	
   129	"There was not. I marked that. I marked also that I have done the sum three times, which I do not ordinarily do with one that has come out."
   130	
   131	The girl looked at him sideways, and for the first time since a stream-bed something in her face eased by the width of a hair.
   132	
   133	"You're as bad as he is," she said. "With his book."
   134	
   135	"I have read his book. I have sometimes thought that he keeps it for much the same reason."
   136	
   137	They went on. The ridge was bare, and the wind came over it out of the west, and behind them the plain lay under the afternoon like a floor that nobody had walked on. He did not look round at it yet. He had the habit of taking one thing at a time, and the thing before him was a mule.
   138	
   139	There was another matter that he turned over as he went, and he did not give that one to the girl.
   140	
   141	He had stood on a shelf that morning with a man's two arms bound in his cord, and a knife in his own hand. The man had been a foot from him. There had been a cloth over the lower part of a face, tied behind, with a knot that Seln could have named. He had wanted a face from those people since a rim above a stair. He had broken a crossbow on a stone for want of one.
   142	
   143	He had not lifted the cloth.
   144	
   145	He had seen that he would not, in the instant when he might have. A face would have been a fact, and he valued facts above most things. It would also have been a hand laid upon somebody who had left off fighting; and a boy had asked a captain for something behind a brow, in three short sentences, and the captain had kept it on a ledge with his own dead under him. Seln did not care to be the one of the eleven who had kept it worse.
   146	
   147	So they were faceless still. He entered that he had chosen it.
   148	
   149	The case lay against his breastbone, under his coat, shut. He had gone up a slab with it there, and over a lip, and had not once thought of it. He marked that as well. It altered nothing.
   150	
   151	The path came down off the ridge half a mile north of the gap, by a long slant in the hill's western side, and they walked back along the foot to the nook.
   152	
   153	She was where the big one had left her.
   154	
   155	She stood on three legs, with the fourth hoof tipped, and her nose in a bag that had been empty for some hours. The three boxes were on her back, and the sack of meal, and a healer's pannier. Not a lashing had shifted. She was asleep.
   156	
   157	The girl stopped in front of her.
   158	
   159	Twelve had gone into that gap with the sun behind them. There had been three bows, and a hill coming down, and a wall made out of nothing; a boy had been killed, and nine people had gone away over a ridge. And fifty yards off, round a corner of rock, a brown mule had eaten her corn to the last grain and gone to sleep.
   160	
   161	"She doesn't know," said Lira.
   162	
   163	"She never does. I have thought before now that it is the reason why she is the easiest of us to be near."
   164	
   165	The mule opened one eye, and found that there was no corn in it, and shut it again.
   166	
   167	Lira put her forehead down against the brown neck, and stood so. He turned his back and looked at the heap in the mouth, where a pool had been, for as long as it was wanted.
   168	
   169	They brought her over by the path.
   170	
   171	She went up the slant well enough, and along the back of the ridge, with the girl at her head; and she came down through the cleft and the stair as she had once come down a notch above a mill, sideways, with her ears back, complaining. The sun was nearly under the hills behind them when they came out of the eastern mouth.
   172	
   173	He looked at the plain then, since there was time for it.
   174	
   175	It had gone grey from edge to edge. A long way out upon it, lower than the rest, there was something that had not. It lay there small and pale, holding the last of the day after everything round it had let go.
   176	
   177	He looked at it for some time, and ruled nothing.
   178	
   179	There was a fire on the open rock below the mouth, and a heap of stones beyond it as high as a man's hip. He counted the fire as he came down to it, since he had counted every fire that he ever walked up to.
   180	
   181	It came to eleven, and a mule, which he had by the rope.
   182	
   183	---
   184	
   185	Brom laid seven bowls.
   186	
   187	He had laid seven the night before, under a step of shale, and a boy on the other side of the fire had laid five. Cael watched him set them down now, on bare rock, with one hand. The big fingers went along the row when it was done, counting.
   188	
   189	Then he looked across the yard of stone that lay between the two crews.
   190	
   191	Nobody was laying that side. The tall man sat with his forearms on his knees and his hands hanging, and the broad man was propped against a pack with his eyes shut. Teague stood beyond them at the edge of the light, looking east, and the lean old woman was at his arm with her board.
   192	
   193	It had been the boy's work. Cael did not suppose that anybody had ever told him to do it.
   194	
   195	Brom got up. He went round the fire, heavily, to the place where a sack of turned bowls lay by a stranger's kettle; and he took out four, and set them along the far side, a yard apart, as the boy had set them. Nobody stopped him. The tall man's eyes followed his hand from one to the next.
   196	
   197	There was a fifth in the sack.
   198	
   199	Brom had it out before he saw what he was doing. He stood holding it. It was of a different turning from his own, darker, with a chip in the rim; and Cael saw that he did not know where on that rock a person was to put such a thing down.
   200	
   201	He had said the night before that he never in his life had wanted so much to see an empty bowl.
   202	
   203	The lean old woman came. She took it out of his fingers, without a word, as she had taken a stone at the edge of a flat. She looked at it for a breath. Then she put it into the bosom of her coat, and went back to a captain's arm.
   204	
   205	Brom came round the fire again, and sat down in his own place, and looked for a while at his two hands as though one of them had done something without asking the other.
   206	
   207	"Eleven," he said.
   208	
   209	It was all that he said, and nobody added to it.
   210	
   211	They ate. Seln gave his finding while the pot went round, in six sentences, and said twice that it was no more than a finding.
   212	
   213	"So they were told to stop us," said Teague. He had heard it with his back to the fire. "Not to see what we'd do. To stop us, with a hill."
   214	
   215	"Somebody was told so. I can put it no higher. I do not know who gave the word, or where; I know that it had changed between a fence and a gap, and that seven people walked from the one to the other."
   216	
   217	"With five of us in their eyes," said Lira. "Standing on that grass."
   218	
   219	"With five in their eyes. They saw nothing that worked there, and I should be glad to think that they carried nothing more than the sight of it. But the sight was enough to alter an order."
   220	
   221	Teague nodded, once, and asked nothing further. Cael understood that he had been handed one more thing for a column in which there were a good many already, and that he would add it up on the road home, by himself.
   222	
   223	Karis had the three boxes off the mule and had counted every lashing. Beside her the old man sat propped against the middle one, with a blanket over his legs; and Lira was on her heels before him with a bowl.
   224	
   225	"I am informed that I cannot lift it," said Vastin. "I have tried the information twice, and I find that it is correct."
   226	
   227	"Then open your mouth," said Lira, "and don't make a speech about it."
   228	
   229	He considered her for a breath, gravely, as he would have considered a clerk who had spoken out of turn. Then he opened his mouth. She fed him as she might have fed a child that she was a little afraid of, and neither of them looked at anybody; and on the far side of the fire the broad man, who had not opened his eyes, let his breath go through his nose in a way that Cael had heard once before, under a step of shale.
   230	
   231	The cloth came when the bowls were done.
   232	
   233	It was the tall man who brought it. He had begged a strip of linen from the old woman's roll, and heated water in his own cup, since there was no kettle to spare; and he wrung the thing out with those big chapped hands until it had nearly left off steaming. He had wrung it too hard. He carried it round to his captain upon both palms, as it had always been carried, and stood there.
   234	
   235	It was not the cloth. Everybody on that rock knew where the cloth was.
   236	
   237	Teague looked at what was held out to him. Then he put his right arm forward on its slat, as far as it would go, and let the man lay it on.
   238	
   239	Cael looked into the fire, since it was not a thing to be watched.
   240	
   241	A shadow had come between him and the embers.
   242	
   243	It was the healer, standing over him, and he had not heard her come.
   244	
   245	---
   246	
   247	She had her sleeves pushed up, and her face in the firelight had got some of its brown back.
   248	
   249	"I've had a shoulder and a forearm since noon, and a knee to look at. The knee's held; he sat, and it never took a pound. I've been too spent to see past the end of my own hands." She looked down at him. "You carried stone for an hour with one arm. You've sat since with the other across you, and you've not once breathed to the bottom. I taped three of those myself, and I know what they sound like when they're let alone. Up, if you can get up. I don't put my hands in anybody at a fire with ten people looking on."
   250	
   251	He got up, by way of his right side and his knees, and went after her round a shoulder of the rock, to a flat place where the fire was only a glow on the stone. There she went down on her heels.
   252	
   253	"Ribs. May I?"
   254	
   255	"Yes."
   256	
   257	"Then lie back. It's flat enough."
   258	
   259	He lay back, and it took him two tries.
   260	
   261	She undid his coat herself, and put her right hand flat upon his left side, low down, over the shirt.
   262	
   263	He shut his eyes, since he knew the way of it by now, and left every door in him standing as it stood. He felt her current set out from under her palm, slow and limping, with the old hitch in it. It did not go far. It went along three ribs, one after another, as a thumb is run along a crack in a plank.
   264	
   265	Then it left them. It went up, through the cage, toward the shoulder; and he felt it turn there, and go down the arm to the palm of his right hand, and come back the same way. It did that twice.
   266	
   267	Her lips had stopped.
   268	
   269	"That's a line," she said. "That's not yours."
   270	
   271	He opened his eyes.
   272	
   273	She was looking at him as she had looked across a ravine, two days before, at a captain's hanging arm. She had read that arm from a baulk of timber, at her stride and a half. She had told its owner what a third time would cost, and had called it a fact.
   274	
   275	"It's mine now."
   276	
   277	She did not move her hand.
   278	
   279	"I counted twelve of yours at a mill," she said slowly, "and I told you that I'd not ask how many more you meant to bring me. You tried to hand me one across a fence."
   280	
   281	"You'd not take a count over iron."
   282	
   283	"No. I'd not." Her eyes went back the way that they had come, toward a glow on a rock, and a man beside it with his arm on a slat. "It's his."
   284	
   285	"It came at the timbers, at a stride. I was behind his shoulder when he let it go." He kept it level, and low, as he had by a weir. "He doesn't know. I can't tell him how it came without telling him how they all came, and that's not mine alone. It's written down beside the old man's, as a debt. I had it lined once on the shale and took my hand away. This morning I let it go, for the first time, on the frame of the second heap. Along the shelf. There was nobody on the road of it; I looked."
   286	
   287	"And it came back along the road that it went."
   288	
   289	"To the place where it leaves me."
   290	
   291	"It leaves him at a wrist." She pressed, very lightly, and he felt all three answer. "It leaves you at three breaks that knit as they pleased under my tape, since I couldn't shut what I couldn't read. You've put a hillside through them."
   292	
   293	"A cartload."
   294	
   295	"Don't mend my sums." But there was no edge on it. She sat back on her heels, and rubbed her eyes with the heel of her hand, and for a breath she looked as old as the woman with the board. "Two are cracked along the old line. The third's whole, and angry. Nothing's gone into anything that it shouldn't. I can ease them, and I will, and I'll tape you so that you remember them."
   296	
   297	"And the term?"
   298	
   299	He asked it since she always set one, and since he owed her the asking.
   300	
   301	She was silent for some time.
   302	
   303	"I'd set you ten days flat," she said. "And tomorrow there's a thing out on that plain, a morning's walk off, that you've come the length of a country to stand in; and you'd get up off my blanket and walk to it. Then I'd have set a term to be broken. I did that once, with a knee, and I learned what it teaches a person." She shook her head. "No term tonight. Ask me again when you've stood where you're going."
   304	
   305	She put both her hands on him then, and he felt the warmth go in along three lines of fire and lay them down, one by one, as a person banks a hearth.
   306	
   307	He lay on the rock and looked up. There was a ridge against the stars, with one square piece gone out of it; and under that, though he could not see it from where he lay, there was a heap of stones as high as a man's hip.

