     1	# Chapter 57 — No Language
     2	
     3	Oryn left him lying when she had taped him, and went on a little way by herself, and sat down at the edge of the rock, where the ground went off toward the plain.
     4	
     5	She had her hands in her lap, and she had carried them so across twenty yards of stone, a little out from her, as a person carries something that has been spilt on.
     6	
     7	She had walked that country for thirty rounds, and people had died on every one of them, and she had never pretended otherwise. They had died before she reached a gate; she had come down a hill-foot more than once, with her pannier, and been shown a blanket in a byre. They had died after she went on. A man whom she had left mending had got up too soon, or a fever that she had turned came back when she was three holds away, and she had heard of it a cold term later from a carter.
     8	
     9	She had never had one go with her hands on him.
    10	
    11	It was not luck, and she did not call it skill; it was a rule. When a thing was past her, she said so, and she took her hands off before it was past everybody. She had said it of a girl's arm on a hillside, and she had said it at a mill, of something that was not an injury at all. A healer who keeps her hands on a body after she has left off being of use to it is doing that for herself.
    12	
