     1	# Chapter 58 — The First Cut
     2	
     3	They went down onto the plain at first light, eleven of them and a mule, and nobody looked round at the gap.
     4	
     5	It was the slowest that they had ever walked. A captain who had built a heap of stones the day before set the pace with his own boots, and it was one that an old man could keep who had been spent to the bed of a stream. Vastin kept it. He had got up by himself in the grey, on the ash, in three movements, and had stood for some time afterward with his eyes shut; and when the healer came to look at him he had said that he was informed there was a thing to be seen at the end of this, and that he did not propose to be carried to it.
     6	
     7	"You'll go at the bow's pace," Oryn had said. "And you'll stop when he stops."
     8	
     9	"I am obliged. I had not supposed that I should outrun him."
    10	
    11	So there were two sticks at the tail again, a pine and an ash, twenty yards apart, and they stopped within a breath of one another as they always had.
    12	
    13	Cael walked at a captain's shoulder, a stride back, with a healer's tape drawn so tight round him that he could count his own ribs by it. He could not carry; the three boxes were on the mule, and nobody had asked him to try.
    14	
    15	The steps of the shale went on for a mile, and then they began to fail. Each was a little lower than the last, and its edge a little rounder, until a person could not have said where one left off; and after that there was only rock, grey and bare, going gently down in front of them under a sky that had nothing in it.
    16	
    17	He had never been in so much light. There was not a tree on that ground to throw a shadow, and no grass to drink it. It came down white, and lay on the stone, and came up again from underfoot, and the eleven of them went through it very small. He thought of a boy who was all wrists, lying on a flat of rock with this in front of him, and he put the thought where he had put the name.
    18	
    19	"It's worn," said Brom.
    20	
    21	He had stopped, and put the side of his boot against a swell of the rock.
    22	
    23	"Look at it. There's not a sharp edge left on the whole of this. I've seen a doorstep go like that, at a hold, where sixty cold terms of boots had been over it. But there's been no boots here. There's not a path." He frowned at the east. "It's all of it worn, and it's all of it worn the one way: down, toward wherever we're going. Like a sill."
    24	
    25	"By what?" said Lira.
