     7	The first twenty strides were over the rows. He did not look down at them. He felt them through the soles of his boots, course after course, each cut as deep as a finger, as a person feels the boards of a bridge; and he kept his eyes upon a star that stood low in the east, a little to the left of where the far rim must be. He had chosen it before he lifted his foot, since there was nothing else out there to steer by.
     8	
     9	*Twenty.*
    10	
    11	Then the cutting stopped, and the grey was bare.
    12	
    13	He had not known how much noise a plain makes until he was off it. There had been grit under his boots for two days, and the rattle of shale; here there was a small dry sound at each step, and nothing came back from it. The floor was as smooth as the lid of a box, and it had no joint in it, and no bed; he had put his weight on such a thing on the ninety-sixth day, and had found that it was all one piece, and it was the same under him now for as far as he could have walked in any direction.
