     1	# Chapter 47 — The Office's Cord
     2	
     3	They went south along the ridge in a captain's file, and for six hours nobody said a word.
     4	
     5	Cael had walked that ridge once before, and never so. He went a stride behind a pair of shoulders that did not look round, with the wind on his right cheek and the pale grass combed flat before it; and whatever he saw, he had been set there to say. He saw a hare, and a hawk over it, and the place where a salt cart had turned in the wet. None of those was worth a fist. He kept them, as he kept everything, and found that being asked to look was heavier work than looking.
     6	
     7	Behind him the file had a sound that it had not had in a meadow.
     8	
     9	It was twelve people and a mule. Six of them stood on one side of a contract, with an old man counted among them now, and a captain's four on the other; and there were two healers, whom the count of ten left out, since a healer is not sent.
    10	
    11	He had them all by ear. There were Lira's boots, and behind them a pair that came down exactly when hers did, a little too carefully: that was Teague's boy, the youngest of his four. There was the creak of Oryn's pannier, and four small hooves, and the tap of the lean old woman's staff, which she did not lean upon. Brom's tread came next. A stride behind that, where it had been ordered, there was Vastin's stick; and last of all came the uneven step of the broad man with the crossbow, whom his own crew called the bow.
    12	
    13	It had a beat of its own: the ash went down, and the stiff leg came through, and the good one after it. He had listened to that beat down three hundred yards of gallery and up a stair; he had never before heard it keep a road. It was slower than the file. But at each flat hand, when the file stood, the stick made up its yards without hurry, and was a stride behind Brom again before two fingers went up.
    14	
    15	Nobody had told the old man how that was to be done. He had looked at the thing for a morning and found the way of it for himself.
    16	
    17	The sun was a little past its height when the road tipped over.
    18	
    19	Seln stood on the brow, small, with his hands at his sides, and he did not lift a fist. Teague went up beside him and stopped, with his thumbs in his belt, and Cael stopped at his left.
    20	
    21	The ridge broke off there like a table's edge. Its western face went down in one long slope of grass to a low place as round as the print of a thumb, five hundred yards from side to side; and in the bottom of that lay the floor, grey and bare, with a small square of dark cut in the middle of it.
    22	
    23	He had lain a day under that square with five people and a bolt. He had come up out of it at dusk without a sound, and walked two hundred yards of ditch with his hands dying on him, and had stood up beside a bar of iron where six hoods could see him. A hundred and eighty-five days had gone over the place since, and a cold term's snow.
    24	
    25	Teague's eyes were on the rim, where they went first on any ground.
    26	
    27	He lifted two fingers of his left hand, and turned them a little. The tall man went away southward along the live ruts without a word, with seven feet of ash on his shoulder. He walked a quarter of the round, and stopped, and grounded the butt in the turf; and Cael knew the spot. That man had stood on it from first light until the middle of a forenoon, with his face bare, while a girl with one arm found an edge sixty times with her boot.
    28	
    29	Teague's boy went west, at a trot, until a hand from the brow slowed him. The broad man sat down where he was, with his bad leg out along the turf and the short walk behind him.
    30	
    31	Nobody had asked for a rim to be held. It was held inside the time that a kettle takes, by three people who had held it before; and then the captain looked down the old road, to where a healer and a mule were coming up to the brow with a small pale woman between them.
    32	
    33	"It's hers from here," said Teague. It was the first thing that anybody had said since the ford. "I'll not ask what she wants with it. I'll ask how long."
    34	
    35	"An hour," said Karis.
    36	
    37	She had the hide under her arm, and she had not taken her eyes from the foot of the old road. Forty paces below the brow two bars of iron stood up out of the turf, eight feet apart, with the grown-over ruts running between; one was scaled like bark, and one was bright.
    38	
    39	There ought to have been a stone between them.
