    41	She had set it there herself, on the hundred and thirteenth day: a grey thing as big as a loaf, picked out of the rut, to mark the stride at which her own sigil went out. It had lain there through a siege and a morning's measuring. It was not there now. In its place the turf was whole; and Cael, coming down to it behind her, saw why. A square had been cut, with a spade, and lifted, and laid back upon the hollow where a stone had bedded itself.
    42	
    43	Seln was on one knee beside it already.
    44	
    45	"Since the thaw," he said. "The cut has not knit. He has put her sixty turfs back as well; I can find the seam of the nearest." He did not touch it. "Somebody has been round this place with a spade and made it agree with his sheet."
    46	
    47	"Which sheet?" said Brom.
    48	
