    58	
    59	She took the cord only.
    60	
    61	It was tarred hemp, no thicker than a pencil, with a brass eye whipped into one end and a knot at every fifth stride. A dead man had carried it down forty steps under that floor and run it half way to a wall. Karis had walked behind it for a whole cold term, in an inn yard, and Cael had not laid a finger on it for a hundred and thirty-one days.
    62	
    63	"There is a line in his book," she said. "*North station to floor.* He has eight of them, and one figure against all eight; and the office has written that same figure on the Stair's sheet in every one of six years. I have it by my own pacing as well. It is nine lengths and a piece, from the old iron to the lip of the grey."
    64	
    65	"Then you've got it three times," said Lira.
    66	
    67	"I have it three times, and never once with this." She ran the cord through her fingers to the first knot. "Four days from here there is a fence where the measure is double, and beyond that a place for which I have only a figure in lengths. Every length in those sheets is a length of this cord, or of its fellow. I am going to lay the office's own ruler on the office's own number, one time, on ground where I know the answer. I am not checking the place; I am checking the cord."
    68	
