    86	
    87	"You. Here."
    88	
    89	Teague had gone back up to the place where the live ruts turned. He stood on the trodden grass of it, and looked at the turf between his boots.
    90	
    91	"Two of them lay on this brow, that day, from first light till the dusk. One had a bow. You came up at them out of that ditch." He did not look round. "Where did they lie?"
    92	
    93	Cael looked.
    94	
    95	There was a brow, and a cold term's dead grass on it, and the new green coming through; a snow had lain there since, and gone. He could have pointed to the place with his eyes shut. He had seen it from below, against a sky, with fire at the end of his arm.
    96	
    97	"There. And a little south of it, two yards."
    98	
    99	"That's remembering. I asked you to read it." The grey eyes came round. "I walked onto this in the dark with a lantern, and I'd never seen them. I had the two places inside a minute. I'll not tell you how. You'll tell me. What does a man want, who's to lie on a hill all day and shoot into a hole?"
   100	
   101	"He wants to see the hole."
   102	
   103	"Go on."
   104	
   105	"And not be seen from it." Cael turned it over. "So he's behind the crest, with only his head past it. That's a strip a yard wide, the whole length of this brow."
