   107	"A hundred paces of it. I'd want it shorter."
   108	
   109	He went at it then as he went at a page. A crossbow had been spanned on that brow eleven times, and a twelfth half done; he had heard the count from a stair. A man who spans puts his boot in an iron and hauls, and he would not do that on a slope, or in soft turf, where the stirrup sank.
   110	
   111	"He wants a flat hard place to stand the bow on. Within a stride of where he lies." He looked along the brow; there were the ruts, where a hundred years of wheels had packed the ground like a floor. "It's the ruts, where they turn. Nothing else up here would take it."
   112	
   113	"And the other? He'd no bow."
   114	
   115	"He'd the old road to watch, and the stone at the foot of it. It's the one way up out of that floor that isn't open grass. He'd want to look straight down it." Cael looked down the old road, and up again. "Two yards south of the first. Where the old road comes through the brow."
   116	
   117	Teague said nothing for a breath. Then he moved his boot, and there was nothing under it but grass.
   118	
   119	"The frost showed me their two shapes, and the print of the iron, eleven times in the one place. I'd have found them without. A piece of ground's a sum, the same as a load; you put down what a man needs, and it comes out as a place." He turned his head toward the rim. "You do sums. Do me this one. I've three and myself, and that whole dish to keep. Where?"
   120	
   121	Cael gave him four places, and had two of them wrong.
   122	
   123	He had set the bow on the west, since the west had the longest view. He was told that a view was no use to a man who took half an hour to get there on that hip, and another half to come back; the bow went on the short walk, always, and the legs went far. He had put the captain on the south. He was asked from which of the four a voice would carry to the other three.
   124	
