   131	They slept that night at a beck, a mile short of the halting-ring, since Karis's hour had been an hour and ten minutes.
   132	
   133	She had come up the old road with the cord coiled over her arm, a little out of breath, and had given her finding to a captain before he could ask for it. It was nine lengths and a piece, to the stride. The cord was a cord. She had said it as she would have read out the weight of a letter, and then she had put the thing back into the tarred case with her own hands and thanked the old man for the loan of his shoulder.
   134	
