   196	
   197	The road swung south at noon on the three hundredth day, and the line did not go with it.
   198	
   199	He had seen the place once, in a soft grey morning, with gnats over the pools. There was the hold's smoke, a mile off down the right hand, going straight up. There were two old ruts that kept on eastward into the low ground, with turf in them. He had not marked what stood between, since nobody in that file had then had any need of it.
   200	
   201	It was a cairn. Somebody had built it at the bend, as high as a man's hip, of flat stones laid with care; and from its foot a third way went off, north, up the side of the hill. It was no wider than a mule, and it climbed aslant through the pale grass to a saddle, and went over. Nothing with wheels had ever gone that way, and by the look of the turf not a great deal on legs; it was a road that was kept open by one person's feet, once in a round, and by whoever came down it to fetch her.
   202	
   203	Teague's hand came up flat, and the file stood.
