   251	The track went under the turf in the last hour of the light.
   252	
   253	They had walked it hard. A tree as thick as Brom's leg stood in one rut, and it had put on a hand of girth; there were the pools on either side, black and still, and the silver-backed leaves coming. Five people and a healer had taken a day and a morning over that track once, in a strip a mile wide, with a hide held open and a man sent a furlong ahead to be sighted on. Twelve went along it now between noon and dusk, since eleven of them had only to follow a quiet man who knew where it came out.
   254	
   255	Twice the stick fell behind, and twice a flat hand went up for nothing that Cael could see; the file stood, and drank, and the stick came up. Nobody remarked on it. A captain who had said plainly that he would not walk ten at the pace of one would stop ten for a hare, it appeared, sooner than let one of them be seen to be waited for.
   256	
