   260	
   261	He heard the boy's breath go in, behind him.
   262	
   263	The four from the house on the rise had seen that place once, from where they stood now. They had found six pairs of breeches steaming on a rope, and a very big man lying strapped. Teague looked at it a good while with his thumbs in his belt.
   264	
   265	The old man had come up last. He had stood a stride behind Brom on the Stair's brow, the day before, and looked down at a grey floor for the whole of an hour without a word; and Cael had thought then that a person who has read six years of sheets upon a place might have little left to say of the place itself. He stopped now at the rise's crown, on the stick, and his eyes went round the edge of the reeds, slowly, the whole way, as they would have gone round a seal.
