/bin/zsh -lc "rg -n -i \"swear|swore|oath|since he was fourteen|when he was fourteen|nineteen\" editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-{01..53}.md
rg -n \"salt road|three carters|two days\" editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-{01..53}.md
rg -n \"It'll leave a line|leave a line|purse|hundred and twenty-three|123\" editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-{01..53}.md" in /Users/drive/fractured-path-monroe13
 succeeded in 0ms:
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-08.md:149:By the two hundred and seventy-sixth the water was over the landing's lowest plank. It came down the colour of a ploughed field, with a noise that could be heard in the inn's kitchen, and it brought whole trees with it, and once a drowned sheep, and once the better part of somebody's footbridge. The cable hung over it in a long belly. The ferryman sat on his keg from first light with his arms folded, and for an hour he did nothing but look. Half the meadow stood on the bank and looked at him, for there were nineteen fires along that grass now, and thirty carts with their shafts down.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-10.md:93:"It was a sound knee when I set out. I do not complain of it. It had sat under a desk for nineteen years and was not consulted."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-04.md:211:They carried the carter up to the lower inn on a hurdle. He was alive, and swearing in a whisper at his own mules, and the keeper had him by the hearth in a blanket before anybody had asked whose he was. Nobody did ask. He was a man off the ice; in that country it was all the name that anyone required of him.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-01.md:15:He was nineteen, and he had no Path. Everybody else whom he knew had been given one at fourteen, at a Kindling, and had been ranked by it ever since; his own Arbiter had woken on its brass plate that day, and taken eleven seconds over him, and gone dark. What answered him instead were fragments. There were ten of them now, pieces of other people's Paths, each one taken in the middle of a fight that somebody had meant, and each one written down in an old man's book with a name beside it and a price.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-06.md:295:*The ferry runs when the ice goes. Karis has a bearing, an index she can't read, and a language she can't read, and she says the answers are in a border archive nobody's opened since before the Compact. Since my Kindling I've stayed out of registries because a registry is where they file you. She wants me to walk into the oldest one on the continent. The kit reads everyone but me. So does the world, mostly. Nineteen. Ice goes next week. West, and inside, for the first time since the academy above Ostrand — which is a sentence I did not think I'd write again.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-11.md:175:Cael had seen it from the well. He knew a dozen things that might have been found for that man to do, since he had spent a cold term learning what a day at Lowmarch was made of. There were reeds to be split, and the ferryman's rope to be turned by a fire, and Hesk's clock to be read against the smith's noon. Any of them would have served. Each would have been a thing made up on the spot, by a boy of nineteen, to spare a man of that standing the sight of his own empty hands; and the man had spent forty years telling such make-work from the other kind at a glance.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-14.md:95:"There's a hole below the road on the north side, where that water goes into the hill. They lie up round it. I've seen five. I'd not swear to five, and nobody who's been nearer has come back to tell me better."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-17.md:87:"I shall not swear to a hand on four words," she said. "I shall not say that it is his, and I shall not say that it is another's. I tell you so now, so that nobody need ask me."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-24.md:181:He passed within two strides of the yard, and of a boy of nineteen who stood against a doorpost with a bowl; and his red eyes did not come that way at all. They were on the floor, and they went along the flags, a joint at a time, as though he were reading how those had been laid as well.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-20.md:35:After him there was a boy with a switch and nine geese, and a woman under a yoke, and two men mending a hurdle; and not one of them gave the six more than a glance, which took in the loads and priced the distance. Cael counted them, since he counted anything. He had been stared at on roads since he was fourteen, by people who had been told what to look for, and for the better part of a year he had walked where there was nobody to stare. This was a third thing. He was a back with a box on it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-20.md:173:That made ten. He had called them fragments since he was fourteen, and pieces, and entries in a list. Lying in the grass he had them as the going-along had them, which was none of those. They were ten currents, and not one of them had a bank. Each ran on into the next as water runs into water, and a healer had found that out before he did, with both her hands, and had told him so on a ridge.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-18.md:131:If he wrote the two days side by side, he would have drawn the line himself. It would not matter that he added no verb. A sum set down with its answer showing is a sentence. Anybody who opened that book would finish it for him: Lira, who had read it, or a healer, who had made him swear by it once. And it was not his alone. A man at a basin had carried it for three days, and had put it into his hands without a word, so that it need never be said.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-21.md:197:"So you'd have had the first of your two," said Cael. "I can't swear it; a plate's not what I've got. But there's nothing in that place that moves, and I think a plate's the same sort of tool as mine. It would have lain flat, and I'd have been two blanks on one file, and they'd have found their word. Nobody has that but you. It's not in the book yet. I've not told her, and she's read every line I ever wrote."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-21.md:237:He had not known that he was afraid of it until the pencil said so. He had kept that question since he was fourteen, on a great many desks, the wrong way up. Registrars had reached for it, and wardens, and an Archmarshal with a plate; and a healer had found the edge of it with her two hands, on a mountain, and had been made to wait a long while for the rest. Every one of them had come to him, and he had never in his life carried it to anybody. And he was walking to a town, three miles off, to lay it down in front of a man whom he had not seen, on the word of a catalogue.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-13.md:173:Cael dropped back to him at the top of the rise. He had not looked forward to it. He was nineteen, and the man beside him had told people for forty years on which side of a line they stood; and Cael had now to tell him how to walk along a road.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-26.md:191:He did not lift them far. They came along the paving at the height of a boot, as a grey keeper's had at a gate. Cael had been read by registrars since he was fourteen, and had learned to meet each of them in the eye, so that it should cost them something. He looked down at his own toes. He had not known that it would be so hard. It was the only thing that he had ever had to set against a pen; and he gave it up, and was a pair of boots among four.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-27.md:285:He had been in a great many buildings since he was fourteen, and in every one of them there had been a drawer. There was a counter at Denvash, with a pen on it. There was a house on a bluff, where a file had grown as thick as a man's hand; there was a district seat, and a borrowed office. He had learned to feel for the drawer on any threshold, as a dog feels for the one board in a floor that gives. Even on the kind side of the Line there had been a book, kept by a man with one arm; that one had been honest, and he had been glad to stand in it, and it had put him eleventh all the same.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-43.md:29:"It's a fair wage," he said. "It's a better one than the first, though it's the same twenty; you didn't know the first time that it could be done." He rubbed his jaw with the back of his wrist. "We're walking east. There's a line that we've undertaken to follow, and it goes clean away from your frost, and I can't tell you which side of a cold term it ends on. I'll not take a hold's silver against a week that I can't swear to be standing in."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-43.md:253:Lowmarch came up out of the dusk on the eastern side as it had come up on the first evening that Cael ever saw it. But the meadow was not as he had left it. There had been nineteen fires along that water on the night before the first boat. There were more than forty now; the holds had opened, and every crew that had lain up for the cold was down to hear what the plank would say. Carts stood wheel to wheel by the smith's rail. There was a light in every window of the lower inn.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-35.md:325:Cael had set his boot on the nineteenth tread, with the case, and the lamp was above him, eight steps up, in Lira's hand. It was a small brown flame in a smoked glass. He had knelt beside it for six days. It went into his eyes now like a handful of sand.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-32.md:201:Cael had been stared at on roads since he was fourteen, by people who had been told what to look for. He knew the look of it through a hood.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-30.md:309:"I was fourteen. I'm nineteen. I'm not there yet either."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-40.md:57:"He was as frightened as that. You can read it off the bill; it's the only part of him that's left." Brom looked into the fire. "I'm not saying that he'd cause. A price isn't a verdict. I've known a man pay a great deal for a bad horse, and swear by it after, because of what he'd paid."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-40.md:179:It was not a ruling; there was nothing in it to hold, or to try again in daylight. It had been read, once, in a voice that shook; and it was either so or it was not, and every person at that fire had already taken it for so. If he wrote it he would be putting it where he put a cord and a hair. He would be making it a thing that he had noticed; and it was not that. It was the ground that he had been standing on since he was fourteen, shown to him from underneath.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-39.md:59:The eyes that looked back at him were tired. They were nineteen years old, and they had heard what he had heard, and they had gone back to holding a lamp.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-52.md:207:He had read that day at a spring, a figure at a time, and laid it against another that he had carried since he was fourteen. He had put the sheet back. He had not written it, and he had told nobody.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-48.md:141:They were all down that slope, and along the foot of the band. He counted nineteen grey backs before he had drawn two breaths. Each of them was bigger than a cart, and plated like a slate roof, and they stood with their horn beaks to the rock.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-48.md:301:So four more had been wanted, and they had been fetched before the light went. The tall man took the first pair, and the boy took the second. Nobody had told him that he might; a captain had only looked at two skins lying on the grass, and then at him. He went down that slope at three steps and a stand, with nineteen grey backs above him and the whole herd gathered to the wet. Cael, who could see a furlong well enough and nothing nearer, watched a small dark shape take as long over sixty yards as it had ever taken over anything in its life.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-42.md:285:*Two hundred and ninety-fifth day. The spring, a furlong south of the border road; nineteen miles and some from the gate. Out at first light by the outer arch: six out, as six in, and none of us written. Sixty yards of four-strand, two marks. A hundred and twenty-eight. The coil rides on the rope-walk's dray, with forty of its kind for the landing. So does Vastin, for a copper a day of his own; he walks the hills. Sixteen of thirty. Lira: thirty. She says that it is an arm.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-45.md:223:*Two hundred and ninety-eighth day. The fork above Millrace; the flat rock. A hundred and twenty-eight. Vastin: nineteen of thirty. He rides, on the field's ruling. Karis opened the dividers. I lent them.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-45.md:279:"Nineteen days," Vastin said.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-37.md:45:"Eighteen," she said, in the forenoon; and at noon, with her bread untouched, "Nineteen."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-37.md:221:*Nineteen. Twelve confirmed fragments.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-49.md:21:Cael looked. The boy was sitting with his back to a tussock, all wrists, chewing as though somebody were about to take the bread away. He had walked a slope under nineteen grey backs at dusk, at three steps and a stand, and had come up it with two skins full. Since then he had carried himself a little differently, like a person who has been given a tool and is not yet sure that he is allowed to keep it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-29.md:283:He had come to that town with one question. He had written at a fire that he was about to hand it to a stranger, and had been afraid of it for two days, in a loft, with a small pale woman's roll across his knee. He need not have been. The stranger had told a gallery, without being asked, that he could not take it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-16.md:249:Beyond the cut's head the ruts came out onto the open shoulder, and the whole country changed. It was high moor, bare and brown, rolling away westward in long swells like a sea that had stopped. The wind came over it steadily from the west, with nothing in it but cold, and it had not left off since the first of them topped the rise. There was no tree. Far off to the north a line of blue hills stood up, with snow on them still. Somewhere under that western sky, not two days off, the country that kept no books came to its end; Cael had crossed that edge once, going the other way, by another road, with his name in every ledger behind him, and he had not turned his head to look at it then either.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-16.md:265:"It is the table that I have been looking for since the night before last. I said that I should count in daylight. I have carried six years of an office's paper for two days up a road and through a fight, and I have not yet turned a sheet of it." She had the flat out from under her arm already. "I want the forenoon. I want pebbles, a great many, about the size of a hen's egg, since there is a wind. And I want every person here who can read to wash his hands in that basin first."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-02.md:7:They walked for two days up the north water under a sky like a pewter dish, and slept the night between in a hay barn that a carter had told Brom of, with the mule's breath going up among the rafters and Seln sitting out his watch in the doorway. Lira set the great boot down whole at every stride of both days. The bow rode on her back with its one bolt; nobody had proposed that she should stay by a hearth while a fence was mended, and nobody who knew her would have tried.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-01.md:9:She was a small brown mule, and she was not his. She belonged to that healer, who was two days off up a shut road, and who had lent her to five people at a hold's gate a long while ago without saying for how long. He led her by the cheek-strap across the landing's planks to the round shed where the ferry's gelding had walked his circle through all the dry months. The gelding was in the smith's stable now, under a blanket. The shed stood empty, with reed hurdles tied between its posts against the wind, and a great wooden drum in the middle of it that had nothing to wind.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-09.md:105:Two beasts leaned. Teague's bow and the boy from the upper inn had their shoulders to the beam behind them, and three carters besides. The drum turned by a tooth, and held, and turned by another.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-08.md:207:He kept to the road, since there was nothing else to keep to. It was a country without a wall or a gate or a board of hours, and for the first two days he met nobody at all. He slept under a pine. He learned to put the bad leg down straight and to let the stick take what the knee would not, and he learned that he could do ten miles so, and not eleven.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-23.md:171:He had written two sentences in a book, at a fire, for a healer who would not take his word. They had been easy to write. The square had been two days off, in a town that he had never seen, and a thing which is two days off costs nothing to forgo. He had not known that it would stand at the head of the only street; that the record he had walked here for would be kept behind a door on its far side; or that the two of them would face one another across forty yards of paving, like a pair of people who have been set to keep each other honest.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-23.md:183:He had done it in the lower room of the inn, which opened off the yard: a long low place with one table down the middle of it and a bench on either side, a hearth at the far end, and a hatch through which bowls came. He had sat on the bench's end with the bound leg laid along it. There was a post behind him, and a strap hung from the post, and he had marked both before he let himself down. He would be able to get up by them. He had sat twice now in two days, and he noted, with the interest that he gave to any finding, that the second time had cost him less than the first.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-51.md:297:He stood and looked at the stone: a row of cut marks, in good light, each a hand's breadth from the next. He had counted the heads of a herd at a furlong, two days since, and had not been out by one.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-41.md:133:Cael did not look round. He looked at his knuckles, and at the flame, and he heard an old man decline to finish a sentence for the second time in two days; there was no sound in it but breath, let go slowly, and then the small scrape of a stroke made on a tally. It appeared that the count of the Stair's had come to twenty-nine, and that nobody at the desk had heard anything else.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-22.md:53:It lay across the roadway under the arch, a single long stone, worn hollow in the middle, with a socket cut in each end where a bar had once dropped. He knew what it was before Seln's hand moved. He had stepped over its fellow going the other way, on another road, two hundred and eighty-two days ago, with four others; and he had begun a count that morning which he had kept ever since.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-22.md:267:And there was a thing that she had not got to at all. She had walked away from it at a fork two days south, with her sleeve half pushed up, and had told a boy that it would keep three weeks. She had read a hundred bodies since she first laid hands on his. She knew a wrist with a stone in it when she met one. She had met something in him a long while back that everything went round, as that carter's current went round a bad join; and she had not been able to say then whether it was a join, or a stone, or a thing that had always lain there. She had told him to leave it be. Then she had walked north, owing him the one reading that might have told her.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-06.md:175:She told it as she would have entered it. The fourth lay two days beyond the fork, under the end of a ridge, and it was twice the size of the others, by the bend of its edge. There was a fence of iron round it, of the registry's pattern, with a bar to every pace and one number on every bar. Something stood in the middle that five people had looked at in the dusk and could not make out.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-06.md:191:She made no reply to that, and he had not looked for one. She had told six people in a loft that at any edge somebody ought to be standing on the right side with her sleeves pushed up; and he watched her understand, with her eyes on a ring of pencil, that five of them had taken her at her word on a hillside two days from anywhere, and had turned round. When she bent to the fourth ring again, she had lost her place on it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-06.md:215:"I'm on the road in the morning. Lowmarch is my first stop after a thaw, and the mill's my second; they have been for thirty rounds, and I'd be walking down to that ferry tomorrow if I'd never set eyes on the five of you." She did not turn round. "So I'll be on your road for two days, since it's mine as well. I'd have that understood. Nobody asked me."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-06.md:231:Cael counted the board, as he counted any room. There was a big man with one arm bound and six bowls laid; and a girl who kept turning her left hand over on the wood, as though it had come back to her from a mender's; and a small pale woman with a hide; and a quiet one with his back to the wall; and at the end, on her feet, a healer who had walked two days down a saddle because it was her road.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-14.md:87:"They all know what a road is. They come up from the river quiet as a burying, two days, and never a finger lit; I've had Iron men walk through that gate who'd let a beast die in the shafts on the moor sooner than bring up the grey. They know. Then they come into my cut." A hand went out flat, and tilted. "There's a seep at the head of it. It comes down the middle of the road, a stride wide, and from the first frost till the last it's glass from top to bottom, in a place the sun never finds. A wheel goes onto that. Then the cart begins to go, backward, down a hill, with a year's goods on her and a man's own two beasts in front. What does the man do?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-14.md:91:"He's Iron, or he's Stone, or he's some small thing that he was given at fourteen and has kept in his pocket for two days. He's had it out before he's thought. Carters declare, on the ice." The factor spat over the wall. "And then they come up out of the sink."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-14.md:217:Cael walked by the three carts. He had not meant to look at them, and he looked: a wheel with two spokes gone, and a barrel-head with a hold's mark burned in it, and under the nearest bed a mule's collar, with the straw coming out. Somebody had driven that cart for two days up a silent road with a Path in his pocket. He had kept it there until the very last hundred strides.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-48.md:321:*Three hundred and second day. The ridge's back, in a hollow. A hundred and twenty-three. Vastin: twenty-three. Oryn is two days past her cairn; she went by the chock-stones and did not look up.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-13.md:27:*Clearance. The cut below the shoulder hold, on the border road, two days west.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-13.md:57:"They're for a slip. There's a cut two days up a road, with something in it that takes carts, and a hold that'll pay twenty to have it out. I can price that. I know what a clearance costs; each of you has a mark on you from the last one." He kept his eyes on the bark. "I'm not ruling on a town. I said that I'd not rule over a thing I hadn't touched, and I've still not touched it. I can't price a town. Every figure that I've got for it is somebody else's."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-13.md:61:"It's the same road, and I can read. I'm not going to stand at this post and pretend that I took a job without seeing where it lay, and I'd not ask any of you to pretend it either." He made himself look at them then: a big man with four yards of webbing on him, and a girl with her left hand in her coat pocket, and a small pale woman holding six years of somebody's counting under her arm. "The boxes come. So do the papers, and the kit. I'll not leave a thing of ours in a store for a week with a river between. When the cut's done we'll be two days nearer than we are this morning, with the road going on in front of us, and I'll know more about what I'm walking with than I do now. That's all that I can honestly say I've decided. It's a job. I'll not make it a vow."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-13.md:83:"It's a clearance. I've written a good many of those taken, and I've written some of them closed with a line drawn under a name. In my second year here a crew of four went up the north water, and one of them bled for two days on a hurdle for want of anybody who knew where to put a thumb. Since then I don't write a clearance taken until I've a healer said. It's not the hold's rule. It's mine." He scratched his stump. "Teague's old woman goes up the cold road with him. So who's yours?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-13.md:105:Cael left the word with Pike, as he had promised a better captain that he would: the cut, two days west by the border road, and whatever lay beyond it, with no day put on the coming back. Pike wrote nothing. He said that he had a season line of theirs with no number against it, and had stopped expecting to fill it by the week.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-13.md:119:"West. There's a cut below a hold, two days up the border road, that's been taking carts. It's ours."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-13.md:143:Vastin had his copper ready. He gave it with the short stiff bow, and stepped over the rail bad leg first, and went aft without being told, to the post where a new strap of stern line had been rove. He stood there through the whole of the crossing with his hands on his stick, a foot from the thing that he had held two days before with his back, and did not touch it. The ferryman, at the sweep, looked once at him and once at the strap. Cael thought that it was the nearest that man had ever come to thanking anybody.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-35.md:159:"That's two in two days," he said. "You'll want a bigger book."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-20.md:141:Vastin did not sit. He put his back against the post and both hands on the head of his stick, and stood with the bound leg thrust out before him, as he had stood at every halt for two days. A man in that binding could get himself down to the ground. It was the getting up again that could not be done without another person's arm; and Cael had watched him measure that at a fold, and at a fork, and at the top of a rise, and choose his feet every time.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-43.md:153:"I did. I have sat on a board in front of six people for two days, going backward, at a copper a day. I find that the ground is no farther off, and that it is free."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-21.md:173:The old head came round a little, and stopped. Cael watched the question arrive. He had seen a great many arrive in that face across a table, each in its order, and he knew the look of this one: it was the obvious next thing, and any officer living would have put it, and put it at once. *By whom?* The healer was two days north, with a mule. There was a boy on a bank who had lately had a hand on his own chest.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-18.md:17:He had not been certain. That was the reason that he had given himself, and it had been a good one for two days.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-18.md:19:A date read once, by lamplight, over another person's shoulder, in another person's pencil, at the end of such a day, by a man who would have paid a year's wages to find it otherwise, was not a finding. He might have taken one figure for another. So might she, for all that she never did. He might, after fifteen years of files, have laid the wrong day at the head of the right one. There were three places where it could have slipped, and he had spent two days on a road going over each of the three.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-18.md:131:If he wrote the two days side by side, he would have drawn the line himself. It would not matter that he added no verb. A sum set down with its answer showing is a sentence. Anybody who opened that book would finish it for him: Lira, who had read it, or a healer, who had made him swear by it once. And it was not his alone. A man at a basin had carried it for three days, and had put it into his hands without a word, so that it need never be said.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-25.md:141:Cael put his boot on the first of the slanted setts with a box on his back and a card going before him in a small pale woman's glove. He marked it, as he had marked a sill, since two days ago he had stood at that kerb and not stepped. It was the same stone; he had only become a different sort of thing to it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-38.md:135:He marked that, and it was done three ways. The red eyes were shut, and had been since before she began. The grey ones had gone to a bay of bundles and were reading the under side of a tag. And the small pale woman, who had asked him to stand there, kept her face to a wall. He had been looked away from by one person in that gallery, two days ago, on purpose and for his own sake. It appeared to have spread.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-34.md:273:"I am not stuck. I have a group of nine signs, and I do not know whether it is one word, or two, or the tail of one and the head of the next. If I knew where his words ended, I could lay them against the old books by their length. I have been guessing at the joins for two days, and I do not care for it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-37.md:289:She had come in at dusk, two days behind her own reckoning and no longer troubling to count them. It was a larger hold than the others, walled in stone to a man's head with split oak above, and there were nine on the bench under its gate-house eave. She went along them at her stride and a half, with two fingers for each wrist: a herdsman's foot that a beast had trodden; a baby with a rash, and a mother who had not slept; a grandfather who wished only to be told that the thing in his chest was the same thing as last year, and it was. She asked each one, and had each one's yes, and it was full dark before she had her hands in the butt.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-37.md:355:She was two days behind, and there was a fifth gate, and a ford, and a mill.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-47.md:231:"He'd had them standing since yesterday. A man rode through, two days gone, and told him there'd be a crew for the dry road; he bought nothing, and he gave no name. He only said." Brom looked at Seln. "I asked what he rode. It was a bay."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-47.md:303:*Known: the keeper had our skins filled before we came. A man on a bay told him two days ago that a crew would want them.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-30.md:203:Cael took the ring from Lira at the Stair's table, as he had on the two days before. He saw a raw hand come out, a little ahead of his own. It had not come far. It stopped a foot from the ring and hung there, and went back to the stick.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-32.md:9:"Fourteen," said Karis. She said it to her notebook. "It closes the number after five. I had the head of that word two days since, and I could not have the tail."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-32.md:39:Cael looked up, at the roof of the last building, where a parapet ran along above the slot in the wall. There was nothing on it. There had been nothing on it for two days, morning and evening, and he had learned to read that as a small grey man doing his work.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-49.md:79:"There's a salt road two days north of this, on the high side, that looks down into here from a long way off. I've had it from three carters and never believed one of them. They say that there's a hole in the middle of this ground where the country's water goes, and a span standing over the hole, sunk down in the dish so that you see only the top of it." He put his thumbs in his belt. "They call it that. The Sunken Span. I took it for a tale about a bridge with no river."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-16.md:103:"A hundred and thirty-eight in hand," said Brom. He put the slip inside his shirt with the purse. "That's his, to draw the tack on. I'll have it after."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-01.md:49:Cael had Hesk's book inside his coat, and he did not need to take it out, since every figure in it had stopped at this river. There were nine lines under five names in a one-armed man's ledger, and a sheet of sailcloth by the inn's hearth with those names on its eleventh line, and neither would change by a stroke while the holds were shut. There were ninety-six marks in a purse. There were twenty-seven uses written against the ten fragments.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-01.md:241:Karis took out the purse. She had paid six to the keeper without looking, four days before, with her eyes on a door. Today she opened her notebook first, and did the sum, and did it again beneath; then she counted twenty marks onto the tarred oak in four heaps of five.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-01.md:303:"Every figure that I keep has stopped," he said. "The purse won't move till the fence. The cloth can't move at all. I've nine lines and no post, and a number that I've promised her not to change."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-11.md:39:"I should not care to have it thought that I walked here to lay a knee before a healer. It was sound when I set out, and it has carried me, and I have no claim on anybody's purse for it. I offer it to nobody. I had rather that were understood."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-10.md:271:The small purse came out on its cord, and a fourth coin went down on the wood. Vastin looked at it for a moment after his fingers had left it, as he had looked at the cut half of the other. It came to Cael that the man had very likely never paid for a bed in his life. An office had sent a clerk before him to every inn on every road that he had travelled, and the reckoning had gone home in a bag.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-53.md:261:*Three hundred and fifth day. A shelf of shale under a step, four miles short of a gap in the hills. A hundred and twenty-three. Vastin: twenty-six. Oryn is five days past her cairn; Brom's week was out today, and she had her hands in a shin tonight.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-27.md:111:Brom looked at Cael, and Cael lifted one shoulder, under the strap. They got the loads off. Brom let his own box down to the floor by inches, with his knees, so that it should make no noise in that place; and the third came off Cael's back, with a third part of fifty-two sheets in it and a purse sealed under five thumbs, for the first time since a frost in an inn yard. Karis did not put anything down. She stood with the flat under one arm and the hide under the other, at the very rim of that good light, and her face had gone as still as water in a butt. She had waited the whole of her life for this room. It appeared that she could wait while a stranger came to the end of his paragraph.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-07.md:97:"It holds it. Your name goes into his book, and he finds it on his list. Before the hour is out he asks you, very civilly, what you carry under that name; and he gives you a slip for it, and keeps it, pending. You are not struck off. You are held. You may not fight on a floor, or draw on a house's purse, or put your name to a contract, until a thing is settled which is not yours to settle." He looked at Karis. "Yours is the same, and it costs you more, since it is the one paper that any archive would honour. And he," with a small nod at Brom, "is Copper in a book, and has a finding unsigned beside it, and both would be frozen as they stand."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-07.md:169:The hide-woman went away muttering, and Cael understood, watching her go, how that healer got through a bench of nine. She had a purse in her that she called her reserve, and a mending drew on it at about the rate of the hurt, a toe for a toe; and so she gave it only where nothing cheaper would serve, and gave words and mutton fat for the rest. Between the child and the carter she sat down on her keg with her head lowered, and her lips moved to fifty.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-09.md:33:"No goods," said Brom. He had the purse inside his shirt, and he put his hand flat on the place. "He said so."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-09.md:247:Vastin had a small purse on a cord. He counted out one coin, and then a second, which had been cut across with a chisel; he looked at the cut edge for a breath, as though it were a kind of seal that he had not met, and laid the half beside the whole on her palm.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-06.md:221:They were six at the lower inn's hearth on the night of the two hundred and sixty-eighth, with a hundred and eighteen marks in the purse.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-06.md:223:The purse had moved by two at the landing. The ferryman had been on his rope-house roof when they walked down the bank, on his knees, with his palm laid on Brom's shingles. It had kept a thaw out, and he said so, and five marks came up through Pike's hand before the supper was on, with a line said twice to go with them. Then he had looked at the brown mule, who was standing at his shed's door as though she had never left it; and he had asked at last. It was three, for the cold term, straw and standing, and he had named it without once lifting his eyes from her.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-40.md:91:Nobody asked him how long a while was; he had given a great many words, for that purse. Cael came round the basket for the paper; there was a bucket beside the girl's that nobody had used, and he sat down on it at last, since his legs had been asking for an hour.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-13.md:109:He did not come to the plank, and he asked nobody anything. Cael saw him go in to the keeper, and come out again putting up his small purse; he had paid for the second night in the loft, and it appeared that he did not mean to have a third. He took up his pack by its one sound strap. Then he stood by the gate with the stick grounded until the mule had gone through it, and Oryn after the mule, with her strip of canvas rolled under her arm. When the last of them was a dozen strides down the bank he came after, at his own pace, and kept that distance.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-13.md:149:Brom had the purse in his fist before the mule was off the scow. He had carried that debt since before the snow: sixty yards of four-strand rope, to a ferryman who would take no silver for it. A brisk man with ink on his cuffs came along the stack to meet him, with a clerk at his elbow.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-13.md:161:"I've no doubt of it. He asked for four." Brom let the end go. "A four's laid round a heart, and it keeps its round in a block, and a ferry's rope lives in blocks. You could give me this, and I'd still be owing him sixty yards of four." He put the purse inside his shirt, where the dead slips lay. "A man lent us a rope and told us what he wanted back. I'll not hand him something else and call it paid, however good it is. When you've four, there's a hundred and eighteen marks will come down this road to look at it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-08.md:151:Seln had forty-six notches. Brom had asked Karis for the purse, and had it in his fist.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-08.md:163:Brom looked at the purse in his hand, and across a hundred yards of brown water at a stack under tarred sheets.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-48.md:99:*Three hundred and first day. A dead hold on the east road, in the lee of its gable. A hundred and twenty-three. Vastin: twenty-two. Oryn is a day past her cairn. A cup of water a head, and the kettle.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-48.md:321:*Three hundred and second day. The ridge's back, in a hollow. A hundred and twenty-three. Vastin: twenty-three. Oryn is two days past her cairn; she went by the chock-stones and did not look up.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-37.md:135:It was Brom's ruling, and he made it at the half-door with the purse in his fist.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-37.md:143:"A hundred and thirty," he said, to the purse.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-22.md:199:"A hundred and thirty-six," he said, to the purse.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-52.md:9:He was grey, and his mouth was shut like a purse. A shin that had lain two ways the morning before took his weight, a little at a time, with a board bound along it. When he had gone the length of the shelf and back he shook the arm off, and stood by himself, and looked at the east as if it owed him money.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-35.md:185:He slid the beckets off. Brom shifted along, and laid one forearm across the lid of the third box, with a purse in it under five thumbs, as a man lays his arm along a gate.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-49.md:259:*Three hundred and third day. A fold behind the brow, above the fourth place. No fire. A hundred and twenty-three. Vastin: twenty-four; he has said that he will not stand it, and it is entered. Oryn is three days past her cairn.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-30.md:155:Cael did not know what had been looked at. It might have been a case. It might have been a door, or a light on a crown, or the whole of fifteen years. He found that he could not ask. He had been given three words by somebody who weighed them like coin, and it was a great many for that purse.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-31.md:263:"A hundred and thirty-four," he said, to the purse.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-47.md:205:"Skins," he said. "There's a gate down there, and there's none for three days after. Mine have their own purse."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-47.md:227:"Four," said Brom. "Meal, and a cheese, and corn for her, and the two skins." He set the sack down. "A hundred and twenty-three. And I never named a thing. He'd the skins filled and standing in the gate, and a price on them."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-47.md:299:*Three hundredth day. The track's end, outside the stone, at the hall's marsh. A hundred and twenty-three: four at a gate, for two skins, meal, a cheese, and corn. Vastin: twenty-one.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-51.md:341:*Three hundred and fourth day. The watch's post, on the shelf above the fourth place. A hundred and twenty-three. Vastin: twenty-five; he stood inside the line this morning, and it took what he carries, and gave it back. Oryn is four days past her cairn. She knit a shin in the ravine's bed and was grey for a quarter of an hour.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-32.md:23:They roped the boxes. Brom took his on the right shoulder, as he had for four days, and Lira got her arms through her beckets, the right and then the left. Cael felt the third box settle between his shoulder-blades, with a purse in it under five thumbs. The old man hooked the tarred case with the head of his stick, and brought it up.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-42.md:21:He counted it onto the head of a tar barrel: two marks, in silver, out of the purse that lay inside his shirt beside a thumbed slip. He watched the coil while he did it, and Cael saw the frown go out of his face a little at a time, like a knot being worked loose with a spike. That debt had ridden in him since before the snow. It had gone over a river and come back, and up a moor, and into a town, and through eleven days under a registry. Here it lay at last, sixty yards of the right kind, across a woman's forearms.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-42.md:33:"That's for the western landing," said the woman. "Three days down the moor road. There's a broker there who's sold three-strand for as long as I've laid it, and a fortnight since he sent up for four. Forty coils. He wrote that a big man from over the water had stood in front of his stack with a full purse, and called his rope the wrong kind, and gone away with the purse; and that he'd not have it said to him twice."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-43.md:21:"I didn't come up onto this top to make you glad. I've a thing to put to you, and I'll put it once, since I was told beforehand what I'd hear." The pale eyes had come to Brom, who had the purse. "That seep'll have its glass back at the first frost. Another den'll find it, in a year, or in five. I said so on that bank with your wet one standing by. I'd as soon not sit in my yard and wait to learn which."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-43.md:95:He had given that word to a captain three times, at a hearth and on a landing. He had never before given it to this man. It was a clerk's word, and he had kept it from him as a person keeps his purse from somebody who once had the counting of it.

