    95	He had heard it read, by a lamp, in Lira's voice, from the far end of a table. There was the printed head. There were four short lines in a quick hand that leaned to the right, as a man writes on his knee. He knew what the first of them said, and the third, and where the fourth broke off. They had gone round in him for three days, with the stripe that lay across the rest.
    96	
    97	The second line he had never seen. It was a date. There was a year of the registry's in it, and a day in that year, in a clerk's figures.
    98	
    99	He read it as he would have read any other, and went on to the line beneath. Then his eyes came back. He had read a great many such dates, upside down, on a great many desks, and they were all of them much alike; but this one he seemed to have met, as a person seems to have met a face in a crowd.
   100	
   101	He did not trust that. He did it as a sum is done.
   102	
   103	There was one date that he knew as he knew his own name. He set it beside the technician's, in his head, and went along the two of them a figure at a time: the year, and then the day. He did it without moving his lips, with his two hands flat on the rock. He did not believe what he had, and he did it again from the far end, backward, from the last figure to the first.
   104	
   105	They were one date.
   106	
   107	He knew the day. It was written in his own file, on the first line of the first sheet. It came after his name and before a word in square brackets, where the registry puts the day on which a person was classed. He had first read it at fourteen, the wrong way up, across a counter, while a woman with a pen made a small mark beside it. Every paper that the Compact had ever served on him had begun with it: a summons, and a challenge under a seal, and an order for a room and an hour. It was the day on which he had stood in a windowless building and put his hand where he was shown. A plate of brass had taken eleven seconds over him.
   108	
   109	He had been waiting for it, he found, without knowing that he waited. A quiet man had stopped like a clock over that date, by a lamp. There was only one day in the registry's whole count that could have stopped him so on Cael's account.
   110	
   111	*Deep layer*, the sheet said, in the first of its lines; and then two words; and then that day.
   112	
   113	He stood very still.
   114	
   115	He had two things. One was a line in a technician's hand, about something under a plate that nobody could open. The other was a date, and the date was his. He could feel the place where a verb would go between them. He could feel a dozen that would have fitted, each of them ready, as the ten were always ready, leaning toward the first stride.
   116	
   117	He had learned from a small pale woman what a verb cost. She had sat up over one in a store behind a chimney, with a cord that read true and a hair in a fold of paper, and had refused to let it finish itself in her own head. He had not understood until now how hard that was.
   118	
   119	He told it no.
   120	
   121	A great many people were classed in a year. On that same morning, in other districts, other children had stood on other plates; a station's man who went below one, somewhere, might have found a thing beginning and written down the day. He did not know that it was his plate. He did not know what a sequence was, or what it meant for one to be running. The sheet did not say what it went with. It had begun to say, and somebody had laid a brush across the rest.
   122	
   123	It was true, every word of it. It did not help at all, and he held to it regardless, as he would have held a rope.
   124	
   125	His left hand had gone into his coat.
   126	
   127	It had Hesk's book half out before he knew what it was about. He had written down everything that had ever happened to him. It was how an old man had taught him to keep from being told what he was: one noted the day, and the count, and the trend. He had the pencil. He had the place, under yesterday's line.
   128	
   129	He looked at the blank beneath it for as long as he could have held his breath, and it frightened him more than the date had.
   130	
   131	If he wrote the two days side by side, he would have drawn the line himself. It would not matter that he added no verb. A sum set down with its answer showing is a sentence. Anybody who opened that book would finish it for him: Lira, who had read it, or a healer, who had made him swear by it once. And it was not his alone. A man at a basin had carried it for three days, and had put it into his hands without a word, so that it need never be said.
   132	
   133	He put the book back.
   134	
   135	He lifted the second row with both hands, and laid the technician's sheet in at its foot, where an office had filed it, and squared the heap upon it.
   136	
   137	"It's whole," he said. "The row. It was only out for a while."
   138	
   139	"I saw where it was." Karis had not looked up; and then she did, and her pale eyes went over his face once, carefully, as they went over a seal. "You read it."
   140	
   141	"I've read it."
   142	
   143	She had been given those words before, in a store, by another man, about the same sheet. He saw her hear them. She did not ask a second time. She wrote nothing, and tied the tape across the second row with a knot that she pulled very tight.
   144	
   145	Lira was looking at him across the rock, with her chin up. She had a look for when he had put a thing away where she could not get at it, and he had seen it perhaps four times in his life. She gave it to him now, and held it, and let it go.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-18.md:131:If he wrote the two days side by side, he would have drawn the line himself. It would not matter that he added no verb. A sum set down with its answer showing is a sentence. Anybody who opened that book would finish it for him: Lira, who had read it, or a healer, who had made him swear by it once. And it was not his alone. A man at a basin had carried it for three days, and had put it into his hands without a word, so that it need never be said.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-40.md:57:"He was as frightened as that. You can read it off the bill; it's the only part of him that's left." Brom looked into the fire. "I'm not saying that he'd cause. A price isn't a verdict. I've known a man pay a great deal for a bad horse, and swear by it after, because of what he'd paid."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-57.md:255:*Turned the Rune fragment on myself. Cleon said don't. The reading only ever told me the still place was nothing — no flow, no current, a hole where a man should have a middle. The Rune sense says it's not a hole. It says it's* built like something *— like one of those floors with nothing standing on it. Same shape to that fragment as the sites are to Cleon's. I'm not going to write what that might mean, because I don't know, and because the last time I wrote something true in this log a healer made me swear an oath about it. The instruments read wronger for me than they did at Lowmarch. The trend's climbing. My Arbiter's still dark—nothing in me to go quiet, same as always. Tomorrow, the oldest one. I've spent the whole year wanting to stand in the middle of it. I think part of me already knows what I'm going to feel. Nothing. I think I'm going to feel nothing, and I think that's going to be the loudest thing that's ever happened to me.*

