editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-42.md:227:He went over that as he walked, since the road asked for nothing but his feet. There had been a boy on this moor fourteen days ago with a date in him that he had not written down, and a page that he had not touched, and a promise in a book. That boy had been afraid of a gate. He had thought that the worst thing a town could do was to write his name.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-21.md:197:"So you'd have had the first of your two," said Cael. "I can't swear it; a plate's not what I've got. But there's nothing in that place that moves, and I think a plate's the same sort of tool as mine. It would have lain flat, and I'd have been two blanks on one file, and they'd have found their word. Nobody has that but you. It's not in the book yet. I've not told her, and she's read every line I ever wrote."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-28.md:101:The seat wanted the maker of the copies to swear to them, and the advocate of record to sign beside his oath. Karis had said at supper that she would not put her name to a page she had not read against the thing it was copied from, and that this was not caution but manners. So the wing's original cards had come up the library stair in two shallow boxes under the porter's oilcloth, with Seln's leave and Bracken's receipt, and the second set of Seln's copies lay open beside them. He had made two of everything. One had gone down to the seat in the house's boards. The other had stayed in the annex, against exactly this.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-01.md:91:The track was there, on the near bank. It was only two ruts with a ridge of grass between them, as it had been on the upland. But it kept to the river and it ran downstream, and that was more than any of them had been promised. Lira opened her own book on her forearm and wrote as she walked, while Karis kept the printed sheet rolled under her arm and did not once unroll it, since there was nothing on that part of it but a list of what the symbols meant.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-52.md:81:"He was asked for a finding in that room, and he said he would be reporting a forger to the forger's own clerks, under an oath he took to them." Seln turned his cup a quarter turn. "He has done the one thing he could, and you heard it entered a week ago as a blank on a form. He will not do a second, and I do not blame him. He would be the first man they searched."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-52.md:331:Brom's hand went up first, as it had in this room once before, the right, like a man taking an oath. Lira's followed, and then Karis's, pale to the second knuckle, and Cael put up his own.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-40.md:205:"The second is a report. I have sworn to every report I ever made, and the oath is made to the office that receives it." He looked at the memoranda, at the nineteen seals. "That office is the one that wrote these. I should be swearing, in my best hand, to the man who forged them, and he would read it with great courtesy and enter it and thank me."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-02.md:39:"This morning I have decided nothing about you." Bracken said it to the tape, knotting it. "I wasn't asked to. Your name is on that record because you're enrolled, on a lawful basis, adjudicated in public, and renewed by a panel in front of the Compact's own chairs. The charter says the record goes, so the record has gone. A registrar who decides things is a registrar who will one day be asked, under oath, why he decided them. In twelve years nobody has asked me that question, and nobody is going to begin with this file." He looked up then, over the counter, and there was something under the dryness of his face that Cael had seen there only once before, on the step of a grain factor's counting house with his hand flat against a door. "When somebody comes to look, and somebody will, they'll find a clerk's routine with nobody's fingerprints on it but the form's."

