editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-12.md:29:He took it again the next morning, and each morning after, standing at the top while the others went down. It was the only book in which any of them was getting better, and he liked to see the page.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-12.md:67:He drew water, and sat on the well's coping when the count ran out, and kept the observation notebook, since there was a good deal to put in it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-12.md:113:"I can't write, and I can't turn a leaf. I've three boxes over my head that I can't open, and a finding in a book I've never read. I shall go out of my mind by noon." She held up her two fists. "I want a table."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-12.md:153:In the middle of the morning Cael read her the finding. He stood at the end of the trestle with Hesk's book open at the one page, and read it from *Finding* to *five dead animals*, slowly enough for a pencil to follow; and Lira took it down into the field archive in her large angry hand. It was the first time a leaf of that book had been read aloud for copying. He turned no other page, and Karis sat with her face toward the barn door until he had shut it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-12.md:265:Cael sat on her other side with her notebook on his knee and her pencil in his fingers. He was the best of her three pairs of hands, for he wrote small and did not argue about a stop.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-12.md:343:He ruled a line beneath them with the edge of the notebook's cover.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-09.md:11:They passed the split shaft, pointing at the sky, and the stony swell, and came to the gully's fan, where the pale stones lay across the valley under a skin of water. Karis stopped there. She stood in the middle of the wet with her boots in it and looked down, and then to the right, at the slope that a grey thing had climbed sooner than set one foot where she was standing; and she wrote a single word, walking, and shut the book.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-09.md:25:She went over them slowly, and Cael knew what she was reading, for he had read it himself in a jug's worth of lamplight three nights ago: four lines in a book, and nothing gone wrong in any of them.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-09.md:55:Karis had drawn her notebook out; she looked round at fourteen faces, and laid it on her knee, shut, with one finger in it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-09.md:73:Karis's finger moved in the notebook.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-09.md:267:"I can do three breaths. What I can't promise is that the Skin'll go down while I'm being bitten. It never has." He said it heavily, as a fact about a load. "Everything I was ever taught says that when teeth come you answer with iron. I won a continental title doing that and nothing else."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-09.md:293:"You own that way, and so do I. You will not use it. There is a bowman with excellent eyes, and a book in a meadow two days west of here, and a ferry; I taught you that arithmetic myself, more or less, by being the other side of it." He looked at Karis. "And so I come to your second clause."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-11.md:167:He had written it in Hesk's book a dozen times, and had never before stood in it with something coming. *One*, and every fragment he owned went dumb. There were no hands to put down. For that one count he was a boy standing in a river with a knife, who could not have shifted his feet for the forty marks.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-11.md:315:"A week before you hold a spoon. Longer for that." She nodded at the notebook's corner, which showed at the breast of Karis's coat, and Karis looked as though she had been sentenced by a court.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-11.md:361:He had Hesk's book on his knee, and the day's heading written, before the woman's lantern had stopped swinging on its nail.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-11.md:371:She had never read a leaf of that book; whatever he showed her of the fragments she saw, and the book she left alone. She turned her face a little toward the wall.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-08.md:19:Nobody was at it, and the book was not out. The slips hung in their row along the tarred oak, and the boy looked down the whole length of them without reading, as someone looks who has no letters and has been told what a thing is like. He found it by the right-hand post. Cael watched him put up his hand and lay one finger on a piece of real paper, low down, on a thorn twig with a wavy line under it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-08.md:31:Cael had never seen him so. The coat with the pinned sleeve was a thing he put on to keep the book, as a smith puts on an apron, and without it the left sleeve of his shirt hung empty from the elbow and stirred a little as he walked. He had the book under the other arm. He did not hurry, and he did not call out to ask the boy's business; he came down to the plank and set the book on it, and bent his bald brown head, since the boy was small.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-08.md:33:It was too far to hear. The boy said what he had been sent to say, and it took him three breaths, and he said it to the mark on the paper. Then he said it over again at the same length. Somebody at a gate had made him learn it by heart, and had made him promise to give it twice, to a man who took everything twice.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-08.md:163:Cael had heard a pencil come out of the spine of a notebook, somewhere on his left, at the words *in the water*. He did not turn his head. He took out Hesk's book while there was still light enough to see the lines, and wrote.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md:51:He got onto the ash by stages. The light was going yellow, and the others had drawn in without being asked; Karis sat on the end of the bench with her notebook shut, and Brom stood behind her, and Seln was a darker place against the gate-post. Lira had not moved from the trough.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md:89:He heard *minutes*, and *both hands*, and *all of me*. He knew what else those words described, and so did four people in that yard, and one of them had gone white to the lips over a shut notebook.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md:221:What he was she left alone. That was a question for people who kept books.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md:261:The small pale one was asleep sitting up, with her head on a box and a book shut under her hand.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md:291:The boy was sitting on the gravel at the water's edge, alone, with a book on his knee. He held one arm in a little against his left side, and under the shirt, when he breathed, something drew a line across his ribs. There was a healer's tape on him. That meant ribs; and ribs, by her rule, were three weeks under linen, counted from whichever day the mountain had done it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md:297:Cael heard the mules on the gravel and shut the book on his pencil.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md:323:He opened the book again while there was light.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-07.md:11:Pike had the book open and the stone on it. He looked up as they came along the eave, and looked again, and put his finger on a leaf without having to turn to it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-07.md:55:"Said to who? It's written. It's in your book."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-07.md:59:"Half this meadow can't read. The book's for me. The saying's for them."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-07.md:63:Cael had been written in a good many books. There was one in a district office with a single word against his name, and one that a mild man had filled for two years, and one on a shelf somewhere with slips in its pocket that no officer would own; and the thing they had all had in common was that they were shut. People had read them about him in rooms he was not let into, and he had stood on a carved number for fourteen sittings and never once been shown the page.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-07.md:65:This book lay open on a plank in the weather, and its keeper stood up and shouted it at anybody who was frying dough.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-07.md:89:Cael did not understand it, and then he did, and the hair stood on his arms. It was no threat, for Pike would write it, exactly, as he wrote everything. He would put down that a captain said eleven dry, and beside it that a witness at the landing had marked ten; and then he would stand up and say both to two hundred people, and say them again when the ink was on. It would be in the book for as long as there was a book, and every hirer who ever asked about that crew would be told that its captain had once stood at this plank and argued with a mark.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-07.md:195:Pike came in at dusk with the book under his arm.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-07.md:205:"She keeps a yard in Ardenmere, a fighting yard. She had me in a book before any registry would write a figure against my name, and she never once got me wrong."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-07.md:209:Cael opened his mouth to say, and found that he had no way of telling it from here. It was west, beyond a line and a hill and a river and the whole of a country, at the far end of everything he knew; and the best keeper of a straight book that he had met out here had never so much as heard the town's name.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-07.md:221:"Four lines. Nobody lost, nobody hurt, nobody knows you." Pike held up his one hand and folded the fingers down as he went. "A boat. A fence. A wagon out of a ditch. A mile behind a mule. I'd hire you tomorrow, myself, for any of the four. But a man comes to me from a hold with something that's eating his stock, and asks who he ought to have. I can't give him a crew that's never yet had anything go wrong." He let the hand fall. "It's a good book. It's a short one."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-07.md:271:The door of the lower inn faced the river, and the west, and the sun had come down until it stood on the ridge they had crossed forty days ago. It came in level across the meadow and through the doorway, the colour of the inside of a peach, and laid a long bright shape down the middle of the room. He turned the book to catch it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-07.md:275:*Thirteenth of thirteen, and Pike gave the reason: it's a good book and a short one. Eleven lines between ours and the top one. The top one is a name, Teague, and it is also on a slip for Thornwater's ford, forty marks, with HOLD beside it. He goes out on the cold road at first light for a week.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-07.md:333:The small pale one had a notebook open on the table, in an inn, among cups, and a skin of some sort pinned under her fingers. As he watched she copied something from the one to the other, and looked at what she had written, and ruled a line beneath it with real satisfaction.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-07.md:349:He was the youngest of them. He had a book in front of him, shut on a pencil, and he sat quite still in the long light from the door with his hands on the cover; and he was not staring, as boys stared who wanted a place in a crew. He was reading. Teague could see the order of it. The boots. The wrist, and the leather on the wrist. The three in the doorway behind, one at a time, left to right. He was going over a stranger as Teague himself went over ground, and he was not troubling to hide it, any more than Teague was.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-07.md:377:It was not the registry's look, which he knew in his sleep; that one went to the place where a designation should be, and then it went through him. This look stopped at what was there: it took his hands, and the book under them, and how he sat. It was asking the question that a one-armed man asked of every slip, and it was content to wait for the answer.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-04.md:39:"Every time you do anything at all, until it's worn in. I had a book for it, on the hill. What to take late, and what to give. I refused something or other every minute of every Fifth-day for two years, and I thought it was the price of being let stay. I didn't know I was learning a trade."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-04.md:79:He had told three people what it cost them, and he thought he owed the book the same account of himself.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-04.md:111:She had come up the bank with her hair stuck to her forehead, and she was reading over his arm as she had read over it since Ardenmere. Brom had the same leave. Karis, who sat ten feet away with her stone, saw what he showed her and never once looked at the book.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-04.md:123:"No. It gets quicker. On the hill, at the end, I didn't hear myself doing it; it was only a kind of leaning back, all day." He shut the book on the pencil. "I thought that was the worst of what they'd done to me, that I'd got so I could do it in my sleep. I'd have given a good deal not to be good at it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-04.md:177:She took out her notebook, and he read it over her arm, since she tilted the page for him.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-04.md:351:It would have been easy to take, for he was tired, and it was meant kindly, and it was the best thing anybody had said to him since a registrar wrote in a margin. But a clerk had written him down at fourteen, in a district office at the far end of the country, and he had been in somebody's book every day since. One morning in a ditch had not unwritten it, and he did not want the first good opinion he earned out here to rest on a mistake.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-06.md:33:Cael looked at Karis. She had the notebook out at last; it had come out of her coat the moment the bar dropped, as though it had been on a spring. She wrote one word at the head of a clean leaf, and looked at it, and drew a single line beneath it. Then she looked up at the woman with the lantern, and he saw the second question arrive in her face, whole, with its grammar. It was the question of what a thing like that could be waiting for.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-06.md:143:The first was the road they had come by, and the second went north from a wicket in the fence, up to grazing, and ended at a stone hut with nobody in it. The third left by a gate on the eastern side and went on up the valley, over a low place in the hills. He had walked two hours along that one, yesterday, with the sun on his head. It was a road like the others, with ruts that had been used, though not lately; and nobody had told him that it was different from the rest, and nobody had told him that it was the same. He turned back at the two hours because he had promised himself that he would be within a fence, any fence, well before the light began to go.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-06.md:165:"From the one-armed man. He's wrote it in the book, and that's it copied. He says you're to read it, and if it's wrong you can tell him so when you're down, and he'll say it over for the meadow after." The carter rubbed his neck. "He never sends silver up a road. It's in his box."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-06.md:217:He had not written much at Thornwater, for the first night he had not trusted his hand, and since then he had drawn water, and carried what Brom could not, and helped Karis to pace out a number along a fence. He was eighteen, and for three days he had been a hired man on a grain hold, and it had suited him better than he would have guessed. But there was a scrap of bark in the pocket of Hesk's book now, where two letters had lain, and it seemed to him that the book ought to know.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-06.md:235:*This one belongs to a one-armed man's book, and the weather. It has four lines in it. I can read all four.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-06.md:239:He read the last line over, and shut the pencil in the book.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-06.md:243:Cael looked where he was looking. There was nothing beside the stone, and the light was going. He buckled the book into the satchel and climbed down the ladder, to draw the evening's water before it was dark.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-01.md:29:He had no Path of his own, and so no tier and no rank, and he said so to himself plainly, because the dark was a good place for plain things. Everybody else he knew had come away from a Kindling at fourteen with one: a single gift, which an Arbiter named and a registry ranked ever after. His own Arbiter had woken too, on a brass plate in a windowless room, and it had taken eleven seconds over him and gone dark, and it had been dark ever since. What answered him now were nine fragments, which was the log's word for them: abilities taken out of other people's Paths, each one inside a fight that somebody else had begun in earnest, and each one written into Hesk's book with a name beside it and a price.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-01.md:87:"That's my name in a book," said Lira. "For a tree."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-01.md:91:The track was there, on the near bank. It was only two ruts with a ridge of grass between them, as it had been on the upland. But it kept to the river and it ran downstream, and that was more than any of them had been promised. Lira opened her own book on her forearm and wrote as she walked, while Karis kept the printed sheet rolled under her arm and did not once unroll it, since there was nothing on that part of it but a list of what the symbols meant.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-01.md:159:Cael had the two letters inside his coat. He had carried them two days in the pocket of Hesk's book, where a Gold's letter had lain for most of a year, and he had not expected to be rid of them before a town. He walked down the bank to her.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-01.md:267:Cael wrote it that night by the fire, in pencil, with the book on his knee.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-01.md:329:Cael sat down on the cart's shaft. He did what he had done on the east tier of a yard every week for two years, with a notebook on his knee, and it steadied him as it always had: he set out the claim, and what stood under it, and then he ruled.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-01.md:365:He wrote the day's lines in what light there was. *Sixth day. Deployment since the Line: none. Ruling: hold.* Then he put the book away and ate cold flour-cake with the rest of them, in the dark, with the river going by.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-03.md:39:"What is his name?" said Karis. She had her notebook open. "I ask because I have been writing *the gelding* for a fortnight, and if there is to be a sale I should like the entry to be right."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-03.md:135:Karis counted it. She had the purse from Ostrand in her lap, and the ferryman's price and the smith's on a cloth beside her, and she counted the whole twice over in her small dry voice, under her breath, and wrote one figure at the foot of a leaf. Then she did a thing Cael had never seen her do with a number, which was to turn the notebook over on her knee, face down.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-03.md:167:So Karis sewed the bag. She had a sailmaker's needle in her kit, for the spines of notebooks, and she closed the canvas with a flat seam and went back over it, and Brom warmed his wax at the fire and laid it along the stitches in a strip as wide as a thumb.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-03.md:215:"You're smiling," said Karis. She was amidships, with her notebook, timing the crossing by Hesk's clock.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-03.md:257:Then he opened the book at their leaf and put the stone on its corner and wrote, and when the ink was down he said the same words over again, exactly, to the same tents.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-05.md:3:By the forty-first day they had three lines in Pike's book and a place at the edge of the carters' fire.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-05.md:7:Nine marks earned, the book said. It did not say that Lira's palms had skinned over, or that Brom could now drop a tree at the first slip and catch it again at the second, or that Karis had held a point unlit to the count of three hundred and then put it out herself. Cael kept those in his own book, at the foot of each day, under a line that had not changed since a ring of churned turf.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-05.md:201:Karis was at the mule's off shoulder with her face as white as her fingers. Her right hand had gone to the breast of her coat, where the notebook was. It stopped there. It came down. He saw it go up again within ten strides, and stop on the same button, and come down; she was a person watching the one thing in a month that she most wanted to write, at the one moment when taking out a pencil might, for all she knew, be a declaration too. He counted, since counting was what he had. She decided against it five times in that mile.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-21.md:11:She had seen him find it. She was winding what was left of the strip back onto its peeled stick, and she spoke across him to Karis, who was sitting up this morning in Seln's coat with her notebook open on her knee and no pencil in her hand.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-21.md:215:Karis had been studying the healer across the flames for some time, with her notebook on her knee. She could hold a pencil again, for as long as it took to write a line, and she had spent her line that evening on the foot of the long leaf. Cael had seen it: *Sixty-three. The ledge. Gone, for thirty-one paces. Cart: no.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-21.md:247:"But you keep a record," she said. "You must. Seven holds, and every one with its sick; what you found, and what you did, and what you said you'd do next time. I should like to see how you've ruled it, for I've never seen a healer's book."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-21.md:259:"That's the book. A healer who needs a page to know who's owed has stopped looking at them."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-21.md:291:He had a book, and she had a route, and he did not think that either of them owned much else.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-24.md:23:The meadow had thinned, for the cold had sent most of the tents home, and there were five fires where there had been a dozen; but the long eave was where it had always been, and under it twelve feet of tarred oak, and behind the oak a bald brown head bent over a book.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-24.md:55:He had been written on a good many boards. A woman in Ardenmere had chalked him up with a question mark, and a panel had posted him at a rate that it had scattered on purpose, and for two years a house had put his name on a card every week because a schedule told it to. Nobody had ever before gone to the keeper of a book and asked who was good, and been given him.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-24.md:75:Pike nodded slowly. He opened the book at the leaf with five names, and set the flat stone on its upper corner, and turned his shoulder to the meadow. There were perhaps thirty people within hearing, and Cael saw the hide-woman among them, with her knife on her thigh, and under the ash an old carter who lifted his chin.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-24.md:127:It was a grey river stone the size of a loaf, and it lay on the upper corner of the open book, as it always lay. Something was under its far edge: a corner showed there, toward Pike's side of the oak, no wider than three fingers, and it was neither bark nor hide. It was paper, of a good cream weight, such as a clerk writes on at a desk.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-24.md:133:Cael leaned a little over the oak. The writing ran away from him, toward the man with the book, and he read it upside down, as he had once read a bailiff's list on a counter in Denvash.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-24.md:143:Cael had no other word for it. He had watched that face for eighty days, and it was never still; it looked at a thing and then looked again, and something in it went on working like a mill between the two looks. Now the one arm lay on the book and the brown eyes rested on a sheet of cream paper, and nothing in the face moved at all.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-24.md:153:Beside Cael's shoulder a notebook shut with a small clap.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-24.md:249:The account was square, as far as coin could square it. There remained a rope with no figure against it, and a line in a book that said *cart lost*, and neither of those could be paid off; they could only be added to, by whatever the five of them did next.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-24.md:267:"He counts what he hasn't done," she said. "That's all that is. Two hundred times he didn't. You'd have gone round it the first time, and the second, and you'd have written both down. And the third time you'd have stood there with your book shut, working out what it would cost to look." She scuffed the great boot along the shadow's edge. "I've watched you price things since a lane in Ardenmere. You've said *not yet* to me, and *not here*, and *no* a hundred times. You don't say *never*. I don't think you know how."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-24.md:277:He thought of a question that stood in his book under the fifty-ninth day, in her words, with nothing written beneath it. She had asked what she was becoming if nobody was measuring, and he had not answered, and nobody had. He did not suppose that a ring on a road would answer it either. But it would tell her one thing plainly, at one stride, without a sheet or a panel or a coach; and he saw that she had heard that in the old man's three short sentences before any of the rest of them.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-02.md:17:He looked for the wall first, since every town he had ever entered had kept one, even Denvash, which was nothing; and a wall meant a gate, and a gate meant a man in it with a book. He looked for the station next, the low stone house with the windowless building behind, and for a board of hours, and for any roof with a seal burned into its sign. His eye went over the place three times in search of them, like a tongue going back to where a tooth has been.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-02.md:57:There were fires here, and he felt Karis see it beside him, for her pencil came out of the spine of her notebook. There were cook-fires at half the tents, and a woman frying something over one, and smoke lying along the river in a low blue shelf. People were talking, too, in ordinary voices, and somebody laughed aloud. Down by the smithy a man was holding the end of an iron bar across the anvil with his bare forearm, and the arm was grey to the elbow, and nobody round him so much as turned to look.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-02.md:147:The left sleeve of his coat was folded and pinned at the elbow, neatly, with a brass pin gone dull. Under the right arm he carried a tall book in boards, and he had a pen behind his ear. He was broad and not tall, with a grey stubble and a brown bald head, and his eyes were the colour of the tar on the plank.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-02.md:155:"I keep the book."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-02.md:163:He set the book on the plank. There was a hole bored in the oak at that place, the size of a hen's egg, and a horn ink-pot sat down in it to its lip, where no elbow could tip it; beside the hole lay a flat grey river stone as big as a hand. He opened the book and put the stone on the corner of the leaf. Cael understood then what it was for, since a man with one arm cannot hold a page down and write on it as well.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-02.md:165:The others had come up while he spoke, Brom large and quiet, and Karis with her notebook shut on her finger. At the plank's far end, reading the last slip as if he had been there for some time, stood a mild man in a dark coat.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-02.md:167:"Copper," said Brom slowly. "And you keep the book."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-02.md:183:Nobody answered him, for Lira looked at Cael, and Cael looked at Brom, and Brom looked at the ground, and Karis opened her notebook as though it might be written there. It had never once come up. They had been four enrollees and a clerk; before that they had been a provision, and a ladder fighter, and a docket, and a research budget, and a man who sent in returns.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-02.md:193:"I've forty headings in this book," he said, "and every one's a name. One name. A man, or a woman, and whoever walks with them." He scratched his jaw with the feather end of the pen. "Well. It'll be five names, then. It takes more ink. Say them slow, and I'll write what I hear."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-02.md:197:A man who had been posted from town to town for fifteen years had been written into a book in every one of them, always by some other hand, and under a grade.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-02.md:211:He ruled the leaf with the edge of the stone, drawing the pen down beside it four times, and when he had five columns he wrote a word at the head of each. Cael read them upside down, across the plank, as he had read a deposit book and an enrollment book and most of what he knew.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-02.md:215:He looked for the sixth, though he knew that it was not there, having counted five; he looked for it all the same, the way you look for a stair in the dark, with a foot. Every book that had ever had his name in it had kept a place for what he was. There had been a column in a district office at the far end of the country with one word in it, and a narrow column with no heading in a station's register, and a line on a form where a registrar had hunted two years for a better word and written *unclassified*. His foot came down on nothing at all, and the jolt of it went through him.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-02.md:227:"No. Not yet." Pike lifted the stone, and blew on the ink, and shut the book. "A line goes in when a job's been witnessed. The book makes the wall, and the wall's inside, by the hearth. You're not on it." He tucked the book under his arm. "Take a slip or don't. I'm at my dinner."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-02.md:241:"I'd mine ready as well," said Brom. "Copper. For good, and I can say why. And then the man with the book's a Copper himself, and he says it the way I'd say I was tall."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-02.md:253:It was a strange lightness, and he was not sure that he trusted it. Out beyond a line he had waited for the ground to tell him he had crossed something, and it had said nothing; today a book had been opened and ruled for him, and it had not wanted to know what he was. Both times he had braced for a step that was not there.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-02.md:275:"Because the other four are facts about a contract. Who wanted what done, and who saw it, and what was paid." Karis laid the pencil in the fold. "That one is a fact about us. It's the only place in his whole book where he writes down anything we did. I should like to know what words he uses."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-02.md:279:*Ninth day. Lowmarch. A river, a ferry, two inns, a smith, a meadow, and no wall. Fires and voices and Paths in the meadow; none of them on the road. A board with fourteen slips on it and not one that prices the crew. A heading in a one-armed man's book: five names, five columns, nothing under them. He asked how old I was. He asked nothing else.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-02.md:283:He shut the book and looked across the fire, to where the gelding had his nose in a net by the cart. Brom had gone over to stand with him, and was counting something on his fingers, slowly, and then he counted it a second time, and from the set of his shoulders he liked the answer no better.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-23.md:5:Cael stayed where he was, on the gravel at the water's edge, with the book shut on his knee and his left arm held in against five bands of linen. Six days ago the side of a mountain had come down on five people and a cart, with a herd of frightened beasts on top of it, and all five had walked off; he had not yet got used to counting them and finding five.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-23.md:229:Karis was awake. She had been awake since noon, sitting up between the boxes with her notebook on her knee, and she had the colour back in her face that six days of sleep could give it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-23.md:241:Lira looked across the fire at Cael, and he kept his eyes on the book.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-34.md:77:He did not read the stamp; he knew what it said. He let his eye rest on it as one lets a hand rest on a latch. A man answers for what he takes: he had said that at noon. Iron of that pattern is counted out across a board by a storekeeper who does not look up, and signed for in a book with ruled columns, and carried away in a case with a strap.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-34.md:105:The camp was stirring when he came down the line. The healer lay as she had fallen. The girl was awake, with her eyes on the sky, and the boy sat beside her with a book shut on his knee and one arm held in his lap like a thing that belonged to somebody else. Karis was sitting up against the box. She had her hand flat on its lid, and he knew that she had not yet opened it to look at eleven sheets, and why.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-34.md:255:Karis had the notebook on her knee. She wrote one word, large, and turned it to him.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-34.md:277:He took the cream sheet out of Hesk's book.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-20.md:89:He had the book, for Hesk's satchel had been on his shoulder when the mountain came, as it was always on his shoulder, and it had gone under him when he fell and taken no harm. He opened it once, in the grey of the morning, and found the page with the last entry. It ended on the word *Sleep*, and above that he had written that the job had no fight at the end of it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-20.md:91:He read it through, and he was not ashamed of it, for it had been true when the pencil made it; but he could find no sentence to set beneath it that was a thing counted, and he would not put down one that was only a thing felt. He shut the book, and that day got no line either.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-20.md:93:Karis made no move toward her notebook either. She slept, and woke to drink, and slept again; and each time she woke she looked at her two hands before she looked at anything else.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-20.md:195:"Iron. Four, if anybody's keeping a book up here."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-20.md:373:It was dusk before Cael opened the book, and it was easier than it had been in the rain. He had five things that were counted.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-19.md:13:He did not count. He had the book open against the shaft, with the leaf turned to the sixty-third line, and he had written the number and nothing after it. Above them the slope was in shadow still. Thirty grey heaps lay along the upper shelf with white on their backs, and not one of them had yet begun to scrape.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-19.md:99:Cael could not see them. Nobody could; a set point was a promise and gave no light. But he had watched her lay them for two years, in a whitewashed room and on a district floor, and each one had always cost her the same small dip of the right hand, as a person dots a page. He counted the dips, and followed her eyes. The first went down eleven paces up the slope, straight above the middle of the wall; the second eight paces from that, down and to the left; and then the third.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-19.md:201:"I couldn't see," he said. "I heard something go. I wouldn't swear to it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-19.md:249:The rock was a gift. It was dry under its roof, and rimed along the outer half where the roof stopped, so she kept in, close under the wall, as she had promised a fire that she would. Her left foot struck and her right came after it. She had the count in her legs. Forty. Fifty. The gorge went by on her right hand, blue and enormous, and she gave it none of her attention; she was reading the shelf five yards ahead of her toes, for cracks and for colour.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-18.md:43:He looked up the slope at thirty grey heaps, and thought of a page in his own book that had her three clauses on it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-18.md:73:"It's clean," said Brom. He had got down on one knee and laid his right hand on the western edge, and then on the eastern. "Look at that. It's not crumbled. The frost's got into a joint and opened it like a book." He sat back. "That's the same height both sides, to a finger. If there was a plank across it you'd never know."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-16.md:185:"Seventy lines." Lira began to laugh, low, mindful of the rule. "Seventy marks. He's being paid by the line." She pointed at the book in his hand. "You've written in one of those every night since a lane in Ardenmere, and nobody ever gave you a copper. Now there's a man with a ford who'll give you a mark every time you rule one off."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-13.md:171:"It says we cleared it. It doesn't say how any one of us did, and it never will, because that's not what his book is for. I've understood that. I'm not stupid." She turned the stick. "I'm saying it's the first time in my life that I've done a thing and not been told how well."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-13.md:175:"I ran thirty yards of gravel at that ford with nothing in my feet," said Lira. "No Wind. No burst. I caught a dog. I've never run like it. I didn't know I could, and I'm telling you it was the best thing I've ever done on my two legs, and it cost—" She stopped. Her hand had gone toward the stave, and she took it back. "It cost what it cost. And there isn't a person alive who can say whether it was good. There's a man with a bow who lifted two fingers. There's a book that says nobody died."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-13.md:195:He had the sentence ready; it had come to him at once, whole, and it was the worst thing he could have said to her. *Isn't it better?* He had sat in a bar of red light ten days ago and written that no instrument out here could read him, and that he was free; and he had meant every word, and he meant it still. Not one book in this country had a column for a tier or a rank, or for what he was, and it was the best thing that had happened to him since a brass plate went dark.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-13.md:203:He took out Hesk's book instead, since it was the one honest thing he had by him. He wrote the day, and the place, and under them her question, word for word as she had put it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-13.md:231:It was the middle of the morning on the sixtieth day. They had crossed the wash at first light, with Brom walking wide of the cart and both hands behind his back, and they had come over the saddle slowly. Pike was not at his plank, and the book was not out; a boy at the nearest fire said that he was down at the landing, since the ferry had brought a sack.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-13.md:277:He went and stood at the end of the plank, to wait for a man with a book. Cael watched him go, a mild man in a dark coat with eight roads in his head and a well's slip over his heart; and he thought that he had perhaps never been of so much use to anybody as by declining to ask a question.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-13.md:281:Pike came up from the landing with a sack over his shoulder and the book under his arm.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-13.md:291:He set the book down, and put the stone on it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-33.md:133:Karis had her notebook open on her knee, and she was writing with the pencil held in her fist. Her knuckles were skinned across both hands, where teeth had gone over them; she had looked at them once and left them. When she had three lines she turned the leaf to the lantern and to him.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-33.md:147:Then she shut the book. She did not lie down. She was looking at his left leg, which lay stretched toward the lamp with the cloth black from the knee to the boot, and he watched her think it through as she thought through everything. The healer was asleep and would be asleep till morning; Seln had the south and Brom the north, and Lira had one arm. That left a person with two skinned hands and no voice.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-33.md:185:Karis had looked at the lantern's glass before she slept, and held up three fingers, which was what an hour had left in it. He set it on the lid of the box and opened Hesk's book to the page that had stopped short. The entry there was in his own hand and a few hours old; it said that the dogs were inside, and that he had told nobody, and he read it as he might have read the writing of somebody he had known as a boy.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-33.md:225:It stood where the word *one* had stood on forty-three nights. He had shut the book at a far lip because he did not know what would be true by dark; and here was a true number, and a large one, and there was not a unit in it that had not been paid for on somebody's body, his own or another's.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-33.md:271:It was the better part of two hours before he opened the book again; and he wrote by starlight, large, one sentence to a line, as Karis had written at this same stone on the night before.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-33.md:275:He shut the book on it, and lay down, and did not sleep for some time.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-14.md:71:He wrote it in the book before the light changed.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-14.md:83:Nobody had gone back to the fires. There were perhaps forty people in the meadow that day, and most of them were within hearing of the eave when he opened the book at the leaf with five names and set the stone on its corner. He had the slip in front of him, soft from being carried in a shirt, with a struck name on it and five strokes, and at the foot a thorn twig drawn in charcoal.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-14.md:133:"He came down the cold road on the fifty-seventh, with his three and his mules, and he walked to the plank before he'd had so much as a wash, and stood in front of the place where that slip had been." He turned the cup. "Then he asked me for it. I'd kept it by, struck, as I'd struck it, and I gave it him, and he read it twice; I counted, since it's what I'd have done. Then he had me open the book at your leaf, and he read your line."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-14.md:441:*Six lines in Pike's book, and the sixth is a well. Earned, sixty-two. In hand, fifty-two. Twelfth. The captain at the top has been in and gone out again on two holds' slips, and has said nothing to us or about us. Pike says that is the ledger.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-15.md:17:He had come out with the book under his arm and his coat on, and he set the flat stone on the page before he so much as glanced at the new slip. He had read it by lamplight when the carter brought it, and had no need to look.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-15.md:31:"It's the same thing with a wheel on it." Pike laid his one hand on the paper. "Oxhollow's six days north-west of this plank, and Thornwater's two days east of it. A cart from the one that wants grain from the other comes round by my ferry, and it's eight days, and eight home. There's high ground between the two holds. The salt crews say there's a shelf along the top of it, a bench, with a gorge down one side. A mule string went over it once, before I'd this book, and came down in three days." He looked at the horned head, twice. "Somebody's told the ford so. The ford wants to know whether it's true before it sends a wheel."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-15.md:41:Karis had her notebook out, and her pencil was already turning in her fingers.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-15.md:59:The first was the plainest: it paid. Seventy marks was more than Pike's book had counted out to them in six lines, and the crew was still four hurts short of whole.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-15.md:103:Cael looked back from the ash. The eave was sixty yards behind him, and under it two men stood at the far end of the plank, where the oak ran out past the right-hand post. One of them had a book under his arm. The other stood with his head bent, as a clerk stands who has come to ask for the loan of a file. He was talking, low, and Pike was listening with his chin down.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-15.md:111:He had told that man at that same plank, ten days ago, that nobody was coming to take his report off him. It had been easy to say, and he had learned since that the saying was the smallest part of it. The promise had to be kept at exactly such moments as this one, when the report was sixty yards off and plainly worth having, and when nobody but himself would ever know that he had turned his back on it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:87:She knew what that was. She had read it in his book before she ever saw it, in a boy's cramped writing, with *sealed* beside it and a mild man's name left out. She had seen it since, a handful of times, and never once in front of anybody who did not already know. It had gone by her elbow like a draught, and then he was on the dog.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:141:She came out. She rubbed her hands together slowly, as she did when they had been in cold water, and she looked across the grass and saw him sitting with a book on his knee.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:145:He had the book open. He had the day's number written at the head of a clean leaf, and nothing under it. He had entered every crossing of that line since the ninety-fifth evening: five people abreast, and Karis's nine in the dark, and each of the trials in its row.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:151:He shut the book on a leaf with one line.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:183:Karis was in front of her. Karis had not said a word since the lane, and could not, and she had her notebook in one hand and her eyes on a thing that she was working out; she walked as she always walked when she was thinking, without the least idea where the ground was. There was a place where a grain cart's wheel had cut the rut a hand deeper than the rest. She put her left foot into it and went over sideways like a dropped rule.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:193:It was too slow, and it was too clumsy. A right arm is not where a left arm would have been. Karis struck it and slid, and went down on one knee in the rut with a jolt that brought a noise out of her throat, a croak, the first sound that she had made since the lane; and the notebook flew.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:207:It was not praise. Lira knew what praise sounded like; she had been given it on painted floors by people with slates. It was a count, said by a person who kept no book, and she found that she would sooner have had it than a sheet of figures.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:241:Lira laughed, and put her right hand to the sling as though it had jarred her, and went on laughing. Karis had her notebook out. She wrote for some time, with her tongue between her teeth, and turned the leaf to the fire.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:351:She was not being short with him. It was only how a healer's table was run: nobody was promised what the hands would find; they were told afterward, whatever it was, to their faces.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-36.md:25:Karis lifted one finger from the notebook. Her throat was the colour of an old apple by that light, and she had spoken twice that day, a word at a time, in a voice like a rasp on wood.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-36.md:45:Karis wrote for the length of three breaths. Then she did a thing that he had never seen her do to that book: she tore the leaf out along its stitching, carefully, and laid it on the boards in front of the lantern, facing the rest of them.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-36.md:57:*Not tested. Not going into the book as though it were.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-36.md:147:Karis had turned the notebook round. She had written four lines on a fresh leaf, and this one she had not torn out.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-36.md:149:*It is wrong. I want the result more than I have ever wanted a result. Both of those are mine. Entered against me, in the book, tonight.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-36.md:155:She looked at him to be sure that he had it, and shut the book.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-36.md:229:The bead went out. He lay down between two sacks of barley with the book under his hand; and in the dark Wind leaned a little toward where Lira was breathing, and the heaviest of the nine shifted, like a sea a long way off, and was still.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-17.md:81:Cael sat on her left with the notebook, and Lira lay on the rock at her right, on her stomach again. Brom stayed under the roof with the cart, and Seln had gone on westward before any of them woke. The sun had not yet reached into the gorge, and thirty heaps of grey stone lay along the upper shelf with frost on their backs.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-17.md:115:"A step, then, from the two it nearly hit, and a step isn't flight." Karis let out her breath. "Give me the notebook; I want to put this one in my own hand." She took it on her knee and wrote slowly, with her lips tight. "*Sound: a rock the size of a keg, striking their own shelf. Felt in the slab here. Two animals moved one step each. No other response.*"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-17.md:119:"That's two." She gave him back the book, and flexed her fingers. "And it's the two I could have guessed, since the carter lit his fire at the wash and nothing came. I'm only being told what I knew."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-17.md:143:"I'd swear to it. I heard its own horn first, and then the slide."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-17.md:229:"The other one I gave you because I couldn't hold a pencil. This I can write myself, and I have. But it's derived, and a derived thing in one hand is an opinion." She was looking at the leaf, and not at Hesk's book, which she had never read. "You had the far end of the line. If you'll put down what you saw there, in your own words, then it's two people's."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-17.md:231:He took out the book and wrote it, with the day at the head: the plates he had been counting, and the big animal stepping before any sound had reached it, and that he had looked for one that went first and found none. He wrote her three clauses under that, and her two words under the clauses. It was the first time that anyone had asked him to keep a finding because his hand would make it worth more, and he wrote it as small and as straight as he could.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-32.md:215:*One. Two.* For ten counts after a lane, everything that he had answered him a beat late. He had written it so in Hesk's book, on a road, from a bout. It was a different thing to run in. His foot came down, and then he felt it come down. The flame leaned, and then he saw it lean.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-46.md:5:Cael took it out of Hesk's book, where it had lain since the ninetieth night with five strokes at its foot. Seln held the two sheets up to the fire, one in each hand, so that the flame stood behind both; and for some time he looked through them, as he had looked through the first on an inn's boards, and read no word of either.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-46.md:17:"The first was a question, and I said so at a stake. It sent a crew to fetch what was not there, in order to learn what became of the crew. A hundred marks is a fair price for a question. It is high, since nobody had ever taken such a paper; but a carter could believe in it." His finger moved to the bracket. "Nobody who has kept a book believes in this. It is two days out and two home, for a case that one person can carry on a strap. There is not a hold on that sheet that would pay thirty for it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-46.md:39:It was Karis, low and quick. She had both hands on her shut notebook, and her eyes were on Seln.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-46.md:97:"I am saying it first, so that it is not taken for a vote on anybody else's arm. I have a cup of barley and a bowl in the middle box. In the cup are the kinds that I should swear to. In the bowl are the kinds that only a second floor can give me or take away, and I have had my thumb over the figure for nine days." Her voice was small and quite steady. "A man with apple trees told me that I should not tell a keeper from an eater in the one basket. I have one basket. I shall have one basket until I have stood on another of those floors with paper in my hand, and here is another, a day and a half from this fire."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-46.md:185:Cael wrote by the fire while Brom banked it, with Lira reading over his arm as she had promised.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-46.md:187:*Hundred and twelfth day. Lowmarch, the meadow. Pike said the eighth line in our hearing, twice. Eleventh; Teague ten up. He says his book has no column for what the line means. A hundred and two in hand.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-46.md:205:The room was thinner still: the three grey men had gone, and their dice with them, and a girl was turning the benches up along one wall. Pike sat where he had sat, under the cloth, with the fire fallen to red and the book shut on his knee.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-46.md:229:"It's a line in a book. Don't thank a man for his book." Pike's one hand lay on the cover. "I said what I'd do with that paper. You've done the other. I'd as soon have you down to tell me which of us was right."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-30.md:29:She did not say anything about her sum. She laid the end of the cord down very carefully on the grey stone, and set her notebook on it for a weight, and then she paced from where she stood to the rim, in four directions, counting. The four counts came out within a stride of each other.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-30.md:61:He paced it from west to east, past her notebook, and had twenty-six strides. He paced it again from south to north, and had twenty-six. By the time he came back with the second figure, nine twists of paper lay opened in a row along the lid of the middle box, each with its chip set back in it and a small cross pencilled on the paper.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-30.md:233:He wrote it there, at the far lip, standing, with the book against his forearm.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-30.md:245:He did not write it. It would not have been true, and he did not yet know what would be. He shut the book on an entry that stopped short, for the first time since he had crossed the Line, and put it away in Hesk's satchel.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-28.md:5:She did not go alone, since Seln had made a rule and she had lost no time in seeing the sense of it. Lira had the watch with her. Lira sat down in the grass ten yards short of the place, with her back turned and her knife across her knees, to look at everything that was not a ring of stones; and Karis went on the last ten yards by herself, with a notebook, and a pencil, and no lamp.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-28.md:7:There could be no lamp. She had lit one in a yard two evenings ago and been absurdly glad of it, and she would not have set a point here for all the paper in Ternhall. She knelt down in the frozen grass on the western side of the stone and laid the notebook open on her knee. The stars were out. She could see the white of the page and not much else, and she resolved to write large, one line to a leaf, and sort it by daylight.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-28.md:51:Her pencil had come down on the page. She looked at the point of it, resting on white paper in the starlight, and she knew exactly what kind of sentence it was about to begin. It would have had *as though* in it. It would have said what the thing was like; and a likeness, set down in a notebook at midnight, is a hypothesis that has not yet had the honesty to call itself one.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-28.md:197:She stood there with her eyes shut, and her lips moved for some time. Cael knew what she was doing, since she had the length of that cord in feet, to the knot, in a notebook that nobody else had read, and had paced her own stride twice against his in the dark. He would have given a good deal to check the sum; he had only her figures for the half of it, and so he waited.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-31.md:5:The sun had gone under the hills while Cael's book was shutting. He had seen the pale thing hold the light on the evening before, from three hundred yards, and had taken it for water; now he stood below its eastern lip, with the rolled edge at the height of his thigh, and saw what it did close to. The long shadows of the stones were gone, and the whole round of it lay level with his hip and gave back the sky, as milk in a pan gives back a window.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-45.md:43:Cael had the first watch. He sat with his back against a chock-stone and Hesk's book shut on his knee, and for some time he left it shut.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-45.md:93:They went down into the meadow with the light going. The old place under the ash was empty, since a good many places were, and Brom unloaded there and rubbed the mule's back with a twist of hay while Seln walked once round the twelve fires and came back and said that there was nothing. A boy came for the standing-money. Karis gave him two coppers, which were too small a matter for her book.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-45.md:95:The plank under the lower inn's eave was bare of its book. Pike had taken it in for the night, as he did when the dusk came early; but a line of yellow showed under the inn's door, and the noise of the room came through the wood.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-45.md:133:Pike had dipped his pen. He did not write with it. The line had been in his book for eleven days. He raised his eyes to the sailcloth over his head, and found the place.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-45.md:157:"On the cloth. I never said the cloth was all of it." He looked at the fire for a breath, and then at his book, and he went on to the book, in the size of voice that he kept for things nobody had asked him. "I've had that sheet thirty years. Forty-odd crews have been on it, one time and another, and gone off it. Some went home. Some the road had. There's not one of the forty that ever went over a line and came back to tell me so; there's none that I ever heard of who tried. I've a line in here now that says it was done." His finger lay on the leaf. "Six in and six out, and the sixth a healer. I'd a good look for the column to put that in. There isn't one."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-45.md:165:He had been a number in other people's books for as long as he could remember. A magister had given him one every week, to the decimal. Vell had kept one for him in a yard. Every one of those figures had been somebody's best account of how much of a thing he was; and here was a book-keeper with one arm, who had never owned an instrument, saying aloud in a room that his own book had a true line in it and was too small to hold what the line meant. Cael did not know what to do with it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-45.md:185:Cael picked the paper up. It was the same weight as the other, which lay folded in Hesk's book; it had the same pricked margin, and one word standing alone at its head, with a stop after it. He held it so that the four of them could see, and read it out low, a stop at a time.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-45.md:193:Pike had his eyes on his book.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-45.md:199:He did not raise his voice for it, and he kept his eyes on the book. He had told a man in this room, on the eighty-ninth evening, that he would have done the same with the first of those papers; and on the ninetieth he had held the first out across his plank to five people and written five strokes on it. He had not said then what he would do in their place. Cael understood that he had been given something that Pike did not give, and that it had not been asked for.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-47.md:121:He took Hesk's book out of the satchel. He had written nothing in it yet that day, and he wetted the pencil and put this down before anything else, with the date over it, so that it should stand first.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-47.md:197:"Now you'll hear what it is," she said, "since you'll want it for your book, and I want it plain."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-47.md:243:At the fork there was a flat rock, and Cael sat down on it with Hesk's book while the two mules were let to blow. The grey had come up the notch backward for a good part of the way. The mill's wheel was still to be heard below them, very small. He wrote under what he had written on a bench.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md:43:"Teague's nine up," Pike said, to the book, asked by nobody. Then, still to the book, at the same size, as if it were the next line and not a different thing: "He read that slip yesterday. Read it twice, same as the boy did. Put it back under the stone and took the fence. I'd have done the same, and I told him, and he said he knew."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md:47:He had read that man's feet in the silt of a ford, and his halts at five halting-grounds after it. He had watched him count three people on a sill before he looked at a room, and had been told by him on a river bank, in a level voice, what a mountain was worth. Now he had the man's answer to a hundred marks as well, at second hand, from a book-keeper who gave out nothing that he had not seen with his own eyes.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md:59:He sent the boy to the smith's with the mules and went up to the plank by himself, as he always did, before he had so much as washed. Pike was taking in his book for the night. Teague read the oak from the left-hand post, a slip at a time, and at the second slip he stopped.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md:67:Pike's stone lay on the book's corner, where it had lain for as long as Teague had been coming to that plank; and under its far edge there was the corner of a sheet of paper. A line of dust lay across the corner, fine and pale, drawn along the stone's rim. He had been four weeks up the north water, and that sheet had plainly lain there for most of them. A waybill does not gather dust under a stone.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md:99:It was all either of them said about it. There was a fence on the oak, four slips along: the axe-head hold's longer fence on the east road, forty marks, HOLD. Teague drew its tack and held it out, and Pike wrote his name to it before he shut the book and tucked it under what was left of his arm.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md:117:She had her notebook shut under both hands, and she did not open it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md:147:He had a good many reasons. He could have said what he had said to Lira under the eave, that he wished to see it, and it would have been true. He could have said that a hold had asked for them by name, and that a crew which was asked for ought to find out what it was made of. There was a rope with no figure against it. There was a cart in a gorge, and a line in a book.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md:179:"Inside the Line, at a desk, from a book of forms. I do not say whose desk. I say that it was not composed on that landing. It was copied there, or else it came over the water as it stands, with the money."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md:193:Karis had opened the notebook after all. She was counting on the edge of a leaf, in small strokes.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md:211:There were five. He drew the cream sheet toward him, and folded it once along its old fold, and put it inside Hesk's book for the night.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md:243:"Then you come down and tell me so, and I say that." Pike was blowing on the strokes to dry them. "I write what was done. There's a hundred in my box against his mark on this paper, and if his mark never goes on it, the box stays shut. I've nothing in the book that pays a crew for walking."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-26.md:15:He knew the first by its writing before he had it fairly in his fingers. It was a narrow upright hand, every letter the same height, and he had read it on a yard's rota slates for two years, and once at the foot of a bout sheet. The second was a square of stiff paper ruled faintly in red, folded in four and tied with a waxed thread. It was a leaf out of a ledger, and he knew of only one person who would have cut a page from her own book in order to write to him on it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-26.md:37:He put them both inside Hesk's book, between the leaves, where a cream sheet had lain the night before. It was a small thing to have decided; but he had gone a long time with nothing in front of him except what he was holding down, and he liked the weight of two unbroken seals a good deal more than he would have liked knowing what was under them.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-26.md:39:They spent the rest of that day getting ready for a road. The Oxhollow carter had his off mule at the smith's for a shoe. Brom went over the three box-ropes a hand's breadth at a time, since they were all the rope that the five now owned. Seln was gone from the meadow between the mail and dusk, and came back with pale dust on his boots, and said nothing; and Cael, who had made a promise at that plank, looked at the dust and then at the fire.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-26.md:235:Karis was on the well's coping with her notebook. She looked up.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-27.md:5:Two of the three boxes were to stay in the loft, and so two of the three box-ropes were free. Brom married them end to end with a splice that he worked by feel, and hung the whole length from a fence-post with a stone on its end to take the stretch out, while she stood by with her short cord, the one that was knotted at every foot. When the rope had done creaking she laid the short cord along it again and again, and at each length she tied in a scrap of red wool from the hold's mending basket. Cael watched her count. She told nobody the figure, and wrote it in her notebook with her shoulder turned, as a person writes down the weight of his own purse.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-27.md:45:They had halted on a level of short turf where a spring came out. Brom had been helped off with the box and lay on his back beside it with his arms spread. The others had gone to the water. Lira had not; she had sat down next to Cael, and stretched out both legs, and was looking at Hesk's book where it lay on his knee.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-27.md:65:"No. I don't suppose she does." She took the leaf from him and read it through again herself, with her lips moving, and Cael watched her come to the end of it and go back two sentences. "She's got a book like the old man's, hasn't she? She writes down what happened. And she's put you in it as a thing that she's never had to write before."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-27.md:73:He had thought about that on the forty-ninth night, and it came back to him now on a turf bank two days past a fence. There was a woman in Ardenmere who kept a straight ledger of a fighting yard, and a one-armed man in a meadow who kept a straight book of a plank; and neither had the least idea that the other was alive. He was written in both. He did not think that any registry on the continent could have said as much of one of its own.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-27.md:101:He put the leaf inside the book, beside the other. There was nothing left now with a seal on it. He did not mind that either, since both of them had turned out to be the kind of letter that keeps.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-27.md:141:Karis had her notebook open, and her pencil was not moving. Cael knew what that stillness cost her. A primary source was sitting across a fire from her, pulling on a boot, and had been round the place thirty times.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-29.md:253:The finding in his book said that the hounds answered what went out of a person; and a fragment answering went out of him, as he had felt it go at a ford, where one burst had drawn three. That was the cost, and he set it down first. Against it there was the hour, at which no hound on any road had ever been seen abroad, and there was the choice of what to ask.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-29.md:275:He walked in, as a man goes who means to look at the ground that he will presently lead people over, without haste, twenty strides toward the ring. A grey boulder lay there in the grass, as high as his hip, and he walked straight at it. He kept his face to the east; if anyone at the stone had glanced his way, there was only a person with a notebook going to see what lay beyond a rock.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-29.md:285:He shut them both. He put Wind down, and closed the read as one closes a book, and they went back to where the other seven lay.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-42.md:95:"I know what you've held. I've watched you not catch a notebook, and a hen." Oryn did not open her eyes. "It's nothing that you did. Be quiet."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-42.md:227:"The third is the edge. A dressed stone is squared to sit on its neighbour, with a face and a bed and an arris between." She set the district hall's chip upright, on a side that was as true as a book's. "I went round every one of those eleven on my knees, and not one has a laid edge. They are not set on that floor. They come up out of it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-43.md:61:"I have four things in the box," she said at last. "A name. Five rows that are one row. A floor that is older than my oldest stone. A bar of iron. I should swear to any of them in front of a seat."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-43.md:229:She put four of the eight into the ring. He counted them, and so did a good many other people, though nobody on that ground said a figure aloud; Karis, behind him, was making strokes in her notebook with the pencil held very small. The boy went out between each pair and came back at a run, with his chin up, as if the bolts had been his own. Not one went over the sack, and not one fell short of it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-40.md:59:"You don't. You know what number it is." She faced him squarely, on her flat foot, with her one arm at her side. "I've had it written in the route book since the shelf, and I've been counting toward it across a mountain. You'd have let it go by. You always let a thing go by when you think that you've not earned it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-40.md:65:Karis had opened her notebook against the purse.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-40.md:85:"There's a which to everything that grows," said the small man. "It's only stones that are all the one thing, and I'd not swear to stones."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-40.md:203:He took Hesk's book out of the satchel. He had to turn past a leaf to reach a clean one, and he knew that the square woman at the end of the bench had seen which leaf it was. He wetted the pencil, and wrote, with the firelight on the page and five people watching his hand.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-40.md:227:The carter stood inside the door with his hat in his hands. He was a short thick man with twenty-two years on one road, and he had driven a light cart up a frozen valley since first light; his eyebrows were white with it. He had held a scrap of bark out across the board, and Cael had had a book in one hand and a pencil in the other. So it was Brom who had taken it and turned it to the fire, and who was giving it to the table now in his slow voice, a stop at a time.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-40.md:235:The other line was a long way west of that hearth, on the far side of the Line, in a registrar's daybook, entered with a withdrawal. It had never been in the satchel. A magister had written it on the last day that Cael stood at that counter, and had asked that he should read it there; and he had carried it off by heart, since that was the only way in which he was let to carry it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-40.md:253:The healer finished with her bread before she looked up. Her eyes went to the bark in Seln's fingers, and then to the book that lay open by Cael's bowl with its pencil not yet dry, and they were as tired and as level as he had ever seen them.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-40.md:297:Karis sat with a wrinkled apple in her hand. Cael had seen that face on her over a column that would not come out. She was not hunting for a way round the man; she was going back along her own reasoning to find the place where it had first gone wrong. Then she drew her notebook toward her, and wrote, and turned it round for the board to see.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-40.md:309:The steading's woman cleared the board herself. She took six bowls on one arm, and left what was not hers to clear: a basket with three brown apples in it, and a book, and a scrap of bark. Cael drew Hesk's satchel up from under the bench to put the bark away with Pike's other lines.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-40.md:355:That was what brought the cold out on him. There was a leaf in the old volume of Hesk's book, the oldest unexplained thing that he owned, with a session's number at its head. Something had once come out of him in a practice yard that was none of his fragments, and it had come from under the breastbone, near the middle of him; a big hand had been laid on him afterward, and its owner had frowned and said that there was a quiet in there, under the noise. He had written the place down. He had turned that leaf over at every inventory since, and added nothing.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-40.md:365:He got up, and took Hesk's book to the loft door, and wrote there by the stars, with the frost coming in round his feet and the pencil cold between his fingers.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-40.md:373:Then he came to the foot of the list, where a line had stood by itself for as long as he had kept this book and the one before it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-40.md:379:He read that through. It was hers to hear before anybody, on a day that she would name, and until then it would lie in a book with its mouth shut.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-40.md:391:Below him the yard was white and empty. On the platform by the western gate a lean man stood where he always stood. Cael shut the book on nineteen, and put it under his hand, and lay down beside a wall of other people's barley.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-48.md:15:Lira had stopped chewing, and Karis, who had her notebook half out of her coat, pushed it back in. Cael had not stirred. There were two words in that answer which he could not make fit.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-48.md:217:Karis drew the mark in her notebook, at its own size, with the number over it, and wrote nothing beside it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-48.md:285:They ate cold. Brom put his boot into the stirrup of a healer's bow, and set the hook on the string, and straightened his back; the string came up the stock and dropped behind its catch, and he laid one of the five bolts in the groove. Karis had her lamp and her notebook and a flask, and Lira drew the knot of her sling tight with her teeth.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-58.md:61:He took out Hesk's book and wrote it standing, with the pencil cold in his fingers.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-58.md:105:He had Hesk's book out, to enter the hour, as Karis did. He wrote something else.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-58.md:113:He put the book away, with both of them in it, and went on.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-58.md:195:Her voice was quite steady. She opened her notebook against the lamp, and did the sum in it, with her lips moving. She had said two hundred of the Stair, by her eye, and had been twenty short and a stride; and he saw the figure that she wrote now, which was four hundred and forty yards, and two strides, from a line that she had not crossed to a middle that she had not seen.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-57.md:39:Seln sat down on his heels at her elbow. He had the limp little book out of his coat already, open at the first page, and he held it to her fire at the height of her eyes, with his thumb beside the line.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-57.md:43:She copied the figure. She copied it into the second leaf, and the third. He turned the book a little toward the light for each, and did not once offer to put it into her hand; it stayed in his, with two of his fingers closed on its spine.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-57.md:53:"A cord knotted at the fives. It is in Brom's case." Seln shut the book. "I can tell you no more of him. He writes a small upright hand, and presses. I have been through a page and a half of it every night since that room, and I could not tell you whether he was tall."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-57.md:55:He went back to the mules, with the book in his coat. She did not thank him. He would have disliked it, and she had the three figures.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-57.md:103:"The man with the book wrote *on bearing*," said Seln. He had not taken the ledger out. "He drew through two, as she said in that room, and he had a third confirmed along it. Hers lie along the same three. I think that we may call it one line, and his."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-57.md:159:He did it on purpose. He had counted that crew on a hundred roads without thinking; today he looked at each of them as he would have looked at a room that he was to leave in the morning. Seln went far out in front, small against the sky, with a case on his chest and a book in his coat. Karis walked with the hide rolled under her arm, and her lips moving. Lira had the healer's bow slung across her back on its strap, and she set the great boot down whole at every stride; she had been given no order to hand that bow back, and she had not offered. Brom led the brown mule on her low side, with the kit on his left shoulder and four yards of webbing holding the rest of him together.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-44.md:25:Karis had her notebook open.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-44.md:53:Her eyes came to Cael then, and rested on him for the length of a breath. He had watched her cross a grey stone eight times at dawn, alone, and had shut a book on it; and he saw that she knew he had kept it shut.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-44.md:61:"One: it is a thing that happens, and I have called it the Quieting. Two: five sigils of five Paths failed at one stride, in one way, and came back so. Three: the floor is older than the oldest stone that I can date. Four: the registry has put iron on the edge of it." She shut the notebook on her finger. "And there is a hypothesis, which is mine, and which is not entered anywhere."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-44.md:95:"That's how you find a handful. One at a time, for money." She nodded toward Hesk's satchel on its nail, where a broker's cream paper lay folded in a book. "Somebody paid a hundred marks to learn whether a crew would come out of the first, and I do not think that he has stopped wanting to know."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-44.md:147:He had not slept. He had come down the ladder with Hesk's book inside his shirt, and she was there already, a square shape against the white of the yard, filling a flask from the dipper. The mule stood at the trough with a blanket on her. There was a pannier on the coping with its strap buckled, and a coil of rope beside it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-44.md:149:"You've that book," said Oryn.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-44.md:155:There was light enough from the frost. He opened the book on the well's rim, at the leaf where an inventory ended, and wetted the pencil, and wrote it under her eyes without hurrying. He had written a leaf once in a loft so that he could never claim to have been surprised, and it had permitted nothing; he wrote this one so that there should be a line in his own hand that he could be held to.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-44.md:205:She looked at the book lying open on the rim.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-44.md:209:He had them. He wrote a note beneath the rule, small, and turned the book round so that she could see it the right way up.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-44.md:285:So she would be at the edge. There would be another of those places; the small one had as good as promised it. Somebody ought to stand on the right side of the stone with her sleeves pushed up.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-44.md:289:He had tried to pay her at the well. She had seen it coming across the yard, in the way that he carried the book; he was a boy who could not bear to owe, and he had wanted to be rid of the debt before she went, as a person wants a splinter out, without much caring how it is drawn.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-37.md:19:He had thought about what it would be. He had lain awake over it in a loft, with the book under his hand. He had been read by a great many things, and the only one of them that he could do himself went down: it opened under his boots like an eye and took the weight of whatever was beneath, a pace and a half, straight into the ground. He had supposed that a healer's reading would do the same, and that he would feel it come down into him from her palm as a plumb-line comes down a wall.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-59.md:55:He read it on his watch, as he had on every night since a room under a floor. He sat with his back to four sleepers and the limp little book open on his knee, tilted to what the embers gave. He did not need the light. He used it regardless, since there is a difference between remembering a hand and looking at it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-59.md:57:It was a small hand, and upright. It pressed. It ruled no margin of its own, having been given a ruled leaf, and it kept inside the blue lines as a careful man keeps to a path. He knew by the slope of the fours that their writer had stood to write them, with the book against his thigh. He knew that the man had been surprised at the eighth of eight figures, since he had gone back and underlined it; and that he had been more than surprised at the line below, which he had gone over twice until the pencil cut.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-59.md:59:Seln had no more of him than that. There was no name in the book, and he had not looked for one; a field ledger is signed for at a counter, and the name stays at the counter.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-59.md:61:He had wanted a face, and had gone up a ridge for one, and had been given six hoods. This was the nearest that he had come. It was a person's own book. In twelve years of that matter he had handled a great deal of paper. Every leaf of it had been written by a clerk, at dictation, for somebody up a stair. Nobody had dictated this. A man had gone alone into a hole in the ground with a cord and a mallet, and had put down what he found there, for himself and for no reader, in the order in which he found it, and had been astonished.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-59.md:73:He shut the book, and put it back inside his coat, against the strap.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-59.md:79:He was walking a furlong ahead of the others, and he put his hand up to his coat as he did a dozen times in a day; and for once he noticed that the hand had two things to find. There was a case, locked, which he had carried shut through twelve years and three postings and across a Line. And there was a book, which he had carried shut for twelve days.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-59.md:87:The book was another matter. It was a page and a half of distances, set down honestly by a man with a knotted cord, and a line that stopped. A person at the back of this file needed it every evening of her life; and she kept a box in which, by her own rule, nothing was written of anybody that he had not heard to his face.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-59.md:93:The big one went by with the mule, and the girl, on her whole foot, and the boy behind her with his arms wrapped about his ribs. The small one came last. She had the hide under one arm and her notebook in the other hand, and she was doing a sum as she walked, with her lips.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-59.md:95:He took the book out of his coat, and held it toward her, shut.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-59.md:101:"All of them. It is a field book. It ought to lie with the papers of the field."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-59.md:113:He could not dispute that, and he did not try. She had the book open before he had turned to go on; she walked the next mile with her nose a hand from a dead man's pencil, and went into a rut to the knee, and did not appear to notice.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-59.md:135:Above the landing there was a long low building with a deep eave. The plank under the eave was bare of its book. There was the smith's chimney, and the taller house on the rise, with two. Along the water lay the meadow, where forty fires had burned in the dry months and twelve when he went south. He counted three.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-59.md:181:He did not shut the book.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-59.md:197:He took it by its corner and swung it on the bench, so that the writing faced the other way. Cael had read every line of that book upside down for a hundred and twenty days, across an oak plank. It lay the right way up before him now. He read the season in a square black hand, and at the end of it a ruled space with nothing in it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-59.md:207:Then he shut the book on the empty place, and put his stone on it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-59.md:209:Cael stood before a hearth with snow melting on his shoulders. A magister had written of him once that no instrument would see his like. He had meant it kindly, and had entered it in a daybook that Cael was not let to carry away. This man had turned his own book about on a bench, in front of fifty people, to show a boy the hole in it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-38.md:71:"There's a book in that loft with a leaf in it that I'm to read."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-38.md:75:He was back before she had moved from the sill, with Hesk's book held in both hands and a straw shut into it at the place. She did not take it at once. She looked at the hand that held it, as she looked at everything of hers first.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-38.md:93:She took the book from him.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-38.md:97:She shut the door and let the peg fall. She did not take the stool. She carried the book to the sill, into the square of light, and laid it open on the strip of canvas among her own things, between the shears and the horn cup; and Cael thought that he had never seen an odder row.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-38.md:125:"I can read. It's on your leaf." She shut Hesk's book on the straw. "Say it to me again when it's cost you something."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-38.md:131:"Now what it did. You'll want that, for your book, and I want it for my hands. I'll tell you what I'd tell a second healer, and no more." She spoke slowly, sorting it. "I felt nothing go. I'd swear to that. Near the end there was something going along beside me, at my own pace. I took it for myself. You hear your own feet, in a long one, when you're tired. Then it wasn't beside me any longer. It was in you, going by itself, and you were different."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-38.md:141:She had brought Hesk's book with her from the sill, and she tapped its cover with one thick finger.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-38.md:177:"I'm not asking you for a promise. I've seen what you do with what you want. I'm telling you what's owed, and I'll say when."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-38.md:181:"Good." Oryn gave him the book. "Now lie down again. Not for me. You're the colour of the wall and you've been standing on that calf for a quarter of an hour, and I've just been told I'm a patient's source, and I'd like a minute to sit with the second thing before I go and eat."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-38.md:183:He lay down. She put the horn cup into his hand, with water in it, and waited until he had drunk. Then she took Hesk's book back and sat down on the three-legged stool herself, for the first time since he had come into that room, with the book on her knee.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-38.md:191:It was still the hundredth morning, and the sun had not yet reached the well, when Oryn looked down and saw that she was holding a book.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-38.md:203:She shut the book and laid her hands on its cover, palms down, and went over what they had found. She did it as her teacher had made her do it, for a second healer who was not in the room, with her knuckles ready to be struck for a guess.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-38.md:235:She had eaten, and had her two hours on her back, with the door shut and the book on the sill. She came out and washed to the elbow in the bucket, and she washed more slowly than she ever did, as a carter walks who thinks that his axle may be cracked and would sooner not hear it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-38.md:255:Oryn fetched the book from the sill and carried it across the yard.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-39.md:33:Karis made one short mark in her notebook. Brom had been waiting his turn, with his eyes on his boots, and he raised them now.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-39.md:45:She had her notebook open on her knee, and she read from it, with a stop for breath at every period.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-39.md:53:"Good." She shut the book. "It hurts. To talk. I shall do it anyway."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-39.md:67:He lay between two sacks with Hesk's book under his hand. Lira had brought it up the ladder at dusk inside her coat, and with it one sentence, said exactly as a healer had said it: that it was a true page, and that true was not the same as paid. He had written nothing in it since.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-39.md:143:He knew that lag, for he had run up a ditch in it with eight hounds behind him. Whenever the heaviest thing in him had been used, everything else answered him a beat late for ten counts or twelve, and he had entered that in Hesk's book as a bill and learned to wait it out.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-39.md:245:Cael wrote one thing in Hesk's book before he went back to the sacks, since he wished it to be in his hand and dated.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-41.md:67:She had sent them away on purpose. She was fond of all four, and she had needed their hands; but there is a part of any counting that must be done by the one person who will have to swear to it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-41.md:89:So she had a count, and no number that she would say. The grains in the cup were the kinds that she would swear to. The grains in the bowl were the kinds that only a second floor could give her or take away. If there was a second floor, and the hook stood wide on it in the same place, then it was a kind, and it came out of the bowl. If it stood narrow, the width on the first floor was not a kind.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-41.md:165:The barn was cold, and smelled of barley and of hens. She sat at the trestle with Seln's coat round her and her notebook open at the five rows, and for once she did not begin by writing.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-55.md:115:She was as wet down the front as a person can be who has lain for an hour on her stomach on a stone with a spring slopping over it. Cael saw her shiver, from the near fire, and saw that she did not know she was doing it; she had her notebook open and three clean leaves before her, and her pencil had already begun.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-55.md:197:There was a leaf in the old volume of Hesk's book with a session's number at its head. He had written a label on that leaf, a long time ago, on a bluff, for a thing that he could not see; it had been a frown on a big man's face then, and a line in an inventory, and he had called it by those few words in pencil ever since, for want of anything to point at.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-53.md:79:They slept that night in a halting-ring, with a fire of Seln's flint, and Cael sat his watch with Hesk's book shut on his knee. He owed a healer two readings of himself yet, on two different days, and he had not laid a finger on his own wrist since she told him *not tonight* between two bars of iron. He left it so. She had asked for a night that was only a night; every one since had been the eve of a floor or the morrow of one, and he thought it likely that this was the eve of another. The debt weighed on him a good deal less than it had on a halting-ground above the wash, and he took that for a sign that he had begun to believe her.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-53.md:109:Seln had his hand flat on the breast of his coat, where a surveyor's book lay.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-53.md:155:"The man with the book wrote that it was confirmed," said Karis. "He did not write that he had stood in it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-53.md:275:She gave it to him before she had thought, and then she stared, and so did the rest of them. He had read out a great many things to that notebook in a hundred days: the width of a road, and the age of a bar, and what a paper meant. He had asked each time that it should be entered as said. He had never once put a mark in it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-53.md:281:"You have written in my book," said Karis.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-53.md:385:*Seln wrote its rule in Karis's book with his own hand. He has never done that.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-53.md:393:He shut the book. Out in the reeds, a long way off, something knocked twice against stone.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-60.md:9:She took the purse out of her coat. Cael had seen that woman pay for a cart, and a boot, and a kid; each time she had opened her notebook first, and done the sum, and done it again beneath. She did not open it now. She put her fingers into the neck of the bag and laid six marks on the board, one upon another, with her eyes on the door behind the chimney.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-60.md:29:Cael did not go to the hearth with the others. He sat down at the meal-sack end of that table, with Hesk's book, while she worked.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-60.md:31:*Hundred and twenty-ninth day, night. A back room with a door on it, in the lower inn at Lowmarch. First snow. Ninety-six in hand. Nine lines in Pike's book, and no number against the season. Eleventh on the cloth. Deployment since the Line: twenty-seven.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-60.md:43:He shut the book on it. On the far side of the lamp a strip of brass was going down a calfskin, a hand's length at a time.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-60.md:51:It was a quarter of an hour before the boat came home again. It brought a wet canvas bag, and the bag went up the bank to the plank under the eave, where Pike had set out his book for the last time before the thaw.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-60.md:61:"For the boy with the book," said Pike. "From a town I never heard of. For the boy with the book. That's nine."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-60.md:77:He wrote them at the end of Pike's plank, on two leaves torn out of Hesk's own book, with the horn ink-pot and a borrowed pen. He had owed both for a long while, and he had been waiting for a thing to say; and he had not expected, when it came, that it would go into so few words.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-60.md:83:*Vell. There's a second ledger on the continent with my name in it, kept by a one-armed man in a town on no map, and this week he stopped numbering me. He said his book was wrong about us by being too small. You said once that somebody should have a record that doesn't answer to the Compact. There are two now. The session still stands. — C.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-60.md:85:There was a third that he owed, to a friend on a bluff who lost to him every time he ran it. He had no line for that one yet. It would go by the first boat of the thaw, and he wrote in his own book that it was to.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-60.md:161:Oryn got down on her heels in the frost, and put both hands round the joint. Her lips went to three. It was all there: the old banks, worn as smooth as a doorstep, and the current going through the place where a ladder had been as though no ladder had ever stood in it. She had promised that knee a look after the thaw, and it had walked over a hill to be looked at before the snow.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-60.md:191:She did not keep a book. She had said so to the pale one across a fire: a healer who needs a page to know who is owed has stopped looking at them. She had never in thirty rounds set down a word about any person whom she had had her hands in. But those five were two days south, with a saddle between that would be shut by morning, and she could not look at them. There was a girl down there with an arm in a knot of hers, who had been told *first snow*; and by the feel of that sky she would be looking at it out of a window tomorrow.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-50.md:101:He sat down beside her, since there was nothing that he could say to it. Her hand found his sleeve in the dark, and patted it twice, clumsily, as she might have patted a book that she meant to come back to.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-50.md:185:He unbuckled Hesk's satchel, with the book in it, and carried it up the stair and laid it on the stone beside Lira's boot.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-56.md:75:He had not set it. The woman with the net had not put her mark on the shingle, and the four marks were lost, as he had said they would be. He had sent his word north by the mill's carter, in so many words, for the man with the book: five strokes to look at, and all of them walking; and the three hundred was to stay where it lay.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-56.md:141:A book had columns. It had one for what was sent for, and one for what came back, and one for what was paid. He had been at the head of Pike's cloth for a good many cold terms by filling those three better than other men. He could think of nothing that this crew had done since the Fallow that would go into any of them.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-56.md:147:It was dusk before he went himself. The boy was sitting alone at the near fire, against a scaled stake, with a book shut on his knee. Teague had stood over a great many men in his time, and it had got him the answers that men give to somebody who is standing over them.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-56.md:169:Nor did he ask whether he might, and he saw why as he sat there. There was only one person whose leave could be asked, and that was the man himself; and to ask him was to ask that he hand over, to a boy at a fire, what he had kept out of every book that was ever opened on him. A healer had told Cael at a well that asking was the least of it. A hand went onto a person when there was something to read *for*. He had nothing to read this man for but the wish to know what he was.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-56.md:201:Cael opened Hesk's book against his knee, and wrote while it was still in him.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-52.md:37:It took Lira the first three cords to learn the trick of it, and after that she did not put a foot wrong. Cael walked behind with the notebook, and wrote each station in a bad hand. She went with the cord's end shut in her right fist and the sling tied high against her collar-bone, a pace at a time, as a child walks along a kerb; and every few strides she tipped her head, as if she were listening, and moved her left boot a hand's breadth out or in.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-52.md:51:Karis went down the page with her pencil, twice. Then she turned back in the notebook to a leaf that was nineteen days old, and laid her finger on the foot of it, and looked from the one to the other.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-52.md:57:"To a stride. I should like somebody to tell me how that comes about by accident, five days' walk apart." She shut the book on her finger. "Nobody will. I am going down to look at a wall."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-52.md:113:"I do not know that they are letters. I said so on a floor nineteen days ago, and I do not know it yet. They are lines, cut in groups, by somebody who meant something, and that is as far as I can swear." Her lamp moved along the course. "It is a great deal farther than I could swear a month ago."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-52.md:131:"A mallet," said Brom. He was naming them as he came to them, like a man going along a bench of tools. "Iron on the head of it. A bar. And that's a book, on its face." He sounded almost injured. "Is that all? I said that a kit was a cart's worth. I said brass."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-52.md:133:"It is a kit for one thing," said Karis. She had come away from her wall at last. "A cord to find how far, and a bar to mark where; a mallet for the bar, and a book to say that it was done. He did not come to map this country. He came to put iron on an edge."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-52.md:137:"They lie in the order that they came out," said Seln quietly. "The cord, and the mallet, and the bar; and the book last and nearest, since a man writes last. Nobody packs so. He meant to take them up again."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-52.md:177:It was Seln who turned the book over, and he did it with two fingers, as he would have handled another man's letter.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-52.md:181:"This is not the clerk," said Seln. "This is somebody who wrote standing up, on his knee. It is his own book."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-52.md:199:"Then this is the book of whoever drove them." Seln kept his eyes on the leaf. "I put five cold terms on the old iron at the stone, and I see no cause to alter that for his book."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-52.md:215:It was so unlike him that all five of them looked up. He was holding the book quite still, a hand's breadth from the horn of the lantern, and his eyes were going along one line of pencil from its beginning to its end and back again.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-52.md:217:"It is not finished," he said. "There is a line, and after it a stroke of the pencil that goes off toward the edge of the leaf, and then nothing. The rest of the book is clean."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-52.md:295:"This one. It's not on the Ring. I looked for it for four days and it isn't on any upright. It's here, on every wall, and it's not in the courses with the rest — it's *between* them. On its own. Here." The pencil moved along the sheet to the next one, and the next. "And here. And here. I've paced the spacing because I pace things. It comes every — " she looked at the notebook, where she had written it, and read it out as a number she had checked twice — "every ten cords and a piece. Two hundred and twenty yards and a stride, to the stride. It recurs, on the wall, at the perimeter's radius."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-52.md:305:"You are setting your cord beside his, and his figure is in a registry book. I have spent fifteen years learning what that office's paper is worth. I should not care to see you rest a finding on a number of theirs."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-52.md:317:He had made up his mind to it while the others were rolling sheets. Seln had laid the mallet in the case, and the bar beside it, and the cord in its coil, and let down the lid; the book he had put inside his own coat, against the strap of the other thing that he carried, and nobody had questioned it. The case lay on the floor with its strap trailing.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-52.md:355:Cael sat down against the grey stone, in the full sun, and opened Hesk's book.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-52.md:361:He shut the book.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-51.md:191:Cael opened Hesk's book on his knee. Lira had put it into his lap when she came over the stone, with the strap done up, and had said nothing; and he held the pencil in his bandaged left hand, since the right would not close.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-51.md:251:It was the second time in three days that Cael had been told so. A one-armed man had said it of a line in a book. This man said it of him, to his face, across a lantern, with no heat in it and no fear; he might have been reading out the weight of a load.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-51.md:261:He had been at the plank before the mist was off the river. The five had gone by it in the dark, two hours earlier, and he had heard the mule. Pike was setting out his book; and there was a shingle on the oak, six along from the left-hand post, which had hung there for nine days because no crew of any standing would trouble with it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-51.md:367:Teague did not answer him. He was looking down the old road, to where a lamp burned beside a grey stone, and a boy sat against a bar of iron with a book on his knee.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-51.md:371:So he knew a little about being something that a book had no line for. It was a very little, for he had kept back one tier, and that boy had no tier to keep back. Whatever he was, it was not written anywhere at all.

