d man's first word to them was "Aye" on the 305th. **The broad man:** right shin (his good leg) broken at the Span, knit by Oryn, boarded, walking on a stick cut from the watch's trestle, at the tail; his crossbow's stock is split by a bolt. **The young one** went over the iron when Teague looked at him ("Four."), held his feet inside the line by watching Lira's boots, "goes wherever he is looked at, a half-beat before he has seen what is there", and nods with his whole head; he is still unnamed (OWNER-pending #54 governs the one saying of his name). (4) **What Teague's four know:** five people stood whole inside the line; a boy kept a captain's left with a stick; nothing of the mechanism, any fragment, the leaf, the lamps page, the slips, the date, or who Vastin was. **The doctrine "behind it" has NOT been told to them; no wall has been drilled or stood.** Vastin entered on the 303rd that he will not stand a wall before his month is out (he stood inside the line at the Span instead, and what he carries went and came back "to the grain"); the 306th is **day twenty-seven of thirty**. (5) **The watch** (seven, hooded, faceless: two crossbows, four staves, one low practitioner) went east through the gap a day ahead, carrying one broken bow; they saw silent Iron take bolts, silent Force shift timber, and five people stand inside the line; they saw nothing work inside. Nobody died at the Span. **Seln** looked at the square notch from a mile on the evening of the 305th: a dry bed going in, the only way through for five miles either hand, no fire, nobody on either wall, not a heel-mark — "I looked at it." — and is to look again from nearer at first light before Teague moves. (6) **Pages, and who holds each:** Karis has the torn ruled leaf with the lamps finding ("They fail the way a hundred lamps fail when one wire's cut. Not the way a hundred souls fall silent.") in the back of her notebook, and under her initial "*Second page: nothing entered. Not for want of a pencil.*"