    13	He logged the half-day as a half-day, one entry at noon, because the plain was the plain and the thing was not and he did not want them on the same line.
    14	
    15	Flat. Grass to the knee, going pale as it went east, and then not grass; shale; and then, in the last mile, a thing under the shale that was not shale, that the boots found before the eyes did — a hardness, level, that the shale lay on in a skin the wind moved. Rock. Not stone laid; rock, the plain's own, ground down. Karis went to one knee at the first of it and put her bound hand flat on it and her good hand beside it and looked along the surface to the rim a mile off and said, "It's been worn to the floor. The whole plain. From here in." She stood up. "Water doesn't do that. Wind doesn't do that in the time there's been wind."
    16	
    17	"What does," Brom said.
    18	
