editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-60.md:5:It was not a log. He kept a log for the day, and it took what the day had brought. This was the other form, which he had learned from an old man at Denvash: the whole of what a person holds, set down in order, with what is owed beneath it. He had written one at Lowmarch, when a river shut, and one in a loft under a town. He did not write one often; he wrote it when a thing was finished, and he wished to know what he had come out of it with.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-60.md:9:*Three hundred and seventh day, night. The rim of the fifth place. A hundred and twenty-three. Vastin: twenty-eight. Oryn is seven days past her cairn. Eleven of us tonight. At first light four go west, and one north.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-60.md:29:*Owed, in money: nothing. Owed otherwise, and entered side by side: to Vastin, the eleventh, which came while he held a wall for us in a stair. To Teague, the thirteenth, which came at a stride while I kept his left. Neither has been told. Neither can be until the whole of it can be, and the whole of it is six people's. I used the second of them yesterday over that captain's head, on a thing that would have killed him. It does not pay the debt. A debt is not paid by something that the creditor is not let to know.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-60.md:33:He had thought, on a shelf, with his forehead on the rock, that he had perhaps paid a little of it, and he had gone over that since. He would not have taken such a payment from another person; and Brom, who priced things, had told him behind a step of shale to pay when it could be paid, and not a day sooner, and not to ease his own back.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-60.md:71:*Nineteen. Your grandmother's line was already drawn. I followed it to the end; both edges agreed. Tell Lira's family I said so. Mind the shape, you said. I'm minding it. — C.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-60.md:97:Across the dead side of the fire somebody had got up. It was the tall man. He had a cup of his own on the embers, and a strip of linen, and he wrung the one out of the other with his big chapped hands, a little less hard than he had the night before; and he carried it on both palms to where a captain lay with his arm along a slat. Cael did not watch the rest. But he marked that there had been a second night of it, and he thought that there would be a third.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-60.md:143:"That it was read right, and that it's a floor. That my crew stood at the edge of it, and that I'm one short." He said that last without any change of voice. "He'll want a price for the line, and I've none to give him. He's never yet written a thing down without one."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-60.md:153:No hand was offered. None had been offered at a bench, either, and Cael had learned since what that man meant by it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-60.md:157:He had done that in a yard for every touch that was fairly taken, and Cael had earned exactly one of those from him in a whole cold term.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-59.md:39:He had stood on that when he was fourteen, with a great many people looking on. Every one of them had seen a sigil light for some child before him, and they were waiting to see his. The plate had lit late. He had stood there for the count of eleven with nothing happening, and had learned in those eleven what it is to be on a floor that was made for everybody else.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-59.md:73:There was something that bore. It lay level under everything, whole, from one side of that place to the other, and nothing whatever was carried upon it. Here he was past the last of the rows, and there was not a mark. And it leaned. The whole bare floor leaned, as a line of cut signs leans, steadily, at he could not have said what.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-59.md:81:It came faster than it ever had. He had looked for the length of five breaths, and he was being charged as though he had read a wall; and he thought, kneeling there with the heels of his hands in his eye-sockets, that it was a fair price. He had asked that tool for more in two nights than its owner would have asked of it in a year.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-59.md:107:His breath was going in and out of him in halves. The water had slowed. He could tell the floor from the sky again, though not much else.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-59.md:251:He had been set there as a mark. It was a kind of posting, and it was the first that he had held since he left a seal in a tray; and he found that he had no wish to end it merely because the thing that it served was done. A round had been shut upon the foot of his stick, six hours since, by a girl walking heel to toe. He had felt the slip of ash touch the wood. Not many acts of his own life had been of such plain use to anybody.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-59.md:275:It had come back, at the stride, to the grain, and he had been glad of it. He marked that he had not been so glad as the first time, and he did not yet know what to make of that.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-59.md:279:He had been at more inquiries than he could number, and he held this one as he had held those, since he knew no other way. A wall had been asked for, by nobody; and he had offered it, by leave of the person entitled, and had raised it in the form that she allowed. It had taken three flights and the side of a hill, and had not let one pebble through. The rule for such a thing had been told, once, in two words, to four people, and had been answered. A boy had gone a stride beyond its edge, for a man who was down.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-58.md:27:"Wind, if you're asking me. And I'd not care to say for how long. I've nothing to price it against."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-58.md:61:"I want a mark that will not move," said Karis.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-58.md:69:"Sit," said Oryn. "On a box. A mark doesn't want legs."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-58.md:93:She did not stop for it, and she did not stop for the rows either, which went by a stride from her left boot, hour upon hour, cut as deep as a finger. He saw her eyes go to them, and come away. She had waited the best part of her life for a wall of that hand, and had learned to read it under a town; and she walked past half a mile of it with her pencil on a column of figures.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-58.md:107:Cael knew it by the tally and by nothing else. He looked across, and there was the whole of that floor between, holding the afternoon; and on the far side of it, on the rim, there was a mark no bigger than a seed. He supposed that it was an old man on a box, though he could not have told a mule from a healer.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-58.md:119:The sun went over, and the water in Brom's skin went down. The fourth station was behind them, and the fifth, and at each of the holes she had done a thing that he did not at first understand. She had made Lira and Seln draw one length of the cord dead straight between two points of the cut. Then she had laid the slip of ash across, at the second knot, from the taut hemp to the lip, and had marked the slip with her thumbnail.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-58.md:123:"It is how I shall know whether it is round. A straight cord laid across the edge of a round thing stands off from it in the middle. If the thing is truly round, it stands off by the same amount wherever I lay it." She held the slip up. There were six nail-marks on it, and he could not have told them apart. "A span and two fingers. I have never had six that agreed so."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-58.md:127:The mark on the rim grew. It became a box, with a brown coat upon it, and an upright stick, and there were people about it now, and a mule. The old man had not shifted. Cael could see, at two hundred yards, that he was sitting exactly as he had been left, with both his hands on the ash and his face to the north, like a post that somebody has driven and gone away from.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-58.md:149:"About fifty. Something over eleven hundred yards; I make it eleven hundred and seven, as near as hemp will say." She looked at the slip of ash, with its eight nail-marks all in the one place. "And it is round. I have measured a millstone that was not so round. I do not know what was used to cut it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-58.md:183:"A person who makes a mark for himself does not make the slip a second time," said Karis. "A person who is copying, and has been told to leave nothing out, copies the slip. I have seen it in a clerk's hand at the foot of six sheets." She laid her palm flat on the notebook. "I have read strata since I was a girl. I have laid these floors under everything that I had. And there has been one more under them, all the time."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-58.md:221:*Three hundred and seventh day. The fifth place, at the end of the line. A hundred and twenty-three. Vastin: twenty-eight; he was the mark that the round closed on. Oryn is seven days past her cairn. Eleven of us.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-58.md:235:"Nine people's marks leave the foot of the ridge where their path comes down, and go south," said Seln. "I followed them a mile this morning before you were awake. They did not come onto this rock. I have seen no smoke in any quarter since."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-58.md:243:He gave the captain the same three sentences that he had given him four nights before, in a fold of the ridge, in the same order; and he did not count the hoods afresh. Then he added one, since it was owed.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-58.md:287:"The same as did the plain. Weather." He took his hand away. "I've priced a good many things. I can't price that. I've not got a figure with enough on the end of it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-58.md:337:Cael saw it take him. The heavy shoulders went back, as though somebody had drawn a pack off them from behind; and the left hand came up, slowly, and closed upon the right wrist, where there was linen, and a slat, and under those a joint that had paid for everything that he had ever done. He stood so for the length of three breaths.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-56.md:33:Each stone was a line drawn low across his left side, from front to back, and he found that he could not draw a whole breath under it. He had not told a healer. She sat against the southern wall with her head on her knees, the colour of old ash, as she had sat in a ravine; and he thought that he had earned the walk up that bed a good many times over, and went down it again.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-56.md:47:She did not tell him that he was not to blame. Cael marked that she did not, and thought the better of her for it. She stood at his elbow with a stone held against her coat, and waited until the tall man came and took it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-56.md:75:It had told him what such places generally told him. There were nine heaps of bedding under the spilled heap, and the place for a tenth, swept and never lain on. There was a kettle. There were two bundles of bolts, with never a thread of colour upon a feather, and a second maul. He had turned over every blanket that he could reach, and there was not a scrap of paper in the whole of it, or a mark burned into a stave, or a name scratched on a spoon.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-56.md:95:At its narrow end there was a mark where a man had been sitting.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-56.md:103:Seln said nothing. He had learned in a long service that there is a kind of return which must be let run to its foot.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-56.md:129:"There was not. I marked that. I marked also that I have done the sum three times, which I do not ordinarily do with one that has come out."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-56.md:149:The case lay against his breastbone, under his coat, shut. He had gone up a slab with it there, and over a lip, and had not once thought of it. He marked that as well. It altered nothing.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-56.md:195:Brom got up. He went round the fire, heavily, to the place where a sack of turned bowls lay by a stranger's kettle; and he took out four, and set them along the far side, a yard apart, as the boy had set them. Nobody stopped him. The tall man's eyes followed his hand from one to the next.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-56.md:299:He asked it since she always set one, and since he owed her the asking.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-56.md:303:"I'd set you ten days flat," she said. "And tomorrow there's a thing out on that plain, a morning's walk off, that you've come the length of a country to stand in; and you'd get up off my blanket and walk to it. Then I'd have set a term to be broken. I did that once, with a knee, and I learned what it teaches a person." She shook her head. "No term tonight. Ask me again when you've stood where you're going."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-57.md:49:They had walked over one at a fence, and she had counted a big man across it. There was one more, which lay out there in the dark, and tomorrow they would come to it. She would stand at that; and then she owed three gates a healer, and she would pay them.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-57.md:53:She had found a road in him tonight that was not his own. It went from the heel to the palm, as straight as a rule, through the middle of the cage, and it had the mark of a captain's all over it; she had read that captain's arm from a baulk of timber, two days since, and she would have known the make of the thing in the dark.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-57.md:87:And she had learned what to do with a pair of hands afterward. It was a small thing, and it was hot.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-57.md:127:It was tarred hemp, no thicker than a pencil, and it lay in his fingers like anything else; he had not known that he remembered the feel of it. He set the eye under Brom's boot, and ran it out to the fourth knot, and marked; and to the fourth again. Then he laid the slip of ash from his mark.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-57.md:177:It was dark. It was as dark as it had been in a meadow and under a town. He had wondered once why a quiet floor took nothing from him, and the answer had turned out to be dull enough: there was nothing lit in him for it to put out. That was the same tonight. He marked that it was, and left it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-57.md:225:This leaned so, steadily, and there was not a mark upon it. There was no sign. There was no letter that a woman with twenty-three of them could ever have turned. It was an instruction with no language.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-57.md:247:*Three hundred and sixth day. The open rock below the eastern mouth of the gap. A hundred and twenty-three. Vastin: twenty-seven; he held a roof today, sitting down, by Oryn's leave, and the knee never took a pound of it. Oryn is six days past her cairn.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-57.md:257:He set the count beneath it, since a count was owed.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-54.md:55:Cael marked that sentence. A captain had taught him on a ridge to mark three kinds of thing in any piece of ground, and this was none of the three; it was only a thing that might be different on another day, and he put it by without knowing what it was worth.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-54.md:93:[[TIER-B: "It's through." — the packet gives the two words after "Seln's reconnaissance" and pins no speaker. Staged here: said over the drawing on the shale when the three ways have been priced (back; the team's own path; round) and only the bed is left. Teague could own it (the ruling; his word at the Span's timbers was "Through."), Seln (the close of his report) or Karis (of the line). The paragraphs on either side stand for any of the three — coordinator to supply protected text]]
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-54.md:117:"I have followed the argument," said Vastin.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-54.md:181:Nobody put it to hands, since it had not been that kind of question. A man had asked leave of the one person who could give it, and had been given it at a price, in front of everybody; and a captain who had built a count upon that wall, and had kept his mouth shut about it for nine days, bent down stiffly and picked up his own pack with his left hand.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-54.md:219:"I'll say when I've seen it," said Teague. "That's the trade. Nobody gets the second half of a field until he's standing in the first." He looked round the ring of them. "A wall buys time. It never bought a man anything else, and I'll not pretend that this one will."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-54.md:361:"I am informed that it can be done sitting," he said. "I had rather have learned it on some other day."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-55.md:9:"They have learned what it is," said Seln.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-55.md:37:It was a single dull knock, like a post being driven; and then the three bows went, together, and he never learned where those bolts fell, since the hill had begun to move.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-55.md:157:Cael marked it. He had seen that man hear one of his own go off a ledge, two days before, and keep his eyes where they were. He looked now, for the length of one breath, at what lay under the high edge of the roof. Something went out of the heavy face then which Cael did not see come back that day. Then the eyes went up to the shelf, and along it, to the stair.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-55.md:227:He could not hold a cartload of stone, and nobody could. But a thing had been said across a boy's head in a meadow, to a carter who wanted a second cart shifted; and he had lined himself against a slab on the shale, the evening before, and had learned where his own hip was.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-55.md:281:They went. The cleft was at the shelf's eastern end, a few strides from the stair's head, and to reach it they had to pass the two men who stood there. Nobody barred them. The first hood came on with a stave half raised, and saw which end of an ash pole was toward him, and went by it at the length of an arm and up into the rock. The others followed, one behind another, quickly, and not one turned to look. The three from the west had to pass a boy who knelt on their shelf with his arms round his own ribs. They went by him at the length of a stave, and nobody lifted a hand.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-55.md:287:Cael had seen him do that in a yard, a whole cold term ago. It was how that man marked a touch that had been taken.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-55.md:303:Seln had taken the high ground from that team by going up the one slope which its own rig had swept bare for him; and an old man sitting in a stream-bed had bought him the time to climb it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-55.md:309:He had not seen it go. He had been upon a stair, with his face to the rock. He saw a heap where there had been a rig, and he saw that somebody had paid for it; and a man who has settled every account of his life out of the one wrist knows the look of a bill, whatever he does not know of the goods.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-55.md:359:There was no blame in it. He looked for the blame, because he could have carried that. It was a captain setting down a sum, as he set down a piece of ground: what a thing did, and what it did not do, and where his own had been standing. It was the second lesson about a wall which Cael had been given by a man who had paid for it. It was a good deal worse than the first.

