editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/state/movement-009/AUTHOR-REPORT.md:103:- **The instruments on the night of the 306th (57:103–165).** He breaks a hold of a hundred and thirty-eight days, saying why. By Karis's lamp, with Karis and Brom laying the Lowmarch measure first (one length, four knots and two strides: forty-two): **in his hand forty-one, three times — a stride short, where it was "a piece"**. The edges against fresh needle-pricks in a box lid: **the Ternhall a hair and a half short, Hesk's two hairs long**, by her kept hair and a second; true in her hands. Her entry (57:159): "Cause: not found. No inference entered." "Two readings do not make a line." He draws none.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/state/movement-009/AUTHOR-REPORT.md:117:- **Chapter 60.** Seln at the ledger: three reaches (a stair, a fence, a hill), each time knowing a little more; no face seen; "I do not think that the third was the last." Karis: sixty-one sheets and a calfskin with five rings; "I have been believed for twenty-three days." Lira's hand on his sleeve in the night. The tall man's second night with the cloth. At dawn: Brom to Teague on the contract's second head ("A count can't do that part"); the broad man stops before the old man, who inclines his head: "Aye."; Teague will put the letter into Pike's hand and tell him "that it was read right, and that it's a floor … and that I'm one short"; "I've kept it twice now … I'd keep it again"; the tall man grounds his butt once to Cael; Teague's lips begin a number and he lifts two fingers instead; they go west with the tall man in front and the captain last, where the bow always walked. Oryn unloads the mule herself, looks at Brom's arm and Karis's hand, each by leave, reads the knee ("It's sound, and it's a day from done"), rides north sideways on the mule toward a salt road "two days up" that Teague had from three carters, and does not look back. Brom will lay her bowl. Boxes: Brom the heaviest, Lira the second, Cael the third (the reserve's, the lightest). Vastin takes up the tarred case unasked and stands a stride behind Brom.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/state/movement-009/AUTHOR-REPORT.md:128:- **Teague, the tall man, the broad man and the lean old woman** left the rim at first light, going west toward the gap and the cairn at its eastern mouth, the tall man in front and Teague last; they are bound for Lowmarch, and Teague carries Cael's letter for the bag.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/state/movement-009/AUTHOR-REPORT.md:132:- Cleon is under Treswick; Pike at Lowmarch; Hesk at Denvash (not on the page).
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/state/movement-009/AUTHOR-REPORT.md:149:**Teague and his three.** Teague: right arm on its slat, the hand not shutting, the splint warning standing; he went up a stair left-handed and drew no weapon; he let nine people go past him to a ridge; he knelt by his own and said his sentence; he went one stride over the fifth line and back and put none of his three across (marker 7); he has said that he would keep Cael's three sentences again. **He does not know that anything came to Cael at the timbers, or what moved the second heap; he did not see it and did not ask.** He knows from Seln that an order had changed between the fence and the gap. He carries the letter and will tell Pike that the bearing was read right, that it ends in a floor, and that he is one short. His term for after is marker 10. The broad man: right shin knit and boarded; **right shoulder bolted on the 306th**, shut by Oryn and bound across his chest; a pine stick; he has said "Aye" to the six and nothing else. The tall man: unhurt; has spoken to the six once ("And then?"). The lean old woman: has never spoken to them; she keeps the boy's bowl inside her coat. None of the four knows the mechanism, any fragment, the leaf, the lamps page, the slips, the date or who Vastin was.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/state/movement-009/AUTHOR-REPORT.md:164:**Who knows what.** The mechanism: six, as before (Oryn among them); Cleon how one fragment came; not Vastin, Teague, Pike or any of Teague's. The thirteenth: Cael, Lira, Brom, Karis and Seln, and now Oryn; not Teague, not Vastin. *Still* / *set* / *bones* and the shape, and that the court's floor reads the same: **Cael alone.** That he turned the twelfth inward on purpose, and that it is written: Cael and Lira. That there was no edge at the centre: Cael alone. Oryn's finding at the cut: the six at the rim. The round's figures, the copied slip, both edges and *no sixth found*: the six, Vastin among them; Teague in outline. Seln's finding about the changed order: all eleven. The date: Cael and Seln, each alone, unchanged. The two seals: Vastin alone.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/state/movement-009/AUTHOR-REPORT.md:180:11. **57:103–165 — the instruments.** He deliberately breaks a hold of a hundred and thirty-eight days to try the cord and both edges by lamp (forty-one in forty-two; a hair and a half; two). The plan fixes the figures and not the occasion. Keep?
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-59.md:17:There was more light under him than over. That was the thing that he could not get used to. The sky had gone the colour of slate, with the first stars in it, and the plain behind him was black; but the floor kept what the day had given it, and gave it up slowly. He could see his own boots, dark, going one before the other upon a pale ground. He threw no shadow. There was nothing above him bright enough to throw one.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-59.md:73:There was something that bore. It lay level under everything, whole, from one side of that place to the other, and nothing whatever was carried upon it. Here he was past the last of the rows, and there was not a mark. And it leaned. The whole bare floor leaned, as a line of cut signs leans, steadily, at he could not have said what.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-59.md:147:He had been turned away from every made thing that he ever stood in front of. A plate had kept him waiting. A registry had found a word for him. A cord came up a stride short in his hand, and a brass rule lied by two hairs. He had thought for the whole of his life that he was a thing with the wrong edge on it, which went into nothing; and it had come to seem the plainest fact that he knew.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-59.md:263:He had told every one of them on which side of something they stood, and it had been his whole trade. He had been good at it, and scrupulous, and he had believed for the most of a long service that scruple was the same as justice.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-59.md:269:He had stepped across it at dusk. It had been one stride, upon a stick, with a leg that a healer had bound; and the thing that had stood beside his face since he was fourteen had gone out.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-08.md:171:Twelve days before that boat, when the count at Lowmarch stood at two hundred and sixty-five, Vastin had come down to the last ford on the east road with a stick in his hand. He had never heard of the count.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-10.md:261:Nobody gave him anything to do. Cael looked along the board for it, as he would have looked for a slip on Pike's oak, and there was nothing: no paper for the man to witness, and no errand to send him on. He was not a hand, and had not been asked to be one. He had been sent into rooms for forty years to say what was in them, and he had come to a country where nobody sent anybody anywhere. He had brought the only load that he had, and he had just set down the first third of it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-18.md:39:He did not look down at the strap across his chest. He had carried that case for twelve years, shut, and it was shut now. He had found before today that he could not hold two closed things up to the light in one morning. He made no ruling upon it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-18.md:239:"He's not been asked," said Lira. "He took a slip." She had come round the slab, and she stood over the small pale woman with her left hand out of her pocket. "You said *ours* at that plank before any of us. I never heard you want a thing before. I'd sooner walk a day and a half behind somebody who wants something than sit at Lowmarch with somebody who's being careful."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-18.md:251:"Six miles," said the healer. She was drying her hands a finger at a time, and she did not look at him. "There's a fold there with a hearth in its corner, where a fire has walls round it. I'll have something to say when we're sat at it. I've put it off since a board at Lowmarch, and I've run out of road to put it off along."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-44.md:13:He had been home from the shoulder hold for a week. He had taken that salt up himself, behind the first carts, when he might have sent it with the bow and saved his own boots; the bow had said so, and had been right. But a cut that had eaten three carts in one cold term had been opened in an afternoon by the eleventh crew on Pike's cloth, and Teague had wished to see the ground.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-44.md:101:The long room of the lower inn was as full as Cael had ever seen it, and Pike had the bench under the cloth.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-44.md:109:Pike took it up, and held it to the candle in his double fashion; a thumb had been set to its foot, in grease, and had left a mark like a crook.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-44.md:133:Pike considered him. Then the pen went into the horn, and he wrote, slowly, in the square black hand.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-44.md:141:"I've nothing for those," said Pike. "It paid nothing, and it's not out yet. They'll stand empty, and I'll say that they're empty."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-44.md:147:"I said that I'd leave that until I'd a column for it," said Pike. "I've not found the column. I've had a hundred and sixty-eight days to look." He dipped. "A blank's a kind of entry, so long as a man knows why it's there. But a blank with nothing against it can't be told from one that was forgotten. So I'll put a day."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-44.md:213:He nodded, by the width of a finger, and it was done, and he had not offered his hand. There was a cloth on a nail behind Pike's head with one name alone on its first line and five in a row on its eleventh; and a contract did not take either of them down. Cael found that he did not wish it to. He would sooner walk east beside a man who was ten places better at a thing, and said so, than beside a friend who had to be reminded which of them the ledger preferred.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-44.md:217:*Two hundred and ninety-seventh day. Lowmarch; the hearth's end, and one on the keeper's settle. Eleven miles, and the ferry. The rope is paid: sixty yards of four, and he would take no fare. Pike has twelve lines, and a thirteenth with nothing in its last two columns. He has put a day against the season. A hundred and twenty-eight. Vastin: eighteen of thirty.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-44.md:221:*Known: Pike had a carter's word for the cut before we were home, and Teague had his terms by heart when he came down the room. I read Pike's line from his own side of the bench.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-46.md:145:*No*, he began to say; and he found that he could not say it without saying it with the whole of himself. A refusal was a holding, and she would feel a jaw go hard. She had felt it once, at the hundred and eighth breath, on another table, and had told him that he was helping.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-07.md:137:Pike had eight slips on his oak by the two hundred and seventy-second morning, and a healer at the end of it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-07.md:143:She had not asked Pike. She had carried a form out of the inn at first light, and set it along the wall, and gone into the wash-house with her sleeves pushed up; and by the time that Pike brought out his book there were six people sitting on it, and three more in the yard, who had not found the courage to sit.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-07.md:145:"That's my eave," said Pike.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-07.md:189:Pike made the new cloth that afternoon, in the yard, on a door laid across two trestles.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-07.md:235:Cael had been asked that by the better captain, in front of Pike, and there were several good answers. A day would have sounded well. So would a reason.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-07.md:243:"I'll leave it with Pike."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-56.md:33:Each stone was a line drawn low across his left side, from front to back, and he found that he could not draw a whole breath under it. He had not told a healer. She sat against the southern wall with her head on her knees, the colour of old ash, as she had sat in a ravine; and he thought that he had earned the walk up that bed a good many times over, and went down it again.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-56.md:221:Teague nodded, once, and asked nothing further. Cael understood that he had been handed one more thing for a column in which there were a good many already, and that he would add it up on the road home, by himself.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-25.md:71:She had met one body in which ten ran together with no bank to any of them. In the middle of the ten there had been a place where nothing ran at all, and everything went round. She had called it a stone, in her own head, on a carter's wrist, and she had called it a join. Sitting here with her hands as heavy as wet sacks, she knew that it had been neither of those, and that she had only reached for the two nearest words. She did not have a word. She had a child, an hour old, that had lain turned the wrong way in the dark and could not have told anybody so; and she had a boy three days south who was walking into a town with a thing in him that he could not read. She had not read it. She had sent him in with a rule about a square, since a rule had been the only thing that she owned to give him.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-25.md:109:It leaned against the pump's trough, tarred and black, with its strap new-stitched in three places, and it had ridden one shoulder since a fork-stone, though nobody had asked for that. A healer had forbidden it, twice, and had been waited out; and the person who kept a book had been told so on a road, to his face, and had neither given leave nor refused it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-52.md:207:He had read that day at a spring, a figure at a time, and laid it against another that he had carried since he was fourteen. He had put the sheet back. He had not written it, and he had told nobody.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-33.md:215:He had taken something from him. He had not reached. It had come as nine of the ten had come, out of standing within a stride of a person who was using what he had in earnest. It had come while that person poured himself into a line on a step, to keep a bolt off a face that he could not read. Cael had stood there and been given it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-26.md:159:It was three short lines in the plain hand, and it was the largest thing that he had entered since a fork. He had half believed, at a board in Lowmarch, that the first blue coat would have her, and that he would lose her to a keeper reading her name aloud. He had said nothing of it, to her or to the book, since a person does not enter a fear about a friend where the friend will read it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-26.md:191:He did not lift them far. They came along the paving at the height of a boot, as a grey keeper's had at a gate. Cael had been read by registrars since he was fourteen, and had learned to meet each of them in the eye, so that it should cost them something. He looked down at his own toes. He had not known that it would be so hard. It was the only thing that he had ever had to set against a pen; and he gave it up, and was a pair of boots among four.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-19.md:21:It was said to the fire, and nobody had asked her anything.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-19.md:277:He had a good deal to carry besides the box, and he went over it, a stride at a time, as he would have gone over a count. There was a job, done and paid, with a hundred and thirty-eight marks to show for it. There was a wall that he had stood behind for the space of one count, and an eleventh thing that had not come of it. There was a mark in a small ruled square, which was on four sheets and an iron stake and which nobody could read. He had a name, short and strange, for a man who sat under a town. He had two sentences in a book, in his own hand, about a flight of steps and a paved place.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-60.md:5:It was not a log. He kept a log for the day, and it took what the day had brought. This was the other form, which he had learned from an old man at Denvash: the whole of what a person holds, set down in order, with what is owed beneath it. He had written one at Lowmarch, when a river shut, and one in a loft under a town. He did not write one often; he wrote it when a thing was finished, and he wished to know what he had come out of it with.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-60.md:17:He had added three words to an entry that had stood unaltered since a session with a healer, on the far side of a cold term. They were not a finding. Karis would have told him so, and she would have been right, since no ruler had been laid on anything. They were only what he had said aloud on a floor, to nobody, with his palm on his own chest. He had put them down because he would not keep a book that left out the one thing which he had walked a year to say.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-60.md:79:"For a ferry. Teague'll have it in a bag at Lowmarch before we've seen a river."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-60.md:85:"Sixty-one. And a calfskin with five rings on it, and a line." She looked down at the roll on her knees. "I ruled that line off the edge of a hide, on a guess, with a strip of brass. I have stood at the end of it. I have been believed for twenty-three days, and I have not been wrong yet, and I am so tired that I could lie down on this rock and not get up."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-60.md:111:"What a thing cost was to be carried the same," he said to the captain. "So far as a cost can be. That was the second head, at Pike's bench. I'd not say that it was."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-60.md:139:"I'll put it into Pike's hand." Teague took it left-handed, and turned it over once, and read the outside, and put it inside his coat. "He'll ask me what was at the end of his bearing. He's two columns empty in that book."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-60.md:151:[[TIER-B: Teague's departure — "The thaw's past," / "Noted," / "After the next thaw." … "Whatever's on the next line. My crew comes." — Tier A 22 heads the unit "Teague:" but does not say which of the first two lines is his and which the answer, and it elides between "After the next thaw." and "Whatever's on the next line." Staged here: dawn of the 308th at the western side of the fire, Teague a stride from Cael with the letter already in his coat, his three standing behind him; the term that he set at Lowmarch ("After the thaw. Whatever's on that bearing. My crew comes.") has been kept, and this is its successor. Nothing of mine stands between his last speech above and this unit; the paragraph below has him go — coordinator to supply protected text]]
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-60.md:255:He slung it above his sound leg, and came across the rock, at his own beat, and stopped a stride behind a big man with a box, where he had stood since a ford. He looked at nobody. He had asked nothing of any of them, that morning or on any other, and nobody had asked a thing of him.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-23.md:23:She did not touch her coat. Cael marked that, since he had been listening for it. At a board in Lowmarch she had laid her hand on the pocket, and said what was in it, and that she had almost forgotten which side; she had spoken then of a certificate, and she spoke now of a wall.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-23.md:63:He had not meant to. He had lain on a pallet with his hands behind his head and listened to the street, and he had found that he could not lie under a town which he had not looked at. He had looked at the foot of it for a day. He wished to see how it stood.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-23.md:115:It came out above the river on the bluff's far side, where the town wall ran along a quay, and there was a landing. He had not seen a flat boat since Lowmarch. There were six of them here, tied nose to tail, and a great wooden wheel on the quay with a man walking inside it, round and round, so that a sack went up on a rope at every dozen steps. A ferry lay at the landing's lower end, on a cable. He looked at the cable's blocks for Brom's sake, and could not have said what lay the rope was.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-22.md:47:The gate was open, and it was nothing like what he had been afraid of since a board at Lowmarch.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-22.md:49:He had made a picture of it in a back room at Lowmarch, from two sentences of Seln's: a gate, with a book in it. In his picture there had been a counter across the arch, and a man behind the counter with a pen, and a line of people giving their names one after another. He had carried that pen with him up the whole of a border road.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-22.md:69:He had come back inside a Compact wall for the first time since he walked down a bluff with a house's certificate in his coat; and he had come as the fifth part of a movement of a stranger's thumb. Nobody had asked what he was. No pen had been lifted. He went on up the paved slope beyond the arch with his box on his shoulders, and his heart going as it had gone on the lip of a cut, and found that he could not have said whether he was relieved or insulted.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-16.md:101:The twenty was counted out on the top bar of the gate, in four heaps of five, and a thumb was set to the foot of Pike's slip, where it left a mark like a crook. Cael had been paid at a good many gates by then, and he had learned to read a hold by how it paid. The factor at the axe-head had counted twice, like a man who has been cheated before; this one counted once, and did not look at the heaps afterward, like a person who has got exactly what was wanted and means to think no more about the price.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-16.md:103:"A hundred and thirty-eight in hand," said Brom. He put the slip inside his shirt with the purse. "That's his, to draw the tack on. I'll have it after."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-16.md:191:*Two hundred and seventy-ninth day. The shoulder hold. The cut is clear of eight: five killed, by a rock twice, by a knife twice, and once by a thing that I shall come to; three through the ice of the sink. Twenty paid at the gate. In hand, a hundred and thirty-eight. Brom's right foot, two toes, ten days. Vastin's right knee: four holes shut by Oryn, the joint bound, on a stick for a month by her word.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-16.md:273:Cael looked at the road, which went on westward over the next swell and out of sight. Somewhere beyond that swell there was a fork, and beyond the fork a rise. He had ruled on a job, and the job was done and paid, and nobody had asked him this morning which way they were walking. They had simply walked.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-21.md:101:"It began with a day, and a word in square brackets, and after those two lines it was instruments: a registrar at a market town, with a counter and a pen; wardens, who came and stood in a yard and wrote; a panel; the frames of a house on a hill, at the end of every term, and the officers who watched the frames. Each of them laid upon you whatever he had been issued, and wrote down what it showed him, and what it showed him went under the last sheet and was sent up." The left hand moved on the stick. "There was not a paper in that folder which was not somebody's reading of you. I have handled a great many files, and I had not before met one in which the subject had been asked nothing and measured so often."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-21.md:151:He had wanted that answer for four days, since a board at Lowmarch, where he had kept the question behind his teeth and let a small pale woman ask hers. He saw now that he had wanted it for a great deal longer. He had turned it over at an academy, and on a bluff, and across two hundred and eighty days of country; and he had tried mercy in the place where the reason should go, and then policy, and then some long game of the seat's, and every one of them had been a thing done to him or for him. He had been given the true one in a borrowed room, and had never quite believed that it was all. It had not once occurred to him that what lay folded inside it might be a man's relief on his own account.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-21.md:237:He had not known that he was afraid of it until the pencil said so. He had kept that question since he was fourteen, on a great many desks, the wrong way up. Registrars had reached for it, and wardens, and an Archmarshal with a plate; and a healer had found the edge of it with her two hands, on a mountain, and had been made to wait a long while for the rest. Every one of them had come to him, and he had never in his life carried it to anybody. And he was walking to a town, three miles off, to lay it down in front of a man whom he had not seen, on the word of a catalogue.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-03.md:197:It was not a thing that a captain liked, to send one of his own to another crew's bench to be taught, and Teague priced it as he priced any stage. It would cost him nothing in marks. What it would cost him was the boy's seeing, every morning for a cold term, that there was a thing which his own captain could not show him. He had known captains who would not pay that; their people stood in the lower half of Pike's cloth, or had left it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-35.md:91:A man had shown a stranger the inside of his trade, for the first time in thirty years, because she had asked him honestly for something that he could not hand her; and the boy who held his lamp had come away with a piece of it. He had not reached. He never did reach. But he would kneel at that elbow tomorrow, and the day after, with the man's own way of looking folded behind his eyes; and each time that the finger went along a row, he would know where it was going before it went.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-35.md:275:The big man asked nothing else. He got up, and turned his bound shoulder to his own load, and swung it there in one heave; and Cael found the third box by its beckets and got it onto his back. The tarred case stood against the wall where it had been left, and he took it up by its strap.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-35.md:335:He had not lied to the man by the width of a hair. He stood on a tread that he could not see, with a box dragging at his shoulders and a strap in his fist, and waited to be asked how he came to know the length of it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-35.md:345:He lifted. A hand that he knew took him by the sleeve, and a lantern that he could not look at went by his face, and a key turned.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-32.md:189:Wind was first, as it had always been, the dry hiss in his feet. *Away?* it said; and there was nowhere on that stair that was away. Under it the millstone went round, and told him the weight of a slab that he could not see, and which way it was falling. Ember ticked in his right hand, and showed him a cord, ten yards off, drawn back along a stock; a single point would have gone through that cord as through a thread. Pressure had drawn itself as tight as a held breath. A slot between two walls was the very thing that it was made for; it would have gone up that stair like a cork up the neck of a bottle. *There?* asked the thin held note, of the place by the parapet where the slot began; and a point set there would have shut the head of the cut like a door.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-32.md:201:Cael had been stared at on roads since he was fourteen, by people who had been told what to look for. He knew the look of it through a hood.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-37.md:191:He had looked at them a good deal, through the goose and the pie, as he looked at an entry that he could not at once place. He had sat at more boards than anybody in that room. At every one of them a cover had been laid for whoever was present, in the order of the roll, and a person who was not in the room had no place in it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-38.md:149:It was the first time that Cael had heard it spoken. He had seen it written, in fresh ink, on a table at Lowmarch; and his hand had lain a hand's width from it for the whole of a lamp.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-38.md:225:She had turned round; it was the first time since the tape was untied that she had looked at anybody, and she looked at him as she had looked across a table at Lowmarch, when a courier's flat lay between them.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-57.md:21:She did not ask because it was polite. She had worked in a house once, inside a wall, where a sheet hung at the foot of every bed, and where nobody had asked a woman from the river sheds one thing from the day that she was carried in. The asking was the whole of the difference between that house and a plank across two kegs. It said that the body on the plank belonged to whoever was lying on it. She had not gone into a shin on a nod, two days since, with the bone lying two ways.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-57.md:107:"You have not touched it in a hundred and thirty-eight days."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-57.md:117:They did it on the flat of the rock, by the lamp, and they did it as it had been done at Lowmarch: one length, and four knots, and two strides. Brom held the brass eye down on a white stone with his boot. Karis ran the cord out to its fourth knot, and Brom came up; she ran it again, and laid the slip down twice. She set a second stone where it ended.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-57.md:255:*Turned the Rune fragment on myself. Cleon said don't. The reading only ever told me the still place was nothing — no flow, no current, a hole where a man should have a middle. The Rune sense says it's not a hole. It says it's* built like something *— like one of those floors with nothing standing on it. Same shape to that fragment as the sites are to Cleon's. I'm not going to write what that might mean, because I don't know, and because the last time I wrote something true in this log a healer made me swear an oath about it. The instruments read wronger for me than they did at Lowmarch. The trend's climbing. My Arbiter's still dark—nothing in me to go quiet, same as always. Tomorrow, the oldest one. I've spent the whole year wanting to stand in the middle of it. I think part of me already knows what I'm going to feel. Nothing. I think I'm going to feel nothing, and I think that's going to be the loudest thing that's ever happened to me.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-06.md:39:They went over in her line: Brom at the rope's length, with the mule's cheek-strap in the same fist, and Karis on the upstream side with both hands shut on the pack-girth, and Cael, and Seln last. The river leaned on them the whole way, and it was so cold that it had no feeling in it after the first breath. He kept his left boot in a groove that he could not see, and his eyes on the back of Lira's neck, and the read shut.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-06.md:195:He had one more thing for her, which was not on the hide. He had carried it for ninety-eight days, and nobody had asked him to bring it over a river. But he was standing at the plank where he had once lain still and let her spend herself on him, and he found that he could not stand there and keep a thing back.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-06.md:215:"I'm on the road in the morning. Lowmarch is my first stop after a thaw, and the mill's my second; they have been for thirty rounds, and I'd be walking down to that ferry tomorrow if I'd never set eyes on the five of you." She did not turn round. "So I'll be on your road for two days, since it's mine as well. I'd have that understood. Nobody asked me."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-06.md:223:The purse had moved by two at the landing. The ferryman had been on his rope-house roof when they walked down the bank, on his knees, with his palm laid on Brom's shingles. It had kept a thaw out, and he said so, and five marks came up through Pike's hand before the supper was on, with a line said twice to go with them. Then he had looked at the brown mule, who was standing at his shed's door as though she had never left it; and he had asked at last. It was three, for the cold term, straw and standing, and he had named it without once lifting his eyes from her.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-06.md:227:"I told you that he would," said Pike.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-36.md:23:He did not write it. He had kept one other thing out of those pages on purpose, at a spring, eleven days ago; he marked that this made two, and that he could not write that either.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-05.md:7:She had chosen her ground with care. The peg stood against the left-hand post of Pike's plank, under the eave. At the yard's far side, by the gate, there was a stone trough with a scratch cut in its foot that morning with her knife. The snow between had been trodden as hard as a floor by a cold term of boots.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-05.md:197:The long dark lay on Lowmarch through all the weeks that followed, when the sun stood up over the eastern hill for five hours and went home. It was in those weeks that Cael filled the last of his seven lines.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-02.md:3:They went out to the fence at first light on the hundred and thirty-eighth day, by the cold road, with the mule carrying meal and two of the keeper's shovels.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-02.md:45:He let them look. Then he gave his terms, and they were as short as a slip on Pike's plank.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-02.md:251:Cael walked the whole fence on the last evening with Karis's notebook, to have the count right for Pike. There were forty-one new posts, with the earth dark about their feet. There were two more with fresh gravel round them, the twentieth and the twenty-first from the gate, in the middle of a run that another crew had set; and beside each, a hand to the north, a round scar showed in the snow where a post had lately stood.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-02.md:267:He paid at the gate, under the axe-head. He counted forty marks onto the top bar, and put a thumb-mark shaped like a wedge at the foot of Pike's slip.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-02.md:279:Pike said their line at dusk on the hundred and forty-sixth, in a room with thirty people in it and a fire that a man could have stood in. He gave it out as he gave everything, with his shoulder turned: a fence, at the axe-head hold, the long one; forty-one posts set and the far side pinned; a herd shifted off the line and nothing run; witnessed. He dipped his pen for the last column.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-02.md:291:Pike looked at him, and looked again, and drew the tack out of the dead slip.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-01.md:113:"You can count posts," said Brom. "I've a fence with forty of them still lying in a heap, by what he told Pike, and one arm to set them with. I'll want somebody on a rail who can see whether they stand in a row."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-01.md:199:Pike brought his book out to the plank at noon on the hundred and thirty-third day, for the first time since the ferry shut, and there were three slips on twelve feet of oak.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-01.md:211:"That's a cold term." Pike set the horn pot down into its hole. "A roof, a well-house and a fence, and the fence is yours. The holds shut their gates and mend what they've got. I've had years with nothing on this oak from first snow to first boat but the nails."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-01.md:221:"Meadow rate is four a month for a crew that's on my sheet," said Pike.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-01.md:223:The keeper said, "Pike," and Pike said, "Eleventh."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-01.md:227:"Standing." Pike considered him in his double fashion, and seemed to find the question a proper one. "That's the hearth's end you're lying at, which is the keeper's floor and my money, and I'll not explain that twice in one day. It's the pot at dusk, a bowl a head; he'll charge you for a second and he'll be right. And it's your names said to anybody who comes asking after a crew, which nobody will, the river being shut. That's three things."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-01.md:239:"He will." Pike dipped his pen. "Four a month, and a cold term's five by my book. Twenty, in front. I'll not chase a crew over ice for a month's standing. Four a month; five months; twenty, in front."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-01.md:247:Pike wrote it, and said it over to the meadow, as he said everything. Forty fires had burned along that water in the dry months, with a cart or a tent to each. There were three now, and a great many black rings in the snow where the others had been; the hide-woman sat at the nearest with a skin across her knees, and she was the only person in half a mile of grass who lifted her head to hear what a crew had paid. Then his pen went down the leaf to the last line under their names, which was the season's, and stopped beside the ruled space at the end of it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-01.md:259:The fifth of them was bent under a mule's belly with both hands on a girth. He had a band of black leather laced on his right wrist; Pike had guessed him a Bronze once, under that eave, and had never been told. Cael put up one hand to that bent back, open, as carters did on this side of the Line when they passed on a road.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-01.md:275:Her hand went up, and Karis's with the pencil in it. Seln lifted two fingers. Cael put up his own, and Pike made five strokes at the slip's foot and said the whole of it twice to the snow.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-01.md:281:*Hundred and thirty-third day. Lowmarch, the lower inn. The cold stay is paid: four a month, five months, twenty in front. In hand, seventy-six. The reserve is not counted here. Nine lines, and no number against the season, on purpose. Eleventh on the cloth. The board has a roof, a well-house and a fence. The fence is ours, and we go out to it on the hundred and thirty-eighth. The roof is ours as well, at five, paid when it has kept a thaw out. The mule stands in the ferryman's shed, at a fee he has not asked for and will.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-43.md:253:Lowmarch came up out of the dusk on the eastern side as it had come up on the first evening that Cael ever saw it. But the meadow was not as he had left it. There had been nineteen fires along that water on the night before the first boat. There were more than forty now; the holds had opened, and every crew that had lain up for the cold was down to hear what the plank would say. Carts stood wheel to wheel by the smith's rail. There was a light in every window of the lower inn.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-58.md:13:Cael walked at a captain's shoulder, a stride back, with a healer's tape drawn so tight round him that he could count his own ribs by it. He could not carry; the three boxes were on the mule, and nobody had asked him to try.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-13.md:21:It was the two hundred and seventy-eighth morning past the Line, and cold, with a white mist on the river that hid the cable's middle. Under the long eave, on twelve feet of tarred oak, Pike had his book open and his stone on the leaf. He was driving a tack with the butt of his penknife into the last clear space at the plank's right hand.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-13.md:25:Five of them read it together from the left-hand post, since Lira and Brom had come round from the well, and Seln had been standing there already, with no wand under his arm. It was a strip of bark, like every other slip on that oak, in the square black hand. Beside the words Pike had drawn a little road going between two heaps, and on the road something with four legs.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-13.md:29:"It's twenty," said Pike. "At the hold's own gate, on the factor's thumb, when the factor's walked it. That's a hold that's lost carts. It doesn't say what to. They never do; they'd have to pay more."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-13.md:41:She had said it small and clear, with the flat under her arm. Cael had known her since a library table in Ostrand, and in all that time he had never once heard her claim a job. She had put her hand up for fences and for roofs when hands were asked for, and had gone where the count sent her; she had never looked at a slip and wanted it. Pike's pen stopped over the horn.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-13.md:79:"Five," said Pike. He made the strokes at the slip's foot, and then he did not dip his pen again. He turned his shoulder to them. "And who's your healer?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-13.md:93:Pike considered her in his double fashion. Six days ago he had had no word in his book for a woman who did what nobody had posted.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-13.md:105:Cael left the word with Pike, as he had promised a better captain that he would: the cut, two days west by the border road, and whatever lay beyond it, with no day put on the coming back. Pike wrote nothing. He said that he had a season line of theirs with no number against it, and had stopped expecting to fill it by the week.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-13.md:261:*Two hundred and seventy-eighth day. A halting-ground on the border road, eleven miles from the river. A slip taken at the plank: a clearance, the cut below the shoulder hold, twenty. Five hands. Pike has a sixth line on it for the healer, of her own road. Vastin walks behind us. He was not asked, and he was not told no. He has the four hands, and he knows where a post stands. He says that he cannot promise to watch one of us bitten, and I believe him. The rope on the far bank was three-strand; Brom would not have it. In hand, a hundred and eighteen.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-12.md:173:*Received of Vastin, at Lowmarch, day two hundred and seventy-seven past the Line: one courier's flat, holding six rows of abstracts in their tapes, one row looked through and no sheet counted; one broker's report; one page, copied by him from a register; one technician's sheet, copied by me this night and laid back in the second row. Read in part. Understood in no part. — K.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-12.md:187:It was not a fall. Brom was off the door before it could be one, and had him by the upper arm, left-handed; Oryn's two hands had come up, and stopped in the air, since nobody had asked her for them. Vastin hung in a large man's grip, open-eyed, and as pale in the face as the paper on the table.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-29.md:157:"Now I've sixty yards of rope to buy going out, and a bowl to carry to a mill." He went down three steps. "He counted my neck. I've been counted by better; Pike never asked me what I was. Mind that landing."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-29.md:283:He had come to that town with one question. He had written at a fire that he was about to hand it to a stranger, and had been afraid of it for two days, in a loft, with a small pale woman's roll across his knee. He need not have been. The stranger had told a gallery, without being asked, that he could not take it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-39.md:79:What he could not get past was smaller than the eleven, and it had no file.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-39.md:175:He asked nothing. He never did, in a street. He walked at the cart's tail with his frame on one shoulder, and waited to be told as much as could be said between a bar and a lane.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-09.md:207:Pike had the bag sorted by the time that the rest of them came up.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-09.md:217:Pike had watched him the whole length of the plank. He had looked, and then he had looked again; and when the stranger reached the right-hand post, he turned his shoulder to the six.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-09.md:223:"He's asked me nothing. He's read my oak like a man going over a bill." Pike scratched his stump. "I've four that were on that boat who'll tell me he kept it on its cable, and one of them's the ferryman, who'd not say a good word for his own mother. That's said. It wasn't posted, and I'll not enter what wasn't posted." He jerked his chin at the brown coat. "What'd he do?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-09.md:235:Pike said it over, to the tents, and did not dip his pen. He seemed neither pleased nor sorry. He had been given a figure that he could believe, and he put it where he put such things.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-34.md:213:He hung the lamp's ring on Lira's fingers, and went down into the dark ahead of them on his quick flat feet. He had asked nothing. He had seen a leg, as he would have seen a cracked shelf, and had made his reckoning of it, which was that the morning would begin late.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-40.md:171:Cael had known that it would come round to him. It had come round at a plank in Lowmarch, and at a spring, and in a loft with a blanket on its window; they had looked at him then for a ruling. They did not look at him now: a girl had sat down again on her bucket and was watching the fire, and a big man was scraping a pot. They were giving him the yard, as they had given it to her.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-40.md:179:It was not a ruling; there was nothing in it to hold, or to try again in daylight. It had been read, once, in a voice that shook; and it was either so or it was not, and every person at that fire had already taken it for so. If he wrote it he would be putting it where he put a cord and a hair. He would be making it a thing that he had noticed; and it was not that. It was the ground that he had been standing on since he was fourteen, shown to him from underneath.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-11.md:7:"There are three reasons," he said. "I had a long road to put them in order, and I shall give you them so. The first is a thing that you did not do. Five people laid a case in front of me that would have shaken any officer in the Compact, and when I had read it, not one of them asked me for anything. I remarked on it at the time. I have been asked for something by every person who ever set a paper before me; it is what the paper is for. You asked nothing. You let me go down a stair with it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-11.md:131:He would still take no wheels. But there were carters on that bank who had waited since the snow and would wait no longer for the sake of a cart, and a dozen of them went down the planks on foot, with their goads over their shoulders, to see to their business on the far side and come back for their wheels when the river allowed. The bag went with them. Pike had made it up at his plank, each piece said twice.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-11.md:175:Cael had seen it from the well. He knew a dozen things that might have been found for that man to do, since he had spent a cold term learning what a day at Lowmarch was made of. There were reeds to be split, and the ferryman's rope to be turned by a fire, and Hesk's clock to be read against the smith's noon. Any of them would have served. Each would have been a thing made up on the spot, by a boy of nineteen, to spare a man of that standing the sight of his own empty hands; and the man had spent forty years telling such make-work from the other kind at a glance.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-50.md:97:It took the most of it. The shaft slid, all the same, and the iron shoe came over the top of his guard and down upon his left collar-bone like the corner of a falling box. He heard his own breath go out. The whole of that arm went white to the fingers. He did not give the stride, and he found afterward that he could not have said why, except that it had not been his to give.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-41.md:5:It was the two hundred and ninety-fourth day, and the yard was grey, with the ashes of a fire in an iron basket and eight bowls stacked on a board beside the pump. He had got himself out of the stall on the stick and one shoulder. The tarred case lay across his thighs already. It had lain in the straw at his elbow on every night since he came down a ladder on a rope; nobody had given it into his keeping, and nobody had asked for it back.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-45.md:91:"Thank you. That is exactly as much as I wanted." Her finger had gone to the hide, and it touched each ring in turn, going east. "The Fallow's newest sheet stands in its sixty-first year, and the Stair's in its ninety-fourth; the hall's is a hundred and thirty-eight. And the place behind the iron—"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-45.md:159:Cael looked along the skin, from the left hand to the right. There were four rings, and a point that was not yet drawn, and a number against each: sixty-one, and ninety-four, and a hundred and thirty-eight, and two hundred and six. They rose as they went. Beyond the last of them, in a strip of shadow, stood something that the office had stopped counting.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-14.md:67:"Pike's five, for the cut. I asked for five, and I see five and two over."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-14.md:81:"You know what took them, or Pike's sent me the wrong five."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-14.md:117:The hollow was round, like a bowl, and it lay in the spur's shadow, where the day never came. There was ice on it. The ice was grey and dull, with water standing on it at the edges, and Cael knew that grey: the shelf at Lowmarch had gone that colour a week before the river took it. At one side, under a lip of rock, there was a black place where it had not shut, and he could hear water going down there, a long way, into the hill.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-20.md:23:Lira laughed. It was one short breath through the nose, and it was the first sound of the kind that Cael had heard on a road since a meadow at Lowmarch.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-20.md:35:After him there was a boy with a switch and nine geese, and a woman under a yoke, and two men mending a hurdle; and not one of them gave the six more than a glance, which took in the loads and priced the distance. Cael counted them, since he counted anything. He had been stared at on roads since he was fourteen, by people who had been told what to look for, and for the better part of a year he had walked where there was nobody to stare. This was a third thing. He was a back with a box on it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-20.md:37:Vastin went beside him on the ditch side, a shoulder off. The stick came down a little after each of Cael's strides, and the tarred case knocked softly against a brown coat, and neither of them spoke; of the six they were the two who found the leave hardest to use. Cael had kept one question from that man since a board at Lowmarch. He thought that the man was keeping something too. He walked like somebody carrying a full bowl.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-20.md:173:That made ten. He had called them fragments since he was fourteen, and pieces, and entries in a list. Lying in the grass he had them as the going-along had them, which was none of those. They were ten currents, and not one of them had a bank. Each ran on into the next as water runs into water, and a healer had found that out before he did, with both her hands, and had told him so on a ridge.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-20.md:271:Cael turned that over. He had kept a question for that man since a board at Lowmarch, and had thought himself the only one of the two who was carrying anything.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-51.md:203:It was four hundred and forty yards, and two strides. He had seen the figure written by lamplight, on a night when nobody would cross. It was a long way to walk with nothing on either hand, and the grey thing grew as they came, and he found that he could not make it stop growing.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-04.md:233:Pike had no slip for it, and he said so that night under his cloth.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-27.md:35:He marked that. Anchor was the eighth of the ten things that he carried, a piece of the craft by which the Compact holds a person where he stands; it had asked his leave to bar every sill and gate since Ostrand, and at an iron door it had asked very low. It asked nothing here. There was nothing in that place to set a bar across, and the fragment seemed to know so before he did.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-27.md:187:He asked nothing more. He let go of the table, and walked to his desk with one hand before him, by habit, as a man crosses his own bedroom in the dark. There was a shelf above the desk, under the lamp's iron arm, and on it a cup, and a jar, and a thick bundle tied with tape; and at its end a long flat thing wrapped in sacking. He took that down. He stood with his back to all of them, and turned the sacking open, and looked at whatever lay in it. He looked from its foot.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-27.md:265:"You'd want me to say it," said Cael. They were looking at him; they had been doing so since a plank at Lowmarch. "It's not a slip. There's nothing to take or leave. She's asked for a week, and it's hers."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-27.md:285:He had been in a great many buildings since he was fourteen, and in every one of them there had been a drawer. There was a counter at Denvash, with a pen on it. There was a house on a bluff, where a file had grown as thick as a man's hand; there was a district seat, and a borrowed office. He had learned to feel for the drawer on any threshold, as a dog feels for the one board in a floor that gives. Even on the kind side of the Line there had been a book, kept by a man with one arm; that one had been honest, and he had been glad to stand in it, and it had put him eleventh all the same.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-30.md:29:Cael lay and looked at the slates that he could not see.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-30.md:103:So it was not that door, and he had known it before he sat down. He had come up here to learn whether he still wished that it were, and he found that he could not tell.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-30.md:155:Cael did not know what had been looked at. It might have been a case. It might have been a door, or a light on a crown, or the whole of fifteen years. He found that he could not ask. He had been given three words by somebody who weighed them like coin, and it was a great many for that purse.

