   276	
   277	"I'll stay," he said. "I said *noted*."
   278	
   279	"You did. I should think that they heard you at the mill."
   280	
   281	He went the colour of a brick, and grinned, which Cael had not seen him do. There was a cloth folded through his belt. It was the one that he wrung out for a wrist on every cold night, and he wore it as another boy might have worn a knife.
   282	
   283	He had wanted for the whole of a road to be something besides the cloth. He had said a figure aloud as he stepped over a bar of iron, so that nobody should forget that he was on the count; and Cael, who had spent a great many nights of his own putting figures into a book for much the same reason, had not found it in him to smile.
   284	
   285	"Where'll you be?" he said.
   286	
   287	"At the back, with the old man."
   288	
   289	"Oh," said the boy.
   290	
   291	It was a small sound. Cael heard it, and thought nothing of it, and remembered it afterward for a long while.
   292	
   293	The boy went back to his own, where the broad man had his crossbow out of its cover and was turning it over in his hands. Somebody had bound the split stock in the night with a wet thong, turn upon turn, and the thong had dried as hard as horn. Cael did not need to be told whose big chapped fingers had done it.
   294	
   295	"It'll loose twice," said the bow, to his captain. "I'd not ask it for three."
   296	
   297	Then two fingers came up, and they went on.
   298	
   299	The healer walked in the middle, with the mule's rope in her fist, as though she had never owned any other road. She was six days past the end of hers that morning. Cael had it in his book, counted from a cairn, and he did not suppose that she had left off counting it.
   300	
   301	---
   302	
   303	The mouth was eight strides wide, and there was nobody in it.
   304	
   305	Brom had hobbled the mule in a nook of the rock, fifty yards short, with her nose in a bag of the mill's corn. She had not looked up when they left her.
   306	
   307	They went in along the foot of the bank, in a knot, as a captain had ordered it. On the right hand the pool lay under the sheer wall, as flat as a slate, with the sky in it. On the left the shale went up at a pitch that hurt the neck. Cael looked along the top of it. There was a line of rock against the brightening east, and two long low shapes upon the line, and beside the nearer one a dead tree, quite white, with a rope about its trunk.
   308	
   309	Nothing moved up there, and nobody called to them.
   310	
   311	At the Span a man had hung a light in the air and told them to stand. He understood from that silence, better than from anything that Seln had counted, that these had not been posted to ask.
   312	
   313	"This is the place," said Vastin.
   314	
   315	He had stopped a yard inside the mouth, where a stroke of charcoal had said. He stood for a breath with both hands on the ash and looked at the stones of a dry bed.
   316	
   317	"I once told a man on a road that I should prefer not to be helped to sit," he said. "I find that I must be. I should be obliged if it were done quickly."
   318	
   319	Brom was behind him already.
   320	
   321	He put his two arms under the old man's, from the back, as he would have taken up a full churn. He bent his knees, and let that weight down toward the stones a hand at a time, with the stiff leg sliding out before it; and it was while his back was bent, and both his hands were full, that the first flight came.
   322	
   323	Cael heard the strings: three of them, close together, like a stick drawn along a paling.
   324	
   325	One bolt went into the stones a yard from Karis's boot. One struck the southern wall and sang off it. The third went through the flesh of Brom's left forearm, a hand below the elbow, where a bruise from another shelf was still going green. It stood there, with its feathers on the one side of the arm and its head on the other.
   326	
   327	He had not been grey, and he could not have been. A man cannot go to stone and set another down gently in the same breath; and Brom had been offered that choice in a stream-bed, with three bows bent on him, and had made it without so much as lifting his head to see what it would cost.
   328	
   329	He grunted, and he did not let go. He lowered the old man the last hand's breadth, until he sat square on the bed with his back straight, and only then did he stand up and look at what was in his arm.
   330	
   331	"That's one," he said.
   332	
   333	The slate came up his neck as he said it, too late for that one.
   334	
   335	*One*, Cael began, since a bow takes something over twenty to bend.
   336	
   337	The old man had not looked round. He sat in a stream-bed as he would have sat a bench, upright, with the bound leg laid out before him like a thing left on a table. He shifted the ash to his right hand. He reached it out behind his own hip, without turning his head, and found a place for its foot among the stones.
   338	
   339	Then he brought it down.
   340	
   341	There was no sound that Cael could have named. A line of dust stood up out of the bed, from the foot of the sheer wall to the foot of the bank, as straight as a cord that a joiner has snapped; and it ran through the stick, and behind an old man's back, across the whole of the mouth.
   342	
   343	The air did not stand up from it, as it had in a cut. It leaned.
   344	
   345	He felt it go over him. It went up from that line and forward, above their heads, eastward, like the lid of a great chest being let down; and where its far corner met the bank, twenty-five strides on and three times the height of a man, a little shale ran, and stopped.
   346	
   347	In him a post asked its two words of the mouth, as it asked them of any line, and he told it no.
   348	
   349	*Nineteen. Twenty.*
   350	
   351	The second flight struck while he was still looking up.
   352	
   353	There were three small flat knocks over their heads, where there was nothing, and three puffs of dust hung there. The pieces came sliding down the air toward the mouth, rattling like dice on a board, and dropped off its edge behind the old man's back.
   354	
   355	Then he could see it, since the dust had shown him where to look. It was a roof, and it had no thickness at all. It began at the stones behind a brown coat, and rose away over eleven people to a high edge against the hill, with the morning coming in under that edge like light under a barn door.
   356	
   357	"It is up," said Vastin.
   358	
   359	His voice was quite level, though his face had gone the colour of the stones that he sat on.
   360	
   361	"I am informed that it can be done sitting," he said. "I had rather have learned it on some other day."
   362	
   363	On the shelf, forty feet above them, three bows were being bent again.

