editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-48.md:123:It was a great whale's back of grass, a mile broad on its crown, with the country falling off on either hand. He had seen it under the clearest sky of his life, with a white stroke of frost in every fold. The folds were green now, and the sky was a soft high grey, and it was warm. That was the trouble with it. Twelve people and a loaded beast had been walking since the marsh on a cup at night and a cup at morning, and by the noon halt the boy's lips had a line of white along them.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-54.md:149:Her two thick hands went round the linen, above the laths and below them, and her eyes shut. Cael watched her lips, which moved for the length of forty breaths and then were still.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-54.md:257:It was the last water that they would drink on that side, and the skins went along the file from hand to hand. Brom was looking at the lashings on a mule, and the lean old woman at a captain's linen. Teague's boy had come across the yard of stone that lay between the two crews, as he had come once before, with nothing in his hand this time.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-54.md:307:They went in along the foot of the bank, in a knot, as a captain had ordered it. On the right hand the pool lay under the sheer wall, as flat as a slate, with the sky in it. On the left the shale went up at a pitch that hurt the neck. Cael looked along the top of it. There was a line of rock against the brightening east, and two long low shapes upon the line, and beside the nearer one a dead tree, quite white, with a rope about its trunk.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-58.md:55:He saw that when they had gone down to it. The grey did not simply begin: a line had been sunk in it, as wide as two fingers and as deep, running away to the left hand and the right until it was lost in the light; and the plain's worn rock ran to the outer lip of that line and went no farther. It was as sharp as if the tool had been lifted out of it that morning.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-58.md:71:So he sat on the middle box, at the first stump, a pace outside the cut, with the ash held upright before him in both hands like a surveyor's pole. He looked along the line to the north, and then to the south, as he might have looked over a form before he signed it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-58.md:87:The line did not seem to bend, and that was the strangest part of the morning. He walked along a cut that ran dead straight before him, for as far as he could see, and there was a floor on his right hand the whole way. Yet when he looked back, at the twentieth length, an old man on a box was no longer behind him. He was behind and to the right, and at the fortieth he was farther round; the straight line was carrying them about a middle that none of them had seen.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-58.md:185:[[TIER-B: "The others are copies." — the packet gives the three words after "this is the first cut" and pins no speaker. Staged here: at the western stump, a hand outside the cut, after Karis has shown the copied slip of the tool on two of her own sheets and said that one stratum lay under all that she had; the conclusion itself is left for this line, and I have not written it in other words. Karis is the natural owner; Lira or Brom could say it to her. The next paragraph stands for any of them — coordinator to supply protected text]]
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-58.md:295:Then she came out, and wiped her eyes with the back of her bound hand, and was not ashamed of it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-58.md:317:She went to the cut by herself, and stood on the near side of it, with her boots together, like a person at the edge of a pond. She put both her arms out in front of her, at the height of her waist, so that her two hands were over the line and the rest of her was not; and she turned the palms down.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-58.md:337:Cael saw it take him. The heavy shoulders went back, as though somebody had drawn a pack off them from behind; and the left hand came up, slowly, and closed upon the right wrist, where there was linen, and a slat, and under those a joint that had paid for everything that he had ever done. He stood so for the length of three breaths.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-56.md:295:"Don't mend my sums." But there was no edge on it. She sat back on her heels, and rubbed her eyes with the heel of her hand, and for a breath she looked as old as the woman with the board. "Two are cracked along the old line. The third's whole, and angry. Nothing's gone into anything that it shouldn't. I can ease them, and I will, and I'll tape you so that you remember them."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-56.md:305:She put both her hands on him then, and he felt the warmth go in along three lines of fire and lay them down, one by one, as a person banks a hearth.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-53.md:205:She did not say them over. She sat with her right hand on her knee and her left, in its linen, laid upon the lid; and after a moment she moved the bound one, without quite meaning to, until it lay flat over the bare wood between the two pages.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-47.md:239:"I shut that shoulder on the two hundred and ninety-eighth. A mend that deep wants a week of somebody's eye, and I've said in a yard what I think of a healer who spends herself on a thing and walks off while it's deciding. That's the three hundred and fifth." Her hand had come up, and a thumb went down. "There's a knee behind it that's on my linen and twenty-one days old. And there's an edge at the end of that brass that's twice any I've stood at, with ten of you going at it. I said in a loft where somebody ought to be."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-55.md:29:She broke the shaft between her two hands, close to the skin, and Brom looked at the roof while she did it. The lean old woman had a strip of linen round the place before the stub had stopped moving.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-55.md:33:At the low end a girl crouched by a brown coat with her knife out, watching the bank. Karis sat beyond her on the stones, with the flat on her back, and her bound hand laid on it as on a child.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-55.md:185:Brom had come up the wedge. There was a hand of broken bolt standing out of his left forearm, with linen round it, and he was the colour of slate to the hair. He looked once at what lay at the roof's edge. He did not say anything. He walked out past it, into the open mouth of the shelter, and turned his face up to the shelf, and stood there like a door that has been shut.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-55.md:347:She did it on the bare bed, with the flat on her back, and she put out her left hand, in its linen, and laid it on his shoulder. She did not say anything, and she did not take it away.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-49.md:125:He took out his knife. There was a patch of bare earth at the fold's bottom where sheep had lain once, and he smoothed it with the flat of his hand, and cut two lines down it, a hand apart, wavering a little.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-49.md:127:"That's the ravine. It's three men deep where it runs beside the fence, and the walls are sheer; I'd not want to go down them without a rope." He set a row of pricks along the right-hand side of it, close together. "That's the iron. And between the two there's a shelf. It's a hundred yards long, by my eye, and a cart wide for most of that. The line's a stride inside the iron, on the far side from the shelf, as it was the last time; Lira stopped a pace above the bars, and said that she'd go no farther."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-60.md:187:Her two hands went round the linen, above the laths and below. It did not take her long.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-60.md:247:Karis had the courier's flat on her back, and the hide under her arm, and her notebook in her coat. She had gone to the cut once more, by herself, when the light came, and had stood there for some time with her bound hand held a little out from her, as though she would have liked to lay it on something; and she did not say what she had looked at.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-51.md:237:He understood why. A man had tried to drive a stake into this grey once, under a floor, and had struck twice, and had laid his mallet down. Nothing went into it. Of everything that a person might brace a heel against in all that country, there was not a thing so hard; and the prop that lay along him knew it, as a mason's hand knows a sound stone. It had found one end of a line, the best that it would ever be offered.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-50.md:133:*Force-adjacent. Teague. Partial. Bronze. Impulse along the line of the frame at contact: from the ground, through the man, out at the hand, and nothing to either side of the line. Needs a thing to push against — the barricade, the ground under the feet; nothing at a distance; nothing from the air. A body's length. The cost back along the same line, every time, at the place the line comes out. Engagement field: close; braced. Tier equivalent unknown. Deployment: none. Thirteenth.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-52.md:13:It was the trodden path that they took, round the outside of the iron. Six of them had walked down that dish to its middle the noon before, and Cael would have gone so again. But there were people in that file with something to lose at a stride, and a beast, and nobody proposed it. They went a quarter of the round with the bars at their right hand. On the far side Karis stopped the whole file for as long as it takes to lace a boot. She stood at a bar that was like every other, and looked back across the dish. From there the grey road on the bridge's back lay end-on to her, like a ruler seen along its edge; and when she turned about, the same line went out before her, over two ridges, to a square gap in the hills.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-52.md:153:Cael had thought that she was done. She had the hide as she wanted it, and the light was going yellow along the shale. But she had turned a leaf in her notebook, to a page that was ruled in columns, and she sat looking down at it with her bound hand laid flat beside it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-52.md:183:Karis's bound hand came down flat upon her notebook.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-52.md:205:It lay open at the blanket's head, with five rows in their tapes and one out. At the foot of the second there was a sheet of thinner stock than the rest. He knew what was on it. There was a line and a half of a quick sloping hand, and a bar of black as broad as a thumb; and between the two lines there was a day.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-59.md:253:So he sat on in the dark, with both hands on the ash, and looked at a line.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-59.md:323:The old sentence began itself in him then, as it did whenever he was tired: the one about a man, and an apparatus. He had been stopped in it twice by a bound hand, and he found that he no longer needed the hand. He set it down unfinished, among the nine orders that he had not drafted on a shelf of shale, and it lay there quietly enough.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-35.md:115:"Of twenty-three," said the man.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-48.md:99:*Three hundred and first day. A dead hold on the east road, in the lee of its gable. A hundred and twenty-three. Vastin: twenty-two. Oryn is a day past her cairn. A cup of water a head, and the kettle.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-48.md:321:*Three hundred and second day. The ridge's back, in a hollow. A hundred and twenty-three. Vastin: twenty-three. Oryn is two days past her cairn; she went by the chock-stones and did not look up.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-46.md:195:He had heard her say before that a thing was past her hands. She had said it on a hillside, of a girl's arm; and she had told him afterward that when she said so he was to believe her, and not stand looking at her as though she had kept something back. He believed her. She had gone round that place for the better part of half an hour, at every height, with all that she had, and she had not come out and said that it was nothing. She had said what she was, and where she ended.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-57.md:225:This leaned so, steadily, and there was not a mark upon it. There was no sign. There was no letter that a woman with twenty-three of them could ever have turned. It was an instruction with no language.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-57.md:247:*Three hundred and sixth day. The open rock below the eastern mouth of the gap. A hundred and twenty-three. Vastin: twenty-seven; he held a roof today, sitting down, by Oryn's leave, and the knee never took a pound of it. Oryn is six days past her cairn.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-55.md:5:It came at the count of twenty-three, and all three bolts went to the one place: low down, by the mouth, at an old man who sat on the stones with his back to them. Cael heard them strike. They broke a yard above the grey head, where the lean of the thing was lowest, and the pieces skittered off behind.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-47.md:227:"Four," said Brom. "Meal, and a cheese, and corn for her, and the two skins." He set the sack down. "A hundred and twenty-three. And I never named a thing. He'd the skins filled and standing in the gate, and a price on them."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-47.md:299:*Three hundredth day. The track's end, outside the stone, at the hall's marsh. A hundred and twenty-three: four at a gate, for two skins, meal, a cheese, and corn. Vastin: twenty-one.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-41.md:55:Karis had the second chair, with a board across her knees and her notebook open upon the board. She read the first line through in the time that it takes to lace a boot; she had every one of the twenty-three letters now, and a hand that had pressed like a mason's did not blur. Then she sat looking at what she had written.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-41.md:197:"It's what it was. I stood where I've stood for thirty years, and said which way a thing leaned. You found out what it said." The red eyes were on the roll of card in her hand. "A finding's yours. You'll have to carry it up into the light and be believed, and I've told you what I think of that. Work's a thing that a man can go back down to in the morning."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-39.md:73:He had been a wall. He did not say that with any pity for himself; it was the plainest account of Iron Wall, and of his office, and of what a seat had used him for. A crowd came to a gate, or a hearing drew too many, and he was sent for. He struck his stick upon the ground, and whatever stood on the far side of the line stayed there. He had done it eleven times for a seat, and twice since for no seat at all. He had believed, each of the eleven times, that the thing behind him was the law, and that it was imperfect, and that it was a great deal better than what would come through if he lifted his heel.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-39.md:93:He began, from forty years' habit, to set it in order. There would be three heads. The first would deal with what he had believed; the second with what he had been shown above Ostrand; the third —
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-37.md:27:The clerk wrote *the same*, and *four*. The old man went down twenty-seven steps on his seat, with his heel in Cael's palm, and said at the foot that he believed he had improved upon his time.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-40.md:61:"He gives what he believed, or wished believed. I've only his word, and he's scraped his own name off it; or somebody has." He shook his head slowly. "I'd want to hear the other side before I'd sign for that. There's nobody left to give it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-40.md:121:"I believed it was imperfect."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-40.md:165:Cael had watched the whole of that from his bucket. He had believed, for the whole of his time on a bluff, that the worst thing which one person could do to another was to write him down. He had now heard a man say across a fire that he would have signed the sheet. He had seen a girl agree that it was so, to his face, and then feed him; and he looked in himself for the wish that she had done otherwise, and it was not there.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-40.md:259:They got Karis up the ladder between them, Lira above and Cael below, more asleep than awake, with her notebook held against her in both arms and the bound hand uppermost. She said one thing on the rungs, quite clearly: she said that there were twenty-three, and that she had them all.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-60.md:9:*Three hundred and seventh day, night. The rim of the fifth place. A hundred and twenty-three. Vastin: twenty-eight. Oryn is seven days past her cairn. Eleven of us tonight. At first light four go west, and one north.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-60.md:85:"Sixty-one. And a calfskin with five rings on it, and a line." She looked down at the roll on her knees. "I ruled that line off the edge of a hide, on a guess, with a strip of brass. I have stood at the end of it. I have been believed for twenty-three days, and I have not been wrong yet, and I am so tired that I could lie down on this rock and not get up."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-58.md:187:She sat back on her heels. Her face was quite white, and Cael had seen it so only once before, in a gallery, on the morning when she said that she could read. He thought that being believed, which had lain so heavy on her beside a lamp two nights ago, could hardly have been heavier than this.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-58.md:221:*Three hundred and seventh day. The fifth place, at the end of the line. A hundred and twenty-three. Vastin: twenty-eight; he was the mark that the round closed on. Oryn is seven days past her cairn. Eleven of us.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-52.md:125:"Letters. Two of the twenty-three." She drew a breath. "I have stood in a gallery under a town with the old town's tally-books open before me, a ladder's height of them. When one of those clerks wanted a year at the head of a page, and was short of room, he did not write its number out. He set down the first letter of each of its words. I must have seen it on forty leaves, and I took no account of it, since I was hunting for numbers written in full."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-52.md:247:"I held a seal for a long while," Vastin said at last. "I was the person who was sent to see that a filing stood. I told you so on the day that I came over a river; and I told you that this thing was buried deeper than anything that I had ever helped to put down." He lifted the red slip, by the width of a finger, and then the dark one. "I have believed, since a morning in a gallery, that it was all of one piece. That whatever was done was done by one hand, to one end, with nobody inside it to say a word against."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-53.md:173:It came to her as she blotted the line, and she set the pencil down. Being believed had been the thing that she wanted, for all of those years, as a person wants a meal; and a man under a town had told her what it was like when it arrived. He had said it with his eyes shut, in the hearing of five people. He had said that it was the end of being able to be wrong.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-53.md:181:It was a great deal heavier than not being believed had been. She had not expected that, and she thought that she ought to have.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-53.md:261:*Three hundred and fifth day. A shelf of shale under a step, four miles short of a gap in the hills. A hundred and twenty-three. Vastin: twenty-six. Oryn is five days past her cairn; Brom's week was out today, and she had her hands in a shin tonight.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-59.md:263:He had told every one of them on which side of something they stood, and it had been his whole trade. He had been good at it, and scrupulous, and he had believed for the most of a long service that scruple was the same as justice.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-51.md:341:*Three hundred and fourth day. The watch's post, on the shelf above the fourth place. A hundred and twenty-three. Vastin: twenty-five; he stood inside the line this morning, and it took what he carries, and gave it back. Oryn is four days past her cairn. She knit a shin in the ravine's bed and was grey for a quarter of an hour.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-49.md:79:"There's a salt road two days north of this, on the high side, that looks down into here from a long way off. I've had it from three carters and never believed one of them. They say that there's a hole in the middle of this ground where the country's water goes, and a span standing over the hole, sunk down in the dish so that you see only the top of it." He put his thumbs in his belt. "They call it that. The Sunken Span. I took it for a tale about a bridge with no river."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-49.md:259:*Three hundred and third day. A fold behind the brow, above the fourth place. No fire. A hundred and twenty-three. Vastin: twenty-four; he has said that he will not stand it, and it is entered. Oryn is three days past her cairn.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-51.md:309:Karis had lifted her head. She looked at him for the space of a breath, and then at the course, and she did not say anything at all. She had measured an inn yard once at forty-two strides in four people's hands, and at forty-one and a piece in his. He saw her remember it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-49.md:65:It ran away on either hand with the curve of the ground, a row of small dark uprights set a pace apart, like stitches along a hem. He let his eyes go along it, as he had once, and under his breastbone a thin held note began to ask at every one. He told it no. Something else in him had begun to ask as well, lower down, in the voice of a post that has been stood in its hole: *This far?* He told that no also, and looked at the middle.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-49.md:299:They went over the brow in the grey, nine of them, in the order that a captain had given the night before, with a big man in front; and Cael, at a captain's left shoulder, a stride back, saw the whole dish open below him without a shadow in it. The fence was a row of stitches. In the middle a grey thing stood on two legs with the last of the night under it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-50.md:7:Nine stood at the shelf's mouth, as a captain had set them. Brom was in front. Teague was behind his right shoulder, with the tall man and his spear on the one hand and Cael on the other. Then came Lira, and Teague's boy, and Karis; then Vastin on his stick; and last the broad man whom that crew called the bow, with his crossbow spanned.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-50.md:329:Karis put out her right hand and looked at the palm of it. The old man was last. He stopped with the stick already over, and the stiff leg not; and for the length of one breath he stood so, with a bar of iron between his feet, as a person stands in a doorway who has forgotten what he came into the room for. His eyes had gone to the air a little to one side of him.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-52.md:13:It was the trodden path that they took, round the outside of the iron. Six of them had walked down that dish to its middle the noon before, and Cael would have gone so again. But there were people in that file with something to lose at a stride, and a beast, and nobody proposed it. They went a quarter of the round with the bars at their right hand. On the far side Karis stopped the whole file for as long as it takes to lace a boot. She stood at a bar that was like every other, and looked back across the dish. From there the grey road on the bridge's back lay end-on to her, like a ruler seen along its edge; and when she turned about, the same line went out before her, over two ridges, to a square gap in the hills.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-52.md:183:Karis's bound hand came down flat upon her notebook.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-47.md:219:Nobody spoke; Lira was looking at the grass, and Karis had her notebook half out of her coat, and pushed it in again. The old man, a stride behind the place where Brom had been, stood with both hands on the head of his stick, and his eyes were on a pannier.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/packets/MOVEMENT-009.md:12:> **[Coordinator rulings after the Movement 2 audit, 2026-10-11 — binding; they override any plan text below that differs.]** (1) **Vastin's second paper is "the page": a COPY in his own hand** of one line from an old register ("—of the Architect: see the lower archive at Treswick, which holds it in full—"). It was never cut from anything and is never called a leaf; "the leaf" is the chamber record of Movement 6 only. (2) **The technician's sheet carries a Compact date (a registry year and a day in it) that is NEVER printed as figures** anywhere in this book; whoever reads it reads a date, and the page does not give its numbers. The office filed that sheet at the end of the second row of the records, and it lies there. (3) **The anomaly column:** on every survey abstract, in every hand, for six years, the entry is the one word *Total* — an entry, not a sum. (4) **The Fallow completion report** carries five strokes for a name and a count of six (locked Book 7), not five written names. (5) **What Vastin has told the six about the layer is four things only:** no key (it cannot be reached); the citation; the suppressed records; the technician's line. He has not defined "classification event" and does not know which event is meant. Nothing on the page says that menders, technicians or any office have tried to open it or know more. (6) **Speakers:** "What follows you," was Lira's; "She's right. Go and lie down, and don't take the stairs fast," was Oryn's. (7) **Deployment since the Line is thirty** at the end of Movement 2 (the Iron read at the landing on the 277th). Purse 118 marks. (8) **Teague's spear** has "he/his" on the page from chapter 7 (OWNER-pending #59 as defaulted). (9) Vastin has shown no Iron Wall, has no stated age, has asked only for a bed, and holds Karis's unsealed receipt. Cael has not touched the page and has not read the sheet's date closely.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/packets/MOVEMENT-009.md:14:> **[Coordinator rulings after the Movement 3 audit, 2026-10-11 — binding; they override any plan text below that differs.]** (1) **Deployment since the Line is thirty-two** at the end of Movement 3 (one Ember grain and one Wind burst away, both at the cut on the 279th). Ten fragments; the eleventh has not come; nothing came of standing two feet from Vastin's wall. Purse 138 marks. (2) **Iron Wall has been shown once** (the cut): a plane from a line struck with the stick, wall to wall, rooted — he may not lift a foot — loud to anything that hunts a Path, and it spent him to the floor. **Vastin's RIGHT knee** was bitten there; Oryn shut the holes and bound the joint with laths for a month from the 279th (stick; no kneeling, no running, no wall); he said "I cannot undertake" and has kept it to the letter. Brom's right foot, two toes, ten days from the 279th. (3) **The date:** Cael and Seln each know that the technician's sheet carries the day of Cael's Kindling. Neither has said or written it; neither knows what the other made of it; nobody has interpreted the sequence. No figures are ever printed, no span of years is computed, and there is **no second calendar or offset** (one registry reckoning only). Vastin does no date arithmetic and knows nothing of it. (4) **The records at the spring:** Karis has matched FOUR sign-groups to the hide and has given NO number of places; no fifth place has been described. The same stroke and bar stands in a ruled square on all four matched places' sheets (in the field: stake thirty-two and the fourth site's thirty-fours only). The remark "Behaves as device. Cannot be." is on the Stair's sheet in the fourth row; its hand is NOT identified. The instruments column reads true on every line. (5) **Seln has taught one thing, to Vastin** ("Draft nothing. Just look."). Cleon has been named (by Karis) and nothing more is known of him than her protected lines give. (6) **Oryn** went north from the fork on the 281st with the brown mule, her linen and her crossbow with one bolt; Millrace in three weeks; she owes Cael the reading ("the one thing I'm walking up that road owing"); the station term is written in the log. Her past as told at the fork: a house for the sick in a city inside the Line, the column for the patient's tier, a woman from the river sheds mended off the sheet; she has not been through a gate since. (7) **Loads:** boxes on Brom (good shoulder), Lira (both arms) and Cael; the tarred kit case on Vastin, taken up unasked at the fork-stone; Karis the flat and the hide, with the copied page in oilcloth in her coat. **Cael laid his hand on that page once, on the rise.** Vastin has walked beside Cael since the rise. (8) **Stillhounds:** a den that has learned a road runs to where a declaration ends (Karis's sixth line). The sixty yards of four-strand rope are still owed to the Lowmarch ferryman.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/packets/MOVEMENT-009.md:16:> **[Coordinator rulings after the Movement 4 audit, 2026-10-11 — binding; they override any plan text below that differs.]** (1) **Who is where on the 284th:** inside the iron door are Cael, Brom, Lira, Vastin and Karis, with three boxes, the tarred kit case, the flat and the hide; Cleon let them in, counted loads and not people, and shut the door. The unnamed yard clerk is outside; **Seln is above ground, NOT on the card or the count**, watching who comes to the door. Oryn is two gates north with the brown mule. (2) **The count:** Karis is the card's unnamed bearer; Cael, Brom, Lira and Vastin are the four hands, entered by count; the clerk asked nothing and wrote *four*. None of the four restricted names has been written anywhere; the asset-restriction is still pending. Cards are good only for the day written on them and are torn at each gate; Iron passes the second wall and Bronze the third by right, on a certificate that is read out and written. The way to the registry's yard is the load lane under the third wall and a cut of **twenty-two steps in five flights, a stride and a half wide, walled three men high with no door or window**; it never crosses the square. Cael has not set foot on the square or its steps and has not looked at the crown; Lira keeps the same discipline unasked. (3) **Cleon's terms** (given at the cook-house on the 283rd): all the sheets, in their places; they come down to him; no names (his is on the card and is never said aloud by him); **nobody tells him what the sheets are or how old until he has looked**; one lamp goes down with them, his, held where he says; no other carried light and no fire of anybody's. He has seen one drawn sheet and knows there are three places and fifty-two sheets, older than what the Compact found; he has not noticed Cael. **His Path has not been shown.** "Rune Path. Bronze." is all that is known of it, from Seln. (4) **Cael:** ten fragments; deployment thirty-two; the fourth and fifth self-readings were made on the 281st, round the still place and not in, uncounted; Tide feels only what flows and returned nothing ("a cord on a stone… *no length*"); he needs an instrument for what does not move, and owes Oryn the telling at the mill. Lira has read the log. (5) **Vastin and Cael:** Vastin has told him why he declined the second reading (the Ostrand case, causal; and a private relief at never learning what a plate would say); Cael has told him that the place was read twice and returned nothing, and nothing of what reads. Ground, not friendship. Vastin carries three sheets under one seal that he will not show; he has sat three times; his right knee is day five of thirty on the 284th (stick; no kneeling, running or wall) and he caught a slip on the steps with his left hand. Brom's two toes: five days left on the 284th. Lira's arm: day nineteen out of wool; her registry paper unshown; the ladder did not pull. (6) **The inn:** the long loft over the stable, by the yard half-door (the painted door has a book), for a week from the 282nd, pot at dusk, seven bowls; **purse 136 marks**. The outer gate counts feet on a bead-frame and writes no name. (7) **Stone:** the grey bridge, the registry's three lowest courses, the undercroft's end and the iron door's frame are one dark dry-laid stone older than the walls; Karis has seen it and stated no conclusion beyond "the bridge was first". The iron door has no lock or handle outside; the clerk knocks. (8) The sixty yards of four-strand were priced at the rope-walk and are to be bought going out.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/packets/MOVEMENT-009.md:18:> **[Coordinator rulings after the Movement 5 audit, 2026-10-11 — binding; they override any plan text below that differs.]** (1) **Eleven fragments; deployment since the Line thirty-two; purse 134 marks** at the end of Movement 5 (the night of the 290th). The eleventh, Iron-Wall-adjacent, came from Vastin in the alley's quiet minute; it did not fill, it *set*; its prompt is *This far?*; it has not been deployed and **Vastin has not been told** (Cael holds that as a debt he cannot yet pay; Lira has read the log). (2) **How acquisition lines are printed in this book:** the protected "Fragment eleven" and "Fragment twelve" units are CAEL'S OWN cataloguing of what he reads — set once, in italics, framed as his plain words; they are NOT printed as a system block and take no FRAGMENT ACQUIRED heading or code fence. (3) **The Deepstacks:** an iron door with a sliding bar inside; twenty-seven steps in three flights of nine; three galleries. Middle gallery about 110 strides, Cleon's sloped desk and fixed lamp at its end, a shelf above it with a jar, a tied bundle ("Paper. Not stone.") and the black oak plank's sacking; the fifty-two sheets on three tables, 11/29/12; the plank stands against the third table. Eastern gallery: the old town's tall tally-books. One CARRIED lamp, Cleon's, held "a hand to the left" (Cael holds it and has begun to put it where the eyes are about to go). The boxes go up at the clerk's bell and come down each morning on a fresh card; the clerk writes *The same* and *four*. (4) **Cleon** knows Karis's name (he heard Brom say it and has used it once); his own is on the card and has never been said aloud. He has shown his Path from OUTSIDE only (bear / carried / nothing; "They're orders."; "Same make."; the limit: it does not work on a river, a man or a tree, and costs his eyes for an hour). **His week runs from the 287th to the 293rd.** He has not noticed Cael and knows nothing of the alley or the mechanism. (5) **Karis** has fourteen of twenty-three letters and reads numbers and number-words only; seven kinds bear, twenty-three are carried, eleven are nothing; the bundle is tied and unread; Vastin's copied page is in her coat, unshown to Cleon. Her LEFT palm has seven stitches of Brom's under a strip of his shirt; she writes with the right. (6) **Vastin:** his right knee went again in the alley on the eleventh day of Oryn's thirty (a wall on it against her order, which he heard and asks that she be told); re-splinted with a slat from Brom's box, twice its size; on the night of the 290th he cannot stand or hold a bowl. He gave his name, rank and office aloud at the yard hatch on the 285th; nothing of it was written. He still knows nothing of what Cael carries. (7) **Brom:** the left (good) shoulder wrenched under a dray on the 286th and worse after the alley; he carries his box on the bound right shoulder, a debt entered for the mill's healers; the broker's offer (a guard's post inside the second wall) was answered "I'll think on it." and declined in fact. Lira: day twenty-five out of wool on the 290th; a bruised right shoulder; she drops from heights without the arm. (8) **Seln:** a registry clerk knows his face and voice (the price of a truthful answer at the hatch); he watches from the roof above the cut. His case is **SHUT** — never "unopened" (this edition's Book 6 opened it for the five) — and its contents are named nowhere in this book. He has seen one watcher three times (horseman's boots with the spur-rests cut away). (9) **The faction** (six, hooded, no faces, no Path opened) knows the loft and has shown that it knows; it saw Vastin's wall, a rock, a knife and a boy who stood still, and nothing of quiet ground or the fragments. Cael has ruled that the cut is not climbed at dusk again. The loft is paid to the 295th.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/packets/MOVEMENT-009.md:20:> **[Coordinator rulings after the Movement 6 audit, 2026-10-12 — binding; they override any plan text below that differs.]** (1) **Twelve fragments; deployment since the Line thirty-two.** The twelfth is **Rune-adjacent, from Cleon** ("given, not taken"), acquired at his "There," on the 291st; it gives single words (*goes*, *sits*, *bears*, *nothing*), reads made things only, and costs the eyes afterward. Its lines are Cael's own cataloguing, printed once in italics in Movement 6 and never reprinted. (2) **How it behaves:** it leans at every made thing, unbidden and OUTWARD, and Cael is learning not to look; it has never been used on a person, the crown or the leaf, and **never turned inward**. He refused the inward turn once, deliberately, until Oryn has been asked. **The first inward turn is unbidden, at Millrace, under Oryn's deep reading** (*still*, then *set*); the deliberate inward turn belongs to the plain's edge in Movement 9. (3) **Who knows the twelfth:** Lira, Brom, Karis, Seln and Cleon. **Not Oryn (owed at the mill, with the readings round the place); not Vastin** (who also still does not know the eleventh or the mechanism). **Cleon's warning** ("If that fragment ever reads something for you that shouldn't be *made* and reads it anyway — …") is known to Cael and Cleon only and is in no book; Cael tells nobody. (4) **The leaf** was read aloud once by Karis and heard by Karis, Cleon, Cael and Vastin; Lira, Brom and Seln read her copy; Oryn has not heard it. **Nobody quotes the leaf's wording again.** The reductions that may be said: *made*; *for a kind*; "the Architect" as a **title** (historical comparison only — "as old as the Architect" — never a present agent); "four hundred years" only where this packet's plan fixes it. (5) **The chronicle's line is marked and nobody speaks of it again**; the hole is never glossed. **Nobody joins the leaf to the lamps, sigils, plates, Arbiters or stations**; no *terminal*, *circuit*, *infrastructure*; no light, plate or instrument is said to attend, notice or want. (6) **Pole two was said aloud at the yard fire and is not in the log.** The line of march from it: "a site at the end of the line that's been quiet since they built the machine" — "I want to stand in the middle of it." (7) **Seln's case is on his chest, shut, contents unnamed; he keeps it "a while longer".** Closed chapter 18 says he has carried it twelve years; print no other count. He will not follow the cut boots again and expects them on the road east. (8) **State at the night of the 293rd:** purse **130**; the reserve sealed in the third box; loft and handcart to the 295th; sixty yards of four-strand still owed to the Lowmarch ferryman; Hesk's dividers and note shut in Hesk's book (never opened on a distance); Karis's fair copy in Cael's coat, the other in her notebook; the skin leaf under Cleon's jar and going to no counter; its back (a list of parts) unread — Cleon's work for the 294th. **Vastin: right knee day fourteen of thirty on the 293rd**, slat-bound, stick, no kneeling, running or Wall; he sleeps in the stable's end stall and rides the handcart. Karis's left palm stitched (Brom's seven); Brom neither arm above the elbow; Lira day twenty-eight out of wool on the 293rd. (9) **Oryn** watched a Kindling at her fourth gate, is two days behind, will not come late to the mill, and means to read Cael "as deep as she went", asked first.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/packets/MOVEMENT-009.md:22:> **[Coordinator rulings after the Movement 7 audit, 2026-10-12 — binding; they override any plan text below that differs.]** (1) **Twelve fragments; deployment thirty-two; purse 127 marks** at dawn on the 299th (130 less two for the rope, less one for meal at the mill; the rope debt to the Lowmarch ferryman is paid). Force was SEEN on the 298th (Teague, at a sunk salt cart: heel to palm in one line, a body's length, the recoil down the same line to the wrist) and **not acquired**; "Don't put it in front of anything that needs doing twice." is Teague's. (2) **The contract** (all Teague's words: "Ten of us on a wall's count. Your six and my four." … "Contract. Not more than that. I'll not have it called more." / "The wall's still the wall."): the ten are Cael, Lira, Brom, Karis, Seln, Vastin; Teague, the tall man with the spear, the bow (a broad man with a bad hip), the young one. **Neither healer is on it.** Shares and costs by the count; **the field is Teague's**; the reading is Karis's ("You'll be asked."). The wall is under a healer's month and is not stood before the month is out. **Teague's four know:** a survey was read at Treswick and gave a bearing; the four ages and "three days past the iron"; that an old man's wall stood in a cut. **They do NOT know** the mechanism, any fragment, the leaf or the list, the date, or who Vastin was. The tall man, the bow and the older healer (a lean old woman with a staff and a board) have not yet spoken to the six. Teague has not yet said "Noted". (3) **March order (Teague's):** Seln ahead; the tall man; Teague, with **Cael at his left shoulder a stride back**; Lira; the young one, watching her feet; Karis, the two healers and the mule in the middle; Brom; Vastin a stride behind him; the bow last. Four hand-signs, Lira's: flat hand *stand*; fist *seen*; two fingers *come on*; hand turned over empty *nothing*. (4) **Vastin: day twenty of thirty on the 299th**; Oryn's linen and two laths; stick; **he walks in the file from the ford** (he rode a tail-board on the level from the 295th to the 298th and walked the hills and the cut). He has said "Noted" twice (the grade; the knee) — it is Cael's word and he says so. He is on the contract's count as one of "your six"; he has not asked to join the six and nobody has spoken of it. He does not know the mechanism, the eleventh (his own), the twelfth, the two words or the date; he knows that he is not told and that it is "six people's". (5) **The grade:** three marks — a stroke alone (a box ruled and EMPTY; no first-tier site exists on any sheet); a stroke with one bar (the four places; stake thirty-two; the dead surveyor's book); a stroke with two bars (six sheets of a fifth place). "A mark is a grade … what he's *for* … how *old* the thing is" (Karis). **Ages counted from the office's first sheet for each place: the Fallow 61, the Stair 94, the Hall 138, the place behind the iron 206**; the fifth place has two unread signs for its figure ("older than two hundred and six"). **Oldest-last.** The office's run from the fourth place to the fifth is "three days past the iron, a little less"; its compass point and its figure in lengths have not been said aloud. The fourth line of the leaf's list: the mark, over *those who are kindled* and *what is set*, level; *three*. Karis has said none of the list's numbers aloud and five of its nine lines are unturned. **Nothing joins this to lamps, sigils, plates, Arbiters or stations**; "four hundred years" stood once, in Cael's log of the 298th, as his own inference — in this movement it may stand only where this packet's plan fixes it. (6) **Hesk's dividers** have been opened twice, by Karis, on Cael's loan; Cael has never opened them. The hide has a clean sheet pinned to its eastern edge with a prick at the divider's point and the pencil line ruled through it and a hand's breadth past. Stake thirty-two is in the tarred case, never driven. (7) **Oryn** knows the two self-readings, the eleventh and the twelfth; her ruling was "not on my plank". **Her finding** ("There's a place in you I can't read because it isn't *flow* …") is known to Cael and Lira. **Cael's own — the twelfth turned inward unbidden under her hands: *still*, then *set* — is told to NO ONE**, and the next word was not let come; Lira knows only that something unasked came and is waiting; the log holds four reasons and the ruling *hold*. Oryn has not heard the leaf ("Tell me on the road, if there's road. Not at my table."). Cleon's warning has "begun to lean". She walks east in the middle of the file with the mule, on nobody's count: three holds owed on her round; "I'm a stop on my own route." (8) **Bodies at dawn on the 299th:** Brom's left shoulder mended, the right re-tied, both arms to shoulder height; Lira's arm released ("It's an arm."); Karis's stitches out, linen on, "Write. Don't lean."; Teague's right wrist in linen under the leather. (9) **Watchers:** a horseman a mile behind on the 296th and a riderless horse in the western alders on the 297th; nothing seen east of the river; no face, name or base. (10) **Seln's case is "shut"** — never write "unopened" or "locked … unopened" (this packet's cutaway note says so and is overridden here); on his chest; contents unnamed; no count of years printed.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/packets/MOVEMENT-009.md:24:> **[Coordinator rulings after the Movement 8 audit, 2026-10-12 — binding; they override any plan text below that differs.]** (1) **Thirteen fragments; deployment since the Line THIRTY-THREE; purse 123 marks** on the night of the 305th. The thirty-third use was the read on the wet ledge at the seep (the 302nd). The thirteenth is **Force-adjacent, from Teague**, acquired at a stride at the Span's timbers (the 304th); its lines are Cael's own cataloguing, printed once in italics in Movement 8 and never reprinted. It does not ask; it **listens**. It has been lined dry once (the 305th: heel to a rib of rock, palm to a slab, every joint in one straight road) and **never let go**: "A trial; not counted." **Its first use belongs to this movement's pass** (on the second stack's brace, along the shelf; never at a person), and the cost comes back along the line to the place where the line leaves him — **the old three ribs, low on the left**. That use is a deployment and is counted. (2) **Who knows the thirteenth:** Cael; Lira; Brom; Karis and Seln by the log. **NOT Teague** (whose it was) **and NOT Vastin** (who also does not know the eleventh, his own): two unpaid debts, entered side by side, which cannot be paid without telling what is six people's. **Oryn has NOT been told** — she refused the count across the iron on the 304th, and Cael means to tell her "when she'll hear it"; "Ribs. May I?" … "That's a line. That's not yours." / "It's mine now." is where she meets it. Teague thanked Cael for keeping his left at the timbers and does not know what Cael came away with. (3) **Teague's right arm is in linen to the shoulder on a slat and the hand does not shut**; Oryn: "A third time on that arm and I'll set it in a splint." He goes up the pass's stair LEFT-handed. **The tall man's spear is HIS** (he / him; this packet's "reverses her spear" is the source's pronoun and is overridden — OWNER #59). The tall man and the older healer have still not spoken to the six; the broad man's first word to them was "Aye" on the 305th. **The broad man:** right shin (his good leg) broken at the Span, knit by Oryn, boarded, walking on a stick cut from the watch's trestle, at the tail; his crossbow's stock is split by a bolt. **The young one** went over the iron when Teague looked at him ("Four."), held his feet inside the line by watching Lira's boots, "goes wherever he is looked at, a half-beat before he has seen what is there", and nods with his whole head; he is still unnamed (OWNER-pending #54 governs the one saying of his name). (4) **What Teague's four know:** five people stood whole inside the line; a boy kept a captain's left with a stick; nothing of the mechanism, any fragment, the leaf, the lamps page, the slips, the date, or who Vastin was. **The doctrine "behind it" has NOT been told to them; no wall has been drilled or stood.** Vastin entered on the 303rd that he will not stand a wall before his month is out (he stood inside the line at the Span instead, and what he carries went and came back "to the grain"); the 306th is **day twenty-seven of thirty**. (5) **The watch** (seven, hooded, faceless: two crossbows, four staves, one low practitioner) went east through the gap a day ahead, carrying one broken bow; they saw silent Iron take bolts, silent Force shift timber, and five people stand inside the line; they saw nothing work inside. Nobody died at the Span. **Seln** looked at the square notch from a mile on the evening of the 305th: a dry bed going in, the only way through for five miles either hand, no fire, nobody on either wall, not a heel-mark — "I looked at it." — and is to look again from nearer at first light before Teague moves. (6) **Pages, and who holds each:** Karis has the torn ruled leaf with the lamps finding ("They fail the way a hundred lamps fail when one wire's cut. Not the way a hundred souls fall silent.") in the back of her notebook, and under her initial "*Second page: nothing entered. Not for want of a pencil.*"; Cael has the folded fair copy of the chamber leaf in Hesk's book; the technician's sheet lies at the foot of the second row in the flat, its day unsaid and unwritten (only Cael and Seln have done that sum, each alone). **Nobody has laid one of these across another; Cael added no page of his own; Vastin's sentence stopped twice.** The two sealed slips are re-pinned under the knot of the oldest row; Vastin knows both seals and has named neither ("That's a crack." … "The Compact," … "Yes. Part of it."). (7) **The fifth place:** a thumbnail ring round the divider's prick on the clean sheet, uninked until corded; "three days past the iron, a little less" (one walked on the 305th); its two corner signs are a year written by its first letters in the old town's count, which Karis worked alone and said once, in the close of the 305th: "Third-tier, documented four hundred years back — as old as the record, as old as the Architect, as old as the machine. The first one they made, or near it." **In this movement "four hundred years" may stand only where this packet's plan fixes it; "the Architect" not at all unless the plan prints it.** Every row on every sheet leans the one way, along the brass, east (one unbidden glance of the twelfth, entered by Karis as Cael's and unchecked). At the Span a course of fifty-two signs counted as fifty-one in Cael's hands, twice (ruling: hold) — the instruments' trend. (8) **Oryn** is five days past her cairn on the 305th (the cairn is at noon on the 300th; six on the 306th, seven on the 307th, eight on the 308th); three holds still owe her a visit and have had word ("a week, or more"). She has not heard the leaf. She knit a shin on the 304th (asked first, answered in a word) and had her hands in it again on the 305th under the older healer's binding. **Nobody has yet died under her hands.** (9) **Bodies otherwise:** Brom, two bruises in "a week of colours", both arms sound to the shoulder, carrying the broad man's pack; Cael, a welt on the left collar-bone and the ribs speaking; Karis, the left palm in linen ("Write. Don't lean."); Lira, the arm whole. **Load:** the mule has the three boxes, the flat, meal, Oryn's pannier and canvas; Vastin the tarred case (cord, mallet, stake thirty-two, never driven); Cael Hesk's book with the dividers shut in it (opened only ever by Karis). (10) **Seln's case is "shut"**, on his chest, contents unnamed, no count of years.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/packets/MOVEMENT-009.md:65:  - Vastin goes down. Karis sits with her bound hand on his shoulder. Teague kneels by his own dead. **"A wall keeps everyone behind it, and one of mine wasn't."** At the far mouth Cael understands that the line was not drawn in ink.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/BOOK_MAP.md:44:- **Body ledger at close:** Brom's forearm is bound (bolt, pass) and he has a week of colours. Karis's left hand is bound and will scar ("It'll leave a line"). The broad man's leg is knit and his shoulder bound. Teague's arm faces a splint warning. Vastin is on day 29 of 30. Cael's ribs "will carry. They'll hurt."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/BOOK_MAP.md:56:4. **KEEP — The anomaly (168th).** The yard paces forty-two strides for everyone and "forty-one and a piece" for Cael, three times. The Ternhall edge reads a hair short in his hand and Hesk's edge a hair long. Karis's ruler sentence is said twice: a rule, then not a rule. Logged: "Claim: something. Evidence: a cord, two edges, a hair. Ruling: hold."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/BOOK_MAP.md:62:10. **KEEP — The handover (277th evening)**, in Vastin's stated order. First, the records: a courier's flat of six years of survey abstracts, the anomaly column ("Total"), six rows, "More than four", and the completion report as the last sheet. Second, the page: "—of the Architect: see the lower archive at Treswick, which holds it in full—". Third, the technician's sheet: the deep-layer line and the brush bar; Karis copies it and writes "not read". Seln goes still and does not say the sum. Vastin's vertigo; Karis owns the response. Cael's hand stays a hand's width from the page. Nobody says *west*.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/BOOK_MAP.md:65:13. **KEEP — The spring (280th).** Three readers: Karis for pattern, Seln for omission, Vastin for procedure. The office's instruments. **"Behaves as device. Cannot be."** Karis stops her lamps sentence with the ruler rule. **The grade:** the same stroke and bar on all four sites, the fourth's twice the size. "Two copies of one ignorance." Vastin's three reflexes: directive, classification, "This has to go up". The second row counts a sheet short in Cael's hands. Seln sets the technician's sheet before Cael without a word. Cael does the sum (the date matches his Kindling day; "six years" is the office's survey-record rounding; no numeric span since Kindling is computed [B7-reconciled 2026-10-11: amendment 7]), does **not** write it down, and puts the sheet back. "You keep drafting orders." / "Habit." / "Out here the habit's the tell. Draft nothing. Just look." Cleon is named for the first time.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/BOOK_MAP.md:74:22. **KEEP — The alley (290th, dusk).** Six faceless attackers: four above with a crossbow, two below with staves, one of the four a signaller on the landing. Vastin roots the cut wall to wall ("there is no round"). Nobody declares. Brom with a rock, Lira's drop, Cael still at the plane's foot ("the reason"), Seln's weighted cord from the roof. The cache was on the roof the whole time. They leave; none taken, no faces. Costs: Karis's left hand (a step's edge), bound by Brom with thread and teeth because no healer inside the gate would touch it; Vastin spent, the knee again. **The Iron-Wall notice** (§10). "I put the wall in front of it anyway. I'd like that entered" / "It's entered." Not told. The log (§10).
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/BOOK_MAP.md:83:31. **KEEP — Millrace (298th evening–299th dawn).** "Eleven off the ridge." / "I know." The two healers look at each other and then at Brom's shoulder. Vastin is ordered to stand ("Nineteen days." / "Then it's eleven more.") and takes the order without walking it back. Oryn's terms restated. "Sixth chair." / "On my terms. Which are the only kind." **She asks each patient first ("May I?").** The readings: shoulder, arm ("Once is once more than I priced"), Karis's hand ("Who sewed this."), the knee ("It held in a cut"; "Noted" / "That's new." / "It's a useful word." / "It's his."). **The deep reading**, with consent. **Rune turns inward unbidden: *still*, then *set*.** Withheld. Oryn's finding (§10). The leaf is offered: "Tell me on the road, if there's road. Not at my table." Twelve and a mule at dawn.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/BOOK_MAP.md:88:36. **KEEP — The pass (306th).** Seln's reconnaissance (the shelf, the rig, nine or more, three crossbows). "It's through." The doctrine is told to Teague's four; **the young one's whole-head nod**. **The tall man [B7-reconciled 2026-10-11: M1 audit; OWNER-pending #59] speaks to the six for the first time.** [B7-reconciled 2026-10-11: amendment 6] The wall is set as a lean-to slant. Brom's forearm is bolted before the wall goes up. The pine and stack fall down the plane and close the mouth behind them. The scree is swept clean. Seln climbs. The broad man is hit at the slant's high edge; the young one drags him and is **killed by the sixth volley**, a stride outside the plane. Oryn's hands on him: the first time they come off with nothing done. The name is said once. [OWNER-pending #54] Seln goes over the lip at the seventh volley. **Cael's first use of Force** on the second stack's brace (stone, along the shelf); the recoil stops in the ribs. Teague takes the stair left-handed. The tall man [B7-reconciled 2026-10-11: M1 audit; OWNER-pending #59] reverses her spear. The team breaks east along the ridge, none taken. Vastin goes down; Karis sits with her hand on his shoulder. Teague kneels. "A wall keeps everyone behind it, and one of mine wasn't." At the far mouth Cael understands the line was not drawn in ink.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/BOOK_MAP.md:229:| 290 | The alley; **Karis's hand cut**; fragment eleven |
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/BOOK_MAP.md:249:Running counts that must reconcile: **Lira's arm** "fifteen days" at 280, "twenty-five" at 290, "twenty-seven" at 292 and "thirty-three" at 298 (counting the 266th as day 1). **Karis's hand** is eight days old on the 298th. **Cash:** 96 → 76 → 116 → 118 → 138 → 136 → 130 → 127 → 123. **Oryn's route:** count "days past the end of her road" from the cairn on the 300th (§12 C3).
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/BOOK_MAP.md:324:- **The alley (M5).** Twenty-two steps in five flights, a stride and a half wide, walled three men high with no doors. There are four at the top (a crossbow and a signaller on the landing) and two coming up from the yard gate with staves. The plane sits at the second flight's top step, wall to wall; Cael stands two feet below it. Brom has a loose step-stone. Lira drops from the second flight. Seln's cord comes from the roof onto the signaller's arm and the rail. It lasts "a minute and some". The opponent's plan was built for a declaration and meets a wall and a rock. Costs: Karis's hand; Vastin past his limit; Brom's good shoulder used wrong.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/BOOK_MAP.md:380:- Karis (B7 edition `chapter-52.md:311`): `A measurement is a fact about a ruler, not about the hand that held it.` [B7-reconciled 2026-10-11: amendment 8]
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/BOOK_MAP.md:395:- **S5:** "You keep drafting orders." / "Habit." / "Out here the habit's the tell." … "Draft nothing. Just look." / "Two copies of one ignorance." Close: "Six years," Karis said. "They measured all of it. They graded it — the sites are ranked by something, and it's the same something in the kit, and I can't read either one." … "Everything I can't read points at the same place." / "Treswick," / "The archive under it. Cleon." / "Who's Cleon?" / "The only historical-Path practitioner registered west of the Line. If the language is anywhere, it's under his hands. A day and a half." … "And then I find out whether I've been right for five years or wrong for five years, and I don't know which I'm more afraid of." [OWNER-pending #52]
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/BOOK_MAP.md:397:- **S7 log:** "Ran the reading on myself again. Same nothing at the center. I think the reading can't do it — you can't measure a stillness with a thing that only feels movement. I need an instrument for what doesn't move. Karis says there's a man in Treswick who reads how things are built. Maybe that's the instrument. Maybe I'm about to hand a stranger the one question I've never let anyone hold. Tomorrow. The walls are close enough now to have a color." ("color" stands in the log, per ruling 19.) Also: "You were the instrument," … "I was the error." / "I declined to read you a second time. You know that. What you do not know is why."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/BOOK_MAP.md:400:- **S10:** "Older than [UNBOUND]," / "I know that hand." / "Being believed — " … "It's the end of being able to be wrong." / "Give me a week." / "I'm sorry," / "Don't be sorry," Seln said. "Be nobody." Log: "Karis has been looking for this room her whole life. Cleon's been sitting in it for thirty years waiting for her. Vastin tried to open a door with his rank and nearly closed all of them — an Archmarshal's worth nothing here, which he's taking about as well as I took [SHATTERED]. Down here the stone's older than the Compact, so the Compact doesn't reach. First place inside walls I've ever stood where nobody can file me, and it's a hole under a city, and it's because everything down here is older than the machine that files. Karis says give her a week. I've got a box to carry and a promise to keep about a station upstairs. I can wait."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/STATE_LEDGER.md:91:| Karis | Holds a bearing, an index and a language she cannot read. Her rule: "A measurement is a fact about a ruler, not about the hand that held it." |
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/STATE_LEDGER.md:148:- **The kit as a tool (flag; as amended by the audit's five line fixes):** the cord is tarred hemp no thicker than a pencil, with a knot at every fifth stride and a brass eye whipped into one end; it is run out to its last knot, **the fourth**, and **"Four knots is a length"** (four five-stride intervals; no yardage is given anywhere); odd strides are taken with a notched slip of ash "a stride long by the dead man's reckoning". Karis cut a peg of peeled hazel to the stake's length. The inn yard, from the plank's left-hand post to a scratch on the stone trough by the gate: **"one length and four knots and two strides"**, forty-two; for Cael the slip laid once from the fourth knot leaves room for only half of it again. Karis measured the cord against the Ternhall brass on the 116th and again on the 170th: thirty-one lays "and a piece under half" to the first knot, unchanged, and the second, third and fourth knots the same.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/STATE_LEDGER.md:149:- **The edges (flag):** each strip's length pricked into the deal with a needle. In Cael's hand the Ternhall strip stops a hair short of its prick and Hesk's goes a hair past; the gap is one of Karis's own hairs, which she keeps in her notebook. True in the other four hands, and true again when he takes his hand away.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/STATE_LEDGER.md:164:**Cael.** Nineteen. **Ten fragments.** **Deployment since the Line: twenty-nine** (twenty-seven at entry; the read on frozen ground at the calf, the 140th; the read on river ice, the 161st). All ten tried in the cold term and written up as *The ten, by use*: Wind (the lock cannot be shortened, only placed), Ember (the burn), Shadow (held, not opened), Compression (both heels; ground worth a load), Pressure (seen coming; narrow and deep; comes back), Blade (short; the steel pays), the read (ground or air; a pace and a half; the eyes), Storm (one lane; ten counts; a fourth untried), Anchor (a point held to thirty; ten, twelve and fourteen feet), Tide (asked of Lira; told no; not used). Shadow used twice in all and shut. Anchor's counted uses still two. Tide: Oryn twice, himself three times, nobody else. **The still place** has not been read or touched. **The instruments:** on the 168th the kit's cord (four knots to a length) measured the inn yard at forty-two strides for Karis, Lira, Brom and Seln and at forty-one and a piece for him, three times; the Ternhall brass read a hair short in his hand and Hesk's a hair long; he has touched none of the three since; his ruling is *hold*; Oryn has been told and has no answer. Nobody has offered a cause. He lost to Teague's spear three to none (148th) and three to one (158th). He has entered Teague as the better captain. He has not ruled on Karis's case. He has written *West, and inside*. Body: sound; knuckles skinned on the 140th; near sight lost for an hour on the 140th and for an evening on the 161st, and back.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/STATE_LEDGER.md:194:- **The anomaly:** the cord reads forty-two strides in four hands and forty-one and a piece in Cael's, three times; Ternhall is a hair short and Hesk a hair long only in his hand; the tools are sound; Karis has preserved one hair; ruling `hold`; no cause or inference (`manuscript/chapter-05.md:3-155,259`). Oryn knows these facts and has offered no theory (`manuscript/chapter-06.md:189-201`).
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/STATE_LEDGER.md:215:- **The three papers.** (1) **The records:** a seat-courier's flat of waxed leather; six taped rows, a year to a row, of survey abstracts measured in lengths, knots and strides, with the dead surveyor's four-sign index printed at their heads; an **anomaly column** in which every sheet, in every hand, for six years carries the one word *Total* (an entry, not a sum; no figure); "More than four" (Karis, counting distinct sign-groups; she gives no number); the Fallow completion report is the last, loose sheet, and carries **five strokes for a name and a count of six** (locked Book 7 ch40, ch43), not five names. (2) **The page:** a COPY in Vastin's own hand of one line from an old register, carried in oilcloth in his coat: "—of the Architect: see the lower archive at Treswick, which holds it in full—". "The Architect" is a title, not a name, and a word Karis has never met in any stratum. It is never called a leaf; **the leaf has not appeared**. (3) **The technician's sheet:** the two protected lines with a date between them, a brush bar across what followed, and Karis's *not read*. **The date is a Compact date, a registry year and a day in it, and is never printed as figures** (the source gives none; no owner decision is needed). The office had filed the sheet at the end of the second row and it lies there again.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/STATE_LEDGER.md:242:- **The flat (repaired):** a seat-courier's flat of waxed leather, "as broad as a man's shoulders and no thicker than two fingers", stamped *BY SEAT COURIER. BY HAND.*; six rows, a year to a row, the newest uppermost, each in a tape; a pocket in the lid. **The abstracts:** printed forms; four signs and a number at the head (the Stair's sheet has the dead surveyor's four signs and thirty-two); a column for the day in the office's reckoning; a measure given in lengths, knots and strides; and a narrow right-hand column whose printed head says that it is for **the anomaly**. **In every clerk's hand, in every one of six years, that column holds the single word *Total*, and nobody has written a second word beside it.** There is no figure and no sum. Karis counts more than four different groups of signs in the newest row, on her fingers, and will not say a number until she has checked it in daylight. No sheets are counted.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/STATE_LEDGER.md:244:- **The sheet (repaired):** thin stock, newer than the rest, a printed head, "a quick sloping hand"; a technician's return ("When a man who services the stations finds a thing that he was not sent to find, he is bound to write it up on such a sheet, on the day, and send it in"). **The office had filed it with the surveys, at the end of the second row; Vastin carried it in the lid to keep it dry; Karis has laid it back at the foot of the second row.** Its date is a registry year and a day in it, in the office's reckoning; **no figures are printed anywhere.** The bar is as broad as a thumb, laid on both sides, cracked in drying; Vastin has seen such a bar "on perhaps a dozen papers in forty years" and never with initials. He does not know which classification event is meant.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/STATE_LEDGER.md:266:**Vastin.** No office, no seal, no rank; he left the seal in a tray "some months" ago and walked three hundred miles. **No age is stated.** A bad knee; raw hands; a left hand that will not lie flat. **No Iron Wall has been shown or named.** He has handed over three things in his own order and holds Karis's receipt in his coat. He has said: that he holds no office and has no sanction; that the layer cannot be opened ("There is no key"), on the authority of the office's own page; that the one line which should name its maker cites a title and sends its reader to Treswick's lower archive (his own copy of it); that nothing of his follows him and the Stair's rim is not his; that the pending paper is unchanged; why he did not take it to a council. He knows the six by name. **He does not know** the mechanism, what Cael carries, the instruments' behaviour in Cael's hand, Karis's case or the sites (beyond hearing Brom say "four on the hide" and Lira name the Stair), who held the abstracts, which classification event the sheet means, or what is under the bar. He has not weighed the sheet's date. He has not read the flat's last row through, and nothing has been said of a directive or an objection. He has not asked to join and nobody has spoken of it. He was sent to bed, began to argue twice, took four stairs fast and the rest slowly; whether that was the order or the knee is not settled.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/STATE_LEDGER.md:272:**Objects and money.** **A hundred and eighteen marks in hand**, unchanged. The reserve sealed. Owed: **sixty yards of four-strand rope** to the ferryman (the cordage is on the western landing; his one hauling rope has two yarns gone). **The hurdle is paid.** **The letter to Ephram is sent.** The three boxes are at the hearth's end, roped. Stake thirty-two has still never been driven. On Karis's table: the flat (the sheet inside it), the broker's paper beside it, Seln's wand beside that, and the copied page at the far end.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/STATE_LEDGER.md:299:- **The slip:** *Clearance. The cut below the shoulder hold, on the border road, two days west.* Twenty. "Ours" (Karis), "Ours" (Brom); five hands; Pike's sixth line: *And a healer; of her own road; at her own price.* Brom holds the thumbed slip.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/STATE_LEDGER.md:301:- **The cut (the author's staging stands; BOOK_MAP §8 allows it).** A hundred strides, a stride of ice down the middle, scree on the south, a low north bank, the sink fifty-five yards below it. Eight hounds (five seen, three hidden). The den had learned the road: it runs to where a declaration ENDS. Karis, Oryn and the mule were at the foot; Vastin, a stride behind Brom, stepped out and **declared Iron Wall** — a plane across the road from a line struck with his stick, wall to wall, two feet from Cael, rooted (he may not lift a foot), loud, spending him to the floor. Brom's rock (twice), Lira's knife with the left hand holding, Seln in the heap; five dead; **three went through the rotten ice of the sink and did not come up**. Costs: Brom's right foot (two toes, ten days from the 279th); **Vastin's right knee** (four holes shut by Oryn after "May I?" / "Yes"; the joint bound with laths; **a month** from the 279th: stick, no kneeling, no running, no wall). Karis's sixth line: *It goes to where a declaration ends, if it has learned that the declaring moves.* Twenty paid; **purse 138**.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/STATE_LEDGER.md:303:- **The spring (the 280th).** Six rows counted twice by two people. Five columns on every abstract: the day in the registry's reckoning; the station (eight points); the measure; *Instruments* ("Cord true. Edge true. Level true." on every line for six years); the anomaly (*Total*). **Karis matches FOUR sign-groups to the hide by their measures (three alike to the stride, one double) and gives no number of places; no fifth place is described** (held for the 298th). The measures have not changed in six years. The remark *Behaves as device. Cannot be.* stands in a ruled space on the Stair's sheet in the fourth row; **its hand is not identified**; the space for remarks is gone from the next year's printing. **The grade:** a ruled square at the head of every sheet with the same stroke and bar on all four matched places, drawn no larger on the place whose measure is double; in the field it is on stake thirty-two and on the fourth site's thirty-fours only. Karis: a class, unread; "Two copies of one ignorance." Nobody says who set it or why. Karis stopped her lamps sentence with the ruler line and did not finish it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/STATE_LEDGER.md:304:- **The date.** Seln drew the technician's sheet from the foot of the second row; the row counted a sheet short in Cael's hands; Seln set the sheet before him without a word. **Cael read the date and knew it for the day of his own Kindling** (the first line of his file). Only Cael and Seln know; neither has said or written it; neither knows what the other made of it; nobody has interpreted the sequence. Karis knows both have read the sheet. **No figures are printed, no span is computed, and there is NO second calendar** (the author's stations'-year offset was cut: the series calendar is the owner's). The sheet lies again at the foot of the second row.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/STATE_LEDGER.md:308:- **The rise (the 281st, near noon):** Treswick a day's walk off — a bluff in a river loop, three walls in tiers, a pale steady light on the crown that Cael does not look at. **Cael laid his hand on the page** (the author's; kept); it stays in its oilcloth in Karis's coat.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/STATE_LEDGER.md:331:- **The abstracts, read in daylight.** Karis counted every row twice and had each counted again; **no figure for any row is printed**. **She gives no number of places.** She sorted the sheets by their sign-groups, matched **four** to the hide by their measures (three alike to the stride, one double), and will not call the unmatched groups places until a sign can be read (17:27–39). **Five columns:** the day, in the registry's reckoning; the station (eight points); the measure; *Instruments*; and the anomaly. No radius of the four has changed by a stride in six years; four surveyors by their figures; three clerks' hands. The fourth column reads *Cord true. Edge true. Level true.* on every sheet. The three oldest rows have a ruled space at the foot headed *Remarks*, empty but once: **the Stair's sheet in the fourth row carries the protected four words, in a hand that is none of the three clerks'; Karis laid the dead man's ledger beside it and will not swear to the hand either way.** From the next year's printing the space is gone. Brom counts the three findings on his fingers (17:61, 17:77, 17:139).
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/STATE_LEDGER.md:337:- **The loads from the fork (r1).** Boxes on Brom (left shoulder), Lira (both arms through the beckets) and Cael. Seln carries none: he goes ahead with his hands empty. **The tarred case was left leaning on the fork-stone; Karis stopped forty strides up; Vastin went back for it unasked, after Oryn was out of sight, hooked its strap with his stick without kneeling, and slung it above his sound leg.** He told Cael so at once: "I waited until she could not be put to the trouble of forbidding it a third time." "It is a cord, a mallet and one stake."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/STATE_LEDGER.md:349:**Cael.** Nineteen. **Ten fragments; no eleventh.** **Deployment thirty-two.** Left palm stung by Ember. He ruled on a job and has made no vow. **He has read the technician's date, laid it against the first line of his own file, and knows it for the day of his own Kindling. He has not written it, has told nobody, and has put no verb to it.** He said "You do now" to Vastin at the hold. He has written in the log, at Oryn's asking, that inside Treswick he does not go near the station. **He has laid his hand on the page once, on the rise, and Karis has it again.** He has seen the light on the crown and turned from it. He carries a box. He asked "Who's Cleon?" and has the name.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/STATE_LEDGER.md:355:**Karis.** Ember; Iron, Rank Three; one point set in the peats. She holds the flat (six rows, counted twice by two people and re-taped, the technician's sheet at the foot of the second), the completion report, the page in its oilcloth inside her coat, her copy, the hide, the kit's ledger and her notebook. She knows: four sign-groups matched to the hide and others matched to nothing, **no number given and none of the others called a place**; five columns; no measure changed in six years; the instruments true; the remark, and that she will not swear to its hand; the box, the same on all four; what an abstract is. She cannot read the index or the box. She began a sentence about lamps and stopped it with her rule, verbatim. She heard "I've read it" from Cael as she had from Seln and asked neither. She named the archive and Cleon. Six findings on her stillhound leaf.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/STATE_LEDGER.md:380:- **Paper custody:** Karis has the flat under one arm and the hide under the other; inside the flat are six counted/re-taped rows with the original technician's sheet restored to the end of row two, plus the completion report and her copy; Vastin's copied page remains in its oilcloth inside her coat; Cael laid his hand on it once, on the rise, and gave it back (coordinator ruling: the author's beat is kept) (`manuscript/chapter-18.md:141–149,155–157`; `manuscript/chapter-19.md:207,271–291`). Karis also has the dead surveyor's ledger/notebook; Seln's case remains shut on his own strap (`manuscript/chapter-17.md:183–187`; `manuscript/chapter-18.md:39–41`).
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/STATE_LEDGER.md:388:**Movement 3 CLOSED (2026-10-11).** Same-author repair r1 (ch13, ch16–19; ch14–15 untouched): the eleven coordinator-applied fixes kept and their joins smoothed (Seln on the north bank beside the factor; Cael had not known that he would say "You do now,"; Vastin names the town); no number of places and no fifth place (Karis counted groups, not places; four matched to the hide; the other sheets' corners turned down, uncounted and undescribed); the second calendar cut (Seln knew the day because he had copied that line of Cael's file twice; Cael laid the date he knows against the sheet's, a figure at a time, twice); three tracked passages re-ordered; the ruler line verbatim at the stopped lamps sentence; the remark's hand unidentified; Brom counts the spring's three findings on his fingers; the loads and the closing file made to meet Movement 4 (**Vastin took the kit case against the letter of Oryn's "you're not to" and told Cael so at once; Oryn does not know**; he walks a shoulder off Cael's left from a dozen strides down the rise). Sol recheck: CLOSE WITH LINE FIXES; all tracked passages RESOLVED; every binding ruling borne out; the settled rulings (the cut's staging, thirty-two, Oryn's past, the hand on the page) confirmed in the surviving prose. Two fixes applied by the coordinator: ch16 "Two days ago, at a board" (was three); ch18 Seln "has spent fifteen years among the seat's paper" (he copied for twelve). The "New canon minted" and "State at movement end" sections above were refreshed from the post-repair author report. Final: 34,751 prose words (about 2% over; accepted — the added scene carries the loads and the seam); gates 0; overlap 0 unprotected (16 protected). Purse 138 marks; deployment thirty-two; day 281, near noon, going down off the rise toward Treswick.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/STATE_LEDGER.md:399:- **Vastin:** "I have papers I will not show. I want that said once, so that nobody asks twice." (three sheets under one seal, in oilskin, beside Karis's receipt). He has certified Treswick on four occasions. His cutaway: "the first honest district". He has sat three times; no age; no wall; **the right knee is day five of thirty on the 284th**; he came down twenty-two steps and caught one slip with his left hand.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/STATE_LEDGER.md:400:- **Seln's report (the 282nd night):** the old town stands on a quarry cut from inside; the lower archive is behind one iron door in the undercroft beneath the registry's yard; a practitioner registered under historical studies has standing entry by the week and may have matter brought down; **"Rune Path. Bronze."**; one clerk keeps the provision's book, which has had one name in it; the form has a line for the matter, a line for its bearer (one), and **a line for hands, entered by count**. Protected: Karis "Four hands." / Seln "Four… By count." / Lira "Four hands," … "I've two." / Cael wrote: *Entered by count. That's the door.* The S8 close verbatim (Cael; Karis), with OWNER-pending #53 ("four years and a hearing") as Cael's rough spoken figure, never repeated.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/STATE_LEDGER.md:402:- **The 284th.** Seln is NOT on the count (a clerk might know his face; somebody must watch the door). **Vastin said "Yes" before a mouth was open** and is the fourth hand. Karis is the card's unnamed bearer; **Cael, Brom, Lira and Vastin are the four hands**. Both upper keepers counted ("One, and four") and tore a corner; the way to the yard goes by the load lane under the third wall and a cut of **twenty-two steps in five flights, a stride and a half wide, walled three men high with no door or window** — never across the square. **Cael did not set foot on the square or its steps and did not look at the crown; Lira kept the same discipline unasked** and walked between him and each alley's end. **Lira's ladder did not pull** ("It's a hill with a tax on it"); protected: "Four hands," (Lira) / "Four." (Cael) / "Right." (Lira); the S9 log verbatim. Her registry paper stayed in her coat. **The yard clerk (unnamed) asked nothing and wrote *four*.** None of the four restricted names has been written; the asset-restriction is still pending.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/STATE_LEDGER.md:403:- **The iron door** is low, of plates laid like slates, with no lock or handle on the outside; the clerk knocks; Cleon opened it from within, counted loads, not people, and shut it behind them. Inside: Cael, Brom, Lira, Vastin and Karis with three boxes, the kit case, the flat and the hide; the clerk and Seln outside. "The tiers closed, and the dark opened."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/STATE_LEDGER.md:425:- **Cleon, as seen.** Not tall; grey hair cut short and badly; a coat the colour of dust; round shoulders; square pale hands with the nails pared to the quick; red-rimmed wet eyes, which he keeps shut while he eats; a voice "like a hinge". He looks at a sheet from its foot upward. He eats at the counter's end by the yard door at noon and goes down within the hour. **Cael knows him for Cleon at the counter** (the name said at a spring) and reads it once, written, at the head of the card in Lira's hands on the 283rd; nobody says it aloud. Karis told him at the counter what the floors are (the protected line: fused, grey, no join; three of them; fifty-two sheets), so his questions after it are of method only (rubbed or drawn; what light; which edge north; how far apart). **His terms (mine, one amended by the audit):** not on a counter; all of them, not a choice; they come down to him and he does not come up; his card, written at the counter (the matter; its bearer, one; hands, a figure), good for the next day only, at the second bell; no names given or wanted; not a word of what she thinks they are until he has looked; **one lamp goes down with them, his, held where he says, and no other carried light or fire of anybody's** (a light already burning below is not excluded). He pushed her sheet back without touching it. He left by the street door and stopped a full stride beyond it for three breaths. **He never looked toward the yard.** The Path is not shown; only that he rests his eyes.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/STATE_LEDGER.md:426:- **The provision, as Seln reports it.** The oldest and shortest leave in the registry's book: a practitioner registered under historical studies has standing entry to a lower archive, by the week, renewed at the yard door, and may have matter brought down to him. When matter is brought the form has a line for the matter (described), a line for **the matter's bearer, who is one**, and a line for hands. **Karis goes as the bearer**; nobody's name is on the card but the practitioner's. One clerk has kept the provision's book at the yard door for many years, with a single name in it. The exposure: the form neither requires nor forbids the clerk to ask who a bearer or a hand is.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/STATE_LEDGER.md:439:**Where everyone is.** Five inside the iron door: Karis, Brom, Lira, Vastin, Cael, with Cleon at the stair's head holding one lamp. The clerk is outside it with his lantern. **Seln is above ground, on no card, where he can see who comes to look at the door**; nobody saw him on the climb. Oryn and the brown mule are at the second of her five gates, north along the hill-foot, with three gates, the ford and Millrace ahead and her day in hand spent. Teague's five are east of the river (not on the page).
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/STATE_LEDGER.md:447:**Karis.** Ember; Iron, Rank Three; shut since the fork. She has said, on the evidence of her own eyes, that the bridge is older than the walls; she has seen the same dark stone under the registry's back and under her bare hand in the undercroft, and has said nothing of it yet. She laid one sheet on a counter, answered a craftsman's questions, and **said that she came to ask how the thing stands, not what it says**. She has agreed to his terms and has kept her case to herself. She carried the card as its bearer; it has two corners gone. She holds the flat, the hide, the page in her coat, the ledger and her notebook. She has told Cael that she cannot tell her happiness from terror. She does not know what Cael meant by "look up" and did not ask.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/STATE_LEDGER.md:459:**Objects and money.** **136 marks** (138 less two at the inn's yard door). The reserve sealed in the third box, which is on Cael's back. Three boxes, the kit case, the flat and the hide are inside the iron door. Karis's fifty-two first copies are back in their box in their order. The card is in Cleon's hand. Still owed: sixty yards of four-strand to the Lowmarch ferryman. The loft is paid for a week from the 282nd.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/STATE_LEDGER.md:472:- **Count and restriction.** Karis is the unnamed bearer; Cael, Brom, Lira and Vastin are the four hands. Seln is not on the 284th count. Both upper keepers counted the card; the yard clerk asked nothing and wrote *four*. None of the four restricted names has been written, so the asset-restriction remains pending (`ch25:97–117,145–171,225–247`; `ch26:183–223`; `book-06 ch58:49–53`).
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/STATE_LEDGER.md:478:**Movement 4 CLOSED (2026-10-11).** Same-author repair r1 (a bounded line repair; nothing re-staged): the nine coordinator-applied fixes kept and their joins smoothed (the boulder image made to agree with "*no length*"; Karis looks at three boxes and a case before "Four hands."; the cook-house questions after the restored exchange are of method only; "Four." given to Cael at the parapet); Vastin's refusal recomposed to locked Book 6 (the Ostrand case stated as the cause, twice, and not taken back; beside it "I had kept my hand off a door for a sound reason, and I was thankful of it for a poor one"; entered "on a form which has no line for why"); the two tracked passages in ch26 re-ordered (Lira prices the ladder aloud and shoulders the box before Cael understands; the clerk writes *four* and blots it before he looks at a face, then opens the door, looks at Vastin, opens his mouth and shuts it); Wind's ceiling removed; **Cleon's name comes to Cael at the cook-house counter and is then read on the card — nobody has said it aloud**; second tellings of the name-versus-count idea trimmed. Sol recheck: CLOSE WITH LINE FIXES; both tracked passages RESOLVED; every binding ruling borne out; the day arithmetic confirmed (161 days since the last self-reading; four days since the board; 282 days since the first sill; Oryn's gates). One punctuation fix applied by the coordinator (a broken dialogue join in ch21). The "New canon minted" and "State at movement end" sections above were refreshed from the post-repair author report. Final: 35,179 prose words; gates 0; overlap 0 unprotected (12 protected). Purse 136 marks; deployment thirty-two; ten fragments; the forenoon of day 284, inside the iron door under Treswick.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/STATE_LEDGER.md:486:- **Cleon.** Protected, all his: "I know that hand." (of the Hall's fourth sheet); "Being believed — … It's the end of being able to be wrong."; "Give me a week." (the 284th); "They're orders."; the two-part limit ("I can tell you these are orders. I can't tell you what they order…"; "It doesn't work on a river, and it doesn't work on a man, and it doesn't work on a tree… It costs me my eyes for an hour after and nothing else."); "Same make."; and the 287th close ("documentation… built the same way twice… never once slept well since."), in which he uses Karis's name (he heard Brom say it). Karis: "Older than [UNBOUND]." **His Path has been shown from OUTSIDE only**: he looks at a made thing from its foot upward, without blinking, and says which signs BEAR (white), which are CARRIED (black) and which are NOTHING (red, packing); his plank's four rows are the same lines as rows on the Hall's fourth, the Stair's ninth and twenty-first, and the Ring's sixth sheets. **On the 287th he set his own week to run from that day to the 293rd, with everything else put aside**; at its end every sheet goes up. The bundle on his shelf ("Paper. Not stone.", from the same shelf as the plank) is tied and unread. He has not noticed Cael, who holds his lamp ("a hand to the left"); he knows nothing of the alley, the fragments or the mechanism.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/STATE_LEDGER.md:494:- **Costs.** **Karis's LEFT palm** cut on a step's edge: seven stitches, Brom's, bound in a strip of his shirt (no healer inside the gate will touch a hand without writing a name; she writes with the right). **Vastin's right knee has gone again on the eleventh day of Oryn's thirty** (a wall on it, against her order, which he heard word for word and asks that she be told): a lath cracked, re-splinted with a slat from Brom's box, twice its size; on the night of the 290th he cannot stand or hold a bowl. Brom: both shoulders. Lira: the right shoulder bruised; day twenty-five out of wool.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/STATE_LEDGER.md:517:- **The decipherment.** (1) The pecked figures equal the number of signs in each row: the cutter counted his own work. (2) The sign-group after each figure is the count in words. Karis tested it without looking at the board: from the oldest of the old town's tally-books (the eastern gallery, third bay on the left, the eleventh shelf) she wrote only the pattern of like and unlike letters in that hand's spelling of twelve, nine and seven, and then laid the pattern against the cutting. It held in every place. The tongue is the old town's "or that tongue's grandmother". **Eight letters** from the board. (3) The carried signs on the floors spell further numbers: *ten* on the Ring's fifth sheet and the number after ten on the Stair's fourteenth confirm the eight; two more letters from the Ring's ninth, confirmed on the Hall's eighth (**ten**, the 288th); three more from the Stair, confirmed on the Hall (**thirteen**, the 289th); one more from the Hall (**fourteen**, the 290th). Three signs are described: a hooked upright, a thing like a gate, a thing like a fork. **She has read no word that is not a number.** One group of six signs stands four times in the Stair's twenty-nine, always after a bearer; she has four of its letters and a word that she wishes to find there, and she has covered it and not written it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/STATE_LEDGER.md:522:- **The alley.** The gate at the cut's foot can be barred from inside the cut. **An iron bar is let into the wall at the cut's mouth, beside the parapet, for people coming up under loads.** Six, hooded, each with a cloth over the lower face: two with shod staves from the yard; above, one kneeling with a crossbow on the highest landing, one with a stave beside him, one who came down to the plane, and one at the rail with a shuttered lantern. The plane stood from the lip of the ninth step, wall to wall and to the sky, seen for a breath as a bloom on the dusk. Two bolts broke on it (the second a hand from Cael's face); a palm and one shoulder were laid on it. Brom dropped his box across the fourth step, tipped up the loose third tread (a slab of dark stone a forearm long and a hand thick), lifted it to his thighs and let it go down the steps at the first stave. Lira came off the landing over the box and drove her knife a third of its length into the second stave's shaft; she took the wall on her right shoulder. Brom's boot put the second man down the steps; one of them then hauled on the box's becket, and Brom sat on the box. Karis's boot went from under her on the dished sixth step; her bare left hand opened on the lip of the step above. The hood on the landing lifted two fingers; the lantern was raised to the parapet; **Seln's cord (a clerk's page-weight: a pear of lead on a thin cord) took the arm to the rail** before the shutter opened. Two low notes whistled. They left at a walk: the bow unstrung, the cord cut in two places, the dead lantern taken up, the gate unbarred. Cael's count was eighty-one. **A stave was left** (a watchman's, shortened, its ferrule filed) and **two bolts** (plain, no fletcher's thread).
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/STATE_LEDGER.md:524:- **Karis's hand.** A straight cut across the heel of the left palm, from the root of the thumb to the wrist's other side; "not deep… only long". Bound in the cut with Brom's neckcloth; sewn in the loft with **seven stitches** of waxed thread on a curved roping needle held in the lantern's flame; the thread bitten; bound with a strip of his shirt. She said her fourteen letters over, one to a stitch. She writes with the right. Why no healer: in the district's house the entry on the sheet at a bed's foot (name, registry, tier) precedes the treatment, and after the fourth bell a card waits until those with names have been seen.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/STATE_LEDGER.md:538:**Brom.** Iron Skin; Copper. **No grey at any point.** Toes done on the 289th. **Left shoulder wrenched under the dray on the 286th and made worse by the slab on the 290th; he cannot raise either arm above the elbow.** He has carried his box on the bound right shoulder since the 286th and took two falling people on that arm on the 290th: owed to two healers at the mill, entered by Cael as his own debt as much as Brom's. He was offered the broker's post and has, in his own mind, turned it down (the factor has been told only "I'll think on it"). **The purse: a hundred and thirty-four.** He sewed Karis's hand. He means to buy the rope going out.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/STATE_LEDGER.md:540:**Karis.** Ember; Iron, Rank Three; no point struck since the fork. She has said "Older than [UNBOUND]" once, at Cleon's table. She has heard Cleon say, of himself, what being believed is, and has known it for her own as well; she told Brom what had believed him (her fifty-two sheets agree with the thing on his shelf). She asked the five for his week and was given it. **Fourteen letters of twenty-three; she can read the cutter's figures and number-words and nothing else, and will not guess.** She knows that seven kinds bear, twenty-three are carried and eleven are nothing; that a bearer heads every run; that the board and the floors carry the same rows. She has not asked for the bundle. **Vastin's page is in her coat, unshown; the title on it has not been spoken.** She holds the flat, the hide, the ledger, her notebook and the day's card. **Left palm: seven stitches, bound in a strip of Brom's shirt.** Three days of Cleon's week remain.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/STATE_LEDGER.md:546:**Cleon.** Rune Path, Bronze; shown from outside only. He has said that he knows the hand; has shown the board; has said what bears, what is carried and what is nothing; has given the limit in both its parts; has said what being believed is to him and asked for a week (the 284th); has re-set that week to run from the 287th with his own page put by; has spoken his close; has Karis's name and has used it once. **He has taken no notice of Cael.** He does not know that anybody was set upon in the cut.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/STATE_LEDGER.md:559:Packet 006 carries the 291st, borrowed handcart, Karis's bound left hand, eleven fragments, and the fact that three clerk questions remain unasked (`MOVEMENT-006.md:26–28`). Its author must additionally inherit the following surviving particulars:
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/STATE_LEDGER.md:563:- **Cleon's knowledge and statements.** Cleon knows Karis's name and has used it once; he knows the hand, has externally demonstrated bearer/carried/ornament and “orders,” has stated both limits, has matched the same make, and has delivered the documentation/built-twice close. His week runs from the 287th through the 293rd. He has not noticed Cael, learned of the alley, shown the inside/lean, or acquired knowledge of the mechanism (`chapter-27.md:187–199`; `chapter-29.md:193–289`; `chapter-30.md:253–279`; `AUTHOR-REPORT.md:117`).
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/STATE_LEDGER.md:566:- **Karis.** Left palm has a long shallow cut and seven stitches, bound in a strip of Brom's shirt; she writes right-handed. No healer inside the gate has seen her. She has three days left of Cleon's week (`chapter-33.md:63–125,277,305`; `AUTHOR-REPORT.md:111`).
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/STATE_LEDGER.md:569:- **Inn, purse, count and day.** Six are in the long loft over the stable; it is paid through the 295th, with pot at dusk and seven bowls. Purse 134, reserve sealed in the third box. The below-ground daily count remains Karis as bearer plus four hands; Seln is never one of them. Night of day 290 is complete; Movement 6 opens day 291. The cut will not be climbed at dusk again, but no further route ruling has been made (`chapter-31.md:265–267`; `chapter-33.md:277–305`; `AUTHOR-REPORT.md:101–103,123`).
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/STATE_LEDGER.md:579:- **Entry seam with Movement 5 ratified** (Sol plan audit): the loft on the night of the 290th; eleven fragments; deployment thirty-two; purse 134; Vastin's knee re-splinted; Karis's left palm stitched; the fifty-two sheets on their three tables.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/STATE_LEDGER.md:591:- **The street and the fire (the 293rd evening).** At the inner stair's head Cael read Karis's page **the right way up** for the first time ("There was no desk on the other side of this."). He told Seln on the carts' street; **both lines are Seln's**: "Made." / "For a kind." At the yard fire Lira read first; the fixed order held; Cleon came above ground once, ate half a bowl and undertook that the skin stays under his hand and goes to no counter. **Seln:** the case is on his chest, **shut**, its contents unnamed; "I'll keep it a while longer." (a decision, not a disclosure; no count of years is printed here — closed chapter 18 says twelve). **Vastin** (protected): "I believed it was imperfect." / "Now you're telling me it was *aimed*." / "At a category. Which turned out to be a person. Who's sitting across the fire."; to Lira's "His?": "No. I read it, after. I did not sign it." / "I would have." Brom priced it and withheld a verdict ("I'd want to hear the other side"): **the record's fear is reported and not endorsed.**
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/STATE_LEDGER.md:594:- **Bodies.** Vastin's right knee: **day fourteen of thirty on the 293rd**, slat-bound; stick; no kneeling, running or Wall. Karis's left palm stitched under linen (she has begun to use it without noticing). Brom: neither arm above the elbow. Lira: day twenty-eight out of wool; right shoulder bruised. Cael sound; eyes recovered.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/STATE_LEDGER.md:605:- **The carts' street and the beam.** (r1: in the yard Seln names the risk before the ruling — "a bar at the foot of it, and a beam, and a man who writes" — and Cael answers that he would "sooner be weighed in front of fifty carters than shut in where nobody's looking"; the card is spelled out as one bearer, Karis, and a figure of four meaning Cael, Lira, Brom and Vastin by count, with Seln on no card.) From the lane's end a broader way opens to the left between the last house and the top wall: a shelf cut into the bluff on its outer side, paved with grooved setts, wide enough for two drays; up-traffic keeps the wall, down-traffic the kerb; it curls in under an archway to **a banded pole let down between two posts**, with the weigh-beam beside it (a pan as broad as a cartwheel on three chains; a man in a leather cap with a ruled slate, eleven years at that beam). His rule: "What goes by the slot's the hatch's. What comes on wheels is mine." **The honest way:** Karis lays the card on his ledge and says that it is matter for the lower archive on its practitioner's card; he reads the card's head with his lips and chalks four weights against that name. No name is spoken; none of the four restricted names is written. He will not weigh a man ("There's no column"). Vastin had given Cael the rule beforehand in the yard ("a load that travels on a practitioner's card is entered to the head of the card… it has an article, which I do not give you") and says one sentence at the beam and no article. Karis tells Cleon that his name is on a slate ("It's been on worse").
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/STATE_LEDGER.md:609:- **What Cleon learns of the 290th:** that Karis cut her hand on a stair ("Which lays?" / "The other."), and no more.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/STATE_LEDGER.md:618:- **The parcel.** The carrier with his hat cut away on one side lies the night of the 292nd at the inn on his way to the western landing with the bag; he knows Brom's back from that landing and breaks the bag "for a thumb that the old man knows" (Brom's thumb, in hub-grease, on his way-slip; a copper of Cael's own). The dividers: two brass legs a hand long, flat on the inner side, points of blued steel in a round of cork, a broad flat rivet, a turned stalk. Karis asks for the loan of them "when I have a hide in front of me again". Cael does not open them on any distance (his ruling of the 168th).
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/STATE_LEDGER.md:623:- **The leaf as a thing.** In a bundle between two thin boards of beech. A single leaf of skin the colour of old horn, stiff, curled at one corner, the lamp showing through it; written with a point and rubbed dark; twenty-six lines on its first face, in the floors' letters; a list on its back, not gone into. A little below the middle a place four or five letters wide scraped pale and thin, with one upright stroke left at its left side. Cleon: "One hand. One sitting. He ruled it first… a fair copy… scraped since it was written. I can't tell you by whom." Its head gives a year in the count that the old town's books keep. **Beneath the lower board: a dozen or more leaves of paper, a year-book of the town in its old ink hand** (floods, a murrain, who was reeve), which Karis reads at sight and puts later than the skin ("It speaks of the walls"). The chronicle's line stands on its first leaf beside a worm-hole or damp-hole a thumb wide.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/STATE_LEDGER.md:626:- **The page the right way up (the §13 lock).** At the head of the inner stair on the evening of the 293rd, by the lamp in Lira's hand, Cael sees the first line of Karis's copy lying the right way up: "There was no desk on the other side of this."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/STATE_LEDGER.md:646:**Karis.** Ember; Iron, Rank Three; no point struck since the fork (she did not light the yard fire). **All twenty-three letters.** She has read the first face of the leaf aloud once, given one account of it, shown Vastin's copied page to Cleon, marked one line of the chronicle and not read on, and made two copies with the gap ruled as an empty box and the one stroke drawn. She has kept two pages in the chamber and at the fire. She has not gone into the leaf's back (a list). Left palm: stitches in, under linen; she has begun to use the hand without noticing.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/STATE_LEDGER.md:652:**Cleon.** Rune, Bronze. He has shown the inside of the Path to Karis; has been told by Cael how the twelfth came; refused to finish a sentence; gave a cup of water and a warning; looks away from the boy on purpose and has kept it for two days and an hour at a fire. He has heard the leaf that lay on his shelf ten years. He has been above ground with the crew once. He has said that the leaf stays under his hand and goes to no counter. Tomorrow: the list of parts.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/STATE_LEDGER.md:660:**Objects and money.** **130 marks** (134 less four for the dinner). The reserve sealed in the third box. Hesk's dividers are shut in Hesk's book; his note with them. The fair copy of the leaf is in Cael's hand; the other in Karis's notebook. The stable's handcart is theirs while the loft is (to the 295th). Still owed: sixty yards of four-strand to the Lowmarch ferryman.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/STATE_LEDGER.md:667:Movement 7 already carries the 294th, all-day list, twelve fragments, Karis's bound hand, Vastin day fifteen and no mule (`packets/MOVEMENT-007.md:28–30`). Its author must additionally inherit:
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/STATE_LEDGER.md:672:- **Object positions at night on the 293rd.** The original skin leaf is between its beech boards under Cleon's jar at his desk; the paper chronicle remains under the registry with its hole slipped/marked; the shelf bundle is untied and the leaf's back/list is unread (`manuscript/chapter-38.md:31–43,163–193`; `manuscript/chapter-39.md:137`; `manuscript/chapter-40.md:289`). Karis's fair copy is in Cael's hand/coat after Seln returns it; Karis retains the second copy in her notebook (`manuscript/chapter-39.md:123–127`; `manuscript/chapter-40.md:249–251`). The fifty-two sheets lie on their three tables one night more, and the black-oak plank remains against the third table (`manuscript/chapter-39.md:117–121`; inherited location `STATE_LEDGER.md:552`). The three boxes, flat and hide are back in the loft; the bearing is on the calfskin in the second box, the reserve stays sealed in the third, and Cael sits against the third (`manuscript/chapter-40.md:267–289`). Vastin is in the end stall; the tarred kit case is last explicit in his hands on the returning cart, and the prose does not state whether it finishes in the stall or loft (`manuscript/chapter-39.md:173–205`; `manuscript/chapter-40.md:253–265`). M7 must not silently choose a different custody if the case matters.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/STATE_LEDGER.md:673:- **Bodies.** Vastin's right knee is day fourteen of thirty on the 293rd, still slat-bound, with stick/no kneeling/no running/no Wall; he sleeps in the end stall and can take only the inner stair on his seat/hands (`manuscript/chapter-39.md:53–113`; `manuscript/chapter-40.md:253–263`). Karis's left palm remains stitched and bound; she writes right-handed but has begun to steady/use the left without noticing (`manuscript/chapter-38.md:227–247`; `manuscript/chapter-40.md:249`). Brom still cannot raise either arm above the elbow; Lira is day twenty-eight out of wool on the 293rd (`manuscript/chapter-34.md:71–79`; `manuscript/chapter-37.md:281–285`).
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/STATE_LEDGER.md:686:- **Entry seam with Movement 6 ratified** (Sol plan audit): twelve fragments; deployment thirty-two; purse 130; the loft and handcart to the 295th; the leaf's back unread; Vastin's knee day fourteen on the 293rd; Karis's stitched palm; Brom's arms; Lira's days out of wool; sixty yards of rope owed.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/STATE_LEDGER.md:687:- **The 294th: the list of parts.** Cleon turned the skin between boards; its back is paler, scored and not inked, **nine short lines, a thing and its number to each**. Karis can spell all nine and turn four (the four things the face says were made); the other five are spelled and unturned, and **she has said none of the numbers aloud**. **The fourth line is the mark's:** the word for the mark is what bears; two runs hang from it LEVEL (those who are kindled; and a six-sign word in the scraped-thin place), and after both the number *three*. Cleon made a second copy, drawn and not read, and gave it to Karis ("Your hand's better than mine on the fourth line."). The fifty-two sheets were counted into three boxes under two hands, K. and V. The clerk wrote *Withdrawn by its bearer* and *four* and ruled the account off. Protected, Cleon's, at the iron door: "Work. Not a finding." / "Mind the stair. It leans." **He is not above ground and did not look at Cael.** This edition has no noun "bearer" for a sign (signs bear, are carried, or are nothing).
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/STATE_LEDGER.md:692:- **Lowmarch (the 297th).** Pike's twelfth line is the clearance (*Eight. Twenty; paid.*); the thirteenth, protected: *Treswick. Survey read. Bearing.*, with price and outcome left empty and said to be empty. **Cael read it the right way up, from Pike's side of the bench.** Pike put a DAY against the season line (nothing printed; "Eleventh. And dated."). **Teague's term, taken as a contract** (protected: "Ten of us on a wall's count. Your six and my four." … "Contract. Not more than that. I'll not have it called more." / "The wall's still the wall." — all Teague's): **ten on the count — Cael, Lira, Brom, Karis, Seln, Vastin; Teague, the tall man with the spear, the bow, the young one; NEITHER healer**; shares and costs by the count; **the field is Teague's** (Cael: "It's yours. I'd have asked you to take it."); the reading is Karis's ("You'll be asked."). The wall has a month on it and is not stood before the month is out. No hand was offered; the cloth still reads first and eleventh.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/STATE_LEDGER.md:698:- **Millrace (the 298th evening).** "Eleven off the ridge." (the woman with the net, as in Book 7) / "I know." (Oryn). Oryn to Vastin: "Stand there."; he stood an hour without a clause. Protected: "Nineteen days," (Vastin) / "Then it's eleven more." (Oryn). Oryn's terms: three holds owed yet on her round; "I'm a stop on my own route."; on nobody's count. "Sixth chair." (Lira) / "On my terms. Which are the only kind." (Oryn); she sat. **Readings, each asked first:** Brom's LEFT shoulder mended (a quarter-hour; she was grey), the right re-tied shorter by the older healer ("That'll do"); Lira's arm ("You listened when it told you the second time." / "Once." / "Once is once more than I priced." — "It's an arm."); Karis's palm ("Who sewed this." — the seven stitches drawn; "Write. Don't lean."); Vastin's knee — he told her of the alley wall himself; "It held in a cut," (Oryn); new linen and two laths; **the month stands as counted**; "Noted," (Vastin) / "That's new." (Oryn) / "It's a useful word." (Vastin) / "It's his." (Oryn). **Purse 127** (meal and a flitch, one mark).
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/STATE_LEDGER.md:701:- **Dawn, the 299th.** "Twelve," … "And a mule." (Seln). The mule carries the three boxes, the flat, a sack of meal, Oryn's pannier and canvas. Karis pinned a clean sheet to the hide's eastern edge, pricked the divider's point on it, and ruled the pencil line from the fourth ring through the point and a hand's breadth past ("I am not going to draw a stop that the paper does not give me").
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/STATE_LEDGER.md:702:- **Bodies at dawn on the 299th.** Vastin: **day twenty of thirty**, Oryn's linen and two laths, stick, walking. Brom: both arms up to the shoulder. Lira: the arm released. Karis: stitches out, linen on, not to lean. Teague: the right wrist in linen under the leather. Cael: sound.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/STATE_LEDGER.md:728:- **The contract (44:165–215), as repaired.** Teague opens with the protected count and **closes** with "Contract. Not more than that. I'll not have it called more."; the four heads stand between in my words (neither healer on the count; shares and costs by the count; the field his; the reading Karis's). "The wall's still the wall." is **Teague's**, to Brom's "You've built your count on a thing that you've never seen stand."; Cael answers that there is a month on it with twelve days to run; Teague: "I'd not ask a thing with a month on it to be shown for a count." No hand is offered.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/STATE_LEDGER.md:737:  - **The office's distance:** one line above the eight stations on the two-bar sheets (the fourth place's four signs; a point of the compass; a measure in lengths); neither is printed; "three such days, less a little". Hesk's dividers are opened for the first time on a distance by Karis, on Cael's loan; the point falls a hand's breadth off the skin, in the brass's shadow. "Three days past the iron." / "As we walk. A little less."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/STATE_LEDGER.md:741:- **The readings (45:317–409), as repaired.** Brom: the older healer unties her own knot; Oryn shuts the wrench in the left shoulder (the arm to the height of his chin); the right is re-tied in two yards; "That'll do." Lira: the protected three lines are Oryn / Lira / Oryn; "It's an arm." Karis: the seven stitches drawn; "Write. Don't lean." Vastin: he has stood at the post the better part of an hour; he tells her of the wall himself; "It held in a cut," **Oryn**, "And then you asked it for a second, with eleven days on it"; the slat off, two new laths, the month as counted; "Noted," **Vastin** / "That's new." **Oryn** / "It's a useful word." **Vastin** / "It's his." **Oryn**, who does not look round at the boy, and whose mouth goes down at one corner.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/STATE_LEDGER.md:746:- **Dawn (46:295–343):** unchanged. Seln: "Twelve. And a mule." Karis rules the line from the fourth ring over the join and a hand's breadth past the divider's prick, with the bound left hand.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/STATE_LEDGER.md:762:**Karis.** Ember; Iron, Rank Three; no point struck. She holds her own copy of the leaf's back and Cleon's drawn copy. She has the fourth line (the mark; those who are kindled; what is set; three), the three boxes with the first empty, *the four series years (61, 94, 138, 206: years counted from the first sheet that the office holds for each place)*, a fifth place of the two-bar mark with no figure, and the office's run to it. She has not said the compass point or the figure in lengths aloud; five lines of the list are unturned. Left palm: stitches drawn, in Oryn's linen; she writes and draws with it. The hide has a clean sheet pinned to its eastern edge, with a prick at the divider's point and a line ruled a hand's breadth past it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/STATE_LEDGER.md:768:**Oryn.** Tide; Iron, Rank Four, provisional. Nobody has died under her hands. She has read Brom, Lira, Karis and Vastin, each asked first, and Cael, asked first, for forty minutes, to her own floor; her finding is given (46:189), standing. She knows of twelve and whose the eleventh and twelfth were; she felt "something turn over, out at the edge" and thought nothing of it. **She has not heard the leaf** and has not been shown the grade. *She has three holds owed yet on this round; her road lies with theirs for a day and some and then turns;* she is on nobody's count and is sent by nobody; she has sat in the sixth place.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/STATE_LEDGER.md:792:- **Bodies/counts at dawn 299:** Vastin day 20 of 30, Oryn's linen/two laths, stick; Brom's left shoulder mended, right re-tied, both arms to shoulder height; Lira's arm released; Karis's stitches out, linen on, scar promised, do not lean; Teague's wrist wrapped; Cael's eyes paid in the dark (`chapter-45.md:295–377`; `chapter-46.md:233–239,299–317`).
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/STATE_LEDGER.md:806:- **The crest (the 303rd, dusk).** The fourth place: a fence of iron bars a pace apart round a bowl; in the middle a grey arch on two piers over a sink — the carters' name, from Teague, is **the Sunken Span**. On a shelf between a dry ravine and the iron: a hut, a fire, kit under a hide, two squared timbers on trestles, **seven hoods (two crossbows, four staves, one low practitioner who shows a white light)**. Protected: "They knew." (Karis); "That iron's been in the ground years. Not one cold term." (Seln); "Drawn?" (Teague); "Nobody declares. Nobody dies. They're seven people on a posting. Noted?" (Cael) / **Teague's FIRST "Noted."** Vastin entered beforehand that he will not stand a wall (twenty-four of thirty).
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/STATE_LEDGER.md:808:- **THE SPAN FIGHT (the 304th, first light).** (1) The watch called "Stand where you are, and declare"; nobody answered. Brom walked the shelf with Iron Skin up, silent, and took two bolts (breastbone; left forearm). (2) Teague went up the middle at a walk with **Cael at his left a stride back**, keeping his left side with the alley's cut stave for three breaths (a stave's shoe on Cael's left collar-bone; he did not give the stride). Teague put the barricade a body's length; the recoil went to the wrist **and then the elbow**. Protected: "Through." (Teague). **THE THIRTEENTH: Force-adjacent, from Teague, acquired at a stride** — protected, Cael's own cataloguing, printed once in italics: "Force-adjacent. Teague. Partial. Bronze. Impulse along the line of the frame at contact … Deployment: none. Thirteenth." It does not ask; it **listens**. **Teague is not told.** (3) The watch reformed with one bow. Teague asked Karis whether the ground past the iron would bear a body ("It is ground."). Protected: Cael's entry before the act, "Nothing was gained inside."; then Teague handed him the ground past the iron ("You've stood on three of them. I've stood on none. Say how.") and Cael gave the order: "By count." **Five went over stake thirty-four: Cael, Lira, Karis, the young one ("Four."), Vastin** ("I am forbidden a wall. I am not, I think, forbidden to stand."); four stayed on the shelf (Teague, the tall man, Brom, the broad man with the second bow). Every ordinary Path went at the stride and came back whole at the stride ("It is all there … To the grain," Vastin); a place of twice the measure does as the three did. The five walked sixty strides inside the iron to the gap and stood: **eleven seconds**. "You'll die in there." (one of the watch) / "We've been in three times." (Cael). Cael refused the eleventh at the gap and the thirteenth at the ravine's lip. (4) **At the fourth second the watch's bow shot at the broad man's; he went over the ravine's lip with a yard of rotten shale, and Teague did not turn.** The seven went east through the gap one at a time and were not followed. **Win sentence (protected):** "The crews had removed the watch's ability to hold the approach by making the only safe retreat a line the watch would not cross and the crews would." **Nobody died. Nothing was opened inside the line.**
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/STATE_LEDGER.md:812:- **The Span itself (the 304th, noon).** Six went down (Karis, Lira, Brom, Seln, Vastin, Cael); Oryn tried Brom at the iron first ("Four … The same.") and would not be told a count across it. The arch is of the grey, in one piece with its piers, a cart's width, **no step**: a road on its back, never wheeled. **Cael stood on the crown: nothing.** The thirteenth listened to the grey; the old three ribs ached low on the left — the cost will come out of him there. Lira found the **square notch** in the hills on the line. Under the arch the sink goes down without a bottom; the piers' inner faces are cut in the floors' hand, and **Karis can read it**: nine sheets drawn. Protected: "Through" (Karis, of a word on the west pier). **Cael counted a course of fifty-two signs as fifty-one, twice** (ruling: hold). The twelfth took one unbidden breath of that course: *Goes.* **The log of the 304th (protected, whole, once):** "Twelfth—no, count it again—thirteenth … Tomorrow I find out what standing in the middle of the first one feels like." (He let "tomorrow" stand: three days by the office's figure.)
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/STATE_LEDGER.md:815:- **THE LAMPS FINDING** (protected, Karis's, aloud, from her table of every crossing by seven people): "They fail the way a hundred lamps fail when one wire's cut. Not the way a hundred souls fall silent." Vastin: "If a man designed the apparatus, then the sigils —" (stopped). Karis: "A measurement is a fact about a ruler, not about the hand that held it." **Two pages:** she tore the ruled leaf out and laid it at the corner farthest from the folded copy, with the whole blanket between and nothing across. **Vastin's THIRD "Noted."** **Cael did not add his own third page** (the technician's sheet stays at the foot of the second row; its day unsaid, unwritten; only he and Seln have done that sum).
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/STATE_LEDGER.md:819:- **Karis (cutaway).** She knows that a six-word sentence exists and neither says nor writes it; the two pages lay a hand apart on the box lid; her entry: "*Second page: nothing entered. Not for want of a pencil.*" *Ruled against the audit:* this is the plan's refusal and is no join.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/STATE_LEDGER.md:844:- **The fight's choreography inside the plan's four exchanges (50).** Positions: Seln in the grass above the path sixty yards east of the gap; the healers and the mule at the old ruts; nine at the shelf's mouth with Brom in front. *One:* two bolts on Iron under a buttoned coat — the breastbone and the left forearm; the neck and hands go "the colour of a slate that is drying after rain"; no sound. *Two:* Teague walks, since the Force wants three breaths of stillness; Cael keeps his left with the shortened watchman's stave from the alley (Seln's, handed over behind the brow) and takes the third stave across the left collar-bone without giving the stride; the tall man keeps the right with the ash crosswise and the steel high; both timbers and their trestles go a body's length back in one piece; five hoods go down; a crossbow's stock breaks under the lower baulk; nobody is under a timber; the wrist clicks and then the elbow. "Through" is Teague's, said to the place where the timbers stood (50:115–117). *Three:* the watch reforms behind the timbers where they lie, one bow spanned, the light relit; Teague cannot shut his right hand; he sees the width past the iron and asks Karis whether it is ground ("It is ground."). **(r1) Cael's entry, made in his head before a boot goes over, "in the tense that it would have to be true in afterward": *Nothing was gained inside.* (50:173–177; the sentence at 50:175). Teague then turns his head for the first time that morning and hands him the ground past the iron: "You've stood on three of them. I've stood on none. Say how." / "By count," said Cael (50:179–185).** Cael over first ("Grass"); Lira a stride in and a stride back ("Gone." / "Back. All of it." — "It's the same"); Karis; the boy when Teague looks at him; Vastin ("I am forbidden a wall. I am not, I think, forbidden to stand."). Sixty strides, two paces inside the iron; the watch's bow cannot turn without giving its side to Brom, the spear and the broad man's crossbow; the empty-handed man hauls back by the collar the one stave who starts for the iron. *The pinch:* eleven counts; **(r1) between two and three the empty-handed man calls "You'll die in there." across the iron, "as a person calls to a child on thin ice", and Cael answers "We've been in three times," for the crew that has stood on three such floors (50:253–259);** at four the watch's bowman looses at the broad man, the bolt takes his crossbow in the stock, his hip goes and a yard of rotten lip goes with it; Teague checks for half a stride and does not turn; the boy looks at Lira's boots and stays; at eleven the first stave is dropped and seven go through the gap singly. Seln counts seven through the gap and again over the second rise, a mile on (51:11).
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/STATE_LEDGER.md:851:- **The piers' inner faces (51:261–327).** Reached from the ravine's bed by a rib of rock on the western side; five courses to the height of a man, in the floors' hand, unworn; Karis draws nine sheets in two hours on a board of Brom's, with a tick where each run ends. **The lowest course of the west pier has fifty-two signs; Cael counts fifty-one, twice, and fifty-two with Lira's finger beside his** (the Book 11 plant; ruling *hold*, 51:351). The twelfth takes that course for a breath: *Goes*, eastward, out of the face toward the other pier. Karis can turn one group in the second course and reads it off the stone once: "Through" (51:329). (r1: she looks under the hide and comes back saying nothing; she keeps no bar.)
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/STATE_LEDGER.md:860:- **Karis's night (53:155–229).** **(r1)** She works the reduction on the lid, twice, and the page does not count with her: "a year that is written by its first letters is a year with a door left open in it"; her entry is "*not shown to be the same year; shown to be near it.*"; she will say the figure to the nearest hundred, and "or near it", once. Being believed is heavier than not being believed. The ruled leaf and her copy of the skin lie on the lid with a hand's breadth of bare wood between; she knows that a sentence exists and that it is six words long, as she once knew the length of a word under a glove; she covers the wood with her bound hand, does not say it, and writes: "*Second page: nothing entered. Not for want of a pencil.*"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/STATE_LEDGER.md:869:**Cael.** Nineteen. **Thirteen fragments. Deployment since the Line thirty-three** (thirty-two, and the read at the seep on the 302nd). The thirteenth (Force-adjacent, from Teague, at the timbers, at a stride) is unused; he has lined it dry once and taken his palm away; he knows that its cost would come out at the old three ribs, low on the left, and has that to tell Oryn "when she'll hear it". **Teague is not told**; the debt is entered beside the first (Vastin's). He has told Lira; Brom has it from her. The twelfth has taken two unbidden looks (the west pier's lowest course; the blanket) and been refused twice; never inward; never on a person. **The two words are still told to no one.** He stood on the Span's crown at noon on the 304th: nothing. He kept a captain's left for three breaths with a stick and opened nothing; he stood inside the line at the pinch with the eleventh asking and the thirteenth listening and told both no. He drew the shelf for Teague and asked one term of his field. He counted a course of fifty-two as fifty-one, twice (ruling *hold*). He has not put the date on any page; he has not written his own third page. He made the entry *Nothing was gained inside* before the crossing, gave "By count" for the ground past the iron when Teague handed it to him, and answered the watch's call ("We've been in three times"). He has heard Karis fix the point and say the figure, once, at the close. He has not touched the cord (a hundred and thirty-seven days on the 305th) or opened the dividers. Body: a stave's bruise on the left collar-bone, ten days from the 304th; the ribs talk when the thirteenth has ground; eyes paid on the night of the 304th and to be paid for the blanket.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/STATE_LEDGER.md:875:**Karis.** Ember; Iron, Rank Three; no point struck. She has: the cord's figure at the Stair confirmed; nine sheets of the piers' inner faces; one word turned on the west pier ("Through"); the agreement of fourteen sheets' rows with the brass, and which way, entered as Cael's and unchecked (she has refused "the next"); the two corner signs read as an old-style year, which her night's reduction shows to be near the skin's year and does not show to be the same; the point ringed with a thumbnail; **the lamps finding**, said aloud and filed on a leaf torn from her notebook, which is now in the back of that notebook with the line "Second page: nothing entered. Not for want of a pencil."; the two slips entered as present, under two unlike seals, unnamed. She has said the figure once, to Cael, at the close. She said "They knew" on the brow. She knows that a six-word sentence exists and has not said or written it. She still has not said the office's compass point or its figure in lengths aloud. Left palm in linen; she writes; she carried a box one-handed.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/STATE_LEDGER.md:883:**Teague and his crew.** His right arm is in linen to the shoulder on a slat; the wrist and the elbow went at the timbers; Oryn has warned him that a third time means a splint. He said "Noted" for the first time on the 303rd (to Cael's term) and a second time on the 304th (to Oryn). He handed Cael the ground past the iron ("Say how."). He did not turn when the broad man fell, and has told the broad man why. He thanked Cael for his left side. **He does not know that anything came to Cael at the timbers.** He knows what lies under the watch's hide and has left the reading of it to Karis. The broad man: right shin knit and bound to a board; a trestle's leg for a stick; his crossbow's stock split; he has said "Aye" to Vastin and to Oryn and nothing else to any of the six. The tall man has said nothing to any of them; he stopped the boy with a hand at the seep, kept Teague's right at the timbers, cut the stick. The young one: stepped over stake thirty-four fourth ("Four"), stood at the pinch, held when the broad man fell, ran when he might; he walked the seep at dusk. The older healer has spoken to none of the six; she set the shin and nodded once to Oryn. **Teague's four have seen:** five people stand inside the line and come out whole; a boy with a stick. **They have not been told:** the mechanism, any fragment, the leaf, the list, the lamps finding, the two slips, the date, who Vastin was, or the doctrine "behind it". No wall has been drilled or stood.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/STATE_LEDGER.md:887:**Objects and money.** **127 → 123** (the 300th: four marks at a hold's gate for two water-skins, meal, a cheese and corn). No other spending from the purse. Coppers, kept apart from it as the closed text keeps them: one for the mule's bucket on the 301st; three of Brom's own on the watch's keg. The reserve is sealed in the third box. On the mule: three boxes (the fifty-two sheets, with fourteen of them and the nine new ones handled on the 305th and put back), the flat, meal, Oryn's pannier and canvas. With Karis: the hide with its pinned sheet (the prick now ringed with a thumbnail, uninked), the Ternhall brass, her notebook (the torn lamps leaf in its back), Cleon's roll, the sheet of three boxes, Vastin's copied page, nine sheets of the piers. With Cael: Hesk's book, the dividers shut in it, the fair copy of the leaf (laid on the blanket folded and returned). With Vastin: the tarred kit case (cord, mallet, stake thirty-two, never driven; the cord was used at the Stair by Karis and put back). The two slips are pinned again under the knot of the oldest row's tape, in the flat. Left at the watch's post: its hut, fire, keg, meal, bolts, mallet, cord, a dozen new bars, a sound crossbow on the baulk, a stave. Ten water-skins between the crews (two bought on the 300th).

