editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-52.md:247:"I held a seal for a long while," Vastin said at last. "I was the person who was sent to see that a filing stood. I told you so on the day that I came over a river; and I told you that this thing was buried deeper than anything that I had ever helped to put down." He lifted the red slip, by the width of a finger, and then the dark one. "I have believed, since a morning in a gallery, that it was all of one piece. That whatever was done was done by one hand, to one end, with nobody inside it to say a word against."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-49.md:61:The ridge broke off there, as it had on the hundred and twenty-third evening, and under it the valley began. Cael had carried that place in his head for a hundred and eighty days. He found that he had it right, and that it was larger. It was a great shallow head of pale ground, scooped out of the hills as a bowl is scooped, and open toward the south; and the light went level across it and laid the shadow of every tussock a yard long.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-49.md:79:"There's a salt road two days north of this, on the high side, that looks down into here from a long way off. I've had it from three carters and never believed one of them. They say that there's a hole in the middle of this ground where the country's water goes, and a span standing over the hole, sunk down in the dish so that you see only the top of it." He put his thumbs in his belt. "They call it that. The Sunken Span. I took it for a tale about a bridge with no river."

