249:Running counts that must reconcile: **Lira's arm** "fifteen days" at 280, "twenty-five" at 290, "twenty-seven" at 292 and "thirty-three" at 298 (counting the 266th as day 1). **Karis's hand** is eight days old on the 298th. **Cash:** 96 → 76 → 116 → 118 → 138 → 136 → 130 → 127 → 123. **Oryn's route:** count "days past the end of her road" from the cairn on the 300th (§12 C3).
333:- **The leaf is read aloud once**, by Karis, in the chamber, on the 293rd morning (M6). It is not quoted again by anyone who heard it. Cael gives Lira the page to read; nobody says it back.
371:22. **Departures (S24):** Teague: "The thaw's past," / "Noted," / "After the next thaw." … "Whatever's on the next line. My crew comes." / Oryn: "The day I have to." / "Noted," / "Everybody says that." … "They'll carry. They'll hurt," … "After the thaw: bring the map, and lie still when I say." / Seln: "Six. Six and no mule. Nothing behind. Nothing beside." / "West," Cael said. / "West," said Brom, and picked up the box. / "Twenty-nine tomorrow," / "Thirty's the month," [B7-reconciled 2026-10-11: OWNER-pending #48]
392:- **Thornwater:** "Five months and twenty days." [OWNER-pending #56] / "That's a season. Take it off." / "Will you mend it." / "Lie still" / "It's an arm. Use it. It'll be slow for a month and it'll ache in the wet for a year and then it won't. Don't fall on it." / "I don't know what that is." … "Neither do you. Write that down too."
394:- **S4 lesson and log:** "The wall held the line," … "And someone else had to be the reason they wanted the line." … "I have never once in forty years needed someone else to be the reason." / "You do now," / "I know fields. I don't know packs." … "I'll learn or I won't." Log: "Vastin fought. He rooted the road and nearly got eaten for it — a wall's no good if you're the only thing making noise behind it. He knows fields. He doesn't know packs. He'll learn or he won't. Watched an Iron Wall go up two feet from me. Filed it. Note the day: an Archmarshal took a wound out here and there was no one to report it to. He looked almost free. Almost."
395:- **S5:** "You keep drafting orders." / "Habit." / "Out here the habit's the tell." … "Draft nothing. Just look." / "Two copies of one ignorance." Close: "Six years," Karis said. "They measured all of it. They graded it — the sites are ranked by something, and it's the same something in the kit, and I can't read either one." … "Everything I can't read points at the same place." / "Treswick," / "The archive under it. Cleon." / "Who's Cleon?" / "The only historical-Path practitioner registered west of the Line. If the language is anywhere, it's under his hands. A day and a half." … "And then I find out whether I've been right for five years or wrong for five years, and I don't know which I'm more afraid of." [OWNER-pending #52]
440:- **C3 — Oryn's days past her road.** S20 has "two days past the end of the road that had been hers" (305th) and S23 "three days past" (307th). The cairn where her road turns north is on the 300th, which makes them five and seven days. **Edition: count from the cairn**, or drop the number.
464:- **Teague's crew never drills under Vastin's wall** before the pass morning, when the doctrine is told to them once.
473:- **Karis cannot read the index as a grade** until the 298th. The leaf is not readable on the page before the seventh day of Cleon's week.
490:Book 9 opens with: the court's centre (no edge; "the same kind of made"); the Rune finding that nobody else knows; the dark Arbiter; the instruments trending wrong; the crack; Vastin unadmitted (Book 9 stages his formal request and the 3–2 vote, with Seln deciding); Oryn's three future holds and "After the thaw: bring the map, and lie still when I say."; Teague's "After the next thaw" and his grief; Cleon under Treswick; thirteen fragments; the leaf's copies with the gap drawn; the mule with Oryn; Cael remains nineteen at the Book 9 opening. Do not place or calculate his next birthday until the owner rules the series calendar. [B7-reconciled 2026-10-11: OWNER-pending #48] Book 9 owns the tomb in the deepest edge territory, the word "recurrence", the Compact's open fracture (Seln's documents), and the Quieting's first visible spread into a city's outer districts. **Do not pre-pay any of these in Book 8.**
     1	# Chapter 40 — The Yard Fire
     2	
     3	Lira read it with her chin down and her lips going, as she read everything, and nobody in that yard helped her.
     4	
     5	It was full dark by then. The lamp over the painted door made a round of yellow on the far side of the arch, and the basket made another in the yard, redder, with the pump and the mounting block standing at its edge. A horse shifted in the stable, and somewhere up the hill a bell was rung for the shutting of a gate.
     6	
     7	Cael went back to the stable wall, and turned his face from her; he had been given that much by three people in a gallery, and he could give it to her.
     8	
     9	He watched the bowls instead. Brom had set eight of them on the board, and for once he was not eating, but held his own on his knee and looked at the steam. Seln had his hands in his sleeves, and the small pale woman sat nearest the basket, very upright, with her eyes shut. On the mounting block an old man held a bowl that he did not lift; and on the sixth bucket, with his back half turned to the flame, sat a person who had not been above ground with them before, and who kept his own eyes closed against the light.
    10	
    11	The steam went up straight, and thinned, and a skin came over the barley in the bowl on the block.
    12	
    13	It took her a long time; she was not a quick reader, and it was not a thing to be read quickly. He heard one word go by under her breath, four times, the same each time, as she said a thing over until it had left off being strange. He knew which word it was, and he looked at the bowls.
    14	
    15	Brom had opened his mouth, once; Seln's eyes had gone to him, and he had put a spoon into it.
    16	
    17	Then the paper was folded, and Cael heard that: three creases, shut one upon another.
    18	
    19	"So the whole time they weren't afraid you'd break something. They were afraid you'd *exist*."
