    39	---
    40	
    41	Brom read it once, with the page held a long way off, and his head on one side, as he looked at a wall that he had been asked to buy; and it gave Cael the length of a slow reader's page to stand under what she had said.
    42	
    43	It had gone into him like a blade laid flat. He had been ready for a harder thing and a softer one, and not for a true one, said plainly, by the one person who had known him when he had nothing in him but a word in square brackets. Every clerk who had ever looked at him over a desk had looked as at a thing that might go off. He had taken it so, and had built himself, a year at a time, into somebody who would not: who stood in corners, and kept twelve doors shut, and told a Path no at a tar barrel with a man five yards away.
    44	
    45	He had been careful not to break anything. Nobody had been afraid of that.
    46	
    47	When Brom had done he did not read it again; he handed it up to Seln, across the fire, and sat for some time with his forearms on his knees.
    48	
    49	"I can't tell you what it means," he said. "She'll not, and she's the one who can read it, so I'll not. I can tell you what it cost."
