    80	
    81	"Nine days," he said, very low. "And a hand sewn. She's earned a bucket."
    82	
    83	Seln had read the page standing, as a man reads who has copied other people's papers for twelve years: once, from the head to the foot, without a change in his face, and Cael did not think that he would ever need to see it a second time. He folded it in three, on its own creases, and did not give it back at once.
    84	
    85	His left hand had come out of its sleeve, and it went to the strap that crossed the breast of his coat, and rested there, in the firelight, in front of six people. Cael had seen that hand go to that strap perhaps four times in all the roads since Ostrand; he had never seen it go there when anybody was looking.
    86	
    87	"I came into this town carrying a thing that I had not decided," said Seln. "I looked at it from a roof, and at three doors, and I ruled nothing. There is a house on that hill which would give a great deal to have it laid upon its ledge; and I have asked myself on a good many nights what it was that I kept it out of." He looked down at the folded page. "I had only ever seen that house from the inside of its own books. It stands upon this. I know now what the office is for, which I did not know at noon; and I had rather know it and carry the thing, than carry it blind." The hand shut upon the strap. "I'll keep it a while longer."
    88	
    89	He put his hand back into its sleeve, and held the page out across the fire.
    90	
    91	Nobody asked him how long a while was; he had given a great many words, for that purse. Cael came round the basket for the paper; there was a bucket beside the girl's that nobody had used, and he sat down on it at last, since his legs had been asking for an hour.
