    93	---
    94	
    95	"Is it true?" said Lira.
    96	
    97	She had not asked the fire; she had turned on her bucket, and asked the man in the dusty coat.
    98	
    99	He had not eaten; he sat with the bowl in both hands, and his eyes shut, and his face turned a little away from the basket. He was silent for so long that Brom began to say that the fellow need not answer.
   100	
   101	"I read how a thing's made," said the voice like a hinge. "I'll tell you that, and no more, since it's all I have. It's one hand. He ruled it before he wrote it. He pressed like a mason and he didn't hurry, and it's as old as it says it is; I'd put my name to that at any counter." The square thumbs went round the bowl's rim. "Whether he's told the truth in it, I can't say. A man can write a lie in a fair hand. I've a hundred and ten strides of them."
   102	
   103	"You said that she'd not like it. A week ago."
   104	
   105	"I said that I'd not slept well on the piece I had. I'd the bones. I'd orders, cut in oak, to build a thing twice, and no notion of what thing." He opened his eyes, and they went to the cobbles. "I've the rest now. I don't know that I'll sleep the better."
   106	
   107	He was silent again, and then he spoke to the yard at large, more roughly.
   108	
   109	"I'll say one thing here, since I'll not say it below with a clerk's bell going. That skin stays where it's lain. There's five storeys of registry on my roof that have never once asked me what's on my shelves, and they'll not learn it from me. It was under my hand ten years when I didn't know what it was. It can lie there now that I do."
   110	
   111	"Good."
   112	
   113	It was Brom, and it was all that he said; he had the ladle in his fist, and he leaned across, and filled the man's bowl to the brim, though it had not been emptied.
   114	
   115	On the mounting block the old man had set his own bowl down.
   116	
   117	He had held it since the pot came, and had not once lifted it. He put it on the stone beside his hip, carefully, as he did everything; and he laid both hands upon the head of the ash, and looked into the fire.
   118	
   119	Cael knew that he was going to speak, and he knew something else, a breath later, from the way that the old shoulders hung. Whatever was coming had not been set in order, and there were no heads to it. The man had the look of somebody about to read out a paper that he had not been let to see beforehand.
   120	
   121	"I believed it was imperfect."
   122	
   123	He said it to the basket, and what came after it was a while coming.
   124	
   125	"I held that for the whole of my service, and was easy in it. Above Ostrand I was shown that the fault was meant. I called it corrupt; I put down a seal upon that word, and walked three hundred miles on it, and thought that I had come to the floor." The stick's foot turned upon the cobbles. "Now you're telling me it was *aimed*."
   126	
   127	He had gone hoarse; he lifted his eyes from the fire for the last of it, and not before.
   128	
   129	"At a category. Which turned out to be a person. Who's sitting across the fire."
   130	
   131	Nobody moved.
   132	
   133	On the sixth bucket the red eyes had shut again, and the grey head had turned a little farther from the flame, and Brom was looking at his boots. Cael sat where he was, with the heat of the basket on his shins; and he found that he could be looked at by that man, across a fire, and bear it.
   134	
   135	It was Lira who answered. She had been waiting, with her hand flat on her knee, as she waited at the head of a bad piece of road.
   136	
   137	"You signed the mark at kindlings."
   138	
   139	"I did."
   140	
   141	"His?"
   142	
   143	"No. I read it, after. I did not sign it."
   144	
   145	The raw hands had tightened upon the ash; he could have left it there, and it would have been true.
   146	
   147	"I would have."
   148	
   149	"Yes," Lira said. "You would have."
   150	
   151	She had not softened it by the width of a hair. She looked at him a moment longer, across the flames, as she would have looked at a figure that she had been asked to check and had found right.
   152	
   153	Then she got up off her bucket, and went round the basket to the mounting block. She took the bowl from the stone beside his hip, which had gone cold an hour since with a skin on it, and tipped it back into the pot; and she ladled it full again from the bottom, where the heat was, and put it into his two raw hands.
   154	
   155	"Eat that," she said. "You said it. That's done. Now you eat."
   156	
   157	The old man looked at the bowl.
   158	
   159	"I am told that it is the wrong coin to say that one is sorry," he said. His voice had very little breath behind it. "I have not found the right one. I shall go on looking."
   160	
   161	"Look tomorrow. It'll go cold."
   162	
   163	He lifted the spoon.
   164	
   165	Cael had watched the whole of that from his bucket. He had believed, for the whole of his time on a bluff, that the worst thing which one person could do to another was to write him down. He had now heard a man say across a fire that he would have signed the sheet. He had seen a girl agree that it was so, to his face, and then feed him; and he looked in himself for the wish that she had done otherwise, and it was not there.
   166	
   167	---
   168	
   169	That left one.
   170	
   171	Cael had known that it would come round to him. It had come round at a plank in Lowmarch, and at a spring, and in a loft with a blanket on its window; they had looked at him then for a ruling. They did not look at him now: a girl had sat down again on her bucket and was watching the fire, and a big man was scraping a pot. They were giving him the yard, as they had given it to her.
   172	
   173	He had been carrying the thing since a desk, and he had it still, level, in both hands.
   174	
   175	He thought of Hesk's book, in his coat, against his ribs, with a pair of brass legs shut in its pages. Ever since a plate lit he had taken whatever was too large for him and put it there, a line at a time, in the order of its coming. He had done so with a word on a plate, and with twelve fragments, and with a place that did not move. He knew exactly how this would go down, if he let it: *Claim. Evidence. Ruling.*
   176	
   177	He did not take the book out.
   178	
   179	It was not a ruling; there was nothing in it to hold, or to try again in daylight. It had been read, once, in a voice that shook; and it was either so or it was not, and every person at that fire had already taken it for so. If he wrote it he would be putting it where he put a cord and a hair. He would be making it a thing that he had noticed; and it was not that. It was the ground that he had been standing on since he was fourteen, shown to him from underneath.
   180	
   181	So he set it down in the only other place there was.
   182	
   183	He got up off the bucket, and stood at the basket's edge, in the light, on both heels, with his hands at his sides. He was not going to say it sitting; he did not know why that mattered, and it did.
   184	
   185	"Since my Kindling I thought the system forgot to make room for me," he said. "It didn't forget. It was *built* so there'd be no room. Four hundred years of a whole world, so that I wouldn't happen."
   186	
   187	He had breath left for three words.
   188	
   189	"I happened anyway."
   190	
   191	The fire ticked.
   192	
   193	He stood in it, and waited to learn what he felt.
   194	
   195	He had expected one of two things. He had thought that it would break him a little, to hear it in his own mouth; or that it would lift him, as a thing lifts when a person has at last got his shoulder under it. Neither came. He was not glad; and he was not sorry for himself, which surprised him more, since he had had a good deal of practice.
   196	
   197	He was only standing; it was a yard, with a pump in it. There were six people round an iron basket, of whom one was asleep and one would not look at him, for his own sake; and he had said the worst thing that he knew, aloud, and the cobbles were where they had been.
   198	
   199	It had not got lighter for being said; he found that he could carry it standing up, which he had not been sure of in a gallery.
   200	
   201	"Yeah."
   202	
   203	It was Lira, and she had not taken her eyes off him since he got up.
   204	
   205	"Now what."
   206	
   207	He looked at her.
   208	
   209	She had asked it as she had called a count at the head of a cut. It was not kindness, or it was the only kind that she had ever thought worth giving him: she had heard the thing, and agreed that it was so, and wished to know what was to be carried next and by whom.
   210	
   211	He had an answer, and he had not known that he had, until she wanted one.
   212	
   213	"Now we find out how old the oldest one is. Karis has a bearing and the marks, and the records to read them against. There's a site at the end of the line that's been quiet since they built the machine."
   214	
   215	He stopped there, since the rest of it was not a plan.
   216	
   217	"I want to stand in the middle of it."
   218	
   219	"That's east," said Brom.
   220	
   221	"It's the way we came. And on."
   222	
   223	"Then I'll want that rope bought before we're through the gate." The big man looked at the sleeping woman, and at an old one with a spoon, and at his own two hands. "And two healers at a mill. We're a poor lot to be walking anywhere."
   224	
   225	"We walked here."
   226	
   227	"We did," said Brom. "I'm only pricing it."
   228	
   229	"It will be watched," said Seln. He had not moved upon his bucket. "The road that you speak of. I should be surprised if a pair of cut boots were not upon it before we are."
