    93	He gave them the count, at the fire, at the rim, in the order he gave every count: the five, and the man on the stick who walked with them and had not asked to, and the three and the healer and the mule; the strides, there and back, which agreed; the floor, level to the middle and nothing on it; the eyes, which were back; the ribs, which were the ribs. Karis wrote the strides down and drew the fifth circle's centre on the map from them, a dot inside the thumbnail, and looked at the dot, and at him, and did not ask, because she had measured one end and he had stood on the other and she knew what standing was and was not. Vastin, on the stick, said, "Twenty-nine tomorrow," to nobody, meaning the month, and Oryn said, "Thirty's the month," and that was the last order she gave the man, and he kept it.
    94	
    95	---
    96	
    97	The ledger of the year, at the rim, by a small fire, with the floor dark behind them and the plain dark ahead and the stumps between, in the plain hand.
    98	
    99	*Three hundred and seventh day, night. The court's rim. A hundred and twenty-three in hand; nothing owed; nothing earned. Nineteen. Thirteen: Wind, Pressure, Iron, Compression, Ember, Shadow, Storm, Anchor, Blade, Tide, Iron Wall, Rune, Force. One anomaly, the still place, read by two instruments — the tenth, which says nothing, and the twelfth, which says a shape — and understood by neither. Not the Quiet. Not a fragment. The same kind of made. That is as far as the instruments go and I am not going past them.*
   100	
   101	*Stood in the middle of the first one. Nothing. Entered.*
   102	
   103	The letter to Hesk he wrote after, on the last of the good paper, at the size Hesk wrote, because a man who has been written to in four lines all his life does not answer in forty.
   104	
   105	*Nineteen. Your grandmother's line was already drawn. I followed it to the end; both edges agreed. Tell Lira's family I said so. Mind the shape, you said. I'm minding it. — C.*
   106	
   107	He gave it to Teague, folded, in the morning, because Teague's crew was going west and the last runner off the deep edge was a captain walking home.
   108	
   109	Teague took it with the good hand and put it in his coat without reading the outside, because the man did not read and had said so, and looked at Cael across the dead fire with his three behind him — the tall woman with the spear, the broad man on the leg that bore, the older woman with the cloth bag — and the gap where a fourth had stood, which he had read past every morning for three mornings and read past now.
