17:There was more light under him than over. That was the thing that he could not get used to. The sky had gone the colour of slate, with the first stars in it, and the plain behind him was black; but the floor kept what the day had given it, and gave it up slowly. He could see his own boots, dark, going one before the other upon a pale ground. He threw no shadow. There was nothing above him bright enough to throw one.
73:There was something that bore. It lay level under everything, whole, from one side of that place to the other, and nothing whatever was carried upon it. Here he was past the last of the rows, and there was not a mark. And it leaned. The whole bare floor leaned, as a line of cut signs leans, steadily, at he could not have said what.
147:He had been turned away from every made thing that he ever stood in front of. A plate had kept him waiting. A registry had found a word for him. A cord came up a stride short in his hand, and a brass rule lied by two hairs. He had thought for the whole of his life that he was a thing with the wrong edge on it, which went into nothing; and it had come to seem the plainest fact that he knew.
