   321	Seln took him down the stair, with a shoulder under his right arm, and said nothing the whole way.
   322	
   323	They were coming out from under the roof by its high end when he reached the bed. A captain had ordered it from the shelf, in three words. The lean old woman had the broad man sitting against the southern wall, in the sun, and the healer was on her heels beside him.
   324	
   325	"Shoulder," Cael heard her say. "May I?"
   326	
   327	"Aye."
   328	
   329	There was a blanket over what lay at the roof's edge. He did not know who had laid it.
   330	
   331	At the low end only three were left. The girl stood up from beside the brown coat, and she spoke to its owner as Cael had once spoken to him on a frozen road.
   332	
   333	"They've gone. Up over the ridge, all nine. Seln counted them. You can leave off."
   334	
   335	"You are certain."
   336	
   337	"He counted."
   338	
   339	Vastin shut his eyes.
   340	
   341	The dust that had been lying upon nothing came down. It fell the whole length of the wedge at once, in one soft grey sheet, like flour through a sieve, and lay along the stones of the bed in the shape of a roof. Above it there was the sky, and a shelf with nobody on it.
   342	
   343	He did not bend over his stick, as he had in a cut. There were no feet under him to stand on. He went over sideways, quite slowly, like a sack that has been stood on end, and lay upon the stones with his cheek on his arm. The bound leg was laid out straight before him still, where a healer had told him to put it.
   344	
   345	Karis had sat down by him before the dust had settled.
   346	
   347	She did it on the bare bed, with the flat on her back, and she put out her left hand, in its linen, and laid it on his shoulder. She did not say anything, and she did not take it away.
   348	
   349	Cael went up the bed, since there was a thing at its other end that he had to stand by.
   350	
   351	Teague had come down. He stood for a breath beside the blanket. Then he knelt, in one piece, as he did everything, and laid his left hand flat upon it.
   352	
   353	Cael stopped a stride behind his left shoulder. It was where he had been put, and he did not know where else a person stood.
   354	
   355	"A wall keeps everyone behind it, and one of mine wasn't."
   356	
   357	He had not turned his head. But Cael knew to whom it was said, since he had given four people a doctrine in two words that morning, and had been answered by one of them.
   358	
   359	There was no blame in it. He looked for the blame, because he could have carried that. It was a captain setting down a sum, as he set down a piece of ground: what a thing did, and what it did not do, and where his own had been standing. It was the second lesson about a wall which Cael had been given by a man who had paid for it. It was a good deal worse than the first.
   360	
   361	They carried the blanket out by the eastern mouth, the tall man and Brom, with one good arm apiece.
   362	
   363	Cael walked behind it, holding his side. The gap opened in front of him, and the walls fell away on either hand; and he came out upon a lip of rock with the whole of the east below.
   364	
