editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-57.md:7:She had walked that country for thirty rounds, and people had died on every one of them, and she had never pretended otherwise. They had died before she reached a gate; she had come down a hill-foot more than once, with her pannier, and been shown a blanket in a byre. They had died after she went on. A man whom she had left mending had got up too soon, or a fever that she had turned came back when she was three holds away, and she had heard of it a cold term later from a carter.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-57.md:15:She went over it, since she went over everything that she had laid a palm to, and she did not spare herself the order of it. There had been a boy on his side on the stones, with a broad fist in his collar. She had gone down at his head; and she had known, in her knees, before she touched him, what she was going to find. A person who has read flow for thirty rounds can tell from a yard off when there is none.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-57.md:17:She had asked him all the same.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-57.md:19:That was the first of the two things which she had to look at tonight, and it was the smaller.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-57.md:21:She did not ask because it was polite. She had worked in a house once, inside a wall, where a sheet hung at the foot of every bed, and where nobody had asked a woman from the river sheds one thing from the day that she was carried in. The asking was the whole of the difference between that house and a plank across two kegs. It said that the body on the plank belonged to whoever was lying on it. She had not gone into a shin on a nod, two days since, with the bone lying two ways.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-57.md:23:She had said the two words to a boy who could not answer them, and had waited for the length of a breath; and then she had gone in without.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-57.md:25:A person who cannot speak has not said no. She turned that over, and it would not quite do. He had not said yes either. If he could have said anything, she would not have needed to ask in such a hurry.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-57.md:27:She found that she would do it again. She found also that she was not going to tell herself that it had been the same as being given leave, since it had not; and she put it where she kept such things, which was in no book, and let it be exactly as heavy as it was.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-57.md:31:She had gone into a great many bodies, and each of them was a country with its water running: fast in one place and slow in another, banked up behind a break, gone thin where a fever had been. That was her whole trade. She found where a stream was stopped, and she let it go on.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-57.md:35:But there had been nothing in them. It had been running a breath before, and it had stopped, all of it together, as a mill stops when the sluice goes down; and so there had been no place for her to push, since a healer cannot mend a channel by filling it from her own, and she had known that since she was younger than the boy.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-57.md:37:So she had taken her hands away, and looked at them.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-57.md:43:It was six days since she had stood at a cairn, at noon, and looked up a mule-path that went north over a saddle; she had counted them. Three gates lay up that path. There was a woman at the middle one who would be near her time by now, and an old man at the last whose chest she listened to at every thaw. She had sent them word from a mill: a week, or more.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-57.md:47:She had walked on east for three reasons, and she said them over. There had been a shoulder that wanted a week of her eye, and the week was out. There had been a knee on her linen; and the knee had sat in a stream-bed today, because she had told it to, and was no worse. The third was an edge. She had said in a loft, on the far side of a cold term, where a person of her trade ought to be standing when a crew walked over such a thing.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-57.md:53:She had found a road in him tonight that was not his own. It went from the heel to the palm, as straight as a rule, through the middle of the cage, and it had the mark of a captain's all over it; she had read that captain's arm from a baulk of timber, two days since, and she would have known the make of the thing in the dark.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-57.md:57:That made thirteen, and she knew how they came to him. She had stood in front of him once, on purpose, and let him have the feel of what she did, since she had sooner be asked than taken from. A captain had not been asked, and neither had an old man, and they did not know that there was anything to ask about.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-57.md:59:That was a debt, and it was not hers. She mended what was brought to her; she did not carry one person's account to another, and she would not begin tonight because she was tired and sore and wanted something that she could put right.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-57.md:61:She had set no term on those ribs, and she looked at that as well.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-57.md:63:What she had told him was true, and it was not the whole of it. The whole of it was that a healer who has just had a boy stop under her hands will tie down every living thing within her reach. She had felt it come up in her when she found those two cracks. *Ten days. Flat. You'll not stir.* It had not been the ribs speaking. It had been the stream-bed.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-57.md:65:She did not set terms out of fright. So she had set none, and he would walk on two cracked ribs to wherever he was going; and they would hold, since she had at least made sure of that much before she took her hands away.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-57.md:67:There was the other place in him, and she did not go round it tonight in her mind. She had gone round it for forty minutes at a mill, with all that she had, and had said what she found: there was nothing there for her kind of hand, and everything else in him went about it. It was not what she had met today. In the boy there had been banks, with the water just gone out of them. In the other there had never been a bank.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-57.md:69:She had no more than that. She had told a girl so, and she would not dress it up at midnight because she was low.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-57.md:75:The lean old woman said nothing. Not one word had come from her to any of the six since a cold term began, and Oryn had long since given up wondering whether it was a rule or only a habit. She had a strip of linen in her hands, and it was steaming.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-57.md:79:It was hot, nearly too hot. Oryn felt it go in at the knuckles, and across the palms, where a boy's coat had been; and she had not known until then that her hands were as cold as the stones.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-57.md:81:She did not thank her, since it did not seem to be what was wanted.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-57.md:83:They had nodded to one another across a shin, those two. One had held her own binding open, at a fire, so that the other could put her hands in under it. Oryn had thought on those evenings that she was being shown a courtesy, as one trade shows it to another. She saw now that she had been wrong about which of the two had the more to teach.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-57.md:91:Oryn looked at that, and knew no more of it than that she had once been told of a ring on a sheet of paper. She had not heard what was written on the leaf in the boy's coat; she had told a girl at a mill why she would not have it read at her table, and she found that she was of the same mind tonight, though a good deal less easy in it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-57.md:93:When the cloth was cold, the old woman took it off, and wrung it out upon the rock. She looked at Oryn once; then she nodded, and got up on her staff, and went back to a man with a bound shoulder.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-57.md:95:Oryn stayed a little longer. She had been asked for a term tonight, and had given none, and she gave one now to herself: she went and lay down beside the mule, on her back, as she had told a great many other people to lie, and was still.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-57.md:101:He did it by turning onto his right side first, and then onto his knees, as he had seen an old man do it for a month; and by the time that he was on his feet, and round the rock again, the fire was a red eye. Beyond it there was a lamp burning on the lid of the middle box, with a small pale woman bent over it, and Brom sitting by with his bound arm across his lap, since he had not yet found the way to lie on it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-57.md:103:"I want the cord," said Cael.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-57.md:105:Karis looked up.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-57.md:109:"I know how long. I said *hold*, in a yard, and I'm not lifting that. I'm taking one more reading for it." He had thought about how to say this, and he said it plainly. "Tomorrow you'll put that cord round the oldest of them, and I'll be standing by with my hands itching for an end. I'd sooner know tonight what my hand's worth on it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-57.md:111:"It is a fair reason. It is also the night of a day on which you have been hurt, and I should want that written upon the entry."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-57.md:117:They did it on the flat of the rock, by the lamp, and they did it as it had been done at Lowmarch: one length, and four knots, and two strides. Brom held the brass eye down on a white stone with his boot. Karis ran the cord out to its fourth knot, and Brom came up; she ran it again, and laid the slip down twice. She set a second stone where it ended.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-57.md:127:It was tarred hemp, no thicker than a pencil, and it lay in his fingers like anything else; he had not known that he remembered the feel of it. He set the eye under Brom's boot, and ran it out to the fourth knot, and marked; and to the fourth again. Then he laid the slip of ash from his mark.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-57.md:131:He looked at that for a breath; and then he lifted the slip, and laid it again from the same scratch, and its far end lay on the stone.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-57.md:141:She pricked the lid of the box with a needle at either end of the Ternhall brass, holding it herself, and did the same for Hesk's, a hand lower down. Then she gave them to him, one after the other, and bent with the lamp.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-57.md:145:She had a hair in the back of her notebook, which she had kept there since the hundred and sixty-eighth day, and it had been the whole of the difference then. She laid it in the first gap, and looked; she pulled a second out of her own head, and laid that beside it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-57.md:155:"It is more than it was. I shall write that it is more." Her pencil was moving. "I shall not write that it grows. I have the hundred and sixty-eighth day and I have this one. Two readings do not make a line, and I told a room so once."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-57.md:159:*Three hundred and sixth day. The open rock east of the gap; by lamp; he had been hurt that morning. The same cord, the same two edges, the same hand. Cord: a stride short in forty-two, where it was a piece. Edges: a hair and a half; two. True in mine, and in Brom's. Cause: not found. No inference entered.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-57.md:163:He stood and looked at a slip of ash on a stone. A cord did not know whose hand was on it. He had told himself as much about a light, in a loft, when it had not been steady for the count of one; made things did not know. He told it to himself again. It was a good deal harder to say with a whole stride lying there on the rock.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-57.md:173:Then he looked at the one thing that he generally left alone.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-57.md:181:He went over it in the order in which it had been given. There had been a cup of water set an inch from his hand. There had been a man with red eyes, speaking to a plank of oak, who said what kind of thing he read and what every such thing had in common. Then there had been a sentence without an end. It was about a thing that ought not to be of that kind, and about the tool reading it all the same.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-57.md:183:He was not to tell that man. He was not to tell anybody. He was only to remember that it had been said.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-57.md:185:He had remembered. For seven days it had cost him nothing, and then it had turned over in him on a plank, beside a stopped wheel, and given him two words. He had not let it give him a third.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-57.md:187:He asked himself whose leave he had.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-57.md:189:He had got round a healer's words once, at a milestone, and had told her so. He took them out now and looked at them honestly. She had ruled, above a weir, that nothing of his was to be turned while her hands were in him. She had said in the same breath that afterward he was on a road, with his own eyes to spend, and that she did not keep people. That was not a thing which he had to bend. It said what it said.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-57.md:191:She had set no term tonight, either. He would not make a leave out of that; it had been given for a reason of her own.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-57.md:193:The man who owned the tool had told him what it was not for. Cael had put that into his own cataloguing in three words. And on a plank it had looked at the middle of him and had not said *nothing*.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-57.md:195:He knew what he was about to be told. That was the truth of it. He had known since the three hundredth breath of a healer's count. Tomorrow he would walk into the oldest of those places and stand in the middle of it; and he would not go there without knowing what he was bringing.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-57.md:199:He did not go to the place. He had been told what a person does with a sore spot. He only let the folded thing behind his eyes come about, as he had stopped it from doing at tables and bridges for fifteen days; and for once he gave it nothing else to look at.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-57.md:213:It was not the tool's own word. It was the man's. He had said that he looked for the bones of a thing before he would look at a letter on it: which part bore, and which was carried. It came as flatly as the others.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-57.md:215:After that there were no more words. It gave him the thing itself, all at once, as it had given him a whole blanket of sheets on the shale; and he had to find his own words for it, which he did slowly, with the water beginning to run from under his lids.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-57.md:219:It lay beneath everything else in that place, level, and whole, from side to side. He had knelt on such a thing on the ninety-sixth day and had found that it was all one piece. And nothing was carried upon this. There was no course on it, and no upright. It was a floor with nothing built on it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-57.md:223:That was the part which he could not get past. A record sat. A man had shown that to a small pale woman at the end of a reed, and a boy holding a lamp had seen it over her shoulder. An instruction did not sit; it leaned, sign after sign, as every row on fourteen sheets had lain one way along a strip of brass.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-57.md:229:[[TIER-B: "The same kind of thing, to that instrument, as the sites were to Cleon's." — the packet gives this as one quoted sentence of narration (BOOK_MAP §2 item 37 has it in lower case, as part of a longer sentence), with no word of how it begins. Staged here: it stands as Cael's own summing of what the twelfth has just given him, in his head, alone on the rock, after the three words and the shape; the fixed log below says the same in his hand. I have not written it in other words. If the full sentence opens differently, the paragraph above ends where it can be joined — coordinator to supply protected text]]
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-57.md:233:There was a step. He could feel where it was, as he had felt a word eight nights ago. He left it alone. It was one look, by a tool whose owner had said that it could not look there; and what he had got from it was a shape, and not a meaning. He would put the shape into the book. He would not put down a syllable of what it might mean, since he did not know, and since a guess written in that hand had a way of becoming a finding by morning.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-57.md:235:He told nobody.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-57.md:237:He went over the three of them, to be sure that it was a ruling and not a flinch. A healer had a finding of her own about that place. It was whole, and it had cost her something to say; he would not lay a second reading across it. Karis had two pages already which she would not have joined, and this was a third of the same sort. She would have told him, besides, that a second glance by the same eye is no second witness. And he had said to Lira that he would tell her on the road.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-57.md:239:A man with red eyes had told him to tell nobody. Cael found that for the first time he was entirely of that mind.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-57.md:241:The pale thing out on the plain had a ring round it now, like a moon in mist, and he could no longer tell where its edge was.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-57.md:245:He wrote it by feel, with his back to the rock and the pencil's butt against his thumb, since he could not have seen a line if he had been given a lamp.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-57.md:247:*Three hundred and sixth day. The open rock below the eastern mouth of the gap. A hundred and twenty-three. Vastin: twenty-seven; he held a roof today, sitting down, by Oryn's leave, and the knee never took a pound of it. Oryn is six days past her cairn.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-57.md:251:*The thirteenth is used. Once: on the frame of the second heap, along the shelf, at nobody. It came back to the three ribs, where I said that it would. Oryn has been told whose it was. Teague has not.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-57.md:255:*Turned the Rune fragment on myself. Cleon said don't. The reading only ever told me the still place was nothing — no flow, no current, a hole where a man should have a middle. The Rune sense says it's not a hole. It says it's* built like something *— like one of those floors with nothing standing on it. Same shape to that fragment as the sites are to Cleon's. I'm not going to write what that might mean, because I don't know, and because the last time I wrote something true in this log a healer made me swear an oath about it. The instruments read wronger for me than they did at Lowmarch. The trend's climbing. My Arbiter's still dark—nothing in me to go quiet, same as always. Tomorrow, the oldest one. I've spent the whole year wanting to stand in the middle of it. I think part of me already knows what I'm going to feel. Nothing. I think I'm going to feel nothing, and I think that's going to be the loudest thing that's ever happened to me.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-57.md:267:"I can hear the water going. That's the thing that only charges when it's looked." She went down on her heels. "You did it on purpose, this time."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-57.md:273:"All of it. I've kept nothing out of the book." He shut it on the pencil, and held it out toward where her voice was. "You've read this whenever you pleased, and I never minded. I'd not stop you now. I'm asking you not to read that one. Not until after tomorrow."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-57.md:277:"Because I know what it looks like, and I don't know what it is; and tomorrow I might. If you read it tonight, you'll walk out onto that plain watching me, and not where you're putting your feet."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-57.md:281:"I asked you at a mill whether it was bad," she said, after a moment. "You said that you didn't know what it was. Is that still the answer?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-57.md:289:"You said the road. I said if there's road. I don't read ahead." Her voice was not quite steady. "I never in my life wanted to so much. Put it away, before I'm a worse person than I am."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-57.md:293:She did not go back to the fire. She lay down where she was, on the bare rock, a yard from him, with her back turned, so that he should be able to hear that she was not looking at anything.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-57.md:295:He sat with his arm across his eyes, and waited for them to finish.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-57.md:297:There was a leaf in his coat that nobody had read but the hand which wrote it. He had told no one. He had not thought, when he made that ruling, how much it would resemble being alone; and he found that it did not, quite, since there was a girl on the rock beside him who had been handed the book and had given it back.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-55.md:3:The third flight was not meant for the roof.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-55.md:11:He was at the foot of the bank, with his back against the rock, looking straight up. He had not raised his voice.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-55.md:13:"They will not spend another on it. One of them has gone to the frame."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-55.md:15:Cael looked where he looked. Under the high edge of the roof there was a strip of shelf to be seen, and the end of a heap, and a dead white tree. Beside the tree a hood was standing with something long across his shoulder.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-55.md:29:She broke the shaft between her two hands, close to the skin, and Brom looked at the roof while she did it. The lean old woman had a strip of linen round the place before the stub had stopped moving.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-55.md:55:It had come down through him like a house. Cael had seen what one hound did to that man, through that wall. He did not know what a hill did.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-55.md:61:"I have perhaps a hundred breaths of it left. I cannot tell you nearer." He drew one, and Cael counted it. "I should be obliged if some use were found for them."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-55.md:65:It was the bank that Cael looked at then, and not the shelf.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-55.md:83:The hoods had not wasted a fifth flight on the roof. Three shapes were going east along the lip, bent low, toward a place from which the high edge would no longer cover what stood beneath it. They were looking for a way in under. And a man with a split bow and one sound leg had gone out to the very end of the shelter to give them something else to look at, for the length of one flight, since there was a small grey shape on a slab that they must on no account look at instead.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-55.md:97:The man was not dead, or near it. He had got his cheek off the stones. He turned his head, slowly, and looked back under the roof for his own, as anybody who is hurt will look.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-55.md:99:Cael saw the eyes go along them. They passed over a captain, who was watching the shelf. They passed over a spear. They came to the boy, and there they stopped, since the boy was the one who was looking at him.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-55.md:101:He did not call. He did not lift a finger. There was no order in it, and no asking; it was only the place where a hurt man's eyes had come to rest.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-55.md:119:The boy sat down. He did it all at once, as he had sat down in the corner of a hut two days before, when everything that could be asked of him had been done; and it was the same sitting down, like a felled thing, with his back against nothing. Then he lay over on his side.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-55.md:133:It was the form, and she kept it. The boy could not give her a word. Cael had heard that woman refuse to go into a broken shin upon a nod; and he saw her wait, for the length of one breath, for an answer that was not coming. Then she put her hands on a person without it. He did not think that she had ever done so before.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-55.md:139:She took her hands away. She held them a little off the coat, with the fingers open, and looked at them. A healer shut what was open, and knit what was broken; she had said above a weir that she mended what was brought to her. Nothing had been put in front of her. It had gone before her hands came down.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-55.md:149:Cael looked back down the wedge, since he could not look any longer at what was in front of him.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-55.md:151:An old man sat at the foot of his own roof with his face toward them. He had seen every part of it, down the whole length of the thing that he was holding up; a man who is the foot of a wall has nowhere else to turn his eyes. He could not get up. He could not have crawled. The plane that he had raised could not be shifted one stride eastward by any power that he owned, since it stood where it had been drawn, as it always had. That was the nature of it, and he had known it for as long as he had held the thing.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-55.md:157:Cael marked it. He had seen that man hear one of his own go off a ledge, two days before, and keep his eyes where they were. He looked now, for the length of one breath, at what lay under the high edge of the roof. Something went out of the heavy face then which Cael did not see come back that day. Then the eyes went up to the shelf, and along it, to the stair.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-55.md:161:A hood was walking east along the lip with a maul on his shoulder. He went past the three with the bows, who were bent over their strings, and came to the second heap, and knelt down by the foot of its frame. He did not strike. He waited there, looking down at the bed.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-55.md:167:The thing along Cael had been at that frame since the dust cleared. It had found grey timber on a ledge of rock, and it had not looked at anything since.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-55.md:175:"With something that nobody'll hear. I can't tell you more; it's not mine alone to tell." He found that his voice was steady. "You said that you'd say when you'd seen it. I've seen my piece. I'm up that slab when you're by it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-55.md:177:"You asked me for nobody declaring."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-55.md:181:The captain looked at him for a breath, as he had once looked along a row of iron.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-55.md:183:"I'll not wait on you," he said.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-55.md:185:Brom had come up the wedge. There was a hand of broken bolt standing out of his left forearm, with linen round it, and he was the colour of slate to the hair. He looked once at what lay at the roof's edge. He did not say anything. He walked out past it, into the open mouth of the shelter, and turned his face up to the shelf, and stood there like a door that has been shut.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-55.md:203:He did not keep the shoulder. A heavy man does not go fast over loose stones with one arm lashed across his breast, and the tall man would not leave that man's side; they went at a hard jog, with the ash held low between them. Cael ran. The stones turned under his boots, and up on the shelf somebody shouted, and it was not a word.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-55.md:209:It was steeper than it had looked from under the roof. He went up it on his fingers and the edges of his soles, as he had seen it done, and the bruise on his collar-bone told him at every reach what a stave weighs. There was a held breath in the soles of his feet that would have set him upon that shelf in one stride, with a crack to it that every hood on the ridge must have heard. He kept it where it was. He went up as anybody would have had to.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-55.md:227:He could not hold a cartload of stone, and nobody could. But a thing had been said across a boy's head in a meadow, to a carter who wanted a second cart shifted; and he had lined himself against a slab on the shale, the evening before, and had learned where his own hip was.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-55.md:233:The line came more quickly than it had behind a step of shale. Perhaps the thing found it for him. The knee went in over the heel, and the shoulder turned and went down; and he was standing in one straight road, from a crack in a ledge to a hand on a post, with every joint in its place.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-55.md:235:He looked along it before he did anything else.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-55.md:239:It wanted one word of him, and it had waited two days for it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-55.md:261:To the west there were three hoods and a small grey man. One of the three had his arms bound to his sides by a thin cord with a pear of lead on the end of it. One had let his bow fall, and was looking at it. The third had backed against the rock with his hands open. Nobody had a string bent. Seln stood among them with a short knife held low, at his own pace, and he had not touched any of them with it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-55.md:275:They looked at the hollow. The thing that they had built to hold a pass lay in it, in a heap, on their own blankets. They looked at a small man standing among their bows, and at a captain on their shelf, and a spear.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-55.md:281:They went. The cleft was at the shelf's eastern end, a few strides from the stair's head, and to reach it they had to pass the two men who stood there. Nobody barred them. The first hood came on with a stave half raised, and saw which end of an ash pole was toward him, and went by it at the length of an arm and up into the rock. The others followed, one behind another, quickly, and not one turned to look. The three from the west had to pass a boy who knelt on their shelf with his arms round his own ribs. They went by him at the length of a stave, and nobody lifted a hand.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-55.md:293:He did not follow them by a step. *Nobody dies*, a boy with a book had asked of his field, behind a brow, when it had cost nothing to ask; and a captain had answered with one word. He was keeping that word on a ledge, with one of his own lying forty feet beneath him. Cael did not think that he would see a harder thing kept if he lived to be as old as the man on the stick.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-55.md:307:He stopped at the hollow. He looked at the heap that lay in it, and at a wedge half drawn from a notch on the lip, where nothing stood any longer; and then he looked at a boy on his knees who was holding his left side with both arms.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-55.md:309:He had not seen it go. He had been upon a stair, with his face to the rock. He saw a heap where there had been a rig, and he saw that somebody had paid for it; and a man who has settled every account of his life out of the one wrist knows the look of a bill, whatever he does not know of the goods.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-55.md:311:He did not ask.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-55.md:329:There was a blanket over what lay at the roof's edge. He did not know who had laid it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-55.md:343:He did not bend over his stick, as he had in a cut. There were no feet under him to stand on. He went over sideways, quite slowly, like a sack that has been stood on end, and lay upon the stones with his cheek on his arm. The bound leg was laid out straight before him still, where a healer had told him to put it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-55.md:353:Cael stopped a stride behind his left shoulder. It was where he had been put, and he did not know where else a person stood.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-55.md:357:He had not turned his head. But Cael knew to whom it was said, since he had given four people a doctrine in two words that morning, and had been answered by one of them.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-55.md:359:There was no blame in it. He looked for the blame, because he could have carried that. It was a captain setting down a sum, as he set down a piece of ground: what a thing did, and what it did not do, and where his own had been standing. It was the second lesson about a wall which Cael had been given by a man who had paid for it. It was a good deal worse than the first.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-55.md:363:Cael walked behind it, holding his side. The gap opened in front of him, and the walls fell away on either hand; and he came out upon a lip of rock with the whole of the east below.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-55.md:369:He looked back. Four people were setting a blanket down on a flat of rock, in the mouth of a gap.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-55.md:371:[[TIER-B: the far-mouth realization — the plan gives it only in summary (at the far mouth Cael understands something about what the line had been drawn in), with the fragment "was not ink" fixed. Staged here: he has come out by the eastern mouth behind the blanket and stands on the lip above the plain; the three paragraphs above bring him to the pencil line and then to the blanket being set down behind him, and stop. I have not written the realization in other words. It closes the chapter — coordinator to supply protected text]]
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-56.md:5:It was Teague's crew that did it, and they did it in an order. Nobody gave the order aloud. Cael stood off with the rest of the six, on the bed, and watched three people go about a thing that they had plainly done before for somebody else; and he understood that no part of it was his to offer.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-56.md:9:It was the one that he had wrung out for a wrist on every cold night since he joined them. She wet it. She washed the face with it, slowly, as she bound a shin, without once looking at her own fingers; and when she had done she folded it small and put it into his two hands, and laid the blanket over.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-56.md:11:The tall man had been gathering all that while. He had brought flat stones up out of the bed, one at a time, and set them edge to edge upon the rock in a shape as long as a person. He lifted the blanket onto it by himself. Cael had not known until then how slight a boy could be who was all wrists and collar.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-56.md:23:Teague looked at him, and past him, at four people standing on the stones and an old man who lay on them.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-56.md:29:It was the only thing that they had been given leave to do, and they did it for the better part of an hour. Brom took two at a time in the crook of his right arm. Lira went farthest down the bed, where the big ones lay, and Seln brought small flat ones that would lock. Karis carried them one by one against her chest, since she was not to lean on a hand; and every stone that came up was taken at the edge of the flat by the tall man or the old woman, and set by them, and by nobody else.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-56.md:33:Each stone was a line drawn low across his left side, from front to back, and he found that he could not draw a whole breath under it. He had not told a healer. She sat against the southern wall with her head on her knees, the colour of old ash, as she had sat in a ravine; and he thought that he had earned the walk up that bed a good many times over, and went down it again.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-56.md:37:A healer had told him that he would not get up, and for an hour he had not. Then he had asked it of Karis; and she had got him onto his feet, with her shoulder under his arm and the stick in his other hand. He stood now at the edge of the flat with the most of his weight upon her, as upright as he had stood at a post in a mill yard. Nobody had told him to stand at this.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-56.md:45:"No. I could not. That is the nature of the thing." He was looking at the cairn. "I have known what it would not do for the whole of my service. I have never before been made to watch what that costs somebody else."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-56.md:47:She did not tell him that he was not to blame. Cael marked that she did not, and thought the better of her for it. She stood at his elbow with a stone held against her coat, and waited until the tall man came and took it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-56.md:51:He had stood by the whole time with his right arm on its slat, and had not carried, since he could not. When the heap was as high as a man's hip he took the stone that the old woman held out to him, left-handed, and laid it upon the top, and pressed it down until it sat.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-56.md:55:Teague straightened. He looked at his own, as he had looked at them on the lip of a ravine: at the tall man, with dust to the elbows, and the broad man on his stick, and the old woman, who was on nobody's count. Cael saw his lips shape the beginning of a figure.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-56.md:59:"We'll lie here," he said. "Out of that gap, on the open rock, where I can see what's coming."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-56.md:65:She had said it before he had finished. She was looking at the ridge, and she did not look at anybody else.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-56.md:71:It went up the stair, and through the cleft at the shelf's end, and out upon the back of the ridge. He climbed it without haste. He had gone up the other way that morning, on his fingers, over rock that had been a quilt of loose stone an hour before; and he found that he did not wish to hurry over anything for the rest of that day.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-56.md:75:It had told him what such places generally told him. There were nine heaps of bedding under the spilled heap, and the place for a tenth, swept and never lain on. There was a kettle. There were two bundles of bolts, with never a thread of colour upon a feather, and a second maul. He had turned over every blanket that he could reach, and there was not a scrap of paper in the whole of it, or a mark burned into a stave, or a name scratched on a spoon.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-56.md:83:He set the two side by side, as he would have set two returns from one office. He did not go past what they said. At the fence they had been told to hold a place, and to see what came to it. Here somebody had been told to stop a crew. And between the one order and the other, seven people had walked east for a day with what they had seen across a row of bars.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-56.md:85:That was a finding. He would give it as one, tonight, and say that it was no more.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-56.md:89:He came up beside her and looked down.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-56.md:97:"He asked me where I'd be," said Lira.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-56.md:99:She had not turned her head. She stood with her arms hanging, looking down at the dust.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-56.md:101:"Teague's boy. At that halt, before the gap. He'd asked what it was like under one, and I told him. I told him that the whole trick was to stay. Then he asked where I'd be, and I said at the back." She breathed. "He watched my boots, at the fence. When the bow went over that edge. I saw him do it; he looked down at my feet, and he stayed where he was, and I thought, good. That's a thing I can give him. I'll stand where he can see me."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-56.md:111:"You were set to keep the one thing which was keeping eleven people alive, and it was kept. I do not tell you so to ease you. It will not. I tell you because it is the entry. You will wish to alter it tonight, and tomorrow night, and it will still be the entry."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-56.md:119:It was true, and he had not meant to say it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-56.md:123:He gave it to her as they walked, since she had asked, and since he had found that a sum which will not lie down is sometimes quieter for being said to another person.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-56.md:125:"A boy was killed by the sixth flight. I went over the lip as the seventh was loosed. There is one flight between the two, and I have asked myself where I lost it." He kept his eyes on the path. "Before the fourth there was no slab. There was a bank that would not have borne a cat. From the foot of the bare rock to the lip took me three flights, at the pace that a man goes who must not be seen. I have made it that each time. It does not come out as two."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-56.md:131:The girl looked at him sideways, and for the first time since a stream-bed something in her face eased by the width of a hair.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-56.md:137:They went on. The ridge was bare, and the wind came over it out of the west, and behind them the plain lay under the afternoon like a floor that nobody had walked on. He did not look round at it yet. He had the habit of taking one thing at a time, and the thing before him was a mule.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-56.md:139:There was another matter that he turned over as he went, and he did not give that one to the girl.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-56.md:141:He had stood on a shelf that morning with a man's two arms bound in his cord, and a knife in his own hand. The man had been a foot from him. There had been a cloth over the lower part of a face, tied behind, with a knot that Seln could have named. He had wanted a face from those people since a rim above a stair. He had broken a crossbow on a stone for want of one.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-56.md:145:He had seen that he would not, in the instant when he might have. A face would have been a fact, and he valued facts above most things. It would also have been a hand laid upon somebody who had left off fighting; and a boy had asked a captain for something behind a brow, in three short sentences, and the captain had kept it on a ledge with his own dead under him. Seln did not care to be the one of the eleven who had kept it worse.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-56.md:161:"She doesn't know," said Lira.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-56.md:165:The mule opened one eye, and found that there was no corn in it, and shut it again.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-56.md:167:Lira put her forehead down against the brown neck, and stood so. He turned his back and looked at the heap in the mouth, where a pool had been, for as long as it was wanted.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-56.md:173:He looked at the plain then, since there was time for it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-56.md:175:It had gone grey from edge to edge. A long way out upon it, lower than the rest, there was something that had not. It lay there small and pale, holding the last of the day after everything round it had let go.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-56.md:177:He looked at it for some time, and ruled nothing.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-56.md:189:Then he looked across the yard of stone that lay between the two crews.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-56.md:191:Nobody was laying that side. The tall man sat with his forearms on his knees and his hands hanging, and the broad man was propped against a pack with his eyes shut. Teague stood beyond them at the edge of the light, looking east, and the lean old woman was at his arm with her board.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-56.md:193:It had been the boy's work. Cael did not suppose that anybody had ever told him to do it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-56.md:199:Brom had it out before he saw what he was doing. He stood holding it. It was of a different turning from his own, darker, with a chip in the rim; and Cael saw that he did not know where on that rock a person was to put such a thing down.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-56.md:201:He had said the night before that he never in his life had wanted so much to see an empty bowl.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-56.md:203:The lean old woman came. She took it out of his fingers, without a word, as she had taken a stone at the edge of a flat. She looked at it for a breath. Then she put it into the bosom of her coat, and went back to a captain's arm.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-56.md:205:Brom came round the fire again, and sat down in his own place, and looked for a while at his two hands as though one of them had done something without asking the other.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-56.md:211:They ate. Seln gave his finding while the pot went round, in six sentences, and said twice that it was no more than a finding.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-56.md:213:"So they were told to stop us," said Teague. He had heard it with his back to the fire. "Not to see what we'd do. To stop us, with a hill."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-56.md:215:"Somebody was told so. I can put it no higher. I do not know who gave the word, or where; I know that it had changed between a fence and a gap, and that seven people walked from the one to the other."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-56.md:221:Teague nodded, once, and asked nothing further. Cael understood that he had been handed one more thing for a column in which there were a good many already, and that he would add it up on the road home, by himself.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-56.md:225:"I am informed that I cannot lift it," said Vastin. "I have tried the information twice, and I find that it is correct."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-56.md:229:He considered her for a breath, gravely, as he would have considered a clerk who had spoken out of turn. Then he opened his mouth. She fed him as she might have fed a child that she was a little afraid of, and neither of them looked at anybody; and on the far side of the fire the broad man, who had not opened his eyes, let his breath go through his nose in a way that Cael had heard once before, under a step of shale.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-56.md:237:Teague looked at what was held out to him. Then he put his right arm forward on its slat, as far as it would go, and let the man lay it on.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-56.md:239:Cael looked into the fire, since it was not a thing to be watched.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-56.md:249:"I've had a shoulder and a forearm since noon, and a knee to look at. The knee's held; he sat, and it never took a pound. I've been too spent to see past the end of my own hands." She looked down at him. "You carried stone for an hour with one arm. You've sat since with the other across you, and you've not once breathed to the bottom. I taped three of those myself, and I know what they sound like when they're let alone. Up, if you can get up. I don't put my hands in anybody at a fire with ten people looking on."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-56.md:257:"Then lie back. It's flat enough."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-56.md:273:She was looking at him as she had looked across a ravine, two days before, at a captain's hanging arm. She had read that arm from a baulk of timber, at her stride and a half. She had told its owner what a third time would cost, and had called it a fact.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-56.md:279:"I counted twelve of yours at a mill," she said slowly, "and I told you that I'd not ask how many more you meant to bring me. You tried to hand me one across a fence."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-56.md:285:"It came at the timbers, at a stride. I was behind his shoulder when he let it go." He kept it level, and low, as he had by a weir. "He doesn't know. I can't tell him how it came without telling him how they all came, and that's not mine alone. It's written down beside the old man's, as a debt. I had it lined once on the shale and took my hand away. This morning I let it go, for the first time, on the frame of the second heap. Along the shelf. There was nobody on the road of it; I looked."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-56.md:295:"Don't mend my sums." But there was no edge on it. She sat back on her heels, and rubbed her eyes with the heel of her hand, and for a breath she looked as old as the woman with the board. "Two are cracked along the old line. The third's whole, and angry. Nothing's gone into anything that it shouldn't. I can ease them, and I will, and I'll tape you so that you remember them."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-56.md:299:He asked it since she always set one, and since he owed her the asking.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-56.md:303:"I'd set you ten days flat," she said. "And tomorrow there's a thing out on that plain, a morning's walk off, that you've come the length of a country to stand in; and you'd get up off my blanket and walk to it. Then I'd have set a term to be broken. I did that once, with a knee, and I learned what it teaches a person." She shook her head. "No term tonight. Ask me again when you've stood where you're going."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-56.md:307:He lay on the rock and looked up. There was a ridge against the stars, with one square piece gone out of it; and under that, though he could not see it from where he lay, there was a heap of stones as high as a man's hip.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-54.md:5:Nobody had moved a stick. A captain had said that nobody would. Eleven people had lain all night under a step of rock, with their loads lashed and their boots on, and had watched a notch in some hills go from black to grey. Cael had not slept for the last hour of it. He had lain on his back and attended to a thing that lay along him from the heel to the palm; and it, for its part, had attended to the ground.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-54.md:11:"I went in. I said that I should go no nearer than a mile, and I have gone a good deal nearer. A mile told me nothing, and I did not care to bring you nothing twice." Seln's voice was as mild as it was at any hour. "There is a pool inside the mouth, under the southern wall, where the last of some old rain lies. I went through it, since a man in water leaves no heel. I lay behind a rock at that wall's foot from the middle of the night until I could see my own hand."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-54.md:17:He let that stand for a breath. Then he gave the rest in order, as he gave anything.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-54.md:23:"They are on the shelf. I counted nine, and I will not swear that it is not ten. There is a hollow in the back wall up there, with a fire in it which cannot be seen from any side but one, and I could not see to the back of the hollow."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-54.md:43:He had stood in a cut once, with a hillside of loose stone on either hand, and had been afraid of what lived in it. He tried now to see a heap as long as a cart let go from forty feet above a bed eight strides wide, with twelve people and a mule walking along the bottom; and he found that he could see it perfectly well, and that there was no place in the picture where anybody was standing afterward.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-54.md:45:"The first stands twenty strides inside the mouth. There is a dead pine at the shelf's lip beside it, standing, and they have roped the tree to the frame, so that the one will bring down the other. The second is sixty strides in."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-54.md:53:"I put my palm on it. It is a slab of bare rock, as smooth as a table, and the loose stone lies upon it to the depth of my arm, like a quilt. It would not bear a child. I could not have gone a yard up it without bringing half of it down about my ears, and the whole of them to the edge to learn what had moved." He considered that, as he considered everything. "Take the quilt off, and anybody could walk up the table."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-54.md:55:Cael marked that sentence. A captain had taught him on a ridge to mark three kinds of thing in any piece of ground, and this was none of the three; it was only a thing that might be different on another day, and he put it by without knowing what it was worth.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-54.md:69:Nobody had needed to ask him this time. He made two lines for the walls, a hand apart, and hatched the left one for the bank; and he set the shelf above the hatching, with two squares upon it for the heaps, and a cross beside the first of them for a tree. At the far end he put a row of short strokes going down, for a stair. Then he drew the path, which he had not seen, as a thread that came in over the top and ended at the shelf's eastern corner.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-54.md:71:Teague looked at it for some time.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-54.md:85:The broad man, who had one leg bound to a board, looked at the ground between his boots. Beyond him an old man stood on a length of ash, as he stood at every halt, with a face that told nothing.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-54.md:87:"You, with the hide," said Teague, without turning. "I said that you'd be asked. Does it go round?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-54.md:89:"The line goes through that gap," said Karis. "I ruled it across two ridges from the back of a bridge, and I have looked along it every hour since. The office's figure is three days from the iron, and it is given along the line. It does not say by which door a person is to come." She had the calfskin under her arm, and did not unroll it. "I can tell you where the place is. I cannot tell you that there is any other way to it than the one in front of you."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-54.md:91:Cael looked at his own drawing. There were two walls, and a floor eight strides wide, and everything that could kill a person stood on the left hand and above. There was no roof in it anywhere, on the thread or on the floor. But he had seen a thing made once, out of nothing, across a road, on which eight hounds had broken like water on a pier; and he had not been able to stop thinking of it since Seln said *shelf*.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-54.md:93:[[TIER-B: "It's through." — the packet gives the two words after "Seln's reconnaissance" and pins no speaker. Staged here: said over the drawing on the shale when the three ways have been priced (back; the team's own path; round) and only the bed is left. Teague could own it (the ruling; his word at the Span's timbers was "Through."), Seln (the close of his report) or Karis (of the line). The paragraphs on either side stand for any of the three — coordinator to supply protected text]]
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-54.md:95:"Then here's what I see in it," said Teague. "The bows I can live with. A bolt's one man hurt, and they've a count of twenty and more between every flight. It's the two heaps. A heap of that size on a bed of that width is a crew gone, and there's no walking through it and no walking under it." He rubbed his jaw with the back of his left hand. "But a rig's one stroke. Once it's fallen it's only a heap. I want the first of them down on nothing."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-54.md:101:He did not look at the old man.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-54.md:103:Cael saw him not do it, since the grey eyes stayed upon a patch of charcoal with a great deal of care. That man had said at a bench, before fifty people, that he would not ask a thing with a month on it to be shown for a count; and it seemed that a captain who will not ask must be careful also where he rests his eyes.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-54.md:105:Cael did not ask either. It was not his knee, and it was not his word that lay on it, since a healer had set that. Nor was the field his.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-54.md:107:He had the like of it in himself. A post had stood in him since a stair under a town, and every line that he came to it asked him the same two words. He had never once given it an answer. He did not know that it would stop a thrown cup, let alone the side of a hill; and a thing that has never been tried is not tried first with eleven people beneath it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-54.md:109:"A wall'd not hold that," said Brom.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-54.md:113:"I'm not saying his. I'm saying any. I've stood under a quarry face when a ledge let go, and I've seen what was left of the shed. Nobody stops a thing like that. Nobody holds a roof up, either." He frowned at the charcoal, and then he put out one thick finger and drew a stroke across the two walls, aslant. "You lean it. A lean-to's not held up by the man who built it. It's leaned on the hill, and the hill carries it, and whatever falls on it runs off down the low side."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-54.md:121:"I believe that I am its next sentence, and that nobody on this rock is willing to say me. I am obliged to all of you for it." He drew a breath through his nose. "Three days ago, at noon, I had it entered that I would not stand a wall before my month was out. It was entered at my own asking, and it is in his book. I was told eleven days more, at a mill, by a person who was entitled to tell me. This morning I have twenty-seven of thirty."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-54.md:125:"I broke your word once, under a town, and I have not walked a mile since without the reminder. I do not propose to break it a second time. I ask whether you will amend it. If you will not, I shall not do the thing; a captain will find some other way, and I shall be perfectly easy."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-54.md:127:Oryn looked at him for a while.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-54.md:137:Cael had known that man refuse to sit before a crew for the whole length of a road, and had been warned beforehand on the one evening when he did. He saw him go down now upon a blanket, before twelve people and a mule, because a healer had said two words; and he saw that it cost him a good deal less than it once had.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-54.md:151:"It's knitting," she said. "It's three days short of done, and it'd tell you so if you stood a wall on it. What I said at the mill is as true this morning as it was then." She did not take her hands away. "How does the thing come down through you? When something strikes it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-54.md:163:It was a small thing to say, and Cael thought that he was perhaps the only person on that flat, besides its owner, who knew the size of it. He had stood two feet from that wall in a cut, and at its foot in a stair; and each time the thing that held it up had been a pair of boots which looked as if they had been let into the ground, and above them a man who had been taught through the whole of a long service that an officer of the floor does not sit.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-54.md:165:"I have held that thing upon eleven fields," he said at last, "and on a road, and in a stair. I have never been otherwise than upright. I do not know that it can be raised by a man who is sitting on the ground."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-54.md:167:"Then you'll find out."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-54.md:169:"On a morning when somebody's shooting," said Lira, under her breath; and across the flat a boy's head came round at words that he had been given once, by a fire, and he went red.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-54.md:171:"And hear the rest, since you asked me fairly," said Oryn. "It'll take everything in you that isn't the knee. I had my hands on you at a mill for the better part of an hour, and I know how much there is. You're not young, and you've walked the country on a stick. When it goes up, you'll drain like a keg with the bung out; and when it comes down, you'll not get up. I can't give that back. It's not a hole in you. It's a spending." She let go of the leg. "The month stands as I counted it. Twenty-seven."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-54.md:177:"I didn't ask it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-54.md:181:Nobody put it to hands, since it had not been that kind of question. A man had asked leave of the one person who could give it, and had been given it at a price, in front of everybody; and a captain who had built a count upon that wall, and had kept his mouth shut about it for nine days, bent down stiffly and picked up his own pack with his left hand.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-54.md:185:"Tell mine what it is to be under one," said Teague. "You've stood by his twice, and I've stood by none."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-54.md:187:He had said nearly the same on a shelf by a fence, two days before, and Cael had answered him in two words. This wanted more.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-54.md:189:His own had come up to the drawing. The tall man stood with his spear grounded, and the broad one leaned on a trestle's leg. The lean old woman had come as well, who was on nobody's count; and the boy was at the front, with his fists shut, looking at a stroke of charcoal as if it might be taken away.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-54.md:193:He looked at a captain and his three, and at an old woman, and he gave them the doctrine in the form that he gave anything which had to be kept.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-54.md:205:It was a deep voice, and unhurried, and Cael had never heard it. He had lost to that man's pole for the whole of a cold term. He had kept the other side of a captain from him at two timbers, and had watched his hand lie upon a boy's breastbone like a gatepost; and in all of that, across a cold term and a road, he had not had one word from him. Lira's chin had come up. Brom had stopped with a strap half buckled.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-54.md:207:The long quiet face was turned to Cael, and it waited.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-54.md:209:He could have drawn something. He had a burnt stick in his fingers, and five people looking at him who had begun to think, somewhere on a road, that a boy with a book generally had the next line; and he found that this was the very reason why he could not hand them one that he had not got.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-54.md:211:"Then I don't know," he said.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-54.md:215:"I've drawn as far as the wall. When it's up, and the first heap's come down on it, there are still three bows on a shelf. There's a second heap, and a stair ninety strides off that goes under both. I've no *and then*. I'd be telling you a tale if I drew one."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-54.md:217:The tall man looked at him for a breath longer. Then he nodded, by the width of a finger, and turned his eyes to his captain.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-54.md:219:"I'll say when I've seen it," said Teague. "That's the trade. Nobody gets the second half of a field until he's standing in the first." He looked round the ring of them. "A wall buys time. It never bought a man anything else, and I'll not pretend that this one will."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-54.md:221:He gave his orders, and they were short: they would go in at the mouth in a knot, along the bank's foot, and not in file. The big one would walk the old man to his place and set him down. The healers would keep the middle, as deep under the thing as it would stand over their heads.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-54.md:237:"She'll stand," said Brom. "There's a nook by the mouth, he says, out of anything's road. I'll hobble her in it myself. She stood a whole cold term in a shed with nothing to look at but a rope."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-54.md:241:"I am carrying what I could not bear to lose," she said. "I find that it is very nearly all of it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-54.md:251:The thing along him had found it from half a mile.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-54.md:253:It did not ask, since it never had; it only went on taking account of whatever stood braced in front of him, and there was more rock in that ridge than it had ever been offered. The old three ribs had begun to speak, low on the left. He walked on them, and thought of a man with an arm on a slat, a stride ahead, who had not been told.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-54.md:257:It was the last water that they would drink on that side, and the skins went along the file from hand to hand. Brom was looking at the lashings on a mule, and the lean old woman at a captain's linen. Teague's boy had come across the yard of stone that lay between the two crews, as he had come once before, with nothing in his hand this time.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-54.md:267:She looked him over, from the collar to the boots, as she looked at a person on the other side of a square of pegs.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-54.md:269:"You're quick. I told you so in a yard. Quick's a fine thing when there's a pole coming. Under one of his, the whole of the trick is that you go nowhere, whatever you see. I stood under it in a stair with a knife in my hand, and I wanted to go six times."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-54.md:273:"Once. I went over a landing. I'd been told that I might."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-54.md:283:He had wanted for the whole of a road to be something besides the cloth. He had said a figure aloud as he stepped over a bar of iron, so that nobody should forget that he was on the count; and Cael, who had spent a great many nights of his own putting figures into a book for much the same reason, had not found it in him to smile.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-54.md:293:The boy went back to his own, where the broad man had his crossbow out of its cover and was turning it over in his hands. Somebody had bound the split stock in the night with a wet thong, turn upon turn, and the thong had dried as hard as horn. Cael did not need to be told whose big chapped fingers had done it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-54.md:295:"It'll loose twice," said the bow, to his captain. "I'd not ask it for three."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-54.md:305:Brom had hobbled the mule in a nook of the rock, fifty yards short, with her nose in a bag of the mill's corn. She had not looked up when they left her.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-54.md:307:They went in along the foot of the bank, in a knot, as a captain had ordered it. On the right hand the pool lay under the sheer wall, as flat as a slate, with the sky in it. On the left the shale went up at a pitch that hurt the neck. Cael looked along the top of it. There was a line of rock against the brightening east, and two long low shapes upon the line, and beside the nearer one a dead tree, quite white, with a rope about its trunk.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-54.md:311:At the Span a man had hung a light in the air and told them to stand. He understood from that silence, better than from anything that Seln had counted, that these had not been posted to ask.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-54.md:315:He had stopped a yard inside the mouth, where a stroke of charcoal had said. He stood for a breath with both hands on the ash and looked at the stones of a dry bed.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-54.md:317:"I once told a man on a road that I should prefer not to be helped to sit," he said. "I find that I must be. I should be obliged if it were done quickly."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-54.md:329:He grunted, and he did not let go. He lowered the old man the last hand's breadth, until he sat square on the bed with his back straight, and only then did he stand up and look at what was in his arm.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-54.md:337:The old man had not looked round. He sat in a stream-bed as he would have sat a bench, upright, with the bound leg laid out before him like a thing left on a table. He shifted the ash to his right hand. He reached it out behind his own hip, without turning his head, and found a place for its foot among the stones.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-54.md:347:In him a post asked its two words of the mouth, as it asked them of any line, and he told it no.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-54.md:351:The second flight struck while he was still looking up.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-54.md:355:Then he could see it, since the dust had shown him where to look. It was a roof, and it had no thickness at all. It began at the stones behind a brown coat, and rose away over eleven people to a high edge against the hill, with the morning coming in under that edge like light under a barn door.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-59.md:5:He counted, since he counted everything, and since a small pale woman had given him a figure to walk to.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-59.md:7:The first twenty strides were over the rows. He did not look down at them. He felt them through the soles of his boots, course after course, each cut as deep as a finger, as a person feels the boards of a bridge; and he kept his eyes upon a star that stood low in the east, a little to the left of where the far rim must be. He had chosen it before he lifted his foot, since there was nothing else out there to steer by.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-59.md:13:He had not known how much noise a plain makes until he was off it. There had been grit under his boots for two days, and the rattle of shale; here there was a small dry sound at each step, and nothing came back from it. The floor was as smooth as the lid of a box, and it had no joint in it, and no bed; he had put his weight on such a thing on the ninety-sixth day, and had found that it was all one piece, and it was the same under him now for as far as he could have walked in any direction.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-59.md:17:There was more light under him than over. That was the thing that he could not get used to. The sky had gone the colour of slate, with the first stars in it, and the plain behind him was black; but the floor kept what the day had given it, and gave it up slowly. He could see his own boots, dark, going one before the other upon a pale ground. He threw no shadow. There was nothing above him bright enough to throw one.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-59.md:19:He did not look back. He had not looked back at the cut, and it had cost him something; there were five people standing at that rim, and a healer, who had each walked the length of a country to it. He had asked them to wait while he went in by himself.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-59.md:27:He went over them, as he might have gone over his pockets. There was a held breath in the soles of his feet, and a deep narrow thing under his breastbone, and a hot grain by the left hand. Something waited, through the whole of his frame, for a load that was worth sending down. There was a lid, which he kept shut. There were lanes, and a thin note, and a short edge; a slow limping current that knew its owner; a post, which had asked its two words at the cut and been told no. There was a rule folded on a shelf behind his eyes. And along him, from the heel to the palm, lay the newest, which had cracked two ribs for him the day before and was listening now to the hardest thing that it had ever stood upon.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-59.md:31:They went round what they had always gone round. He did not go to look at that. He walked.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-59.md:39:He had stood on that when he was fourteen, with a great many people looking on. Every one of them had seen a sigil light for some child before him, and they were waiting to see his. The plate had lit late. He had stood there for the count of eleven with nothing happening, and had learned in those eleven what it is to be on a floor that was made for everybody else.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-59.md:41:He had been walking away from that plate ever since. He saw now, on a pale floor at the far end of the country, with five people waiting behind him at a cut, that he had also been walking toward this.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-59.md:53:He had turned the folded thing upon himself, the night before, on a shoulder of rock, and he had known then that he would do this as well. A reading that has been taken once is a hope; and he had the other half of the comparison lying under his knees, half a mile wide, where nobody would see his eyes afterward.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-59.md:55:He did it on purpose. He meant to write that down, since it was the first time that he had ever looked at a made thing with that tool and meant to; all the others had been taken from him between one breath and the next. He would count it. A thing that is chosen is counted.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-59.md:63:He had known that it would say so, and his heart struck once all the same.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-59.md:71:Then it gave him the shape, with no more words, as it had given it in the dark.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-59.md:77:He knelt there and went over the two of them, as Karis would have laid two sheets side by side with the lamp low. He could find no difference. There was the order of the words, and the flatness of them; there was the thing beneath that carried nothing, and the lean, and no letter. If he had been handed both with his eyes shut, he could not have told which was a floor in a plain and which was a place under his own ribs.

