editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-55.md:23:"Arm," said Oryn. "May I?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-55.md:131:"May I?" said Oryn.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-55.md:325:"Shoulder," Cael heard her say. "May I?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-54.md:141:"May I?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-54.md:143:"You may."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-60.md:51:"Then I shall add one thing to it, which you may enter or not. Those people have reached for us three times. They came up a stair under a town, and they stood to a pair of timbers at a fence, and yesterday they let a hill go." His voice was as mild as ever. "Each time they have gone away again, and each time they knew a little more than they had known. I have never once seen what any of them looks like. I do not think that the third was the last."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-60.md:183:"Knee," she said. "May I?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-60.md:215:She did not look back, as she had not looked back at a fork-stone. Cael watched her go along the rim of the court, and past the third stump, and out onto the plain, until there was only a small square shape above four legs, going steadily north.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-60.md:267:He looked once more at the court.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-59.md:199:"You've been on your knees," said Oryn. "And you've been at the thing that bills your eyes. I can see both from here." She came the last three paces. "I told you last night to ask me again when you'd stood where you were going. You've stood. May I?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-59.md:291:The fire had gone down to a red eye, and the court had not gone down at all.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-56.md:253:"Ribs. May I?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-58.md:33:It lay low. That was what Cael had not been able to picture, for all the nights that he had tried, since he had thought of something that stood up. The ring on the Fallow stood up, and so did the bridge; the Stair went down, and a hall had a roof with a marsh on it. This did neither. The worn rock ran in to an edge, and stopped, and beyond the edge there was a floor. He had seen the court of a great house once, at Ostrand, through a gate, with every door round it shut: a paved place, open to the sky, with nothing standing in it. It was the only thing that he had ever looked at which was at all like this. He called it the court, in his own head, from that hour, for want of any better word.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-58.md:67:"I am told that it is the one thing which I may be relied upon to do," said Vastin.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-58.md:83:Brom walked outside them all, with a skin of water on his good shoulder and his bound arm in his coat. Up on the lip of the dish a tall man stood with a spear, quite still, looking outward. A quarter of the way round, on another swell, a captain stood with his arm on a slat. He had said one thing when he saw the court. He had said that there was no cover on it for a mile, and nobody on it, and that he would see to the rest of the country while they played with string.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-58.md:357:[[TIER-B: the S23 close — "Every other one, yours worked and ours died." / "Yes." / "You think this one's different." / "I think this one's the first one they made," … "I think if any place on the continent is going to tell me what I am, it's the one they built to make sure I'd never be." / He stepped over the line. / His Path did not die. It also did not *notice* it should have. / "Wait here," he said, and did not look back, and walked toward the center of the oldest quiet, alone. — the plan calls this "Lira's fixed exchange" and gives it with one elision, so no speaker is pinned line by line and the break inside the long speech is not shown. Staged here: dusk at the western stump; Lira at Cael's left shoulder, the other four of the six behind them, Oryn by the mule, Teague's three turned away; the first and third lines hers, the second and fourth his; nothing of mine stands between her coming to his shoulder and this unit, and it closes the chapter. OWNER-pending #57's sentence stands inside it as the plan prints it — coordinator to supply protected text]]
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-58.md:133:There was not room for another length. Lira let the eye fall; she took the slip from Karis, and went on along the lip with it, laying it end over end, and saying the figures herself.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-59.md:315:They had a thing among them which he was not told, and he had known that since a spring. *It's six people's*, the boy had said, on a road, and Vastin had found it the most courteous refusal that he had ever been given. He did not guess at it. He had spent a working life guessing at what people kept, with a seal to make them show it, and he had come out here in part to leave off.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-57.md:249:*The gap was kept. Nine, with three bows, on a shelf; two heaps of stone rigged to fall. Twelve of us went in. Eleven came out. Teague's boy went a stride past the edge of the wall, for the bow, and was killed there. I heard his name once. I am not writing it; it was not said to me.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-55.md:41:They did not fall alone. The bank took fire from them, as dry grass takes it. From the top to the bottom the loose shale let go, all at once, like a blanket that somebody has dragged off a bed; and the noise of it was not a noise, but a thing that stood in his chest and shook.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-55.md:67:He had been taught to see a thing that was out of its place. The whole of that slope was out of its place. It lay in the mouth behind him, under a tree. What stood where it had been was a slab of grey rock, as bare as a washed slate, going up at a steep pitch from the stones of the bed to the lip of the shelf. There was not a loose plate left on it from end to end of the gap. The first heap's fall had taken the quilt off the table.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-55.md:221:He did what anybody would have done beside a thing that was going. He let the maul fall, and jumped back from it, flat against the inner wall.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-55.md:245:The frame went, with everything behind it. It did not fall outward, as it had been built to fall. It was put. The uprights, and the prop, and the cartload of loaves went together along the shelf, a body's length, as a drawer goes home into a chest; and they stopped dead in the mouth of the hollow, and came apart there against the back wall, as a load comes apart when the cart stops short. The fire went out under them. A handful of grit ran off the lip, and fell on the bed where two men had passed.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-55.md:261:To the west there were three hoods and a small grey man. One of the three had his arms bound to his sides by a thin cord with a pear of lead on the end of it. One had let his bow fall, and was looking at it. The third had backed against the rock with his hands open. Nobody had a string bent. Seln stood among them with a short knife held low, at his own pace, and he had not touched any of them with it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-55.md:289:Seln had let the cord fall from a pair of arms himself, and stood back; and that one went last, rubbing his wrists. At the cleft he turned his hood toward the shelf for the space of a breath. There was nothing in it. There never had been.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-60.md:277:He had spent the whole year learning that the world was built to prevent him — four hundred years of tiers and registries and a machine that lit for everyone but him, all of it laid down so that what he was would never come again. And at the center of the first place they ever made, the oldest silence on the continent, the one built before there was a word for it, he had felt no exclusion and no prevention and no wall. He had felt at home. He did not have a word for that yet. But he knew, now, walking back out across a floor older than the system that had expelled him, that it was not an accident — that the place built to end him was the one place he fit — and that the difference between *the system was built to exclude me* and *the system was built to prevent me* had a third sentence past it, waiting, that he was not ready to write. Someone had made the pattern. He was, he was beginning to understand, the pattern. That was as far as he could say.

