/bin/zsh -lc "/opt/homebrew/bin/rg -n -i '\\b(year|years|season|spring|summer|autumn|winter|metre|metres|kilometre|kilometres)\\b' books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-{21,22,23,24}.md" in /Users/drive/fractured-path-monroe13
 succeeded in 0ms:
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-24.md:17:Not on himself. On the floor: on the thing under his boots, as Cleon had turned it on a board and a leaf and the inside of a chamber's walls — the eyes to the unfocused place, which on a floor a thousand metres across at dusk was everywhere; the lean found; the thing not described but read. He had turned it on a Span's piers from a stride and had it agree with Cleon's board. He had turned it on the still place against a pack in the dark and had come up with bones and a floor with nothing built on it and an instruction with no language, and had entered the nearest words and marked them nearest. He turned it now on an actual floor with nothing built on it, and let it look, and it looked.
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-24.md:31:He had predicted it. He had written it down the previous night, in the plain hand, in the dark, and had thought, writing it, that it was the truest thing he had put on the page in a year, and he stood at the middle of the oldest quiet anyone had ever made, with the eyes coming back slowly and the floor coming back with them, grey, level, to a rim of stumps a thousand metres round with eleven people at it who were too small to count, and there was nothing.
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-24.md:43:The twelfth had told him, at a hundred strides and against a pack: the floor and the still place read the same, to an instrument built for made things. The same grammar. The same lean. A made thing and a made thing. And between a made thing and a made thing there is no edge, as there is no edge between two stones cut from the same face and laid together by the same hand; the edge is where a made thing meets a thing that was not made, and he had been feeling that edge at every rim for a year and calling it an anomaly, and it was not an anomaly. It was a seam. And there was no seam here, because whatever was under his boots and whatever was under his ribs read, to the one instrument that could read them both, as the same kind of thing, and between the same kind of thing and the same kind of thing there is nothing to feel.
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-24.md:73:She had said she would not read him again until he had stood where he was going, and he had stood there, and he came over the line between two stumps with the straightedge from Denvash in his hand and the eyes nearly back and the ribs saying their one thing, and she was there, a stride outside, with her hands out of her coat, and she said, "May I?" and he said, "Yes," and she put two fingers on his wrist and the flat hand on the ribs and went in, in front of ten people, as she had never once done in a year, because a finding taken the minute after the thing is a different finding from one taken the night before, and she wanted it the minute after.
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-24.md:97:The ledger of the year, at the rim, by a small fire, with the floor dark behind them and the plain dark ahead and the stumps between, in the plain hand.
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-24.md:111:"Spring's over," Teague said.
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-24.md:115:"Next spring." It was not an offer. It was a term, stated once, at a rim, by a man who kept his own ledger and had put a line in it in a stream-bed and was going to walk back past the stone that line was under, and who had said at a plank that it was a contract and not more than that and had been wrong about the *more*, and knew it, and was not going to say so, because a captain does not. "Whatever's on the next line. My crew comes."
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-24.md:127:"Everybody says that." She looked at him once more, the look she had given a knee — a thing read, and priced, and not finished — and then at the ribs, without touching them, and said, "They'll carry. They'll hurt," and did not say the rest, because he knew the difference and she had taught it him. "Spring. Bring the map. Lie still when I say." And she turned the mule's head, and went, square to it, on her terms, which were the only kind, and did not look back, because a healer walking her circuit does not look back at a crew walking off it, and the crew did not need her to, and watched her anyway until the plain had her.
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-24.md:131:He catalogued them at the rim, because it was the last halt of the year and he wanted the count. Lira with the third box and two arms. Brom with the first and the bound forearm and the good one, pricing the stumps a last time by eye, because they were the largest made thing he would ever price and he wanted the number kept. Karis with the case, and the map in the bound hand with five circles on it and a line through all five and nothing past the fifth, folded, and the notebook she had been told not to write in, open. Seln at the plain's edge with the cache, giving the count as he gave it at every halt, to the plain, flatly — "Six. Six and no mule. Nothing behind. Nothing beside." — and not saying *nothing ahead*, because for the first time in a year there was a thing ahead that was not on any line, and Seln did not count what he could not see.
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-24.md:133:And Vastin. On the stick, on the twenty-ninth day of a month, with the sigil on his hand lit again a stride from the stumps and dark a stride inside them, standing with the five as he had stood with them at a fork and a counter and a cut and a shelf and a stream-bed, unadmitted, an ally who had not asked, and did not ask now, and was not asked, because the asking was a thing that would happen on a road, if there was road, and the man had learned in a season what a road was for.
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-24.md:139:He had spent the whole year learning that the world was built to prevent him — four hundred years of tiers and registries and a machine that lit for everyone but him, all of it laid down so that what he was would never come again. And at the center of the first place they ever made, the oldest silence on the continent, the one built before there was a word for it, he had felt no exclusion and no prevention and no wall. He had felt at home. He did not have a word for that yet. But he knew, now, walking back out across a floor older than the system that had expelled him, that it was not an accident — that the place built to end him was the one place he fit — and that the difference between *the system was built to exclude me* and *the system was built to prevent me* had a third sentence past it, waiting, that he was not ready to write. Someone had made the pattern. He was, he was beginning to understand, the pattern. That was as far as he could say.
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-22.md:5:Teague's crew did it. Cael catalogued the order, because it was theirs and he wanted it kept as theirs: the tall woman first, with the spear laid down for the first time in a year and a stone the size of her head lifted in both hands; the broad man on his stick, who could not lift and did, one-handed, small stones, one at a time, from the stream-bed to the place, and did not stop when the older woman with the cloth bag put a hand on his good shoulder, so she took it off; Teague last, with the wrapped arm at his side and the other doing the work of two, laying the stone as he read a job, in order, from the feet up, as if the order mattered, which it did.
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-22.md:15:Eleven, and a mule. He entered the count. It was the first time all year that the count had gone the other way. He had counted up at every halt since a landing in the snow — five, six, twelve — and had thought of the counting as a thing that went one way, as a ledger's lines do, and it did not, and he had known that in the abstract for five years and had it now in the particular, and the particular was a boy with a pack too big for him.
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-22.md:29:"Then it costs what his costs." She took the hand off. "You put a thing through yourself today that goes out along one line and comes back along it, and it came back into the ribs I've been reading since a marsh, because that's where the line stops in you, and it's going to stop there every time, and you know what a wrist looks like after twenty years of it." She did not say *don't*. He noted that she did not. "Lie still when I say. That's still the term. I'm not saying it tonight."
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-22.md:39:Cael could see him not say it. A man who reads his crew in order, face after face, does not stop reading them because there is one fewer; he reads them in the old order and comes to the gap and goes on past it, and the going-on-past has a shape, and Cael had watched the man for a year and could see the shape from across a fire. He did not enter the number either. It was not his to enter.
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-22.md:61:Not by itself, this time. He turned it, as Cleon turned it, with the eyes going to the unfocused place between a thing and himself, which in the dark against a pack was nowhere, and was easy; and with the lean found — the direction a maker puts in a thing; which mark bears, which is borne — and held; and he turned it inward, all of it, toward the middle, toward the place under where a healer's front hand had lain on a table in the autumn, the place the current went round, and let it look.
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-22.md:77:That was the second thing, and it came as the first had, as the nearest shape: a floor. Seamless. Level. Cut to bear a thing that was not on it — the rows all going somewhere and the somewhere empty; the bearers with nothing borne; all of it *ready*, as the Ring had been ready and the Stair had been ready and the Span had been, for four hundred metres of quiet round a made thing, ready for a thing that had not come or had gone. An instruction with no language. He had watched Cleon say that a row was an instruction and that he could not say what it instructed; this was that, and it was inside him, under the ribs that a healer had been reading since a marsh, and it instructed nothing he could read, and it *leaned*.
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-22.md:85:Not the same thing. He had no instrument that could say that and no leave to guess it. The same *kind*. A made thing's inside, read by a Path built for made things, returning what that Path returned when it was turned on a floor: a lean, a structure, a readiness, an instruction it could not read. Cleon had turned his on a chamber's worth of floors and a leaf and a board and had come up with *this is a stair* and had never once said what the stair was for. Cael had turned his on the one place in himself that nothing else could read and had come up with the same grammar, and he sat with that and did not turn it into a sentence, because the sentence was six words long and he had watched a woman refuse a six-word sentence at a fire the night before and had entered the refusal as all that a year had been for.
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-22.md:95:The Arbiter first. He checked it as a man checks a lamp he knows is out: the mark given at the kindling, at fourteen, in a hall, that had lit for every boy on that floor and had not lit for him, and had not lit since, in five years, at any floor, at any line, in any quiet. It was dark. It had always been dark. He went to the place where it should have been and there was the place and nothing in it, as there had been on a floor at the Ring with every other sigil in the party going out at a stride and his not going out because it had never been on. Nothing in him for the Quiet to take. There had never been. He entered *unchanged*, and the date, because a finding that has not changed needs a second date and not a second description.
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-22.md:99:He had the numbers from Lowmarch in the log, from the first week of the winter, in the plain hand, and he read them by the fire's smear before he touched anything: *the yard, forty-two strides for everyone and forty-one and a piece for me, three times; the Ternhall edge touching three and the fourth a hair short in my hand; Hesk's edge a hair long.* A cord and two brass edges that read everyone but him. He had had it then as a hold, with Karis's rule beside it, and he had carried it since, and at the Span, on the crown, Karis had counted a row of sheets near his hands and it had come out a sheet short, again, as at a spring, and she had looked at his hands, once, and said nothing, and he had entered that under the same hold.
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-22.md:107:At Lowmarch a hair. Now more than a hair. A cord a piece short in the winter and a whole stride short tonight. The instruments read wrong for him and for nobody else, and they read *wronger* than they had, and he had two dates and two numbers and a direction, which was a trend, and Hesk had said that a man who catalogues everything eventually needs to note the trend, and he noted it, and could not source it, and did not try. He put the two facts beside each other on the page — *the instruments read wronger; the oldest one is a half-day east* — and did not draw a line between them, because a line was a thing you drew when you had measured both ends, and he had measured one and was going to stand on the other tomorrow, and standing was not measuring either.
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-22.md:115:*Turned the Rune fragment on myself. Cleon said don't. The reading only ever told me the still place was nothing — no flow, no current, a hole where a man should have a middle. The Rune sense says it's not a hole. It says it's *built like something* — like one of those floors with nothing standing on it. Same shape to that fragment as the sites are to Cleon's. I'm not going to write what that might mean, because I don't know, and because the last time I wrote something true in this log a healer made me swear an oath about it. The instruments read wronger for me than they did at Lowmarch. The trend's climbing. My Arbiter's still dark—nothing in me to go quiet, same as always. Tomorrow, the oldest one. I've spent the whole year wanting to stand in the middle of it. I think part of me already knows what I'm going to feel. Nothing. I think I'm going to feel nothing, and I think that's going to be the loudest thing that's ever happened to me.*
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-21.md:23:"And then?" the tall woman said. It was the first thing she had said to any of the six in a year.
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-21.md:53:He heard the cut. A rope, up on the shelf, going as a rope goes when a blade is on it and the weight is waiting, and then the pine — dead, grey, the length of three men — turning on its wedge over the stack's lip and going, and the stack going with it: the stone, head-sized and larger, that nine people had spent a season putting on a shelf for this, coming off the lip in one body and down the scree in a sound that filled the hundred strides wall to wall.
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-21.md:57:He saw it hit. He had seen bolts hit the plane and go off it, and a crossbow's whole spring spent into it at a cut, and he had never seen this: a shelf's worth of stone and a tree coming down a slope at the speed a slope gives a thing, onto a plane a man was holding across a stream-bed with a stick planted and a knee that had been told a month. The plane held. That was what it was for. The stone hit the slant of it and went along the slant, as water goes along a roof, and the fall went down the plane's face, from its high edge at the scree's foot to its low edge at their backs, and off it, and piled — behind them. Across the mouth they had come in by, under the low edge, filling the one stride the plane had left open. The pine on top of it. A cart's width and a half of the pass, closed, with a dead tree across it and the stone to the height of a man, and eleven people and a healer's roll on the floor between that and the shelf, and no way out of the hundred strides but forward, under the rig, past the crossbows, and Vastin, who could not move, at the plane's foot with his face the colour of the stone.
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-21.md:71:He was a third of the way up before Cael found him, because a man who has spent fifteen years not being where he is looked for does not become easy to find on a bare slope; he was flat to it, moving when the dust moved and still when it did not, with the cache on his back and nothing in his hands, on the one slope in the pass the team had ruled out because the team had built it to be ruled out, and he was going up it at the speed of a man who has looked at a thing since noon the day before and decided.
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-21.md:91:Then she took her hands off, and put them on her own knees, and did not say anything, because there was nothing to say that a healer says, and the boy in the stream-bed had stopped being a thing that could be read, and she did not pretend that he had not. That was the first time Cael had seen her hands come off a person with nothing done, in a year of watching them, and he had not known until he saw it that a healer's hands coming off a person was a thing with a sound, and it was no sound at all.
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-21.md:109:The brace went a body's length. Which was his, from heel to palm, and it was enough: the timber went out from under the stack and the stack went, not down the scree, because he had set the line to take it along the shelf and not off it, but *along*, onto the shelf's far end, where three hoods with crossbows had been standing and were not, because a thing that has been holding a shelf for a season and starts to move is a thing that everyone on the shelf moves away from. The high ground was Seln's. The far end was stone. The near end had the shelf's own stair down it, cut for the team's coming and going, and Teague was on the stair with the tall woman, and Lira was past both of them, because Lira was faster than anyone in the pass and had been since a hall.
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-21.md:113:He had known it would. He had it from a card and from a meadow and from a Span, and he had it now: the cost going back the way the push had gone, through the hand and the arm and the shoulder and down, and stopping — not at the wrist, as Teague's did, because he had not been doing it for twenty years and the line had not yet found its weak place in him — in the ribs. The ribs that had been quiet since a marsh. They said so. They said it once, in the register Oryn used for a finding, which was plainly, and he heard it, and did not sit down, because Seln was on the shelf and the stair had people on it.
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-21.md:137:He knelt as he read a job. That was the thing Cael had watched him do since a board's room in the autumn — read his people in order, one face after another, before a thing was decided, and move them to where it was going to be — and he was doing it now, with one of them, on the stone, in the order he had always done it, except that the face did not read back, and the man knelt there anyway with the wrapped arm at his side and the good hand on the boy's chest where a healer's had been, and did not say anything, and did not look up, and the pass let him.
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-23.md:29:He saw it from the last hundred strides, between two stumps, and stopped, and so did everyone, because it was the fourth site and the Ring and the Stair and the Hall laid together and pressed flat: a level, seamless, grey floor, a thousand metres across by a woman's eye, with nothing on it — no ring, no stair going down, no hall's walls, no arch — nothing at all, from the stumps at its rim to the middle, five hundred strides off across it, which looked, from here, like more floor. The rock of the plain came up to the stumps and stopped at the same level as the floor, so that the floor was not raised and not sunk; it was the plain, made into a floor, and the plain had been worn down to be level with it, or the floor had been laid to be level with the plain, and he did not know which and neither did Karis, and she said so.
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-23.md:39:Lira on the line. She had the job from the Ring and the Stair and the fourth: walk the perimeter with her sigil lit, a stride at a time, until it went out, and step back until it came on, and call it. She did it as she had done it at four sites, and it went out at the stride, at a place a pace inside the stumps' inner faces, and she said "Gone," and stepped back, and said "Back," and Karis put a stone down, and they went on. Cord and stride and stone. Brom marking. Seln at the mule with the case, keeping the count of the plain, which was eleven and a mule and nothing else, because there was nothing else, because there was nothing on the plain to hide anything behind, and Seln, who had spent fifteen years using the ground, stood in the one place on the continent where there was no ground to use and did not like it and said so once, to the mule.
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-23.md:83:Vastin. He came to the line last, on the stick, on the twenty-eighth day of a month, and stood at it, at a stumps' width from Cael, and looked at the mark on his own hand, which was dark, and had been dark at four lines this year and had been lit at every other line of his life, and he said the one sentence, and it was not the one Cael had expected, and it was the right one.
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-23.md:85:"I stood at a line for forty years," Vastin said, "and told people which side of it they were. I never once asked who drew it." He looked at the floor. "This is where they drew it."
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-23.md:93:Nobody answered that. He did not, because he did not know what she meant by it and did not want to, yet, and she did not say it again, because a healer says a finding once, and it stood there between the two of them at the line, unanswered, and the book of the year did not have a column for it, and he let it stand.
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-23.md:103:Lira, beside him, said the thing she had said at the Ring, in the autumn, at the first line any of them had ever stood at, and she said it as she had said it then, plainly, to him.

